(1941, 1, 'Weekly Schedule', 'Reading U3', 49, '', '<h2>Rozvrh na tĂ˝den</h2>        <h3>Fill in ÂdictionaryÂ (nominative)forms of the days of the week using case chart</h3>        <p><img width=\"338\" height=\"457\" src=\"weekly1.gif\">        <img width=\"249\" height=\"455\" src=\"weekly2.gif\"></span></p>        <h3>Fill in the schedule according to text (use case charts to determine nominative forms of items)</h3>        <table border=\"1\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" width=\"100%\">          <tr>            <td width=\"52\">              <p align=\"center\"> </p>            </td>            <td width=\"80\">              <p align=\"center\"><b>9:00-10:00</b></p>            </td>            <td width=\"80\">              <p align=\"center\"><b>10:00-11:00</b></p>            </td>            <td width=\"80\">              <p align=\"center\"><b>11:00-12:00</b></p>            </td>            <td width=\"80\">              <p align=\"center\"><b>12:00:13:00</b></p>            </td>            <td width=\"80\">              <p align=\"center\"><b>13:00-14:00</b></p>            </td>            <td width=\"80\">              <p align=\"center\"><b>14:00-15:00</b></p>            </td>            <td width=\"80\">              <p align=\"center\"><b>15:00-16:00</b></p>            </td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td width=\"52\">              <p align=\"center\"><b>PO              </b></p>            </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td>              <p align=\"center\">obÄd             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td width=\"52\">              <p align=\"center\"><b>ĂT              </b>            </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td>              <p align=\"center\">obÄd            </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td width=\"52\">              <p align=\"center\"><b>ST              </b>            </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td>              <p align=\"center\">obÄd            </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td width=\"52\">              <p align=\"center\"><b>ÄT              </b>            </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td>              <p align=\"center\">obÄd            </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>          </tr>          <tr>            <td width=\"52\">              <p align=\"center\"><b>PĂ              </b>            </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td>              <p align=\"center\">obÄd            </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>            <td valign=\"top\">  [$1, text 10]             </td>          </tr>        </table>        <h3>Complete these        tasks:        </h3>        <p>Find the words <i>d</i><i>Älat,        po </i>in the dictionary and trace the origins of        appropriate names of weeks.        <p> [$1, textarea 90,2]         <p>From what words are <i>Ätvrtek,        pĂĄtek, sobota </i>derived?        <p> [$1, textarea 90,3]         <p>Guess what the word <i>st</i><i>Ĺed        </i>means from the name of one of the days.  [$1, text 90]  ', 3, NULL),
(1942, 6, 'Pronunciation Summary', '', 49, 'Pronunciation Summary', '<P>Czech prounciation is not particularly difficult. About as hard as it is to pronounce any language, if it does not happen to be the one you grew up with. It is because your muscles just find it hard to adjust to new things. It is true, however, that pronunciation is often the most difficult part for adult learners, and very rarely do they achieve native-like accent.</P>\r\n<P>It is still important to practice pronunciation when learning Czech because Czechs tend to value correct pronunciation over grammar. A student of Czech will have an easier time trying to converse with the natives when observing the correct pronunciation patterns.</P>\r\n', 3, NULL),
(1943, 6, 'Vowels', '', 49, 'Vowels', '<P><SPAN lang=CS>There are a few peculiarities in Czech that tend to cause problems for English-speaking mouths. The most troublesome is the pronunciation of <B>vowels</B>. First, no matter where they are in the word, Czech vowels never loose their composure, and are always pronounced fully and unflaggingly. Second, each Czech vowel can be either short or long. Long vowels are exactly two times longer than their short brothers. It is very frequent that vowel length changes meaning of words significantly. For instance, <I>dÄl<U>a</U> </I>(cannons) is differentiated from <I>dÄl<U>ĂĄ</U> </I>(he is working) only by the long <I>ĂĄ </I>in the latter, and there are even more embarrassing examples.<I> </I>The length of vowels is completely independent on the <B>stress</B>. The stress is unexceptionably on the first syllable in all Czech words. It is a very frequent phenomenon that a long vowel occurs in an unstressed position like in <B><I>dÄ</I></B><I >l<U>ĂĄ</U></I>. There are two diphthongs <I>ou, au </I>and <I>eu </I>which are pronounced fully, too.</SPAN></P>', 3, NULL),
(1944, 6, 'Consonants', '', 49, 'Consonants', '<P class=NormalFirst style=\"MARGIN: 0cm 0cm 0pt\"><SPAN lang=CS>Much less troublesome are Czech <B style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\">consonants</B>. There are many that are the same as or very similar to those in English and in Czech. There are five, however, that are not in English at all: <I style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: normal\">ch, Ä, Â, Ĺ,<B style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\"> Ĺ</B></I></SPAN></P>\r\n<UL>\r\n<li><EM>of the above <B style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\">Ĺ</B> is the most feared one; it is famous for its quirky nature and soft gurgling sound; it is the sound that Czech children learn the last (around 5 Â 6Â years), and a certain portion of the population, including President Havel never learn to do it quite right; except for its fame and difficulty (it has been claimed that Czechs invented it deliberately to make Czech harder to learn) <B style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\">Ĺ</B> has little importance for learning the language, and many Czechs will be disappointed if you learn it well, which you very likely will </EM>\r\n<li><EM><B style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal\">ch </B>is the same as in Scottish </EM><SPAN style=\"FONT-STYLE: normal\">loch,<EM> but mispronouncing will cause no disasterous misunderstandings;</SPAN></EM>\r\n<LI><EM><B>Ä</B>, <B>Â</B>, <B>Ĺ</B>, on the other hand have no claims to fame but play much more important role in the language because they often distinguish between the different meanings of similar words</EM>\r\n</UL>\r\n<P>Much more important are sounds which are in English, too, but are pronounced just a little differently in Czech. For instance, <I>k, p, t </I>don\'t have aspiration, so it will be difficult for you to distinguish them from <I>g, b, d</I> when they are the first sound in a word. Czech <I>r</I>\'s are rolled very much like the Scottish <I>r</I>\'s. Finally, unlike English, Czech <I>h</I> is voiced, which, I dare say, makes it the most difficult of Czech sounds for English speakers. Other than that, there\'s nothing in Czech that will surprise you.</P>', 3, NULL),
(1945, 6, 'Assimilation', '', 49, '', '<P><SPAN lang=CS>There are a few things that happen to individual sounds when put together. Especially consonants are vulnerable. If two consonants meet, the second always makes the first change its voicedness.<A href=#_ftn1 title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn1\" name=_ftnref1><SPAN class=MsoFootnoteReference><SPAN style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\">(1)</SPAN></SPAN></A> For instance, in <I>kde</I> the voiced <U>d</U> changes the unvoiced <U>k</U> so that it is pronounced as voiced <U>g</U>. The pause is unvoiced so ending consonants are always pronounced unvoiced. Additionally, the following word always forces the change on the final consonant of the preceding word. Voiced consonants without an unvoiced pair (i.e. <I>m, n, l, r, j</I>) and <I>vÂ </I>do not usually trigger assimilation.</SPAN></P>\r\n<DIV style=\"mso-element: footnote-list\"><BR clear=all>\r\n<HR align=left width=\"33%\" SIZE=1>\r\n\r\n<DIV id=ftn1 style=\"mso-element: footnote\">\r\n<P class=MsoFootnoteText style=\"MARGIN: 3pt 0cm 0pt\"><A title=\"\" style=\"mso-footnote-id: ftn1\" href=\"#_ftnref1\" name=_ftn1><SPAN class=MsoFootnoteReference><SPAN style=\"mso-special-character: footnote\"><FONT size=2>(1)</FONT></SPAN></SPAN></A><FONT size=2> Voicedness is a feature of consonants which is present in next to all languages. A consonant is <I>voiced</I> if you produce a vibration in your larynx while pronouncing it e.g. <I>v</I>, and <I>unvoiced </I>if you don\'t e.g. <I>f</I>. Voiced and unvoiced consonants are pared together. See the <I>How to pronounce</I> section for recorded examples.</FONT></P></DIV></DIV>', 3, NULL);
in   making Airbuses. But Daimler\'s aerospace </p>                        <p>Information   should flow up the overseas</p>                        <p>Sun is still trying to work   out how to get these </p>                        <p>Take a multinational   company, give it 30 </p>                        <p>of firms within Hanson. The   use of offshore</p>                        <p>talk   of Euro-managers, he expects the group\'s </p>                        <p>South   African style, it also owns large stakes in</p>      </td>      <td width=19>              [$1,text 20]<br>       </td>      <td width=302>                     <p>,   now called Deutsche Aerospace, want to take</p>                        <p>to   the parent; decisions would be decentralised</p>                        <p>to   work independently but co-operatively.</p>                        <p>, a staff of 50,000 and a   budget for its main unit</p>                        <p>complicates things further.   In 1989 Hanson under</p>                        <p>will continue to be managed   largely by locals</p>                        <p>outside mining, which are   worth another 9 billion</p>      </td>     </tr>            </tbody>   </table>      <p align=center><br> </p>      <p>Â </p>      <p><b>6Â Â Â Â Â Â  Bid types.</b></p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Magnetic\'s board rejected TT\'s bid as `derisory, unsolicited, unwelcome,  and totally inadequateÂ´. Match these adjectives, and others describing takeover  bids, to their meanings.</p>      <p>Â </p>   <p>If a bid is abortive it is [/unsought/unwanted/fought against/insultingly  low/not enough/unsuccessful/improved].</p>                                                         <p>If a bid is contested it is [/unsought/unwanted/fought against/insultingly  low/not enough/unsuccessful/improved].</p>                                                         <p>If a bid is derisory it is [/unsought/unwanted/fought against/insultingly  low/not enough/unsuccessful/improved].</p>                                                         <p>If a bid is inadequate it is [/unsought/unwanted/fought against/insultingly  low/not enough/unsuccessful/improved].</p>                                                         <p>If a bid is sweetened it is [/unsought/unwanted/fought against/insultingly  low/not enough/unsuccessful/improved].</p>                                                         <p>If a bid is unsolicited it is [/unsought/unwanted/fought against/insultingly  low/not enough/unsuccessful/improved].</p>                                                         <p>If a bid is unwelcome it is [/unsought/unwanted/fought against/insultingly  low/not enough/unsuccessful/improved].</p>                                                         <p>Â </p>        <h3><b>7Â Â Â Â Â Â  Giants and midgets. </b></h3>        <p>Complete this extract using the expressions listed.</p>      <p>(<b>a</b> occurs four times, <b>c</b> three times and the others once each.)</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p><b>a</b> junk bonds Â Â  <b>c</b> corporate Â Â Â Â  <b>e</b> takeovers </p>      <p><b>b</b> junk bond Â Â Â  <b>d</b> equity Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  <b>f</b> leverage</p>      <table border=1 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0>     <tbody>       <tr>      <td width=611 valign=top>                     <h1>ACQUISITION VIA JUNK</h1>                        <p><b>Â </b></p>                        <p>During the 1980s, new issues of [$1,text 12] multiplied   tenfold as more  and more companies issued large quantities of low-grade debt   to financeÂ [$1,text 12]  or defend themselves against being taken over. The   development of this market for low-gradeÂ [$1,text 12] bonds was largely the   brainchild of the investment banking firm Drexel Burnham Lambert.</p>                        <p>Â </p>                        <p>The result was that for the first timeÂ [$1,text 12]   midgets were able to take control of [$1,text 12] giants, and they could finance   this activity by issues of debt. However, issuers ofÂ [$1,text 12] often had   debt ratios of 90 or 95 per cent.</p>                        <p>Â </p>                        <p>Many worried that these high levels ofÂ [$1,text 12]   resulted in undue risk and pressed for legislation to ban [$1,text 12]. Between   1986 and 1988 Campeau  Corporation amassed a huge retailing empire by   acquiring major department  store chains such as Federated Department Stores   and Allied Stores.</p>                        <p>Â </p>                        <p>Unfortunately, it also amassed $10.9 billion in   debt, which was  supported by just $l billion ofÂ [$1,text 12]. So when in   September 1989 Campeau  announced that it was having difficulties meeting the   interest payments  on the debt, theÂ [$1,text 12] market took a nose-dive and   worries about the riskiness  ofÂ [$1,text 12] intensified.</p>                        <p>Â </p>      </td>     </tr>            </tbody>   </table>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Â </p>      <h3>8Â Â Â Â Â Â  Company relationships. </h3>      <p><b>Â </b></p>      <p>The following excerpt, from the <i>Financial Times, </i>looks at the whole  area of strategic alliances. It also uses the language of personal relationships.  Match the expressions to their definitions and then use the expressions to  complete the article, rearranging the paragraphs into a logical order. </p>      <p>(One of the expressions is used twice.)</p>      <p>Â </p>   <p><b>1</b> alliances - [/people involved in a sexual or romantic   relationship/relationships  formed between countries or   organizations/insect trap known for being very  difficult to   get out of/sexual or romantic relationships/relationships lasting only a short time]            </p>   <p> </p>            <p><b>2</b> lovers - [/people involved in a sexual or romantic   relationship/relationships  formed between countries or   organizations/insect trap known for being very  difficult to   get out of/sexual or romantic relationships/relationships lasting only a short time]            </p>            <p><b>3</b> liaisons- [/people involved in a sexual or romantic   relationship/relationships  formed between countries or   organizations/insect trap known for being very  difficult to   get out of/sexual or romantic relationships/relationships lasting only a short time]            </p>            <p><b>4</b> one-night standsÂ - [/people involved in a sexual or romantic   relationship/relationships formed between countries or   organizations/insect  trap known for being very difficult to   get out of/sexual or romantic relationships/relationships  lasting only a short time]            </p>            <p><b>5</b> spider\'s webÂ - [/people involved in a sexual or romantic   relationship/relationships  formed between countries or   organizations/insect trap known for being very  difficult to   get out of/sexual or romantic relationships/relationships lasting only a short time]            </p>        <p>Â </p>      <table border=1 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0>     <tbody>       <tr>      <td width=611 valign=top>                     <p align=center><b>DANGEROUS [$1,text 20]</b></p>                        <p align=center><b>Â </b></p>                        <p>[$1, select:/1/2/3]<br>  Equally, does IBM really understand where it   figures in Toshiba\'s strategy  of [$1,text 20] with all and sundry? The same   applies within the US itself, to IBM\'s place in Apple\'s growingÂ [$1,text 20] of   relationships -Â  Â­and vice versa.</p>                        <p><b>Â </b></p>                        <p>[$1, select:/1/2/3]<br>   AMD,   in its trumpeting of this week\'s agreement with Fujitsu, seemed to take   little account of the Japanese company\'s extensive network of relationships    with western companies, including ICL, Siemens and Amdahl. Does it understand    how it features, and could be manipulated, in Fujitsu\'s global battle against    IBM?</p>                        <p>Â </p>                        <p>[$1, select:/1/2/3]<br>   [$1,text 20] may give companies unprecedented flexibility. But, by the same token,  they render them unstable.   To revert to analogies from personal life, it  is far easier to control one\'s   destiny if one has just a couple of mutually  compliantÂ [$1,text 20] than if one tries   to run a series of ever-changingÂ [$1,text 20]</p>                        <p>Â </p>                        <p>Â </p>      </td>     </tr>            </tbody>   </table>           <br> ', 1, NULL),
