(915, 13, 'Matt Blewett', 'matthew.blewett@tiscali.cz', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'See Scott\'s comment.  I can try sending you some mp3\'s which may be helpful, if you\'re interested give me your email address.', '2003-04-04 06:04:40', 912, 1),
(916, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'v centru?', 'I learned yesterday that when saying something like \" ObÄd v centru je asi 150 Kc nebo 250 Kc\", that the \'v\' is not pronounced with a v sound but rather with a slight \'s\' sound.  My question is this: is it used like this in formal Czech as well or only when using every day speach?  Also, when there is another word that starts with a \'c\' following a \'v\' is it also pronouced the same way using the \'s\' sound?\r\nThanks!\r\nScott', '2003-04-05 02:43:15', 0, 0),
(917, 15, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'News to me (Re: v centru?)', 'Hi Scott,\r\n\r\nIf \"v\" before \"c\" is pronounced similarly to \"s\", that\'s news to me! My experience - and textbook doctrine - has been that v changes to f before, among other things, c, but I\'m open to learn new things.\r\n\r\nFor the rules governing the changing of voiced consonants to unvoiced, see http://www.bohemica.com/czechonline/reference/pronunciation/contrasts.htm. \r\n\r\nThis page covers the issue perfectly except for ignoring the fact that some times, the second consontant does not govern the first in Bohemia, but still sometimes does in Moravia. Not sure what sets these siutations apart, I mean, I\'m not sure if there\'s any logic in it. The most famous example is \"na shledanou\", which is pronounced as \"na schledanou\" in Bohemia, but sometimes? often? pronounced \"na zhledanou\" in Moravia. Theoretically it should be standard Moravian pronunciation, but I have trouble hearing it. Maybe I just have a tin ear.\r\n\r\nBy the way, if you have any Czech friends with whom you speak frequently, you can often hear the voiced/unvoiced conversions in practice in their English. For example, if you meanly force them to say \"I put my hat on my head\", it may come out as \"I put my het on my het.\" In other words, besides changing short a -- which is nonexistent in Czech -- to short e, they might unconsciously apply the voiced-at-end-of-sentence-is-unvoiced rule, changing the \"d\" to a \"t\". This also happens a lot with the \"z\" in the pronunciation of English plurals: \"I bought some bunss\" instead of \"I bought some buns.\" What\'s interesting is that, as far as I can tell, there is the occasional voiced/unvoiced conversion, or at least pair, in English: \"She owns catss.\" vs. \"She owns catz and (or: catzand) dogs.\"\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-04-07 16:18:38', 916, 1),
(918, 13, 'Panagiotis Asilanis', 'asilan@LAR.FORTHNET.GR', 'Translation', 'I know a girl who is from Prague. Please help me how can I say to her :\r\n1) I like you very much \r\n2) Would you like to go out with me ?\r\n\r\nSend me the answers and also the way they are pronunciated. I thank you in advance Panagiotis ', '2003-04-08 01:32:32', 0, 0),
(919, 15, 'Jakub', '', 'Re: v centru?', 'It must be definitely a missspell/misshear. There is no reason to pronounce \"s\" in this position.', '2003-04-08 10:50:20', 916, 0),
(920, 13, 'Jakub', '', 'Re: Translation', '1) MĂĄm tÄ moc rĂĄd. [ma:m tje mots ra:t]\r\n2) Âla bys se mnou ven? [Âla bys se mnou ven] (question - raising intonation)\r\nor Âla by ses se mnou projĂ­t? [Âla by ses se mnou proji:t] (question - raising intonation)\r\n\r\n[Â] = like \"sh\" in english', '2003-04-08 10:58:59', 918, 0),
(921, 15, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: News to me (Re: v centru?)', 'V odstavci \"Voiced vs. unvoiced\" (.../contrasts.htm) chybĂ­ jeÂtÄ jedna dvojice:\r\n\r\n    afrikĂĄty   c - dz', '2003-04-08 14:09:31', 917, 0),
(922, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: News to me (Re: v centru?)', 'I agree Erik.  It was a surprise to me as well.  My fiance corrected me with this when I was asking her a question.  She said to pronounce the v as in \"v centru\" as an \'s\' or more of a \'vs\' sound.  One factor may be that she is from Morava.  Vsetin to be exact, where many people speak with Moravian accents :).  That is one factor that affects my Czech haha!  She doesn\'t pronounce the voiced/unvoiced sounds at the end of English words as much now that she has lived in London for 2 years, but when I first met here she did quite a bit.  However, I have friends in Czech who do.  They all laugh at my funny American accent when I speak Czech, because of the fact that I do the same thing.  \r\n\r\nThat none of you have heard of this rule before, maybe it is just Moravian slang.  What do you think?  I was under the impression that when you say this that you would make an \"f\" sound as you have all stated, but she confides that it is easier and more normal to make a \'vs\', or perhaps the way she made it sound it was \'fs\', sound before beginning the next word when it starts with a \'c\'.  Is it common when one is not paying attention to anunciation to make a sound like this when saying \'v centru\'?  Any comments or suggestions are greatly appreciated.  I would rather speak proper than with a Moravian accent :).\r\nThanks.\r\n\r\nScott  ', '2003-04-10 00:36:08', 917, 0),
(923, 13, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'Wow Erik!!!  Thank for the advice about not relating any one word in Czech to another in English.  That is so right that I always ahve to think outside the box.  I didn\'t expect such a vivid explanation to that simple question.  You are awesome! \r\n\r\nThanks again,\r\nScott.', '2003-04-10 01:03:55', 893, 0),
(924, 15, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: News to me (Re: v centru?)', 'Hello Scott,\r\n\r\nActually, the \"Moravian\" accent is kind of like the \"US Southern\" accent - if you live there, you find it\'s actually a collection of accents. (I personally live in Brno. Admittedly, it\'s not a typical Moravian dialect region - it\'s kind a mix of standard spoken Czech and Brno idiosyncrasies. They\'re mostly in vocabulary, but in a thick Brno \"hantec\" accent there are pronunciation quirks, like Ăł for ou, as well.)\r\n\r\nI have heard a lot of curious pronunciations, but have yet to have heard \"vsentru\" or \"fsentru\". I would expect \"ftsentru\" just about anywhere. Keep in mind that the \"ts\" is kind of on the border with being alien to English - of course we have t, and we have s, but the transition between them in the Czech \"c\" is so fast that it kind of feels different than \"just\" ts. It helps to think of it one sound, even though it \"is\" two.\r\n\r\nAlso keep in mind that joining of one-letter prepositions and the words following them is standard Czech pronunciation (on all language levels), and it might be what your gf was pointing to. \"fcentru,\" \"fpraze,\" \"vbrnÄ,\" \"zhoĹÄicĂ­,\" \"spepĹem,\" \"skama seÂ?\" (\"odkud jsi?\" - prob. Moravia-specific - z kam-a seÂ), \"zbrna,\" etc. etc.\r\n\r\nBy the way, there\'s nothing wrong with speaking with a regional accent. It impresses some people when your acquaintance with the language is visibly tied to some real language-region, and not an imaginary \"national accent.\" At least in the first years after the Revolution, this was all the more true because textbooks taught spisovnĂ˝ Czech and acted like you could work with it in real life. When I started forcing people to speak with me to get my learning past the textbooks, I felt like I was learning the language all over again. NO j\'s at the beginning of to-be, HARDLY ANY ĂŠ endings (replaced by Ă˝), COUNTLESS weird yet ubiquitous endings like the dual-number -ama on tacked onto just about anything a speaker feels like (s kĂĄmoÂama), CONSTANT \"zeÂivotnÄnĂ­ neÂivotnĂŠho\" as I call it even with totally \"unjustifiable\" situations - \"mĂĄÂ s sebou mobila?\" - let alone ones with some excuse - \"kup mi ledĹĂĄka [chocolate-covered cream pop]!\", \"dĂĄm si panĂĄka [a shotglass or, here, a shot],\" \"naÂel jsem kozĂĄka [a mushroom type],\" etc. etc. \r\n\r\nOne more example that deserves its own sentence: WEIRD-ASS **androgynous** say-what-the-foreigner-isn\'t words, like hadr/hadra [rag]. (Me: mĂĄÂ hadru? Czech: ne, hadr nemĂĄm. Me, one month later: mĂĄÂ hadr? Czech: ne, hadru nemĂĄm.) Fortunately, the only examples I know are hadr and ĹĂĄdek/radka. Well, I do encounter one other pair, prostor/prostora, but apparently it has some logic to it and it isn\'t all that common anyway.\r\n\r\nSorry for the long aside. In short - don\'t fret about speaking perfectly, don\'t worry about picking up local quirks, and fight the good fight against the lazy urge of both you and your Czech friends to speak English, because even today, you\'ll probably be missing out on a lot until you move past textbooks.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-04-10 16:43:20', 922, 1),
(925, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Translation', 'Hi Panagiotis,\r\nInteresting point, my perspective is slightly different from Jakubs. It\'s probably just me, but if you\'re trying to ask her out, I assume you\'re not (yet) on very intimate terms, so I personally would be a bit careful with expressing \'I like you\' in Czech (\'mit rad\' feels to me quite strong, I might run if I was told by someone I didn\'t know well... but again, that\'s me, she might like it, depends). \r\nA clever evasion here might be either a comment on something (a feature) you like about her or just saying that you like being/doing xy with her, which could then lead nicely to the invitation (again, I personaly would feel more comfortable to accept if you invited me \'somewhere\' not just vaguely \'out\').\r\nWhat do you think? Have you already asked her or would you like some additinal translation (how are you actually going to communicate on the date?)\r\nAnyway, fingers crossed,\r\nJana', '2003-04-10 21:17:42', 918, 0),
(926, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: News to me (Re: v centru?)', 'Hello Erik,\r\n\r\nThank you so much for your help.  I cant tell you how much your advice has opened my eyes to some things.  I guess you could say that I am not looking inside of the box anymore :).  \r\n\r\nI do understand what you mean about the Southern Accent, as I live in Texas and have been here since I was 5.  I was born in England, and had a suffolk accent then when I came here I lost that and gained a Texan accent.  After much work, my father broke me of that and now I guess I have a slight way of pronouncing some words like a Texan, but for the most part I sound like a midwestern speaker.  So, being under so many accent changes I have analized our language and find it intriguing.  Especially Texan slang as it is a mixture of British, Southern, and Mexican accents.  People here say things like \"Ya heard \'bout that game t\'morra. -Yup.- Ya \'anta go? -Dunno- Ya\'an to? -Aight.\" It sounds funny, but when you disect these words, they are a mix of different variations that have been adapted over the past few centuries.  I enjoy figuring out how a certain dialect and words in that dialect came to be.  \r\n\r\nI said all of that to say that you are right and since I am more than likely going to have a Moravian accent, I should appreciate its distinctness.  It is hard however to do much talking with a native at all right now as I am still here.  However, in August I will be moving to Norwich where I will have more opportunities to speak Czech.  A few years from now I plan to move to Czech (more than likely Moravia),  upon where I plan to be an English teacher.  \r\n\r\nI am looking forward to moving past the textbooks and actually having conversations.  Thanks for all the help and encouragement.  Oh, yes I do the \"ts\" sound in \'c\' down.  My pronunciations are pretty good, despite the occassional studdering around the \'Ĺ\' in some words.  It is not hard to pronouce the sound itself, but it is hard when it is in certain letter orders to do so, as my mouth just doesn\'t seem to form into some positions :).  I thought it was funny how one computer store salesman in ZlĂ­n told me that my \'Ĺ\' sounded the same way his 6 year old son pronounced it. \r\n\r\nSorry for my large aside, but maybe that helps you to understand my situation.\r\nHave a great day!\r\nScott', '2003-04-11 01:56:52', 924, 0),
