(1251, 13, 'Cyndi', 'cdwyer@lps.org', 'Re: phrase', 'Kevin,\r\n\r\nThanks for the information.  Could you please post it with the haceks so I can have it properly inscribed.  I will just give it as a late present.  Thanks so much.\r\n', '2003-12-30 09:18:16', 1246, 0),
(1252, 13, 'don', 'donek@xs4all.nl', 'Re: help - translation', 'Thanks, Jakub, I rather like the second version and will use it.\r\nIf I can help anybody with English or Dutch, don\'t hesitate to mail.\r\nHappy New Year!\r\nDon', '2003-12-30 15:34:06', 1249, 1),
(1253, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Levou botu', 'hi mark!\r\ni think it\'s got to do with something called \"dokonavost\", i can\'t think of a direct parallel in english but it\'s got to do with distinguishing wether or not the action carried by the verb has been finished or not (possibly, it can also refer to one-time vs. repetitive action, to be sure pls look up the precise definition, i\'d hate to confuse you). \r\n\r\nhere the meaning of the sentence is +/- \"he\'s put his shoe on his left foot and now he\'s putting on the right one\" but in more word-for-word translation the left shoe has been already put on and now the right is being put on.\r\n\r\nwhat exactly do you mean by \"verbs like...\"? i\'d say \"obouvat\" is used quite frequenly (well, as frequently as one needs to refer to the action). if you meant the \"obout\" vs \"obouvat\" difference, i\'d say some verbs can be modified this way to mark the timing of the action like (\"obleknout x oblekat\" - put on/wear about clothing, \"obout x obouvat\" is only for shoes) but some other verbs would sound funny/oldfashioned.\r\n\r\ni hope this helps a bit (this is quite advanced stuff, congrats and fingers crossed). happy new year,\r\njana', '2003-12-30 15:34:15', 1247, 0),
(1254, 13, 'mark', 'mh@oosterhof.be', 'Re: Levou botu', 'thanks for the reply, Jana.\r\nPfff, Czech is really difficult. What I meant with \"verbs like..\": In Dutch we have an equivalent for the verb obleknout-oblekat (zich aankleden)which is frequently used. We also have an equivalent for the verb obout - obouvat (zich schoeien = which is only used for shoes)but in Dutch \"zich schoeien\" sounds oldfashioned. These little differences between languages make the study often hard. \r\nHappy Newyear\r\n', '2003-12-30 16:48:43', 1253, 1),
(1255, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: phrase', 'Cyndi, with czech keyboard now working, mĂĄme rĂĄdi naÂe vnoÄata hopefully is correct along with your name', '2003-12-31 00:02:16', 1251, 0),
