(1274, 13, 'Hanka', '', 'setkani mozne az do soboty', '...pokud by stale mel nekdo zajem, jsem v Glasgowe az do soboty 1.2. Rada bych si popovidala:)', '2004-01-27 14:02:50', 1265, 0),
(1276, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: anglicismy v cestine..', 'Kdyz by nikdo neprisel s \"trefou do cerneho\", asi bych doporucil navstevu web stranek z oboru a trochu primeho vyzkumu. Budete muset sama sestavit nejake kriteria, podle kterych odlisete \"vicemene univerzalne\" anglicismy od \"zargonu jen nekterych\". Asi bude nejjednodussi pouzivat jako zdroj jen stranky o webu psane pro laiky.\r\n\r\nSefredaktorkou casopisu Computer byla a mozna jeste je byvala prekladatelka, nebo presneji receno zargonem lokalizatorka, software a i moji byvalou kolegyni (i kdyz jsme se temer neznali). Dokonce i psavala kdysi (a mozna jeste pise) o jazyce a pocitacich do Computru. Mozna Vam pomuze.', '2004-01-30 18:42:37', 1264, 0),
(1277, 13, 'Markos', 'jrs@bigvalley.net', 'Czech for exchange program...', 'Hey everyone! \r\nI hope you don\'t mind me bugging you.\r\nI am a high school student in California and have sparked an interest in spending a year abroad with the AFS (an American foreign exchange program). My attention was drawn to the Czech Republic because I thought it would be neat to see where some of my family came from. However, when I did some research on the language, I became a bit discouraged because the grammar appears to be very difficult! I speak German, but I didn\'t think that it would help because it isn\'t a Slavic language like Czech. I\'ve have always heard that immersing yourself in a culture will drastically improve learning a language, but I was wondering if any of you might be able to give some input. During my stay there, would it take months before I would be able to communicate effectively? Is Czech a language that doens\'t take too long to \"catch on\" to? Any advice would be much appreciated...thank you!', '2004-01-31 04:44:53', 0, 0),
(1278, 13, 'Jakube', '', 'Valentines Day!', 'Can somebody please translate \"Happy Valentines day, my crazy little gypsy !\" into Czech for me ??\r\n\r\nThanks very much,\r\n\r\nJakube', '2004-02-02 01:07:08', 0, 1),
(1279, 13, 'mynamemilan@seznam.cz', '', 'Re: Czech for exchange program...', 'If I were in your place,I would come to the Czech republic without any knowledge of the language.If you speak German than you must know DIE ĂBUNG MACHT DEN MEISTER.And practising here is the best thing you can do.If you have any questions,send me it,I\'ll be helpful.Cheers.', '2004-02-03 04:08:28', 1277, 0),
(1280, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: conjugations -- la plej utila pagxo', 'V ÄasovĂĄnĂ­ ÄeskĂ˝ch sloves jsou chyby. Jedna je systematickĂĄ: u vÂech sloves chybĂ­ 3. os. sg. ind.', '2004-02-03 20:43:08', 1242, 0),
(1281, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech for exchange program...', 'hi markos,\r\n\r\nyou\'re right, it takes time to cope with a new langugage. it\'s hard to say how much time it could take you (took me ages and still does) but your german would probably help quite a bit (czech has gramatical gender concept as german and 7 cases on the top of it. czech morpology is quite complex but spelling is much more phonetic than in english. also, it\'s relatively flexible in wordorder). \r\n\r\nthe good news is that many czechs speak english or german (i\'d say most young people english and some of the older german) and there\'s a large english speaking community in prague (why don\'t you have a look on the expats pages?).\r\n\r\nthe best advice i can think of is to consider what exactly you expect from your stay and how comfortable you feel about coping with language (and culture) barrier.\r\n\r\ngood luck,\r\njana', '2004-02-04 15:46:00', 1277, 0),
(1282, 17, 'Marco Bucchianeri', 'bucchia85@inwind.it', 'Pomoc! Help!', 'Hi! I\'m an italian guy, and I\'m interested in learning Czech language, because on April I\'ll go to Prague in a school trip and I want to know the local speech, and I want to speak it as best as I can.\r\n\r\nTo the creator of this site: I find your grammar very completed and I\'m learning all about nouns, adjectives, verbs, pronouns, and now I\'m ready to compose easy sentences and I think I\'m almost ready to compose the first complicated sentences. The only problem is that I can do this only by writing. The pronunciation is my problem. I think I learnt how to pronounce all consonants, the \"Ĺ\" also, but I could not understand in any way how to pronounce the vowels, because my computer can\'t read the files you put as example for vowels.\r\nSo, I would ask you if you could have these files in a different form, as .mp3 or .wma or .wav or if you could send me a page, where you could explain me this pronounce by writing, by making comparison with the english ones.\r\nI will be eternally grateful with you. In advance, thank you very much.\r\n\r\nTo the readers of this forum: If you also can help me, I\'ve written my e-mail adress. I will be eternally grateful with you also!\r\n\r\nThank you very much! Na shledanou!', '2004-02-07 04:16:29', 0, 0),
(1283, 14, 'David', 'd.carr@baesystems.com', 'Re: Pivo in Praha 6', 'Yes - good bar.  I\'d been trying to remember its name. Any idea? Good food, too.  I had the fried goose liver.  But isn\'t it actually on Evropska?  \r\n\r\nI visited it last year with a couple of simpatico Italians.  We discussed attitudes to nudity in different cultures.  To the British, the attitude to the pictures would normally be \"nudge nudge, wink wink\".  To the Italians (and presumably the Czechs), the attitude is \"What beautiful pictures\"  (And Americans?...well!  at least they weren\'t wearing silver nipple shields!)\r\n\r\nI asked a waitress, and most of the pictures are by Tono Stano and Frantisek Drtkol (the R is the one with the hat which nobody can pronounce).  The National Gallery has lots of their stuff, and the shop sells an excellent book of Czech nudes over the last 100 years or so.', '2004-02-09 19:38:47', 1272, 0),
(1284, 14, 'David', 'd.carr@baesystems.com', 'Re: Slang, idioms, and other such fun things', '\"zakecat se\" definitely applies to me!  Here\'s something:  English has lots of words and idioms to do with ships.  I\'ll bet there is no easy Czech word for \"the horizontal mast that sticks out at the front of a sailing ship\".  In English - \"bowsprit\"  This must make the novels of Patrick O\'Brian pretty much untranslatable.\r\n\r\nI heard on the radio the other day that there is a Northern Indian language that has words for \"to express anger with a sideward glance\", \"to pretend to love someone\" and \"the noise made by mud and water when you stick your hand into a hole to catch a crab\".', '2004-02-09 21:00:18', 308, 0),
(1285, 13, 'Marco Bucchianeri', 'bukkia85@libero.it', 'Pomoc! Help!', 'Hi! I\'m an italian guy, and I\'m interested in learning Czech language, because on April I\'ll go to Prague in a school trip and I want to know the local speech, and I want to speak it as best as I can. \r\n\r\nTo the creator of this site: I find your grammar very completed and I\'m learning all about nouns, adjectives, verbs, pronouns, and now I\'m ready to compose easy sentences and I think I\'m almost ready to compose the first complicated sentences. The only problem is that I can do this only by writing. The pronunciation is my problem. I think I learnt how to pronounce all consonants, the \"Ĺ\" also, but I could not understand in any way how to pronounce the vowels, because my computer can\'t read the files you put as example for vowels. \r\nSo, I would ask you if you could have these files in a different form, as .mp3 or .wma or .wav or if you could send me a page, where you could explain me this pronounce by writing, by making comparison with the english ones. \r\nI will be eternally grateful with you. In advance, thank you very much. \r\n\r\nTo the readers of this forum: If you also can help me, I\'ve written my e-mail adress. I will be eternally grateful with you also! \r\n\r\nThank you very much! Na shledanou!', '2004-02-10 02:38:06', 0, 0),
(1286, 13, 'Marco Bucchianeri', 'bukkia85@libero.it', 'Pomoc! Help!', 'Hi! I\'m an italian guy, and I\'m interested in learning Czech language, because on April I\'ll go to Prague in a school trip and I want to know the local speech, and I want to speak it as best as I can. \r\n\r\nTo the creator of this site: I find your grammar very completed and I\'m learning all about nouns, adjectives, verbs, pronouns, and now I\'m ready to compose easy sentences and I think I\'m almost ready to compose the first complicated sentences. The only problem is that I can do this only by writing. The pronunciation is my problem. I think I learnt how to pronounce all consonants, the \"Ĺ\" also, but I could not understand in any way how to pronounce the vowels, because my computer can\'t read the files you put as example for vowels. \r\nSo, I would ask you if you could have these files in a different form, as .mp3 or .wma or .wav or if you could send me a page, where you could explain me this pronounce by writing, by making comparison with the english ones. \r\nI will be eternally grateful with you. In advance, thank you very much. \r\n\r\nTo the readers of this forum: If you also can help me, I\'ve written my e-mail adress. I will be eternally grateful with you also! \r\n\r\nThank you very much! Na shledanou!', '2004-02-10 03:27:52', 0, 0),
(1287, 14, 'Kiley', 'kmcermak@earthlink.net', 'Gypsies', 'Are Bohemians ancestorially gypsies?', '2004-02-10 22:29:57', 0, 1),
(1288, 13, 'Hanka', '', 'Re: Valentines Day!', 'Rekla bych, ze neco jako: Stastneho Valentynka, ma blazniva cikanecko!\r\nHanka', '2004-02-11 23:23:09', 1278, 0),
(1289, 14, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: Gypsies', 'Kiley, There quite a few romanies, or gypsies in Czech but I doubt many Czechs would appreciate a gypsy ancestry. They are a slavic people with I guess bits and pieces of Vikings, Germans and probably a few others. However in my experience there is a significant prejudice against minorities such as the gypsy. Without it getting to heavy it might be interesting what others think on this', '2004-02-12 03:39:45', 1287, 0),
(1290, 13, 'Chris', 'idriveatruck@earthlink.net', 'Bride and Groom', 'My aunt is getting married and her fiance has a strong Czech background. My sister wants to get a personalized wedding gift saying bride and groom in Czech. Can someone please tell me what bride and groom are in Czech? I\'m pretty sure bride is nevesta, but I don\'t know groom. Any help is greatly appreciated. Thanks.', '2004-02-13 02:47:19', 0, 1),
(1291, 13, 'Jakub', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: Bride and Groom', 'bride: nevÄsta (neve^sta)\r\ngroom: Âenich (z^enich)\r\n\"To the bride and groom\": \"NevÄstÄ a Âenichovi\" (Neve^ste^ a z^enichovi)\r\n(encoding windows-1250)\r\n', '2004-02-14 04:48:53', 1290, 0),
(1292, 13, 'Pavel', 'patt@email.cz', 'Re: Bride and Groom', 'Bride is \"nevesta\" and groom is \"zenich\". If written correctly in Czech, \"nevesta\" has a little hook above the second E and \"zenich\" has the hook above Z. :-).\r\n', '2004-02-16 02:49:40', 1290, 0),
(1293, 13, 'Pavel', '', 'Re: conjugations', 'Verbs are conjugated only in present in Czech. Otherwise, there is only one past tense which is composed (to be + past participle).', '2004-02-16 02:59:16', 1239, 0),
(1294, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Gypsies', 'Are all people from the Czech Republic, or from Bohemia (the CR\'s western part) of the Romany (gypsy) ethnic group? Certainly not. Gypsies are a minority  -- maybe 2%, probably less -- of the people in the Czech Republic as a whole.\r\n\r\nAre all people who are ethnically Czech ethnically Romany? No, they are two vastly different ethnic groups, of which the most striking difference is much darker skin among Romanies. Their heritage leads back to India; Czechs\' leads back to the Russian steppes or something (and includes many more centuries spent in Bohemia).\r\n\r\nViewing even a single sentence of racist trash regarding Romany makes me dang well sick. I hope that none enters this discussion.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-02-16 13:43:30', 1287, 0),
(1295, 13, 'Pete', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'vchod - east/exit', 'Is there an interesting reason the words in czech for \"east\" and \"exit\" are the same?  And are the other compass directions (jih, zapad, sever) much the same in other slavic languages or are these words unique to czech?\r\n\r\n', '2004-02-17 03:43:58', 0, 0),
(1296, 14, 'kiley', 'kmcermak@earthlink.net', 'Re: Gypsies', 'It seems that more Bohemians would be gypsies because the word Bohemian means Gypsy', '2004-02-17 07:37:11', 1294, 1),
(1297, 14, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: Gypsies', 'Bohemian does mean Gypsy? Maybe in your language.\r\nIn Latin, Bohemia (or Boiohaemia) means \"country of the Boii\".\r\nBTW, Gypsy means (ethymologically) Egyptian = another mistake of yours!\r\n\r\nBoii: a Celtic tribe, one section of which settled in Cisalpine Gaul around Bononia (Bologna, Italy) and another in what was later Bohemia, to which it gave its name.\r\n\r\nBohemia: (Czech Czechy, German BĂśhmen) historic country of central Europe that was a kingdom in the Holy Roman Empire and subsequently a province in the Habsburgs\' Austrian Empire. Bohemia was bounded on the south by Austria, on the west by Bavaria, on the north by Saxony and Lusatia, on the northeast by Silesia, and on the east by Moravia. ...  (Encyclopaedia www.Britannica.com)\r\n', '2004-02-17 17:39:18', 1296, 0),
(1298, 13, 'Jakub', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: vchod - east/exit', 'Yes, \"vĂ˝chod\" means both East and exit, because East is the direction where Sun exits up (literally \"vychĂĄzĂ­\") from the under-horizon.\r\nAnd \"zĂĄpad\" is the direction where Sun falls down (\"zapadĂĄ\").\r\n\r\nIn Slovak it is the same, including \"sever\" - North and \"juh\" - South (\"jih\" in Czech).\r\n\r\nIn Polish \"wzchod\" and \"zachod\" are  similar to Czech. But North is \"polnoc\" - midnight and South is \"poludznie\" - midday or noon.\r\n\r\nI don\'t speak Russian or any other Slavic language, so I cannot give you examples in another languages.\r\n', '2004-02-17 22:10:30', 1295, 0),
(1299, 13, 'Dominik LukeÂ', 'czechonline@bohemica.com', 'Re: vchod - east/exit', 'Actually, English uses a similar word for \'slunce vychĂĄzĂ­\', i.e \'the sun is coming out\' which is more or less a literal equivalent.', '2004-02-17 22:18:01', 1298, 0),
(1300, 13, 'Jeff Grant', 'ardra@paradise.net.nz', 'Czech Palindromes', 'Greetings from New Zealand!I have made a lifetime study of palindromes in various languages.apalindrome is a word that reads the same backwards as forwards,such as \"madam\" in English and \"muzum\",dative of \"muzi\"(man)in Czech.\r\nI have seen the following Czech palindromes on an Internet list(accents omitted)and am hoping someone can tell me what the words mean.Thank you.\r\nCITIC,JETEJ,JEZEJ,JECEJ,METEM,MAVAM,MIJIM,MYM,NEPOTOPEN,NEZASAZEN,NEZARAZEN,\r\nSLILS,VYMYV,VEDEV\r\nI have also noticed the following words used on Czech sites on the Internet.Are they proper Czech words? If so,I would like to know what they mean too.Thanks.Is it possible some may be Slovak/ian not Czech?\r\nUOBOU,VOJOV,VYKYV,VYZYZ\r\nI am currently compiling a dictionary of palindromes,and it would be nice to have some more Czech examples.\r\nAny help you can give me would be greatly appreciated.Thank you.\r\nOur daughter had nearly a year in Sevetin as an exchange student in 1994,but her language skills aren\'t quite good enough for these words!  ', '2004-02-18 12:35:17', 0, 0),
(1301, 13, 'Gloria', 'glomc@dakotacom.net', 'navrhnout', 'Ahoj, all!\r\n\r\nI am still struggling with slovesy.\r\n\r\nThe latest is the sloveso \'navrhnout\' which my slovnik says\r\nmeans to suggest, draft, propose, etc.\r\n\r\nIn the various cases that would be what?\r\n\r\nI am a bit dense about the forms for this one.\r\n\r\nDekuju,\r\n\r\nGlo', '2004-02-19 05:21:37', 0, 0),
(1302, 13, 'Gloria', 'glomc@dakotacom.net', 'navrhnout', 'Ahoj, all!\r\n\r\nI am still struggling with slovesy.\r\n\r\nThe latest is the sloveso \'navrhnout\' which my slovnik says\r\nmeans to suggest, draft, propose, etc.\r\n\r\nIn the various cases that would be what?\r\n\r\nI am a bit dense about the forms for this one.\r\n\r\nDekuju,\r\n\r\nGlo', '2004-02-19 05:21:40', 0, 1),
(1303, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: vĂ˝chod - east/exit, vchod means entry, entrance.', 'In Russian:\r\n\r\nvĂ˝chod  -  vostok (east, orient),  but voschod solnca (= sunrise, sunup)\r\nzĂĄpad   -  zapad (west, occident), but zachod solnca (= sunset, sundown)\r\nsever   -  sever\r\njih     -  jug\r\n', '2004-02-19 13:46:19', 1298, 0),
(1304, 14, 'Milan', '', 'Re: Gypsies', 'It must be really interesting school you are studying...............:-))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))', '2004-02-20 00:36:03', 1296, 0),
(1305, 13, 'David', '', 'Cheating Boyfriends and Girlfriends!!!!!!!!!!!!!!', 'This is a bit unusual to ask I know but can somebody translate the following into czech language for me : \r\n\r\n\"You don\'t know me, but I know you ! I think you should know that your boyfriend Pavel is having sex with my girlfriend.\"', '2004-02-21 22:20:28', 0, 0),
(1306, 13, 'pm', '@', 'Re: Cheating Boyfriends and Girlfriends!!!!!!!!!!!!!!', 'Ty mÄ neznĂĄÂ, ale jĂĄ tebe ano! MyslĂ­m, Âe by jsi mÄla vÄdÄt, Âe tvĹŻj kluk Pavel spĂ­ s mou holkou.', '2004-02-22 23:18:33', 1305, 0),
(1307, 13, 'vp', '', 'Re: Cheating Boyfriends and Girlfriends!!!!!!!!!!!!!!', 'MyslĂ­m, Âe bys mÄla vÄdÄt, Âe se pĂ­Âe BYS.', '2004-02-23 13:20:02', 1306, 0);
\". This sounds rather colloquial to me (and not only to me), as it is used only a few years, but it\'s O.K. to use it at everyday speach now.\r\n\r\nBut in written or formal language you should not use first person plural \r\nimperative on \"jĂ­t\" and then use infinitive verb. You should change the \"main\" verb directly.\r\n\r\nThe Milan\'s examples then change to\r\nLet\'s have a drink. -- Napijme se. or Popijme.\r\nLet\'s have a chat. -- PopovĂ­dejme si.\r\nLet\'s have a smoke. -- ZakuĹme si.\r\n\r\nMilan, would you translate \"Let\'s go.\" as \"Poj\'dme jĂ­t.\" nebo \"PojÄme.\"?', '2004-04-18 02:28:45', 1347, 0),
(1350, 14, 'a_7', 'amraam_7@seznam.cz', 'Re: burning the witch-april 30', 'This tradition symbolizes the end of winter. Winter - cold, ugly, is burned as spring comes. The death of the witch means the end of winter :)', '2004-04-25 21:47:14', 1319, 1),
(1351, 17, 'a_7', 'amraam_7@seznam.cz', 'Re: hovorovĂŠ vĂ˝razy', '1 & 2 - sprĂĄvnÄ\r\n3 - dostat na frak =  utrpÄt nÄjakou Ăşjmu', '2004-04-25 21:52:44', 1197, 1),
(1352, 13, 'Milan', '', 'Re: Let\'s do', 'Dear J\r\nyou are right ,it is not literary Czech.But I would really translate Let\'s go as Pojd\'me since I don\'t find any trouble to say it I think it is possible to read it in books as well.Bye.', '2004-04-25 23:42:52', 1349, 0),
(1353, 13, 'Paul', 'Psmith325ti@aol.com', 'How to say....', 'Can somebody tell me how to wish somebody a \'good relaxing holiday\' and \' take care\'\r\nThanks', '2004-04-27 23:38:24', 0, 0),
(1354, 13, 'amathes', '', 'Re: How to say....', '\'good relaxing holiday\' = uÂij(te) si dovolenĂŠ\r\n\'take care\' = mÄj se', '2004-05-09 20:14:41', 1353, 0),
(1355, 13, 'Sharon', 'sharon.z@polokwane.org.za', 'Translation', 'Hi, \r\n\r\nI\'m having visitors from Czech in two weeks time and would be very gratefull if somebody could assist me with the translation & pronounciation of a few words.  \r\n\r\nBreakfast\r\nBrunch\r\nLunch\r\nSupper\r\nWine list\r\nSoup\r\nVenison\r\nSauce\r\n', '2004-05-12 18:56:18', 0, 1),
(1356, 16, 'Valja', 'vpopovich@yandex.ru', 'Looking for a Czech tutor in Kijev', 'Hello\r\nI am looking for a Czech native speaker to take private lessons in Kijev, Ukraine.\r\n\r\nMy tel. No. is +38 050 9396932\r\nEmail vpopovich@yandex.ru\r\n\r\nAny info would be very much appreciated.\r\n\r\nThank you\r\n\r\nValja', '2004-05-12 19:44:07', 0, 1),
(1357, 14, 'T', 'Trany13@hotmail.com', 'individual debt in the Czech Republic--jail time?', 'Does anybody know what happens to a person in regards to an unpaid debt in the Czech Republic?  I have heard that the person who does not pay goes to jail.  So, is there a \"debtor\'s prison\"?  Thanks--I\'m curious.', '2004-05-16 19:36:59', 0, 0),
(1358, 13, 'Pavel', '', 'Reply to sorry', 'Can you help me - what should I say in English, when somebody says sorry to me. Is it \"no worries\"? Thank you very much.', '2004-05-17 01:59:14', 0, 0),
(1359, 13, 'Mira', '', 'Re: Reply to sorry', 'Well, I\'m American, and here we\'d only say \"no worries\" in reply to \"sorry\" mockingly or ironically, or if we had delusions of being Australian. So I\'d say \"no problem\", or \"don\'t worry\", or something of that nature.', '2004-05-17 07:32:15', 1358, 0),
(1360, 13, 'Pavel', '', 'Re: Reply to sorry', 'Thank you very much. It is a very frequented situation and I had never known what to say before you answered me.', '2004-05-18 18:27:53', 1359, 0),
(1361, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Translation', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nin all of the below, keep in mind that my \"y\"s are as in yellow, and my \"h\"s are just used to indicated that it\'s an (English) short vowel, nothing more. \"Zh\" below should be pronounced s-as-in-pleasure. \r\n\r\nBreakfast - snĂ­danÄ (SNYEE dahn yeh)\r\n\r\nBrunch - doesn\'t really exist in Czech, a good translation might be \"vÄtÂĂ­ snĂ­danÄ\" (VYEHT shee   SNYEE dahn yeh) -- literally \"a relatively-large (or quite-large) breakfast\") -- a Czech reading this answer and who recovers from the initial shock of my not translating it as \"a larger breakfast\" might be able to help. You might also try \"menÂĂ­ obÄd\" (MEN shee OH byehd), a quite-small lunch, or the two in short succession.\r\n\r\nLunch - obÄd (OH byehd)\r\n\r\nSupper - veÄeĹe -- this is a hard one because of the Ĺ. Try (VEH cheh zheh). Zh is not the correct pronunciation of Ĺ, but your aim here is to be understood (which you will be), not to shock them with your perfect pronunciation.\r\n\r\nWine list -- the literal equivalent \"seznam vĂ­n\" (list of wines - Czech uses \"of\" a lot because it doesn\'t have to use \"of\" to say \"of\"... well, never mind) is as far as I know not used in Czech. Try simply \"vĂ­na\" (VEE nah) -- wines. If there\'s any confusion, you can fall back to \"seznam vĂ­n\" (SEHZ nahm   VEEN).\r\n\r\nSoup -- polĂŠvka (POH leh fkah). The \"yeh\" should be pronounced a bit longer -- say 50% longer -- than the average length you\'re giving to a syllable. This is what\'s called a \"long vowel\" in Czech. (Their long vowels change only in length of pronunciation. They\'re generally marked by the slanting accent over the vowel. Some of the phrases above had long i, but \"ee\" in English tends to naturally have the right length for this.)\r\n\r\nVenison - apparently zvÄĹina (ZVYEH zhee nah). I\'m not enough of a fajnÂmekr (a jovial-to-ironic word for connoisseur) to be sure.\r\n\r\nSauce - omĂĄÄka (OH mah chkah). Again, drawl out the \"ah\", as it\'s a long vowel.\r\n\r\nIf you suspect your browser isn\'t displaying accented Czech letters properly, here they are in the awkward \"low Czech support friendly\" version. \' is a bottom left to top right accent, and ^ is the \"bird\'s wings\". Yeah, it\'s upside down. I\'ve put them after the letters they\'re above:\r\n\r\nsni\'dane^\r\nve^ts^i\' sni\'dane^ / mens^i\' obe^d\r\nobe^d\r\nvi\'na / seznam vi\'n\r\npole\'vka\r\nzve^r^ina\r\noma\'c^ka\r\n\r\nDobrou chuÂ (Bon appetit)!\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-05-19 19:14:54', 1355, 0),
(1362, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: individual debt in the Czech Republic--jail time?', 'Generally, they go to the Bahamas ;-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-05-19 19:27:42', 1357, 0),
(1363, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Let\'s Do and Let\'s Go', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nHere\'s an \"outsider\'s\" view:\r\n\r\nPojÄme really is quite common. It has in my opinion the advantage of sounding like a suggestion rather than a command. Napijme se sounds to my ears slightly authoritarian and maybe a little too formal. That\'s one disadvantage of the fact that Czech for foreigners textbooks are generally so insistent on spisovnĂĄ Czech -- if you\'re not going to be in formal situations, it\'s so formal as to sound a bit odd.\r\n\r\n\"Let\'s do\" is a bit tricky, because it\'s a rather odd phrase in English.\r\n\r\nLet\'s do lunch -- when it comes to business, here it\'s America that\'s more informal than Czech. Though it depends. More importantly, there\'s no Czech phrase that so plainly carries the meaning \"...and discuss this at that lunch.\" Maybe \"PojÄme si o tom popovĂ­dat na obÄdÄ\", \"PopovĂ­dejme si o tom na obÄdÄ\", \"Dejme si obÄd, tam si o tom popovĂ­dĂĄme.\"\r\n\r\nLet\'s do the time warp again - PojÄme si jeÂtÄ jednou zatanÄit time warp. ;-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-05-19 19:38:16', 1346, 0),
(1364, 13, 'Sharon', 'sharon.z@polokwane.org.za', 'Re: Translation', 'Hi Erik\r\n\r\nThank you very much for your time and detailed explanation, you were of great help.\r\n\r\nSharon \r\nSouth Africa', '2004-05-19 20:05:16', 1361, 0),
(1365, 13, 'Patt', '', 'Re: Translation', 'Wine list can be more accurately translated as \"vinnĂ˝ lĂ­stek\", a term that is used in Czech (similar to \"jĂ­delnĂ­ lĂ­stek\" = menu). Pavel from Brno.', '2004-05-20 22:21:14', 1361, 0),
(1366, 13, 'Al', 'wwadomr@aol.com', 'List of Months', 'I would like to get names of months in Czech/English> Thaks you very much in dadvance for your prompt response.\r\n\r\nResoectfully yours, Al   ', '2004-05-28 23:27:15', 0, 0),
(1367, 13, 'Pavel', '', 'Re: List of Months', 'January  leden \r\nFebruary  Ănor \r\nMarch  bĹezen \r\nApril  duben \r\nMay  kvÄten \r\nJune  Äerven \r\nJuly  Äervenec \r\nAugust  srpen \r\nSeptember  zĂĄĹĂ­ \r\nOctober  ĹĂ­jen \r\nNovember  listopad \r\nDecember  prosinec ', '2004-05-29 01:51:16', 1366, 0),
(1368, 16, 'ashe', 'bluevolcanicash@netscape.net', 'good site?', 'does anyone know where i can get a czech pen pal. i hear that talking is a good way to learn', '2004-05-29 04:14:26', 0, 0),
(1369, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: Reply to sorry', 'To put an english UK perspective on this we have varied responses to someone saying sorry. \'No worries\' will have Austrailian connotations, but is  acceptable, its quite common nowaday\'s (lightheartedly). We\'re more likely to say something like \'thats okay\' or it \'it doesn\'t matter\'. We might even say if indignant \'I should think so!\'. More formally or in  writing we sometimes say \'apology(ies) accepted\'.', '2004-05-29 21:11:55', 1360, 0),
(1370, 13, 'greg', 'punkrocker_blazinfireatx20042006@yahoo.com', 'how would you say this in Czech', 'how would you say \"what\'s up\" as a slang meaning in Czech?', '2004-06-02 06:57:51', 0, 1),
(1371, 14, 'Greg', 'punkrocker_blazinfireatx20042006@yahoo.com', '', 'What things or expressions do the Czech people consider rude or insulting?\r\nand what other languages are spoken in the Czech republic other than Czech or Slovak?', '2004-06-02 07:02:22', 0, 1),
(1372, 14, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: Languages in CR.', 'The Czech language is the only language spoken in the Czech Republic. The Slovak language has a special legal status. Every ethnic group has his own language, of course, but ethnic minorities are small in our country.\r\n\r\nCzech + Moravian 94.4%, Slovak 3.1%, Polish 0.6%, German 0.5%, Silesian 0.4%, Roma 0.3%, Hungarian 0.2%, other 0.5% (1991)', '2004-06-02 15:08:16', 1371, 0),
(1373, 13, 'Jakubeku', '', 'Re: how would you say this in Czech', 'Co je???', '2004-06-04 03:51:49', 1370, 0),
(1374, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: how would you say this in Czech', 'I\'d say you would only say that if you meant \"What\'s up\" in the sense of \"What\'s wrong?\" Otherwise, how about \"Co je novĂŠho?\"\r\n\r\nErik\r\n\r\n', '2004-06-04 17:21:22', 1373, 0),
(1375, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Languages in CR.', 'Silesians with their own language? News to me...', '2004-06-04 17:23:50', 1372, 0),
(1376, 14, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: Languages in CR.', 'Some people declared themselves to be Silesians. Maybe they also invented their own language.\r\n\r\nDitto for the Moravians.\r\n\r\nThe original census data (1991) were:\r\n\r\nCzech 81.2%, Moravians 13.2%, etc. (I merged Czechs and Moravians arbitrarily.)', '2004-06-07 15:44:56', 1375, 0),
(1377, 13, 'Sharon', 'sharon.z@polokwane.org.za', 'Pronounciation', 'Hello\r\n\r\nPlease help with pronounciation of dekuji - thank you\r\n\r\nThank you in advance\r\n\r\nSharon', '2004-06-07 18:54:28', 0, 0),
(1378, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Pronounciation', 'DYEH koo yee\r\n\r\n--\r\nErik', '2004-06-09 21:33:01', 1377, 0),
(1379, 13, 'Sharon', 'sharon.z@polokwane.org.za', 'Re: Pronounciation', 'Dear Erik\r\n\r\nThank you, I\'m going to visit friends in Czech in August and am trying to learn the basics.  At least I can say please and thank you and pronounce it correctly.\r\n\r\nRegards\r\nSharon', '2004-06-10 18:34:37', 1378, 0),
(1380, 13, 'Laura Hadley', 'lhadley@cs.umd.edu', 'Czech and Slovenian translators needed for children\'s books', 'My name is Laura Hadley, and I work with a project called the International ChildrenÂs Digital Library, at www.icdlbooks.org.  It showcases childrenÂs books from around the world on the web.   Currently we are trying to include information on our books (such as the summary) in the original language of the book in addition to English.  We have a few books in Czech and Slovenian, and are having a hard time locating volunteer translators for this project in this language.  The book information to translate is fairly brief, so time commitment would be minimal.  This is a wonderful way to give back to the world community and help children.  Would it be possible to send this out to any possible Czech and Slovenian speakers you know that might be interested?  Please let me know if you have any questions or if you need more information at lhadley@cs.umd.edu.\r\n\r\nThank you very much,\r\nLaura Hadley\r\n', '2004-06-10 19:44:40', 0, 0),
(1381, 14, 'Greg', 'punkrocker_blazinfireatx20042006@yahoo.com', 'Should I learn Czech?', 'I am really interested in learn Czech.But I wanted to know how the women look over there. Because if I were to learn Czech and not put it to good use because, I dont like any of thier women then Its put to waist. So what are the features/characteristics of the women there? Do they look similar to German women? Do most of them have blue eyes?? If any one could message me on this information I would really appreciate it. Thanks!', '2004-06-11 08:10:38', 0, 1),
(1382, 13, 'greg', 'punkrocker_blazinfireatx20042006@yahoo.com', 'In what countries in Europe do they speak Czech?', 'Do you know how many countries in Europe speak Czech? Because I want to learn a popular Slavic language besides Russian. And I\'m having trouble with choosing that one language. So could you give me some advice?', '2004-06-11 08:16:00', 0, 1),
(1383, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech and Slovenian translators needed for children\'s books', 'ok, Laura, can\'t promis much time or prompt responses but can try to have a quick look on the texts.\r\npls get in touch (slavek777@hotmail.com),\r\njana', '2004-06-14 20:36:37', 1380, 0),
(1384, 14, '*', '', 'Re: Should I learn Czech?', 'Come and see...\r\nI suppose you won\'t be disappointed. Nothing more can be said.', '2004-06-15 05:01:36', 1381, 0),
(1385, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: In what countries in Europe do they speak Czech?', 'One country in Europe speaks Czech - the Czech Republic. Another country understands but does not speak it - Slovakia. \r\n\r\nYou won\'t find a Slavic language other than Russian that\'s spoken in more than one country, as far as I know. Perhaps Serb or Croat, but the post-Yugoslavian countries are kind of miniature (and many Serbs probably won\'t like hearing Croat and vice versa, which is why there are now Serb and Croat even though until recently one spoke of Serbo-Croat). If you\'re going for raw speaker count, perhaps Polish would be better. Though we\'d all be happiest here to see you speaking Czech ;-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-06-16 14:16:17', 1382, 0),
(1386, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech and Slovenian translators needed for children\'s books', 'I\'m about the same as Jana here... will do what I can, won\'t be much, but I\'ll be glad to help as much as I\'m able. The address is (in antispam form) erik at sky dot cz.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-06-16 14:18:29', 1380, 0),
(1387, 16, 'Alena', 'alena13@telus.net', 'Problem with registration', 'Hi,\r\nI have some problems registering myself in the teachers\' database on this website. It says I have to be a user of \'global internet\'...whatever that is.\r\nCan somebody help?\r\nThanks, Alena', '2004-06-18 04:59:59', 0, 0),
(1388, 13, 'Pete', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'zapĹedenou', 'What verb does \"zapĹedenou\" come from in:\r\n\r\n  Je sama pĂ­snĂ­ zapĹedenou v Äase\r\n\r\nThe translation I have says \"woven\" but I can\'t make it fit a conjugation of tkat.\r\n', '2004-06-20 22:03:45', 0, 1),
(1389, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: zapĹedenou', '\"zapĹedenĂ˝\" is not \"zatkanĂ˝\", but metaphorically it is equivalent (spinned in time, woven in time)\r\n\r\npĹĂ­sti = to spin (to make thread)\r\ntkĂĄti = to weave (to make cloth, fabric)', '2004-06-22 16:47:57', 1388, 0),
(1390, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: zapĹedenou', 'Hope you don\'t laugh at me for nitpicking a minor point, but isn\'t it about time to just let the \"-i\" infinitives die? Nobody uses them in real life, and many dictionaries are starting to use the real-world ones as well. (That\'s my memory of the LEDA dictionary at least, and a quick check of some on-line translation dictionaries shows the same.) Using them seems hyperformal to me.\r\n\r\nJust a thought. :-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-06-22 19:01:51', 1389, 0),
(1391, 16, 'NK', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: good site?', 'http://www.aj.cz/penfriends/default.asp', '2004-06-23 20:15:03', 1368, 0),
(1392, 13, 'Pete', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'Re: zapĹedenou', 'Thanks for the answer. Could you tell me one more thing. \r\n  \r\n  Je sama pĂ­snĂ­ zapĹedenou v Äase\r\n\r\nIs it that pĹĂ­sti is being conjugated into the \"present participle\" instrumental case in order to go with pĂ­snĂ­ being in the instrumental case. If this is right, what does use of instrumental case indicate here?\r\n\r\n', '2004-06-24 05:12:03', 1390, 1),
(1393, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: zapĹedenou', '(Ona) je pĂ­snĂ­ zapĹedenou ...\r\n\r\nOna - subject\r\nje pĂ­snĂ­ ... - predicate\r\nzapĹedenou - attribute of \"pĂ­seĹ\" (it must be in the same case)\r\n\r\nWhen the predicate is in the nominative, it expresses a substantial (fundamental) quality of the subject of the sentence. \r\nWhen the predicate is in the instrumental, it expresses an acquired and transient quality of the subject. \r\n\r\nSome examples: \r\n\r\nA) (nom. is necessary)\r\n\r\n\"Velryba nenĂ­ ryba.\" (The whale is not a fish.) \r\nThis is a generally accepted fact, something that is not changeable. \r\nIf you (wrongly) said \"Velryba nenĂ­ rybou\", it would sound like the whale is not a fish today but may be a fish tomorrow.\r\n\r\n\"Eva je mladĂĄ dĂ­vka.\"\r\n\r\nB) (instr. is necessary) \r\n\r\n\"Petr je uÄitelem.\" (Petr is a teacher.) \r\nHe\'s a teacher because he decided to become one. He may change his profession later.\r\n\r\n\"Eva je ÂĂĄkynĂ­ naÂĂ­ Âkoly.\"\r\n\"Otec je Älenem spolku chovatelĹŻ holubĹŻ.\"\r\n\"VĂĄclav Havel jiÂ nenĂ­ prezidentem ÄR.\"\r\nAlso: \r\n\"Hudba je mu vÂĂ­m (instr. of \"vÂe\").\"\r\n', '2004-06-24 17:22:38', 1392, 0),
(1394, 17, 'Oranje', 'oranje@knvb.nl', 'D&#234;kuji', 'D&#234;kuji from Holland!\r\n\r\nczechia rules :-)', '2004-06-24 21:43:11', 0, 0),
(1395, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: infinitiv', '\"Nobody uses them in real life, ...\"\r\n\r\nTo je pravda. V bÄÂnĂŠm kontextu znĂ­ infinitiv na -ti divnÄ, aÂ smÄÂnÄ.\r\nMimo bÄÂnĂ˝ kontext nebo jako izolovanĂŠ slovo je infinitiv na -ti vĂ˝raznÄjÂĂ­, ale pĹesto znĂ­ vÄtÂinou neutrĂĄlnÄ.\r\n\r\nNapĹ.:\r\n\r\n\"TkĂĄti je infinitiv.\" (netypickĂŠ pouÂitĂ­ - infinitiv tkĂĄti je zde podmÄt, nezĂĄleÂĂ­ na jeho vĂ˝znamu, ale formÄ)\r\n\r\n\"Co znamenĂĄ to weave?\" - \"TkĂĄti.\"\r\n\r\nv ĂşÄetnictvĂ­: \"MĂĄ dĂĄti\" - \"Dal\" (to je ustĂĄlenĂ˝ obrat)\r\n\r\n---\r\nVe slovnĂ­cĂ­ch je to tĂŠmÄĹ jedno. MĂĄm slovnĂ­ky s infinitivy na -ti (vÄtÂinou do r. 1970) i s infinitivy na -t (po r. 1970). KdyÂ je pouÂĂ­vĂĄm stĹĂ­davÄ, vĹŻbec nevnĂ­mĂĄm rozdĂ­l. Ve slovnĂ­cĂ­ch mĂĄ infinitiv na -t velkou vĂ˝hodu v tom, Âe sazeÄ uÂetĹĂ­ velkĂŠ mnoÂstvĂ­ liter \"i\". :-)\r\n\r\n---\r\nV humoristickĂ˝ch romĂĄnech je infinitiv na -ti hojnÄ vyuÂĂ­vĂĄn. PoruÄĂ­k Dub, v civilu profesor ÄeÂtiny, ĹĂ­kĂĄ napĹ.:\r\n     \"MuÂ ten, pane plukovnĂ­ku,\" pĹedstavoval Âvejka, \"dÄlĂĄ ze sebe blba, aby kryl svĂ˝m idiotstvĂ­m svĂĄ darebĂĄctvĂ­. Nejsem sice obeznĂĄmen s obsahem dodanĂŠho s nĂ­m spisu, nicmĂŠnÄ domnĂ­vĂĄm se, Âe ten chlap zase nÄco provedl, ale ve vÄtÂĂ­m mÄĹĂ­tku. Kdybyste dovolil, pane plukovnĂ­ku, abych se mohl seznĂĄmiti s obsahem spisu, mohl bych vĂĄm rozhodnÄ dĂĄti eventuelnÄ urÄitĂŠ direktivy, jak s nĂ­m naloÂit.\" (Dub pouÂĂ­vĂĄ inf. na -ti, ale nenĂ­ dĹŻslednĂ˝)\r\n\r\nJĂĄ vĂ­m, \"Âvejk\" je starĂ˝ vĂ­ce neÂ 80 let, nicmĂŠnÄ si myslĂ­m, Âe inf. na -ti lze podobnÄ vyuÂĂ­t i dnes.\r\n\r\n----\r\n(Toto je mĹŻj subjektivnĂ­ nĂĄzor, lidĂŠ mladÂĂ­ mohou mĂ­ti jinĂ˝. :-)\r\n\r\nS pozdravem\r\nVladimĂ­r\r\n', '2004-06-25 13:42:00', 1390, 0),
(1396, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: infinitiv, intrumentĂĄl', '\"...infinitiv tkĂĄti je zde podmÄt,...\" Oops! Ouha!\r\n\r\nhloupĂĄ chyba - sprĂĄvnÄ mĂĄ bĂ˝t \"podmÄtem\" (instr.) - infinitiv nenĂ­ podmÄt, leÄ ve vÄtÄ mĹŻÂe bĂ˝t podmÄtem', '2004-06-29 17:28:29', 1395, 0),
(1397, 15, 'Baptsite', 'fantabenz@hotmail.com', 'young french in pragua', 'Hi everybody,\r\nmy name is baptiste, I\'m 17 and i\'m french.\r\nI really wanna organise a trip in a great capitale of europe with some friends. I thought at Vienna and sure Pragua, to learn a lot about your culture. I want to discover lots of things and to meet lots of young people from there, that\'s why i let this message on the forum, if some of you wants to join me by mail and help me to discover your culture but also to discover mine, and maybe to meet in pragua, there is no problem,\r\njoin me on fantabenz@hotmail.com\r\nI\'m really interested by your culture and I already thank you \r\nFrenchly\r\nBapt', '2004-06-29 18:37:58', 0, 0),
(1398, 14, 'Baptiste', 'fantabenz@hotmail.com', 'a french in pragua', 'hi i\'m a young french man who really want to learn more about your culture, and i\'m decided to come in Pragua with some friends...\r\nI\'m just looking for some young czech who would help me to know more about your capital, about youngs there, about you....\r\nWe are ready to share lot\'s of things, so send me a mail at fantabenz@hotmail.com if you\'re ready to help us....\r\nThank you very much\r\nBaptiste\r\nfrance', '2004-06-29 18:43:52', 0, 0),
(1399, 17, 'baptiste', 'fantabenz@hotmail.com', 'a french in pragua', 'hi i\'m a young man who really wants to discover your wonderful country, that\'s why i\'m looking for some people to talk with, who could teach me lot\'s of things about your culture.\r\nI love my country and i love europa and travels, i\'m ready to share lot\'s of experiences,\r\nlet me know if you want to help me...\r\nfantabenz@hotmail.com\r\nThanks\r\nBapt\r\nFrance', '2004-06-29 18:47:56', 0, 0),
(1400, 14, 'Hesia', 'hesia@post.cz', 'football', 'Hallo\r\n\r\nI would like to write you that in Poland we enjoy every Czech victory, we keep finger\'s crossed for you, we are with you with whole our hearts ;-)\r\nGood luck !\r\n\r\nHesia', '2004-06-30 02:05:15', 0, 0),
(1401, 14, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: football', 'where were you last night then', '2004-07-03 04:08:28', 1400, 0),
(1402, 13, 'Paul', 'Psmith325ti@aol.com', 'Translation', 'Hi, hope someone can help, how do you say \'how are your parents?\' and \'Lot\'s of love\'\r\nthanks', '2004-07-07 00:13:50', 0, 0),
(1403, 13, 'Kim Van Dyke', 'kvandyke@berkshire.net', 'Questions about free online text translator', 'Hi, everyone. I am curious about the accuracy of a free online text translator. (Translate Now! on the left column at http://foreignword.com/Tools/transnow.asp?p=files/f_source.htm). I am trying to conduct a small e-mail business transaction and need to ask a czech-speaking person the following: \r\n\r\nWhat payment methods do you accept?\r\n\r\nThis is the result:\r\n\r\nJakĂ˝ mzda metody Äinit tebe pĹijmout?\r\n\r\nIs that even close to how it would really be said in czech? How does it actually translate? Would I czech laugh his/her head off if I sent that?\r\n\r\nThanks for your help!\r\n\r\nKim\r\n\r\n', '2004-07-16 05:44:33', 0, 1),
(1404, 13, 'Nadia', 'tobiskova@atlas.cz', 'Re: Questions about free online text translator', 'Hi Kim,\r\n\r\nThe translation is very far from how it would be said in Czech. The message we (Czechs) learn from the translated text is something like \"What salary of method make you receipt?\" \r\n\r\nNadia', '2004-07-20 19:54:50', 1403, 0),
(1405, 13, 'Charles Marik', 'tmarik3@excite.com', '', '', '2004-07-21 07:45:41', 0, 0),
(1406, 13, 'Charles Marik', 'tmarik3@excite.com', 'name meaning?', 'hello, my family name is Marik. When my ancestors came to america the name was originally Marikova but shortened around 1880. I was just wandering if there was any translation for either Marik or Marikova. Any help would be greatly appreciated.\r\n', '2004-07-21 07:47:16', 0, 1),
(1407, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: name meaning?', 'Marik, in Czech MaĹĂ­k (hacek above r, long i) is probably derived from MĂĄĹĂ­ (in English Mary), i.e. Mary\'s husband (maybe Mary was a dominant wife) or son (Mary was single).\r\n\r\nIn the Slavic languages the suffix \"-ova\" forms the feminine surnames, e.g. NavrĂĄtilovĂĄ (her father - NavrĂĄtil), thus MaĹĂ­kovĂĄ (Marikova) is a woman (a daughter or a wife of MaĹĂ­k).\r\n\r\nAnother meaning (in old Czech):\r\n\r\nmaĹĂ­k - fribble, waster, prodigal; derived from the verb \"maĹit\" = to mar (time, money)\r\n\r\nBTW, \"KĂĄja MaĹĂ­k\" (KĂĄja = Charlie) was a popular book for boys.\r\n\r\n', '2004-07-21 13:13:41', 1406, 0),
(1408, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Questions about free online text translator', 'Like Nadia said.\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, the current generation of online translators, especially those available for \"minor\" languages like Czech, is very inadequate for Slavic languages, which use quite different syntax from English and which indicate grammar using word endings. Even the best online translators only make a halfhearted attempt at grammar and syntax; the one you quote didn\'t even do that; just a series of \"first translations in the dictionary\" for each word in a row, with no accounting for word endings (except in the case of tebe... and it got that one wrong).\r\n\r\nFind a good CZ->EN human translator -- they\'re not expensive by US standards -- and forget about online translations for at least a decade or so.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-07-21 20:46:01', 1403, 0),
(1409, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Translation', 'how\'re your parents = jak se maji rodice?\r\nlots of love does not have direct equivalent (at least i can\'t think of one). if you mean it as a card/letter ending, i tend to use \'zdravim\' (zdravit = +/- to greet) but i guess it does not really convey the meaning. hm, can\'t really think of anything suitable, probably \'myslim na vas/tebe\' (i think of you-plural/you-singular, you\'re on my mind). \r\n\r\nhope this helps a bit,\r\njana', '2004-07-21 21:03:52', 1402, 0),
(1410, 14, 'Petr', '', 'Re: Should I learn Czech?', 'So what are the features/characteristics of the women there?\r\n\r\nThey are beautiful, charming and loving, good in cooking and every other type of domestic service. I think, they are very practical, clever a foresighted.\r\nMany of them have no problem to spend time at home and raise their children.\r\n(Of course, all of this depence on the type of woman - if you are looking for a women in a bar or at disco, most of thing written above is not true :)\r\n\r\nDo they look similar to German women? \r\n\r\nNo way!!! German women do look more as if they were \"Barbie dolls\"\r\nCzech women have beautiful eyes, although most of them do not have blue ones.\r\n\r\nAbout learning Czech language:\r\nMy friend - Czech girl married an American who was learning Czech - she appreciated that. But you should do that because you really want to, not just because of some girl :)\r\n\r\nGood luck!!!', '2004-07-22 04:43:10', 1381, 0),
(1411, 13, 'De Zikis (nee: Janda)', 'dzikis@midwestern.edu', 'Checkmark', 'My grandmother use to say that she would put a hachic (?) by an item on a list.  Meaning that she would put a hash mark or check mark.  I would like to know the spelling and the pronunciation of this word (if there is such a word?).\r\nThank you.', '2004-07-24 00:15:01', 0, 0),
(1412, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: Checkmark', 'The spelling is HACEK and for the pronuntiation see Merriam-Webster On-line Dictionary.\r\n\r\nhttp://www.m-w.com/cgi-bin/dictionary?book=Dictionary&va=hacek&x=13&y=11\r\n', '2004-07-26 13:41:58', 1411, 0),
(1413, 14, 'jana', '', 'Re: Should I learn Czech?', 'Greg,\r\nI\'ve been resisting quite nicely but I think I\'ll comment a little after all.\r\nBasicaly, surprising as you might find it, the command of Czech language is not limited to females and is fully unrelated to any kind of colouring (or any other \"parameters\", for that matter). I don\'t quite understand your motivation but learning a new language can be a very stimulating and enriching experiance worth your while, needless to say a way to widen your cultural horisonts.\r\nGood luck with learning whatever language you decide to go for,\r\nJana', '2004-07-28 20:16:16', 1381, 0),
(1414, 14, 'Hesia', 'hesiula@post.cz', 'Re: football', 'I was drinking with my Czech friends! ;-(\r\n;-)\r\n\r\nHesia', '2004-07-29 17:27:08', 1401, 0),
(1415, 21, 'Dominik Lukes', 'dlukes@bohemica.com', 'Problem se zarovnavanim', 'Je treba opravit textbox, aby se zarovnavalo (wrap), kdyz studentyi pisou souvisly text. (v tuit-ew).', '2004-07-30 16:56:51', 0, 0),
(1416, 13, 'cory', 'cniedfeldt@rsh.net', 'czech words', 'Hello, \r\nI\'m trying to learn how to say a few phrases in Czech.  They are\r\n\"What is your name?\"\r\n\"Do you like it here in America?\"\r\n\"You are very nice and very pretty\"\r\n\r\nThank you', '2004-08-09 21:15:57', 0, 0),
(1417, 13, 'hombre_sincero', '', 'knedliky', 'Hi there good people!\r\nI love \"knedliky\", but how should I correctly translate it in English? :-)\r\nThanks in advance ', '2004-08-10 20:12:23', 0, 0),
(1418, 13, 'hombre_sincero', '', 'knedliky', 'Hi there good people!\r\nI love \"knedliky\", but how should I correctly translate it in English? :-)\r\nThanks in advance ', '2004-08-10 20:27:26', 0, 0),
(1419, 13, 'Jirina Fuchsova', 'jfuchs@sprynet.com', 'Re: Questions about free online text translator', 'The existing so-called on-line trnslators\r\nwere devised by people who, unfortunately,\r\nhad no idea what Czech language really is...:-)\r\n   I would suggest the same as the contributor\r\nbefore: avoid \"on-line translator\" like a\r\nplague and contact the nearest living\r\nexperienced translator.\r\n   The previous person contributing here\r\nwas right: when one recives the fruits of\r\nlabor from on-line translator, the only thing\r\nleft to do is to bang your head against\r\n a wall in utter frustration, or, laugh\r\nthe next 24 hours at the least...\r\n   P.S. This advice comes from experience.....:-)', '2004-08-12 08:48:16', 1408, 0),
(1420, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: czech words', '\"What is your name?\" - Jak se jmenujes? (informal)\r\n\"Do you like it here in America?\" - Jak se ti/Vam tu libi? (ti = informal, Vam = formal)\r\n\"You are very nice and very pretty\" - careful with compliments, quite significant culture differences, nice in this sense = mila/mily (mila = fem, mily = masc) and pretty = hezka/hezky (again, a=fem, y=macs). my best advice would be to try a bit more indirect ways (that\'s very nice of you = to je od tebe/Vas moc mily)\r\nhope this helps,\r\njana', '2004-08-18 16:49:01', 1416, 0),
(1421, 13, 'Jira', '', 'Re: knedliky', 'Dumplings, I think.', '2004-09-01 18:35:47', 1418, 0),
(1422, 13, 'mike kren', 'mjkren@comcast.net', 'Re: knedliky', 'Small dumplings\r\n', '2004-09-11 02:24:48', 1418, 0),
(1423, 13, 'mark', '', 'Lhota', 'Driving through the Czech republik, I noticed that the word \'Lhota\'is frequently used in placenames. But I couldn\'t find it in my dictionary. Does the word \'Lhota\' means something???', '2004-09-14 15:02:30', 0, 1);
