(1712, 13, 'George', 'happy2woof@hotmail.com', 'Re: translation please?', 'Hi Simona,\r\n> \r\n> Thank you very much for your help! I was thinking that no one \r\n> would be able to help me. Here is the text of the message. I \r\n> am sorry if the message is too personal but I do not know \r\n> what Petra will say!\r\n\r\nPlease send me your email address so I can send you Petra\'s message to me.\r\n\r\nThanks!!!!!', '2005-07-12 06:05:43', 1703, 0),
(1713, 13, 'Thomas', 'tomasbb@eresmas.com', 'Re to myself', 'Sorry, I rush into the forum I hadn\'t read \"Bookstore\" in this site. So seems that Filipec\'s is what\'s available. But I still think has gotta be some school dictionary for children usable by foreigners.', '2005-07-19 18:49:15', 1704, 0),
(1714, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Why names of months are so different from other languages?', 'August is from Roman Emperor Augustine. July is from Julius Caeser. English, Italian and even Russian are very similar. December is similar in Russian language. But why Czech language is so different? Tell me the reason/origine of names of month in Czech language.\r\n(Cech beginner from Japan.)\r\n', '2005-07-22 13:15:47', 0, 0),
(1715, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Why names of months are so different from other languages?', 'Whatever I say, VladimĂ­r will say it better, so this is just a holdover until he arrives. :-)\r\n\r\nThere was a period, during the era of Hapsburg domination, where Czech was nearly \"rescued from the dead,\" as it was in danger of going extinct -- Czechs were required to speak German. In the 18th century, there began a reawakening of the Czech national identity, and part of that effort was the revitalization of the Czech language. Since this was an artificial process -- specific decisions on the new face of the revitalized Czech language rather than the way a language naturally develops -- there are many words that can be traced back to specific \"reawakeners\". One of these is a man named Josef Jungmann. (It\'s ironic that a reawakener of the Czech language and identity has such a German name.) And it is often said that \"Jungmann devised the names of the Czech months.\" Whether or not this is actually true or just a myth, I don\'t know, though. My intuition is that this is incorrect -- that he picked up or at least worked with existing folk month names.\r\n\r\nI\'d also like to add my intuition that other languages had their own month names and lost them for Latin-based names for reasons of convenience, whereas having *Czech* names for the months was a matter of national pride for the reawakeners. Again, this is only my intuition. \r\n\r\nEven though this is one of the most asked questions about Czech, I know of no reliable online source of information on the etymology of months, let alone in English. However, I did find just now a few spots of reasonable veracity. Most useful was this page:\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://www.libochovice.cz/ln/art_pub_detail.asp?art_ID=589\">http://www.libochovice.cz/ln/art_pub_detail.asp?art_ID=589</a>\r\n\r\nThe alternative theory for bĹezen comes from this page and exact copies of it:\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://voda.webz.cz/ces.html\">http://voda.webz.cz/ces.html</a>\r\n\r\nSo for what it\'s worth...\r\n\r\nLeden --> The month of led (ice)\r\nĂnor --> The vnoĹenĂ­ (sinking) of ice during the February thaw\r\nBĹezen --> One explanation is the budding of the bĹĂ­za (birch) and the start of the time when it releases sap, formerly used as an elixir; the other explanation is that important kinds of livestock are bĹezĂ­ (pregnant) at this time.\r\nDuben --> The budding of the dub (oak).\r\nKvÄten --> The whole of nature kvÄte (is in bloom).\r\nÄerven --> The time for gathering the nachtovĂ˝ Äervec (some sort of bug, whose name recalls the word Äerv which, today at least, means \"worm\"; you can see the entry from the archaic OttĹŻv slovnĂ­k nauÄnĂ˝ <a href=http://kaktusy.box.sk/vyuzitie/kosenila.php>here</a>) used for ÄervenĂ˝ (red) dye.\r\nÄervenec --> See Äerven. \r\nSrpen --> The month of the srp (sickle), the harvest month.\r\nZĂĄĹĂ­ --> Hmm, they say here that it\'s Za (during) the ĹĂ­[je] (having sex) of game animals. I knew that about ĹĂ­jen, but zĂĄĹĂ­? Plus bĹezen! THOSE ANIMAL-SEX-CRAZED MEDIEVAL CZECHS!!\r\nĹĂ­jen --> Game animals having sex.\r\nListopad --> The pad (falling) of listy (leaves).\r\nProsinec --> The Libochovice source says \"prosinÄti\" (until 50 years ago or so, it was standard for the infinitives of Czech verbs to be written with an utterly superfluous -i at the end until someone realized that was stupid), meaning prosvĂ­tati, meaning prosvĂ­tat, meaning \"to shine through,\" meaning heck if I know what they were thinking of. A <a href=\"http://www.muzeum-teplice.cz/historie/em0404.htm\">less reliable-seeming source</a> (one that says Jungmann invented the Czech month names) links it to \"prasinec\" (pig roast, something quite common in old-time Czech decembers). That explanation sounds to me to be a little bit too appealing to be true.\r\n\r\nJust FYI: Note that -en is a dead suffix; it is not used for word formation in modern words.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-07-22 16:17:40', 1714, 0),
(1716, 13, 'Milan Pokorny', 'czechlanguage@hotmail.co.uk', 'To Erik Piper', 'Dear Erik\r\nI have been watching your answers here to some people and it amazes me how much you know about my mother tongue.Therefore I would like to ask you.Are you English or any other national? Why are you interested in Czech ? I am sorry if I am too curious so if you prefer to send me a mail I will be happy to read it.If you do not want to reply then that is fine too .Thank you Milan', '2005-07-22 20:35:11', 0, 1),
(1717, 13, 'tom', 'ymmot24@comcast.net', 'Re: hospoda, hospodarnost, Hospodin', 'My last name is \"Hospod\" and from what I know it is of Old Church Slavonic origin and it means \"Lord\". i think that is the farthest back the meaning of the word goes.', '2005-07-26 09:52:00', 1618, 0),
(1718, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: To Erik Piper', 'I *live* here. :-) In the Czech Republic, I mean. Been here since fall of 93. So I *have* to be interested in Czech. I like helping other people and relaxing from my work at the same time, so I check this board and try to help if I can, whenever I can remember to do so.\r\n\r\nI\'m American.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-07-26 14:16:13', 1716, 0),
(1719, 13, 'Lizzie', 'slizz_@hotmail.com', 'names', 'My grandmother recently passed away and at her funeral I found out that her family (who came over from czechoslovakia) called her cachi (I spelled it phonetically, I don\'t know how it was actually spelled).  Her name was Caroline and so I was wondering if this is a common nickname for Caroline or if it means something else?\r\nthanks so much', '2005-07-30 06:12:38', 0, 1),
(1720, 14, 'KateĹina', '', 'Re: Is Czech food good?', 'Good^^', '2005-07-31 20:13:21', 219, 0),
(1721, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Re: Why names of months are so different from other languages?', 'Thanks Erik,\r\nIt was a very kind information. I appreciate it very much. As a matter of fact, I have been to Praha on July 28-30 and just returned to Japan. I bought some books on Czech languages. The reason I started to learn Czech was the suprise of the similarity of Czech and Russian languages, the latter I learned when I was in collage 30 years ago.\r\nI am preparing a Homepage of Introductory Czech langauage for people who knows Russian language.\r\nIf you happened to find any interesting topics from Czech/Russian similarity/disimailarity, put it on the Board. \r\nThank you very much. \r\nnomonomo', '2005-08-01 06:33:41', 1715, 0),
(1722, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: names', 'There is a name Karolina in Czech, but it would be far more typical to use the nickname KĂĄja (kah - yah) for that name. The nickname KĂĄÄa, in the sixth \"case\" -- form of the word -- which is used for addessing people, is informally KĂĄÄi (kah - chi), and thus probably what they were saying. This nickname, however, is associated with the name KateĹina (Catherine); why they were using it for a Caroline is a mystery to me.\r\n\r\nErik ', '2005-08-01 13:33:56', 1719, 0),
(1723, 15, 'inn26', 'inn26_katia@yahoo.com', 'Additional incomes for students...', 'Additional incomes for students, frequent travellers and dynamic individuals with flexible approach to their job-related duties. Inn26.com World Accommodation Guide is looking for Advertising Sales Agents worldwide - aged over 18, with excellent interpersonal and presentation sklills, positive attitude and great work ethic. \r\nPayment is based upon performance. High starting commissions. More information on http://www.inn26.biz and http://www.inn26.com. ', '2005-08-01 20:24:33', 0, 0);
