(1758, 14, 'Czechoslovak Society of Arts and Sciences (SVU)', 'SVU1@comcast.net', 'New book about Czechs and Slovaks in America', 'New Book about Czechs and Slovaks in America \r\n\r\nOn the occasion of his 75th birthday, the Czechosloslovak Society of Arts and Sciences (SVU) has just published a volume of selected writings of Miloslav Rechcigl, Jr., who is the Society\'s current President. It comprises general surveys, essays, reflections and personal insights of the author relating to the history and the contributions of Czech and Slovak immigrants in America and their descendants. \r\n\r\nThe material covered is based on Rechcigl\'s extensive research, which he began soon after his arrival in the US in 1950. It encompasses a wealth of information about the Czech and Slovak immigrants in America, from the time of the first known Bohemian who put his foot on American soil toward the end of 16th century, some thirty-five years before the arrival of the Pilgrims at Plymouth, MA in 1620, to date. Dr. Rechcigl has always been very intrigued by the life and accomplishments of Augustine Herman, the first historically known Bohemian to permanently settle in America a - the eminent Maryland and Virginia pioneer map maker, the Bohemian Jesuit missionary work in Latin America in the late 17th century, the Moravian Brethren?s Diaspora and their cultural contributions to America, and the immigration and the achievements of the Jewish pioneer settlers from the territory of the former Czechoslovakia, which is reflected in the monograph. Covered are also numerous contributions of Czech and Slovaks to the growth and development oi the United States, including science scholarship. \r\n\r\nApart from American ethnic history, Rechcigl has become an authority on the Czech American bibliography and genealogy which is the reason why the editors have also included his writings in this area. It complements the rest of the material remarkably well and enriches its contents and the utility of the volume as a documentary reference.The writings are based on the heretofore unpublished author\'s manuscripts, as well as on his selected periodical articles and book chapters that have been, for the most part, brought up to date. The volume is supplemented with Rechcigl\'s biographical sketch and his fascinating and provocative self-assessment ?What makes him tick? from his yet unpublished Memoirs, and the listing of his writings relating to the subject of this monograph, as well as articles about him \r\n\r\nThe monograph is a tribute to Mila Rechcigl for all his work of almost fifty years which he has devoted to building SVU, an international society aimed at the advancement of the Czech and Slovak studies worldwide and the fostering of intellectual collaboration with the Czech and Slovak Republics, which extends to almost fifty fears of his professional life and to his extraordinary scholarship relating to the history and contributions of Czechs and Slovaks abroad.We highly recommend this timely and unique publication to anyone who is interested in the history and contributions of Czech and Slovak immigrants in America. The book is an important source of information much of which has not been generally known. \r\n\r\nThe book which normally sells for $40, can be purchased by SVU members for $20 plus $5 for postage and handling. Please send your orders with your check to SVU Treasurer Frank Mucha: 36 West 88th St., New York, NY 10024. \r\n\r\n\r\n                                                                                 SVU Press Release \r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n', '2005-08-23 18:14:16', 0, 1),
(1759, 14, 'Czechoslovak Society of Arts and Sciences (SVU)', 'SVU1@comcast.net', 'Re: Czech immigration to Americas?', 'New Book about Czechs and Slovaks in America \r\n\r\nOn the occasion of his 75th birthday, the Czechosloslovak Society of Arts and Sciences (SVU) has just published a volume of selected writings of Miloslav Rechcigl, Jr., who is the Society\'s current President. It comprises general surveys, essays, reflections and personal insights of the author relating to the history and the contributions of Czech and Slovak immigrants in America and their descendants. \r\n\r\nThe material covered is based on Rechcigl\'s extensive research, which he began soon after his arrival in the US in 1950. It encompasses a wealth of information about the Czech and Slovak immigrants in America, from the time of the first known Bohemian who put his foot on American soil toward the end of 16th century, some thirty-five years before the arrival of the Pilgrims at Plymouth, MA in 1620, to date. Dr. Rechcigl has always been very intrigued by the life and accomplishments of Augustine Herman, the first historically known Bohemian to permanently settle in America a - the eminent Maryland and Virginia pioneer map maker, the Bohemian Jesuit missionary work in Latin America in the late 17th century, the Moravian Brethren?s Diaspora and their cultural contributions to America, and the immigration and the achievements of the Jewish pioneer settlers from the territory of the former Czechoslovakia, which is reflected in the monograph. Covered are also numerous contributions of Czech and Slovaks to the growth and development oi the United States, including science scholarship. \r\n\r\nApart from American ethnic history, Rechcigl has become an authority on the Czech American bibliography and genealogy which is the reason why the editors have also included his writings in this area. It complements the rest of the material remarkably well and enriches its contents and the utility of the volume as a documentary reference.The writings are based on the heretofore unpublished author\'s manuscripts, as well as on his selected periodical articles and book chapters that have been, for the most part, brought up to date. The volume is supplemented with Rechcigl\'s biographical sketch and his fascinating and provocative self-assessment ?What makes him tick? from his yet unpublished Memoirs, and the listing of his writings relating to the subject of this monograph, as well as articles about him \r\n\r\nThe monograph is a tribute to Mila Rechcigl for all his work of almost fifty years which he has devoted to building SVU, an international society aimed at the advancement of the Czech and Slovak studies worldwide and the fostering of intellectual collaboration with the Czech and Slovak Republics, which extends to almost fifty fears of his professional life and to his extraordinary scholarship relating to the history and contributions of Czechs and Slovaks abroad.We highly recommend this timely and unique publication to anyone who is interested in the history and contributions of Czech and Slovak immigrants in America. The book is an important source of information much of which has not been generally known. \r\n\r\nThe book which normally sells for $40, can be purchased by SVU members for $20 plus $5 for postage and handling. Please send your orders with your check to SVU Treasurer Frank Mucha: 36 West 88th St., New York, NY 10024. \r\n\r\n\r\n                                                                                 SVU Press Release \r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n', '2005-08-23 18:15:45', 1608, 1),
(1760, 13, 'pfk', 'mk85346@alltel.net', 'script for the written Czech language', 'Hello, Where can I find the Czech alfabet in various \r\nwritten language forms? I am not sure what time period\r\nI am looking for, but 18th or 19th century written forms\r\nwould be best. I am trying to translate a 1914 hand written\r\nletter and am having a hard time recognizing the letters.\r\nThank You,\r\nmk85346@alltel.net', '2005-08-24 00:00:33', 0, 0),
(1761, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: script for the written Czech language', 'You can find various manuscripts via this search:\r\n\r\nhttp://images.google.com/images?q=rukopis&hl=en&btnG=Search+Images\r\n\r\n...but as far as a table saying \"this letter of the alphabet looked like this in this era\"... I don\'t think you\'ll have much luck. If you upload it to an upload service like http://www.uploadit.org/ and publish a link to where you put it, I and the other sage folks here can take a look at it.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-26 17:13:40', 1760, 0),
(1762, 13, 'Isabelle', 'info@labradorclothing.com', 'Can anyone translate this czech review?', 'Hi,\r\nWe had a review of our website on a czech website, but are unable to understand what it says, could anyone give us an overall translation?\r\n\r\nThanks.\r\n\r\nIsabelle.\r\n\r\nKolonne Uniform\r\nproÄ si domĹŻ poĹĂ­dit labradora/rku\r\n\r\nNe nebudu vĂĄm kazit obÄd (Äi veÄeĹi nebo co zrovinka dÄlĂĄte) lĂ­ÄenĂ­m nechutnĂ˝ch sexuĂĄlnĂ­ch praktik, kde figuruje velkĂ˝ ÄernĂ˝ pes, \"OcelovĂ˝ Dan II\" a velkĂĄ zĂĄsoba bateriĂ­ Duracell, kterĂŠ vydrÂĂ­ tak dlouho a vÂechny ty nechutnosti. MĂĄlem bych zapomnÄl na sklenici malinovĂŠho dÂemu, dva kusy policejnĂ­ch pout (vzor CzechTek05) a dvanĂĄct velkĂ˝ch kosetnĂ­ch svĂ­cĂ­ s portrĂŠtem JanaPavla II. Labradora si do panelĂĄku radÂi nepoĹizujte, mĂ­sto toho si poĹiÄte pÄknĂŠ triÄko. Naivky a naivovĂŠ se mohou oblĂŠknout u Naive Clothing. Docela dobĹe ÂĂ­lenĂĄ je i prezentace triÄkĂĄrny Tight Knickers. Dramaticky budete vypadat v obleÄenĂ­ z Drama store, kde najdete hadry rĹŻznĂ˝ch znaÄek, knĂ­Âky, ÄasĂĄky a rĹŻznĂ˝ kravinky. Dobrou stravou (pastvou) pro oÄi jsou i vÄciÄky z masa a sĂ˝ra.\r\n', '2005-08-27 17:21:17', 0, 1),
(1763, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Re: Names of floors; Russian is American style', 'I noticed. Russian is American (Japanese) style; that is the floor one up from the ground floor is \"vtaroi etaji\" (the second floor). \r\nBut in Czech language it is \"prvni\" the first floor. The English style.\r\nDoes anyone know why Russian and American have this system?', '2005-08-29 06:17:09', 1755, 0),
(1764, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'ĹĂ­ci and ĹĂ­ct are the same word?', 'Some dictionary site \"ĹĂ­ct.\" And other \"ĹĂ­ci.\" I imagine this is because of the history of the change of writing rule.\r\nPlease tell me the history of these two words.\r\n', '2005-08-29 06:23:05', 0, 0),
(1765, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: ĹĂ­ci and ĹĂ­ct are the same word?', 'These are just the -[something]ti and -[something]t endings for the same verb\'s infinitive, something that exists for many (all?) verbs in Czech, not just here.\r\n\r\nAs far as I know: the -[something]ti endings for infinitives instead of -[something]t endings are formerly (until about 50, 70 years ago) required, then for formal speech only, and gradually becoming archaic. I would never use them in speech unless I was in a very formal situation (and I would be a little worried about misusing them, and also worried about sounding too informal by not using them -- formal writing makes me nervous overall. Generally I try to aim for the \"high end of spoken Czech\" -- not risking -ti endings, but also not using colloquial -Ă˝ and colloquial -ama endings, etc.). I sometimes use them in formal writing. With any highly formal trait there\'s always the risk of being hyperformal. (Sometimes in relaxed situations people use hyperformal style just to be funny, but this is hard for a foreigner to do well.)\r\n\r\nCheers!\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-08-29 18:31:03', 1764, 0),
(1766, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Can anyone translate this czech review?', 'Kolonne Uniform\r\nor: on why you should get yourself a Labrador for your home\r\n\r\nNo, I don\'t want to spoil your lunch (or supper or whatever you\'re doin\' right now) by describing the disgusting sexual practices where the big black dog \"Iron Dan II\" and Duracell batteries \"built to last\" come into play, or all the other disgusting things. And I almost forgot to mention the glass of raspberry jam, two sets of police handcuffs (CzechTek 05 style) and twelve large church (?) candles with a portrait of John Paul II. I\'d advise you *not* get a Labrador; buy yourself a nice t-shirt instead. The naive and naif among you can get them at Naive Clothing. Another nicely crazy thing here is the presentation of the Tight Knickers t-shirt enterprise. You\'ll look dramatic in clothing from the Drama store, where you\'ll find clothing of various brands, books, mags, and various silly stuff. The meat and cheese items are also a feast for the eyes.\r\n\r\nWeird review, huh?\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-29 18:38:42', 1762, 0),
(1767, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Re: ĹĂ­ci and ĹĂ­ct are the same word?', 'Thanks, Erik. Your response is always quick. I appreciate it.', '2005-08-30 07:56:26', 1765, 0),
(1768, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Is \"Ăşsta\" singular or plural?', 'I understand that the mouth is a concept of a vacant space. One thing. It is not lips or tongue. But do Czech people understad moouth as an organ composed of two parts? Just as lips? Or it just happened that expressions of mouth and mouths are the same? Ooooo. This stupid question never let me sleep at night. ', '2005-09-01 06:14:45', 0, 0),
(1769, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Is Ăşsta singular or plural?', 'Ăsta is a noun of a type called \"pluralia tantum\", which is just a fancy Latin way of saying \"always plural, even when it\'s singular.\" Another common example is dveĹe. So Ăşsta is actually nom. pl. of \"Ăşsto\", but \"Ăşsto\" sort of doesn\'t exist.\r\n\r\nI won\'t really answer the philosophical part of your question, because I can only give silly answers. The only answer I can give is because \"one day, Czech speakers reached the consensus that a singular can never be used for certain nouns, and Ăşsta is one of them.\"\r\n\r\n(They are often (always?) things that come in pairs, and I\'m thus tempted to talk about them in relation to the phenomenon called the \"duĂĄl\", which always deals with things in pairs and leads to formally required (as opposed to colloquial) 7th-case -ama endings. Fortunately, I don\'t understand duĂĄl much, so I won\'t muddy this discussion much with it.)\r\n\r\nBy the way, when you need to \"count\" these words, you use... :\r\njednĂĄ Ăşsta\r\ndvoje Ăşsta\r\ntroje Ăşsta\r\nÄtverĂĄ (I think) Ăşsta\r\n...if you need to count higher, ask somebody else. If you need to decline those adjectives CORRECTLY, also ask somebody else, though I think it\'s \r\njedna Ăşsta\r\nz jednĂ˝ch Ăşst\r\nk jednĂ˝m ĂşstĂĄm (???)\r\nna jedna Ăşsta\r\njednĂĄ Ăşsta! (try finding a practical reason to use THAT phrase!)\r\nv jednÄch Ăşstech (???)\r\njednama Ăşstama (?)\r\n\r\n...and a different set for dvoje/troje, and a different set for ÄtverĂĄ :-/ This is one of the parts of Czech where I am at my weakest.\r\n\r\nIn practice, you will \"of course\" when speaking with friends hear jednĂ˝ Ăşsta, not jednĂĄ Ăşsta. Unless your friends are ministers or professors. :-)\r\n\r\nFun fact: An old Czech saying is that it\'s not for nothing that we have dvÄ ruce a jen jedna Ăşsta. (One mouth; one \"set of lips\" I guess, even though lip is \"ret\".)\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-09-01 21:06:16', 1768, 0),
(1770, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: Is Ăşsta singular or plural?', '\'Plurale tantum\' is a noun which is formally in plural, but denotes a single object. It is not related to the dual number. Often it denotes a thing which can be divided in two identical or symmetrical parts: e.g. nĹŻÂky, kleÂtÄ (scissors), kalhoty (pants), etc. but not necessarily: hodiny (clock, litterally hours). Sometimes the plural has no apparent reason: jĂĄtra (liver).\r\n\r\nAgreement in number and gender:\r\n\r\nTy hodiny se zastavily. (fem. plur.)\r\nJĂĄtra alkoholika jsou zvÄtÂenĂĄ. (neutr. plur.)\r\nĂsta se zavĹela.\r\nAtĂŠny (Athenae, in Old Greek and Latin also plurale tantum) byly zaloÂeny ... (fem. plur.)', '2005-09-02 13:53:05', 1769, 0),
(1771, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: Is Ăşsta singular or plural?', '\"Jeden\" is declined like \"ten\":\r\n\r\nta jedna Ăşsta (mÄsta)\r\ntÄch jednÄch Ăşst (mÄst)\r\ntÄm jednÄm ĂşstĹŻm\r\nta jedna Ăşsta\r\njedna Ăşsta!\r\n(o) tÄch jednÄch Ăşstech\r\ns tÄmi jednÄmi Ăşsty\r\n', '2005-09-02 15:07:47', 1769, 0),
(1772, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Re: Is Ăşsta singular or plural?', 'Thank you very much, Erik and Vladimir. Now I can sleep well. (By the way, Japanese language does not have plural forms of (almost all) nouns.)', '2005-09-05 06:03:39', 1771, 0),
(1773, 13, 'seija', 'natsi@heathen.zzn.com', 'What are these words in english: \"DuÂe nesmrtelnĂ˝ch\"', '?????????\r\ncould someone translate this please:\r\n\r\nDuÂe nesmrtelnĂ˝ch', '2005-09-08 23:45:48', 0, 0),
(1774, 13, 'seija', 'natsi@heathen.zzn.com', 'Re: What are these words in english:', 'DuÂe \r\n\r\nthat word is perhaps \"soul\" or \"inner\"\r\n\r\nbut what is \"nesmrtelnĂ˝ch\" ???', '2005-09-08 23:49:48', 1773, 0),
(1775, 13, 'JV', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: Names of floors of a building', 'Don\'t forget the difference between patro and podlaÂĂ­.\r\n\r\npĹĂ­zemĂ­ = 1. podlaÂĂ­\r\n1. patro = 2. podlaÂĂ­\r\netc.\r\n\r\nNotice that only podlaÂĂ­ is technically correct term, while patro is semi-colloquial.\r\n\r\nJV', '2005-09-09 16:20:17', 1752, 0),
(1776, 13, 'jv', '', 'Re: What are these words in english:', 'Perhaps \"souls of immortals\"?', '2005-09-09 16:27:23', 1773, 0),
(1777, 13, 'maly zajic', 'tibunny3@yahoo.com', 'milenka - milenec', 'Ahoj everyone,\r\n\r\nPlease forgive me if the topic of this post offends some of you, but I would like to have a clarification on \"milenka\" and its masculine counterpart. I have heard a few explanations from czech women and men, but none were really satisfying in my opinion. It seems that the word \"milenka\" describes a lover, but without emotional bond, as opposed to \"milacek\". Is that right? If so, I\'m curious to hear your opinion on it, because in english/french/german, there\'s no such word to distinguish a purely physical relationship.\r\n\r\nDiky moc :0)', '2005-09-09 16:35:56', 0, 1),
(1778, 13, 'maly zajic', 'tibunny3@yahoo.com', 'milenka - milenec', 'Ahoj everyone,\r\n\r\nPlease forgive me if the topic of this post offends some of you, but I would like to have a clarification on \"milenka\" and its masculine counterpart. I have heard a few explanations from czech women and men, but none were really satisfying in my opinion. It seems that the word \"milenka\" describes a lover, but without emotional bond, as opposed to \"milacek\". Is that right? If so, I\'m curious to hear your opinion on it, because in english/french/german, there\'s no such word to distinguish a purely physical relationship.\r\n\r\nDiky moc :0)', '2005-09-09 17:06:03', 0, 1),
(1779, 13, 'Dominik LukeÂ', 'forums@bohemica.com', 'Re: milenka - milenec', '\"milenka\" really means mistress with all the emotional and sexual implications you find in English. \"milenec\" is the male counterpart to \"mistress\" which English does not have. \"milĂĄÄek\", on the other hand, describes emotional bond only - it can be used about friends and family or even animals.\r\n\r\n', '2005-09-09 17:07:57', 1777, 0),
(1780, 13, 'zajiceck', '', 'Re: milenka - milenec', 'Thank you Dominik for your useful explanation. Again, I had clearly the impression, talking to czech people, that addressing a woman as \"Milenko\" meant that there was only sexual connotations to the relation, and nothing emotional. They told me that if you did not care about the person, you should use \"Milenko\" and if you did, \"Milacku\". But maybe it\'s just their interpretation.', '2005-09-10 14:13:39', 1779, 0),
(1781, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: What are these words in english:', 'The spirit/soul (probably spirit) or spirits/souls of the immortals.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-09-12 17:17:42', 1773, 0),
(1782, 13, 'Craig', 'craig_leigh_harrison@yahoo.co.uk', 'Please could somebody translate this message of my girlfriend', 'Stra&#185;n&#236; moc mi chybĂ­&#185; a mĂĄm t&#236; stĂĄle p&#248;ed o&#232;ima.V Praze jsem &#232;ekala u&#190; \r\njen chvĂ­li, dala jsem si pĂĄrek v rohlĂ­ku.Cesta byla v pohod&#236;, &#232;etla \r\njsem si a u&#190; mi chybĂ­ jen 50 stran.U&#190; se t&#236;&#185;Ă­m a&#190; zase napĂ­&#185;e&#185;, zachvĂ­li \r\np&#249;jdu ven asi na kole, proto&#190;e je tu zase horko - 35 stup&#242;&#249;. Tak aĹĽ se \r\nti da&#248;Ă­ v prĂĄci, miluju t&#236; !!!!!!!!!\r\n', '2005-09-29 02:20:23', 0, 1),
(1783, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Please could somebody translate this message of my girlfriend', '\"I really miss you and can always imagine you in my mind. In Prague, I only kept on waiting a little bit longer; I had a Viennese hot dog. The trip was fine; I was reading and I only have 50 pages left. I\'m already looking forward to your next letter, in a while I\'ll be heading out on my bike, because it\'s hot here again -- 35 degrees. [95 degrees F.] So -- good luck in your work, I love you!!!\"\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-09-30 14:33:13', 1782, 0),
(1784, 14, 'ajjus', 'catch_cas@yahoo.com', 'desperately seeking sunshine', 'Hello all,\r\nI tried everything and this will be my last try. I\'m trying to find my friend, who I\'m unable to get in touch with.\r\nFirst name: Gabriela/Gabina\r\nLast name: Vbracikova (not sure of the spelling)\r\nCity: Prague\r\nHer email: sunshine@email.cz\r\n\r\nShe\'s been emailing me, but unfortunately my replies to her get bounced back. Looks like she has some kind of email bocker.\r\n\r\nI will truely appreciate any help offered in trying to find my lost friend.\r\nHere\'s a link to my post regarding this in another forum.\r\nhttp://www.myczechrepublic.com/boards/viewtopic.php?t=3582 \r\n\r\nthanks!\r\ncatch_cas@yahoo.com', '2005-10-02 23:15:29', 0, 1),
(1785, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: desperately seeking sunshine', 'I could try to email her in your name, in case my address is better for avoiding the spam filter, but I really would feel a bit strange doing so if I couldn\'t mention your name in the header... and \"catch cas\" and \"ajjus\" just don\'t feel like a well-guaranteeed of letting her know it\'s about you. :-) May I ask for your first and last name or at least first name and last initial, so I can mention them in the subject line? I\'ll give it a whirl if you do.\r\n\r\nThere is a realistic chance I\'ll have better luck -- Yahoo accounts are among the most frequently blocked by various ISPs due to the historically high spammer population there. The weird \"connection\" failures reported by the one guy on that other thread aren\'t too encouraging, but hopefully these were a temporary or unnecessary problem.\r\n\r\nBest regards,\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-10-04 16:58:18', 1784, 0),
(1786, 13, 'Pete', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'byvat, frequentative', 'Can someone tell me why bĂ˝vat is used in the following sentence rather than just a normal past tense?\r\n\r\nAby se uĹednĂ­k nezdrÂoval mÄnÄnĂ­m penÄz bĂ˝vĂĄ u pokladny tabulka s nĂĄpisem \".....\"\r\n\r\n', '2005-10-12 03:54:40', 0, 1),
(1787, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: byvat, frequentative', 'One usage of the frequentative is for the same purpose as the English phrases \"tends to smurf\", \"usually smurfs\", and \"generally smurfs\". So the quoted sentence means:\r\n\r\n\"To prevent unnecessary delays for clerks exchanging money, there is usually a sign saying \".....\" next to the register.\"\r\n\r\nFor what it\'s worth, the sentence literally says:\r\n\r\nIn-order-that himself clerk/bureaucrat/official does-not-delay by-the-exchanging of-money there-tends-to-be by register table/small-sign with writing-on-a-sign \"....\". \r\n\r\nCzech is pretty weird for a Western-language speaker if you look at it literally. :-)\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-10-12 15:39:45', 1786, 0),
(1788, 13, 'Maria', 'm8m@iname.com', 'Urgent Help with EN to CZ small editing of translation.', 'I am flying to Prague for urgent business on Friday. I am intolerant to foods with dairy (milk) products and gluten and was hoping someone could help with a small piece of text to help me in restaurants etc. I have the text in English and a similar text in czech (which needs editing). Could someone help me make the small changes to the czech text to match the english?\r\n\r\nEnglish:\r\n\r\nI have an illness and have to follow a strict gluten free and milk/milk product free diet. I may therefore become very ill if I eat food containing flours or grains of wheat, rye, barley and oats or any milk or milk products.\r\n\r\nI can eat food containing rice, maize, potatoes, all kinds of vegetables and fruit, eggs,  meat and fish Â as long as they are not cooked with wheat flour, batter, breadcrumbs or sauce or any milk products.\r\n\r\n&#8298; Does this food contain flour or grains of wheat, rye, barley or oats or any milk products? If you are at all uncertain about what the food contains, please tell me. \r\n\r\n&#8298; Can you recommend a meal / food?\r\n\r\nThank you for your help\r\n\r\n***************\r\nCzech: (this needs to be changed to incorporate the intolerance to milk and milk products as per the enlgish above) \r\n\r\nOnemocnÄl jsem CeliakiĂ­ a musĂ­m dodrzovat pĹĂ­snou glutenovou (bezlepkovou) dieu. \r\n\r\nMohl bych vĂĄznÄ onemocnet, kdybych jedl ptraviny obsahujĂ­cĂ­ mouku nebo pÄeniÄnĂĄ zrne, jeÄmen, oves, Âito. \r\n\r\n\r\nObsahujĂ­ tyto potraviny mouku nebo pseniÄnĂĄ Äi Âitna zrna, jeÄmen, oves? \r\n\r\n\r\nJestliÂe si nejste jist sloÂenĂ­m potravin, prosĂ­m ĹeknÄte mi to. \r\n\r\n\r\nMohu jĂ­st potraviny obsahujĂ­cĂ­ rĂ˝Âi, kukuĹici, brambory, zeleninu a ovo&#339;, vajĂ­Äka, sĂ˝ry, mlĂŠko, maso a ryby, pokud nejsou pĹipravovĂĄny s \r\npÂeniĂŠnou moukou, zĂĄsmaÂkou, osmaÂenĂ˝m chlebem nebo s omaÄkou. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nDĂŠkuji VĂĄm. \r\n\r\n\r\nMany Thanks\r\nMaria\r\nm8m@iname.com\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n', '2005-10-13 07:14:51', 0, 1),
(1789, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Urgent Help with EN to CZ small editing of translation.', 'Hi Maria,\r\n\r\nI\'m not Czech and therefore the above may contain grammatical errors, but considering that your health is at line, I figure it\'s better to have something (as long as it\'s understandable) on time than to have Shakespeare too late.\r\n\r\nThe Czech text cited diverges content-wise in a few spots from your English text; in these spots I\'ve gone by the English text.\r\n\r\nThe second bullet point at the end of the Czech text doesn\'t match your second point at all, but is still perhaps useful. (It reads: if you are not sure of a particular food\'s composition, please say so.) I\'ve included it, as a third bullet point in list.\r\n\r\nBest regards,\r\n\r\nErik\r\n\r\n--\r\n\r\n\r\nTrpĂ­m nemocĂ­ celiakie a musĂ­m proto dodrÂovat pĹĂ­snou dietu bezlepkovou a bez mlĂŠÄnĂ˝ch vĂ˝robkĹŻ. Mohla bych totiÂ vĂĄznÄ onemocnet, kdybych jedla potraviny obsahujĂ­cĂ­ mouku nebo pÄeniÄnĂŠ zrno, jeÄmen, oves, Âito, nebo mlĂŠÄnĂŠ vĂ˝robky. \r\n\r\nNaopak nejsou problĂŠmem jĂ­dla obsahujĂ­cĂ­ rĂ˝Âi, kukuĹici, brambory, jakĂĄkoli zelenina Äi ovoce, vejce, nebo rybu - pokud ovÂem nejsou vaĹenĂŠ s pÂeniÄnou moukou, tÄstem, osmaÂenĂ˝m chlebem atp., ani s omĂĄÄkou nebo mlĂŠÄnĂŠ vĂ˝robky.\r\n\r\n* ObsahujĂ­ tyto potraviny mouku nebo pseniÄnĂĄ Äi ÂitnĂĄ zrna? jeÄmen? oves? \r\n\r\n* MĹŻÂete doporuÄit s ohledem na tuto situaci nÄkterĂŠ jĂ­dlo?\r\n\r\n* JestliÂe si nejste jist sloÂenĂ­m potravin, prosĂ­m ĹeknÄte mi to. ', '2005-10-13 20:49:05', 1788, 0),
(1790, 13, 'Eva Knight- Malciak', 'evamalciakova@seznam.cz', 'Re: What are these words in english:', 'It means The souls of the immortals ones.', '2005-10-16 02:12:12', 1773, 0),
(1791, 13, 'Eva Knight-malciak', '', 'Re: milenka - milenec', 'Hello!\r\nI suppose that \"lover\'in English means, unlike in Czech, somebody having a sexual relationship without neccessary being in love with that person.If you want to imply that there is something emotional going on you would probably use word \"moje laska\"- my love. You wouldn\'t use \"milacek\' when speaking about that person. You use it when talking to that person.So the word milenka in czech is rather a cold word, used  mainly for somebody having an affair when married.( on ma milenku, milence). I hope this helps.\r\n\r\nBest wishes Eva Knight-Malciak\r\n    ', '2005-10-16 02:37:33', 1778, 1),
(1792, 14, 'Ela Szlufik-Szczesna', 'giwia@gazeta.pl', 'looking for a Bohemian pen pal', 'I have Bohemian roots. My ancestors came to Poland from Bohemia in 19c .\r\nI am married, about 40 years old, looking for a pen pal who would like to tell me more about the motherland of my ancestors', '2005-10-16 17:20:58', 0, 1),
(1793, 13, 'questionnnnn', '', 'what does this mean?', '\"valim se ve statlu a su moc\"\r\n\r\n\r\nthx!', '2005-10-18 05:59:33', 0, 0),
(1794, 13, 'magda', 'alcor69_69@yahoo.com', 'I found a great way to call home to Ostrava', 'I have been using www.callingforcheap.com\r\n\r\nThought you all might like to know and they have a very good speed dial option.', '2005-10-20 06:25:54', 0, 0),
(1795, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: what does this mean?', 'I\'m sitting around in Brno and I\'m very [ends here].\r\n\r\nLooks like it got cut off.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-10-20 14:42:36', 1793, 0),
(1796, 13, 'steve', 'stelingouk@aol.com', 'accusative or locative?', 'How would I translate the following sentence? Children start school at 5 years old.\r\nDeti zacinaji skolu v pet let (acc) or v peti letech (loc)\r\n\r\nDekuji za pomoc.\r\n\r\nSteve', '2005-10-23 02:20:28', 0, 1),
(1797, 13, 'Quinn', 'quinn_romance@yahoo.com', 'Proud of Czech Heritage', 'I just learned from some distant relatives about my Czech heritage, and I\'m so proud of what I am. It\'s really cool to have people ask you, \'Can you tell me more about the Czech people and your heritage?\'. I\'ve only been asked a few times, but each time I explained it, a wash of pride swept over me. I love the Czech people. (Super random? Of course.)', '2005-10-24 06:40:45', 0, 1),
(1798, 13, 'bunny', 'tibunny3@yahoo.com', 'Re: milenka - milenec', 'Ahoj Evo,\r\n\r\n:) Sorry for the late reply, I rarely check that email...\r\n\r\nThank you for your explanation, although it still is not clear to me.\r\n\r\nDo you agree with:\r\n\r\nlaska: big love & sex\r\nmilacek: maybe love & sex\r\nmilenec: no love & sex\r\n\r\n:)\r\n', '2005-10-27 04:34:40', 1791, 1),
(1799, 13, 'Jakub', '', 'Re: accusative or locative?', 'These two variants are both possible: \"v peti letech\" or \"ve stari peti let\".\r\nIs that the answer you want or is it even more confusing?\r\nJakub', '2005-10-31 02:57:41', 1796, 0),
(1800, 13, 'steve', 'stelingouk@aol.com', 'acc or loc?', 'These two variants are both possible: \"v peti letech\" or \"ve stari peti let\". \r\nIs that the answer you want or is it even more confusing? \r\nJakub \r\n\r\nI\'ve had to start this as a new topic as my reply doesn\'t seem to appear. What case is \'peti let\' in the phrase \"ve stari peti let\"?\r\n', '2005-10-31 03:35:35', 0, 1),
(1801, 17, 'Val', 'valkovalenko@hotmail.com', 'Books in Chech', 'Kde muzu koupit czeski knihi v Londyne?\r\nWhere could purchase books in Czech in London?\r\n', '2005-10-31 03:56:32', 0, 1),
(1802, 13, 'Jakub', '', 'Re: acc or loc?', '\"ve stari\" - loc\r\n\"peti let\" - gen\r\n\r\nLit. \'at the age of five years\'\r\n\r\n\"v peti letech\" - loc\r\n\r\nLit. \'at five years\'\r\n\r\n', '2005-10-31 17:18:06', 1800, 0),
(1803, 14, 'Jakub', '', 'Re: looking for a Bohemian pen pal', 'Sorry, can\'t help you. I\'am from Silesia and live in Moravia :(', '2005-11-01 20:40:53', 1792, 0),
(1804, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: acc or loc?', 'The preposition \"v\" is rarely used with the accusative case.\r\n\r\nvÄĹit, doufat v nÄkoho, v nÄco (acc.)\r\n\r\ne.g. VÄĹĂ­me v Boha (acc.). We trust in God.\r\n', '2005-11-03 12:59:25', 1800, 0),
(1805, 17, 'TomĂĄÂ ĹĂĄda', 't.rada@centrum.cz', 'Jen tak', 'DobrĂ˝ den.\r\n\r\n         PochĂĄzĂ­m z ÄeskĂŠ Republiky a narazil jsem na vĂĄÂ web.Moc se my lĂ­bil,a tak jsem se rozhodl,Âe vĂĄm napĂ­Âu.JĂĄ bydlĂ­m kousek od Prahy v Kralupech nad Vltavou.A odkud jste vy ???UÄĂ­m se AngliÄtinu.ZdravĂ­ vĂĄs TomĂĄÂ.', '2005-11-07 00:03:28', 0, 0),
(1806, 17, 'sifak', 't.rada@centrum.cz', 'Re: :o)', 'JĂĄ pochĂĄzĂ­m z ÄeskĂŠ Republiky,jestli si se mnou chceÂ Äesky psĂĄt,napiÂ mi na email t.rad@centrum.cz.Äus\r\n', '2005-11-07 00:08:37', 1120, 0),
(1807, 13, 'Kristina', '', 'Re: milenka - milenec', 'I know when my boyfriend stopped referring to me as milenka, when he said ty jsi moje mila, then I knew we had progressed.  He has subsequently said to me in conversation, that to a czech man - Milenka je jenom na sex, Kristino.', '2005-11-15 11:00:33', 1791, 0),
(1808, 13, 'Pete', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'Jen & Pouze', 'Which ones of these fits which senses of \"only\".\r\n\r\nIs it \"pouze\" is a bit like \"exclusive for...\" as in \"only for adults\" whereas \"jen\" covers the other senses eg. \"I have only five carrots left?\"\r\n\r\n', '2005-11-17 04:59:27', 0, 1),
(1809, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: Jen & Pouze', 'Hi!\r\nIn my opinion \"jen\" and \"pouze\" are completely interchangeable. I can\'t realize any case where one of them can go and the other cannot.\r\nJakub', '2005-11-17 19:03:51', 1808, 0),
(1810, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: Jen & Pouze', 'I can:\r\n\r\n- SmĂ­m si vzĂ­t?\r\n- Jen si dej!\r\n', '2005-11-18 22:32:01', 1809, 0);

