(1955, 1, 'Prepositions and pronouns', '', 49, 'Complete appropriate  pronouns', 'ZnĂĄÂ mĂŠho bratra Petra? ÄekĂĄm tady na [$1, text:nÄho]. MyslĂ­m na [$1, text:svĂŠho] bratra? MusĂ­m pro [$1, text:nÄho] koupit dĂĄrek. DĂ­vĂĄm se [$1, text:na] desku za dvÄstÄ korun. Je na nĂ­ modernĂ­ americkĂĄ hudba. Bratr [$1, text:ji] mĂĄ rĂĄd. MĂĄme taky sestru Janu. Chceme pro [$1, text:ni] koupit velkĂ˝ dĂĄrek spolu. Jana si chce koupit nÄjakĂŠ levnĂŠ auto. LĂ­bĂ­ se jĂ­ jedno starĂŠ Volvo. DĂ­vĂĄ se na [$1, text:nÄj] Äasto v autobazaru.', 3, NULL),
(1956, 1, 'Examples of all TuiT capabilities', '', 49, 'Examples of all TuiT capabilities', '<TABLE cellSpacing=0 cellPadding=5 width=\"100%\" align=top border=0>\r\n<TBODY>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD>[$1, text 20]</TD>\r\n<TD>Textfield</TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD>[$1, text:text] </TD>\r\n<TD>Textfield with correct answer</TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD>dobr[$1, text 1:Ă˝] </TD>\r\n<TD>\r\n<P>Textfield with one letter answer (cloze).</P></TD></TR>\r\n<TD>[$1, select:/in/(on/at] time </TD>\r\n<TD>\r\n<P>Pull down choice menu</P></TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD>a) [$1$0, checkbox] b) [$1$0, checkbox] c) [$1$0, checkbox] </TD>\r\n<TD>\r\n<P>Multiple choice (more than one option possible)</P></TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD>\r\n<P>a) [$0, radio x] b)Â [$0, radio x] c)Â [$0, radio x] </P></TD>\r\n<TD>Multiple choice (one option only)</TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD>[%Sample text] </TD>\r\n<TD>Field for displaying examples and hints</TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD>[\"Hint:Entering and diplaying hints through alert!] </TD>\r\n<TD>Pop-up clues, cues and hints</TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD>[{text] </TD>\r\n<TD>Self correcting field (correct answer \'text\')</TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD>\r\n<P>[{name=t1]<BR>[{target=t1:This is your clue] </P></TD>\r\n<TD>Clue displayed in a different field</TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD>[^malĂ˝] </TD>\r\n<TD>Looking up a word in the dictionary</TD></TR>\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD>[^malĂŠho:malĂ˝]</TD>\r\n<TD>Looking up a conjugated/declined word in the dictionary</TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE> \r\n<P>\r\n[$1, textarea 60,4]<br>\r\nTextbox for essay type questions<p>\r\n<a target=new href=http://www.bohemica.com/files/tagdescriptions.htm>Click here to see the tags necessary to create these exercise items.</a>', 1, NULL),
(1958, 1, 'Bussiness English - tyden 1', '', 53, '', '       <h1><a name=_Toc461865075>1 Firmy a spoleÄnosti</a></h1>    <h2><a name=_Toc431613685></a><a name=_Toc441911620></a><a  name=_Toc461865076>1.1Â Â Â Â Â  Business and businesses Â PodnikĂĄnĂ­ a podniky</a></h2>    <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=40%>   <tbody>     <tr>       <td valign=top>business<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>       <p>obchod, podnikĂĄnĂ­; podnik</p>       </td>     </tr>     <tr>       <td valign=top>company<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>firma, spoleÄnost<br>       </td>     </tr>     <tr>       <td valign=top>firm<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>firma<br>       </td>     </tr>     <tr>       <td valign=top>concern<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>podnik; koncern<br>       </td>     </tr>   </tbody> </table>    <p>Â </p>    <p><b>Business</b> is the production, buying and selling of goods and services.</p>    <p>Â </p>    <p>A <b>business, company </b>or <b>firm</b> is an organization that sells  goods or services.</p>    <p>Â </p>    <p>A business may also be referred to formally as a <b>concern.</b></p>    <p>Â </p>    <p>The business of America is <u>business</u>.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>Unless you understand how money flows through a <u>business</u>, you will not understand the operation.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>Mr GatesÂs boyhood pastime was computer programming. Today Microsoft, his <u>company</u>, is the worldÂs most successful supplier of computer software.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>Imagine Japan allowing Sony to be the only Japanese <u>firm</u> to make a product like Walkman.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>Du Pont, a chemicals <u>concern</u> based in Wilmington, Del., brings its extensive agricultural research staff to the project.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>Â </p> <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>   <tbody>     <tr>       <td valign=top>enterprise<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>       <p>podnik; podnikĂĄnĂ­</p>       </td>     </tr>     <tr>       <td valign=top>free enterprise<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>svobodnĂŠ podnikĂĄnĂ­<br>       </td>     </tr>     <tr>       <td valign=top>private enterprise<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>soukromĂŠ podnikĂĄnĂ­<br>       </td>     </tr>     <tr>       <td valign=top>commerce<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>obchod, podnikĂĄnĂ­; obchodnĂ­ vztahy; komerce<br>       </td>     </tr>     <tr>       <td valign=top>commercial<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>obchodnĂ­, komerÄnĂ­<br>       </td>     </tr>   </tbody> </table>    <p>Â </p>    <p>A business may be referred to approvingly as an <b>enterprise</b> to emphasize its adventurous, risk&#8209;taking qualities, and business in general may be referred to in the same way, for example in combinations such as <b>free enterprise</b> and <b>private enterprise</b>.</p>    <p>Â </p>    <p>Business is also referred to as <b>commerce</b>. This word, and its related adjective <b>commercial</b>, are often used to distinguish the business sphere from other areas such as government or the arts, or to distinguish it from non&#8209;money&#8209;making activities. </p>    <p>Â </p>    <p>There is now greater emphasis on terms like project and venture, setting up an <u>enterprise</u> that will run for a time and then be sold on.</p>    <p><br> [$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>Americans have an ease with individual <u>enterprise</u> and risk that few Europeans share.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>The preservation of a system of <u>free enterprise</u> is essential to economic freedom.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>Government in Malaysia has promised to reduce its interference in the economy and rely on <u>private enterprise </u>to pace economic growth.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>The governmentÂs concern is to ensure that schools are teaching skills which are useful to industry and <u>commerce</u>.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>Kangaroo hunting is not just a hobby but a lucrative <u>commercial</u> business.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>   <b><br clear=all>  </b>   <h2><a name=_Toc431613686></a><a name=_Toc441911621></a><a  name=_Toc452353954></a><a name=_Toc461865077>1.2Â Â Â Â Â  From multinationals to small firms<br>  Od nadnĂĄrodnĂ­ch koncernĹŻ aÂ po malĂŠ firmy</a></h2>    <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>   <tbody>     <tr>       <td valign=top>corporation<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>spoleÄnost, akciovĂĄ spoleÄnost, korporace<br>       </td>     </tr>     <tr>       <td valign=top>corporate<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>podnikovĂ˝, spoleÄenskĂ˝ (<i>smlouva</i>); celospoleÄenskĂ˝<br>       </td>     </tr>     <tr>       <td valign=top>multinational<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>mnohonĂĄrodnĂ­, nadnĂĄrodnĂ­ (<i>podnik</i>)<br>       </td>     </tr>     <tr>       <td valign=top>big business<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>velkĂ˝ podnik, korporace, prĹŻmyslovĂ˝ gigant<br>       </td>     </tr>     <tr>       <td valign=top>       <p>small and medium-sized companies<br>       </p>   </td>       <td valign=top>malĂŠ a stĹednĂ­ firmy, malĂ­ a stĹednĂ­ podnikatelĂŠ<br>       </td>     </tr>     <tr>       <td valign=top>small business<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>malĂĄ firma, malĂ˝ podnik<br>       </td>     </tr>     <tr>       <td valign=top>small firm<br>       </td>       <td valign=top>malĂĄ firma<br>       </td>     </tr>   </tbody> </table> <p>Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â </p>      <p>Large companies are referred to as <b>corporations,</b> especially in the United States.</p>    <p>Â </p>    <p><b>Corporate</b> is used to describe things relating to a corporation, or to corporations in general. </p>    <p>Â </p>    <p>Large companies operating in many countries are <b>multinationals.</b></p>    <p><b>Â </b></p>    <p><b>Big businesses</b> can refer to large business organizations or to any business activity that makes a lot of money.</p>    <p>Â </p>    <p>Small companies are referred to as <b>small businesses</b> or <b>small firms.</b></p>    <p><b>Â </b></p>    <p>Unlike some languages, English does not have an everyday term for <b>small and medium-sized companies, </b>apart from this rather clumsy expression.</p>    <p>Â </p>    <p>The South depends more on small and medium-sized businesses for jobs than on large <u>corporations</u>.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>The decision was taken to sell BPÂs three <u>corporate </u>jets.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>Some of the operations of a typical <u>multinational</u> are more global than others.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>In Korea, democracy has slowed some government decision-making. But it has also cut the power of <u>big business</u> groups and the bureaucracy, in the process creating freer markets and more opportunities for <u>small businesses</u>.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p align=center>Â </p>    <p>Tourism in Hong Kong is <u>big business</u>: it is the third largest source of foreign exchange and employs, directly and indirectly, 180,000 people.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>Women run 25 per cent of existing <u>small businesses</u>, but 33 per cent of new businesses are started by women.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>...the <u>small firm</u>Âs greatest handicap: not size, but loneliness.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <p>...the South-West of Germany, a haven ofÂ  <u>small and medium-sized companies</u>, or ÂMittelstandÂ as they are known.</p>    <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>        ', 1, NULL),
(1959, 1, 'Bussiness English - tyden 2', '', 53, '', '          <h2><a name=_Toc452353955>1.3Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  Industries and sectors Â OdvÄtvĂ­  a sektory</a></h2>      <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>    <tbody>      <tr>        <td valign=top>industryÂ <br>        </td>        <td valign=top>odvÄtvĂ­, prĹŻmyslovĂŠ odvÄtvĂ­; prĹŻmysl<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>sector<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>sektor; odvÄtvĂ­<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>public sector<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>veĹejnĂ˝ (<i>stĂĄtnĂ­</i>) sektor<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>private sector<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>soukromĂ˝ sektor<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>manufacturing sector<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>vĂ˝robnĂ­ sektor, zpracujĂ­cĂ­ prĹŻmysl<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>service sector<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>sektor sluÂeb, terciĂĄrnĂ­ sfĂŠra<br>        </td>      </tr>       </tbody>  </table>   <p>Businesses may be classified according to which <b>industry</b> they are  in: for example construction, oil, banking, food.</p>        <p><b>Sector</b> is used to mean industry in the same way, particularly by  specialists such as financial journalists, but it is more often used to talk  about different parts of the economy in combinations such as <b>public sector  </b>and <b>private sector</b>, or about types of business in expressions like <b>service sector</b> and <b>manufacturing sector.</b></p>      <p><b>Â </b></p>      <p>ArdenÂs customers are primarily in the <u>packaging industry</u> but also  the <u>electronics sector</u>.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Many people in the <u>private sector</u> had to take very low pay increases  this year.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>The French government is thinking of introducing German-style worker participation  into the state industries. This suggests that <u>public-sector</u> companies  will not be free from political interference, a fact that deters private,  profit-minded investors.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>One industry that has kept the <u>manufacturing sector</u> going for much  of this year is aircraft.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Many young workers are taking jobs in the rapidly growing <u>service sector</u>  Â banking, computer programming, financial services.</p>        <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>   <p align=center>Â </p>        <h2><a name=_Toc452353957>1.4Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  Public sector and private Â VeĹejnĂ˝  a soukromĂ˝ sektor</a></h2>      <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>    <tbody>      <tr>        <td valign=top>nationalized<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>zestĂĄtnÄnĂ˝, znĂĄrodnÄnĂ˝<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>nationalization<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>zestĂĄtnÄnĂ­, znĂĄrodnÄnĂ­<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>privatized<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>privatizovanĂ˝<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>privatization<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>privatizace<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>state-owned<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>stĂĄtnĂ­, stĂĄtem vlastnÄnĂ˝<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>sell offÂ <br>        </td>        <td valign=top>odprodat<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>sell-off<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>odprodej<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>utilities<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>podniky veĹejnĂ˝ch sluÂeb, veĹejnĂŠ sluÂby, ÂinfrastrukturaÂ<br>        </td>      </tr>       </tbody>  </table>   <p><br>  When a private company is bought by the state and brought into the public  sector, it is <b>nationalized </b>in a process of <b>nationalization. </b>A  nationalized company is <b>state-owned.<br>  <br>  </b></p>        <p>When the state returns a company to the private sector in a <b>sell-off,</b>  it is <b>privatized.</b> This is <b>privatization.</b></p>      <p><b>Â </b></p>      <p>The first companies to be <b>sold off</b> in a privatization programme  are often the companies responsible for the public supply of electricity,  water and gas: the <b>utilities.</b></p>      <p><b>Â </b></p>      <p>Â Â Â  LEXIKOLOGICKĂ POZNĂMKA <br>   Privatize, privatization, nationalize, a nationalization se v britskĂŠ angliÄtinÄ  pĂ­ÂĂ­ rovnÄÂ privatise, privatisation, nationalise, and nationalisation.</p>      <p><b>Â </b></p>      <p>British Rail stations worth about &#163;10 billion will be <u>sold off</u> under Government plans to <u>privatise</u> the network.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Malaysia is five years into an initial <u>privatization</u> program. To  date, 22 government-owned concerns have been privatized, including a lottery,  the national airline and shipping companies, regional water <u>utilities</u>,  highway construction projects and a commercial tv station.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Moving even further from the Swedish model, Sweden is to sell 35 <u>state-owned</u>  companies. The <u>sell-off</u> could raise as much as $50 billion.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Government intervention in manufacturing investment is a bad joke in every  for&#8209;GodÂs&#8209;sake&#8209;letÂs&#8209;privatise&#8209;it <u>nationalised</u> industry from Buenos  Aires to Omsk.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>              <p>Mr Salinas has undone two gross acts of confiscation, namely the nationalisation  of the banks in 1982. The other was the <u>nationalisation</u> of the oil  industry by Lazaro Cardenas in 1938. The oil business is the biggest sacred  cow of them all.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Â </p>      <h2>1.5Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  Stakes Â PodĂ­ly</h2>      <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>    <tbody>      <tr>        <td valign=top>shares<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>akcie; podĂ­ly<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>equity<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>(<i>kmenovĂĄ</i>) akcie<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>holding<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>podĂ­l, pozice<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>shareholding<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>(<i>akciovĂ˝</i>) podĂ­l<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>shareholder<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>akcionĂĄĹ, podĂ­lnĂ­k<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>stake<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>podĂ­l, balĂ­k (<i>akciĂ­</i>), hmotnĂ˝ zĂĄjem<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>hold a stake<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>drÂet akcie<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>majority stake<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>vÄtÂinovĂ˝ balĂ­k<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>controlling stake<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>kontrolnĂ­ balĂ­k<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>minority stake<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>menÂinovĂ˝ balĂ­k<br>        </td>      </tr>       </tbody>  </table>   <p>Â </p>      <p>If Company A owns <b>shares</b> or <b>equity </b>in Company B, A has or  <b>holds a stake, holding </b>or <b>shareholding </b>in B.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>If A owns less than half the shares in B, it has a <b>minority stake </b>in  B.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>If A owns more than half the shares in B, it has a <b>majority stake</b>  or <b>controlling stake</b> in B.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>If you have shares in a company, you are a <b>shareholder.</b></p>      <p><b>Â </b></p>      <p>One aircraft industry analyst said, ÂIf GPA is to survive, it must raise  more <u>equity</u> from its existing <u>shareholders</u>.Â</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>The Spanish banks have the sorts of links with the corporate world that  are typical of the German banks, with the banks holding large <u>stakes</u>  in almost all the major Spanish companies. Some of the banks have begun to  rethink this situation.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>The position of the Fayed brothers, proprietors of Harrods, who have <u>held</u>  a 10% <u>shareholding</u> in Sears for years, is becoming very interesting.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>The very rich Marc Rich is to retire from the company he founded 15 years  ago and will reduce his <u>controlling stake</u> to 15% within five years.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>McPherson always takes a <u>majority stake</u> in the businesses he invests  in and he likes a hands-on role in the management.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>        <p>Eurocom has been attempting to raise its <u>minority stake</u> in WCRÂs  ad business to a majortiy.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4] </p>           <br> ', 1, NULL),
(1960, 1, 'Bussiness English - tyden 3', '', 53, '', '          <h2><a name=_Toc452353958>1.6Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  Parents and sisters Â MateĹskĂŠ a sesterskĂŠ spoleÄnosti</a></h2>      <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>    <tbody>      <tr>        <td valign=top>holdingÂ company<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>holding, holdingovĂĄ spoleÄnost<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>subsidiary<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>filiĂĄlka, dceĹinnĂĄ spoleÄnost, divize<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>wholly-owned<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>stoprocentnÄ kontrolovanĂĄ<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>parent company<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>mateĹskĂĄ spoleÄnost<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>sister company<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>sesterskĂĄ spoleÄnost  <br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top> brother-sister company<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>sesterskĂĄ spoleÄnost<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>group<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>(<i>finanÄnĂ­ n. prĹŻmyslovĂĄ</i>) skupina; koncern<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>conglomerate<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>konsorcium<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>giant<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>obr, mamutĂ­ podnik<br>        </td>      </tr>       </tbody>  </table>   <p><br>  A <b>holding</b> or <b>holding company </b>is one that holds stakes in one  or more <b>subsidiaries</b>. If it owns all the shares in a subsidiary, the  subsidiary is a <b>wholly-owned </b>one.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>A holding companyÂs relationship to its subsidiaries is that of a <b>parent  company</b>, and the subsidiariesÂ relationship to each other is that of <b>sister companies</b>. A holding and its subsidiaries from a <b>group</b>.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>A <b>conglomerate </b>is a group containing a lot of different companies  in different businesses. Journalists also refer to large groups as <b>giants</b>.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Â Â Â  LEXIKOLOGICKĂ POZNĂMKA <br>  NÄkteĹĂ­ lidĂŠ povaÂujĂ­ vĂ˝raz sister company za sexistickĂ˝ a pouÂĂ­vajĂ­ mĂ­sto  nÄj brother-sister companies.<br>  <br>  </p>      <p>Newspaper Publishing, the <u>holding company</u> which owns the daily and Sunday Independent titles, announced the plans to increase the Spanish/Italian  stake as a part of a range of financing options.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Mr Fitzpatrick did not gain control of Goodman Holdings, the Irish-registered  <u>parent</u> of the maze of Goodman companies, until last Friday. Goodman  Holdings has <u>subsidiaries</u> in Liechtenstein and the Channel Islands,  which control investments around the world.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>General Motors is, in effect, an ÂallianceÂ in which the principal ÂpartnersÂ  are not merely <u>wholly owned subsidiaries</u> but elements within a single  integrated management structure. Toyota is a much weaker confederation.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Oakland tries to avoid contracts with companies that it lists as doing  business in South Africa. The list covers companies with license agreements,  though it still doesnÂt touch <u>brother-sister companies</u>.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>...the personal stereo invented by electronics <u>giant</u> Sony.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>The fate of the sportswear company Adidas was decided yesterday. The French  businessman Bernard Tapie has sold his controlling stake in the <u>group</u>  to a group of investors for DM615 million.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Pearson, a publishing-to-tableware <u>conglomerate</u> that controls the  Financial Times, Penguin books and 50% of The Economist, bid for Thames Television,  BritainÂs biggest independent TV producer. Thorn EMI, a music-to-lighting  group, accepted, agreeing to sell its 59% stake in Thames for &#163; 99 million.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <b><br clear=all>   </b>    <h2><a name=_Toc452353959>1.7Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  Launching a bid Â NabĂ­dka na akvizici  firmy</a></h2>      <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>    <tbody>      <tr>        <td valign=top>take over<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>pĹevzĂ­t (<i>kontrolu and nÄÄĂ­m</i>); zĂ­skat (<i>firmu</i>)<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>takeover<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>pĹevzetĂ­, akvizice<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>takeover bid<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>nabĂ­dka na pĹevzetĂ­<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>takeover target<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>obÄÂ akvizice<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>launch a bid<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>uÄinit nabĂ­dku na pĹevzetĂ­<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>hostile bid<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>nepĹĂĄtelskĂĄ nabĂ­dka, Ăştok na firmu<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>acquire<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>zĂ­skat<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>make an acquisition<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>uskuteÄnit akvizici, zĂ­skat<br>        </td>      </tr>       </tbody>  </table>   <p><br>  There is excitement when Company X is attempting to gain control of Company  Y in a <b>takeover bid</b>, perhaps by increasing its holding or stake in  Company Y if it already owns shares in Y.</p>      <p>Company X makes or <b>launches a bid </b>against Company Y, the <b>takeover  target</b>.</p>      <p>If Company Y does not want to be taken over, the bid is <b>hostile</b>.</p>      <p>Other ways of saying that one company is <b>taking over </b>another are  to say that one company is <b>acquiring </b>another or <b>making an acquisition</b>.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Tokay, the worldÂs oldest wine appelation, is being <u>acquired</u> by  a Japanese distiller and a French insurance group.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Medeva, the fast growing pharmaceuticals group, rose 19p to 224p on the  news that it has <u>made</u> another <u>acquisition</u> in America. Medeva  is to buy Adams Laboratories, a Texas drugs manufacturer, for $77.2 million.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>TT Group, the packaging company, has launched its second hostile takeover  bid in little more than a year, with a &#163;6.8 million offer for Magnetic Materials  Group, the magnetic components manufacturer. TT won a long running bid battle  for Crystalate last August. The 37p share offer is equal to the price paid  by TT on Tuesday for a 9.4 per cent stake, bringing its total holding in the target company to 16.4 per cent. This compares with a market price of 41p before the announcement of the bid. MagneticÂs board rejected the bid as Âderisory, unsolicited, unwelcome, and totally inadequateÂ.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <b><br clear=all>   </b>    <h2><a name=_Toc452353960>1.8Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  Predators, raiders and white knights  <br>   Âraloci, ĂştoÄnĂ­ci a bĂ­lĂ­ rytĂ­Ĺi</a></h2>      <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>    <tbody>      <tr>        <td valign=top>predator<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>Âralok; predĂĄtor, Âelma<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>prey<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>obÄÂ<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>raider<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>ĂştoÄnĂ­k, nĂĄjezdnĂ­k<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>corporateÂ raider<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>obchodnĂ­ agresor<br>        </td>      </tr>       </tbody>  </table>   <p><br>  The takeover process is often described in terms of one animal hunting another:  a company may be referred to as a <b>predator</b>, and the target company  as the <b>prey</b>.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Predators are also referred to as <b>raiders </b>or <b>corporate raiders</b>.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>The company, and the sector, have deep problems to cope with and an uncertain  future ahead. In their present state, they are sitting ducks for European  or American predators looking for an entree to the British insurance sector.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>The group could fall prey to one of the industryÂs hungrier predators.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Lee Iacocca, ChryslerÂs boss, explored the possibility of a takeover of  General Motors, on behalf of a corporate raider in 1987, but dropped the idea after deciding Âit might be easier to buy GreeceÂ.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]<br>  <br>  </p>      <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>    <tbody>      <tr>        <td valign=top>fend off a bid<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>odrazit nabĂ­dku<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>ward off a bid<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>uchrĂĄnit se pĹed pĹevzetĂ­m<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>poison pill<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>otrĂĄvenĂĄ pilulka<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>white knight<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>bĂ­lĂ˝ rytĂ­Ĺ<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>greenmail<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>vydĂ­rĂĄnĂ­ hrozbou pĹevzetĂ­<br>        </td>      </tr>       </tbody>  </table>   <p><br>  A company wishing to resist, <b>ward off</b>, or <b>fend off </b>being taken  over has a number of options.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>It may devise plans that give existing shareholders special rights, or  it may make itself less attractive to bidders by selling off a valuable part  of the company, or holding on to an unattractive one. Actions like this are  <b>poison pills.</b></p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Or it may persuade a friendly partner, a <b>white knight</b>, to take a stake in the company, thus preventing a complete takeover by a hostile bidder.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Bidders may agree to withdraw their bid if paid enough money for the shares  they hold in the target company. This is <b>greenmail.</b></p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>The agreement will help Navigation Mixte <u>fend off</u> potential raiders.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>London Weekend TelevisionÂs attempt to attract a <u>white knight</u> to  help it <u>ward off</u> the unwanted bid from Granada has collapsed. US West,  the giant US telephone company and one of the largest cable operators in the UK, was interested in a stake of 29.9 per cent in the London ITV company Â the largest amount possible without triggering a full bid.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>UK merchant banks do not appear attractive at the moment. Indeed, TSBÂs  chairman, Sir Nicholas Goodison, perhaps should consider holding on to Hill  Samuel: it is a <u>poison pill</u> against predators who might want to swallow  the whole group.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>...greenmail transactions, in which the target of a takeover attempt buys  off the hostile bidder by repurchasing any shares that it has acquired. <u>Greenmail</u>  means that these shares are repurchased by the target at a price which makes  the bidder happy to agree to leave the target alone.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Â </p>    <b><br clear=all>   </b>    <h2><a name=_Toc452353961>1.9Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  Leveraged buy-outs and junk bonds<br>  Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  Akvizice na dluh a druhoĹadĂŠ dluhopisy</a></h2>      <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>    <tbody>      <tr>        <td valign=top>leveraged buy-out<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>akvizice na dluh<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>LBO<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>akvizice na dluh (<i>zkratka</i>)<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>junk bonds<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>druhoĹadĂŠ dluhopisy<br>        </td>      </tr>       </tbody>  </table>   <p><br>  In a <b>leveraged buy-out</b>, or <b>LBO</b>, a company is acquired by a  group of investors, often financed by heavy borrowing. The debt is then paid  out of the target companyÂs operating revenues or by selling its assets.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>The borrowing involved in LBOs is often high-risk debt called <b>junk bonds</b>.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>LBOs financed by junk were frequent in the 1980s and after an absence following the excesses of that period, they are now occurring again.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Â Â Â  LEXIKOLOGICKĂ POZNĂMKA <br>   Buy-out se tĂŠÂ pĂ­Âe buyout.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>ÂJust BusinessÂ explains many business issues usually shrouded in mysticism  and emotive language. For instance it made me feel at home with <u>junk bonds</u>  for the first time. These are simply fixed-interest securities with less than investment-grade level rating. They are in themselves a borrowing instrument,  which are neither good or bad, but have acquired a bad name because of their  use in certain criminal transactions.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>An example of a solid company that seems to have thrived on the strength  of junk-bond financing is FMC Corp., which makes armored vehicles and other  military equipment. In 1986, the company, its shareholders and employees bought out its stock in a <u>leveraged buy-out</u> financed by junk.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>          <br> ', 1, NULL),
(1961, 1, 'Bussiness English - tyden 4', '', 53, '', '          <h2><a name=_Toc452353962>1.10Â Â Â Â  Joint ventures and alliances Â SpoleÄnĂŠ  podniky a aliance</a></h2>      <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>    <tbody>      <tr>        <td valign=top>joint venture<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>spoleÄnĂ˝ podnik, joint venture<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>alliance<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>(<i>strategickĂŠ</i>) spojenectvĂ­, partnerstvĂ­; aliance<br>        </td>      </tr>       </tbody>  </table>   <p><br>  Two or more companies may decide to work together by setting up a <b>joint  venture</b> or <b>alliance</b> in which each holds a stake.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Lord Weinstock, the managing director of GEC, yesterday for the first time publicly ruled out a takeover bid for British Aerospace and said that he would prefer to see <u>joint ventures</u> between the two companies.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Over-the-counter products account for about an eighth of Wellcome sales,  but Mr Robb said this did not give the operation sufficient sale. Wellcome  would be looking for an <u>alliance</u> or joint venture to boost the business.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Â </p>      <h2><a name=_Toc452353963>1.11Â Â Â Â  Mergers Â FĂşze</a></h2>      <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>    <tbody>      <tr>        <td valign=top>merge<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>spojit se, splynout; fĂşzovat<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>merger<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>fĂşze, spojenĂ­<br>        </td>      </tr>       </tbody>  </table>   <p><br>  When two companies combine, usually voluntarily, they <b>merge </b>in a <b>merger</b>.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>...LordÂ  McGowan and Lord Melchett, who created ICI 66 years ago by <u>merging</u>  Nobel Industries and Brunner Mond in an agreement made on board the liner  Aquitania as it sailed from New York to London.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Bibby is getting tough with unwilling Spanish bid target Finanzauto. The  agribusiness group has abandoned efforts to sweet-talk the Caterpillar vehicles  distributor into a friendly <u>merger</u> and has launched a &#163;75 million bid instead.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <b><br clear=all>   </b>    <h2><a name=_Toc452353964>1.12Â Â Â Â  Restructuring Â Restrukturalizace</a></h2>      <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>    <tbody>      <tr>        <td valign=top>diversified<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>diverzifikovanĂ˝; rĹŻznorodĂ˝<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>core business<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>hlavnĂ­ pĹedmÄt Äinnosti, hlavnĂ­ oblast podnikĂĄnĂ­<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>profit centre<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>zdroj zisku, tvĹŻrce zisku<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>focus<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>ohnisko; zamÄĹit se, soustĹedit se<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>assetsÂ (<i>pl.</i>)<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>jmÄnĂ­; majetek (<i>zejmĂŠna dobĹe likvidnĂ­</i>)<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>non-core assets<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>majetek, kterĂ˝ se nevyuÂĂ­vĂĄ k hlavnĂ­mu pĹedmÄtu Äinnosti<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>sell off<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>odprodat<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>sell-off<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>odprodej<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>spin off<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>zbavit se, odprodat<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>spin-off<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>odprodej; <i>ale i </i>vedlejÂĂ­ produkt<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>dispose of<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>zbavit se (<i>Äeho</i>)<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>disposal<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>vyĹazenĂ­; odprodej<br>        </td>      </tr>       </tbody>  </table>   <p><br>  A group containing many types of business is <b>diversified</b>. A groupÂs  basic business activity, perhaps the one it originally started with, is its  <b>core business</b>.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Separate business activities may be viewed as <b>profit centres</b>, each  responsible for generating profit.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Businesses are often encouraged to concentrate or <b>focus</b> on their  core activities and to <b>sell off, spin off</b> or <b>dispose of</b> non-essential  <b>assets</b>. These assets are often referred to as <b>non-core assets</b>.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>A sale of assets in this way is referred to as a <b>sell-off, spin-off</b>,  or <b>disposal</b>. A <b>spin-off</b> can also refer to a business that has  been spun off.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Â Â Â  LEXIKOLOGICKĂ POZNĂMKA <br>   Sell-off a spin-off se tĂŠÂ pĂ­ÂĂ­ jako dvÄ slova nebo jako jedno slovo.<br>  <br>  </p>      <p>...3M, a <u>diversified</u> multinational that makes an astonishing 60,000  different products.</p>      <p>...firma 3M, nadnĂĄrodnĂ­ koncern s ÂirokĂ˝m zĂĄbÄrem a sortimentem ÄĂ­tajĂ­cĂ­m  neuvÄĹitelnĂ˝ch 60 tisĂ­c rĹŻznĂ˝ch vĂ˝robkĹŻ.</p>      <p>Since taking over at Nokia two years ago, Mr Ollila has set about completingÂ   the companyÂs transformation from a sprawling industrial conglomerate to a group <u>focused on</u> telecommunications and electronics. More than 60 per cent of group sales is accounted for by its telecommunications divisions.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>PlesseyÂs non-telecommunications activities split into 23 businesses, self-contained <u>profit centres</u>, each with a manager bearing full profit responsibility.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>The Adelaide Steamship group yesterday announced plans to sell many of  its assets and concentrate on the <u>core business</u> of retailing. The <u>sell-off</u> could include AdsteamÂs controlling stake in Markheath Securities and its substantial holdings in Australian wine, timber, food, processing and real estate industries.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>TSB has been shedding <u>non-core assets</u> for the last two years to  concentrate on its main banking and insurance business.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>After a promise to shareholders that it would return to its core photographic  and health businesses, Eastman Kodak is to <u>spin off</u> its $4 billion  chemical subsidiary, the tenth largest in the United States, by the end of  the year.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Mr Simons said he had no plans to <u>sell off</u> large numbers of Gateway  stores. ÂIf there are to be <u>disposals</u>,Â he said, Âthey will be in handfuls rather than large numbers.Â</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Â </p>      <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>    <tbody>      <tr>        <td valign=top>divest<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>zbavit<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>divestment<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>deinvestice; odprodej<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>pull out of<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>odejĂ­t z (<i>odvÄtvĂ­</i>)<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>restructuringÂ <br>        </td>        <td valign=top>restrukturalizace<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>rationalization<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>racionalizace<br>        </td>      </tr>       </tbody>  </table>   <p><br>  If a company sells a business, it <b>divests</b> itself of that business.  <b>Divestment</b> refers both to the action of selling a business and to the whole process of selling businesses in this way.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>If you <b>pull out of</b> a business activity, you abandon it, perhaps  as a part of a programme of <b>restructuring</b> or <b>rationalization</b>:  reorganizing a business with the aim of making it more efficient and profitable.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Â Â Â  LEXIKOLOGICKĂ POZNĂMKA <br>   Rationalization se v britskĂŠ angliÄtinÄ tĂŠÂ pĂ­Âe rationalisation.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Turnover fell by 9 per cent to &#163;1.97 billion mainly because of <u>divestments</u>.  The group said a further 17 non-core businesses had been sold during the year and the disposal programme was now complete.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>AdiaÂs <u>divestment</u> programme is behind schedule.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>The next <u>restructuring</u> move is likely to be the sale of NokiaÂs  80 per cent holding in Nokia Tyres, a move signalled by Mr Ollila last month.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <p>Mr Araskog ruthlessly hacked back the sprawling ITT empire, not only selling  off most of the businesses acquired by Mr Geneen, but even <u>pulling out</u>  of the telecommunications business on which the group had been built. <u>Rationalisation</u>,  however, did not succeed in transforming ITTÂs lacklustre financial performance.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>    <b><br clear=all>   </b>    <h2><a name=_Toc452353965>1.13Â Â Â Â  Management buy-outs Â Odkup managementem</a></h2>      <table cellpadding=2 cellspacing=2 border=1 width=60%>    <tbody>      <tr>        <td valign=top>management buy-out<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>odkup managementem<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>MBO<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>odkup managementem<br>        </td>      </tr>      <tr>        <td valign=top>venture capital<br>        </td>        <td valign=top>spekulaÄnĂ­ kapitĂĄl<br>        </td>      </tr>       </tbody>  </table>   <p><br>  When a group is restructured, the managers of a business that is to be sold  may want to buy it themselves in a <b>management buy-out</b> or <b>MBO</b>,  usually in combination with an organization providing finance in the form  of <b>venture capital</b>.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Â Â Â  LEXIKOLOGICKĂ POZNĂMKA <br>   Buy-out se tĂŠÂ pĂ­Âe buyout.</p>      <p><br>   RJB Mining was the subject of a <u>management buy-out</u> last year that  left Mr Budge and other directors in control of 25% of the equity, with the  reminder in the hands of <u>venture capital</u> groups led by Schroders.</p>      <p>[$1, textarea 60,4]</p>      <br> ', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `exercises` (`ex_id`, `ex_type`, `ex_name`, `ex_description`, `ex_creator`, `ex_task`, `ex_story`, `ex_difficulty`, `ex_options`) VALUES
(1962, 1, 'Bussiness English - test', '', 53, '', '        <h3> </h3>   <h3><b>1Â Â Â Â Â Â  Business partners. </b></h3>      <p><b>Â </b></p>      <p>The words in the box frequently occur after `business\'.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <table border=1 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0>     <tbody>       <tr>      <td width=628 valign=top>                     <p>Â </p>                        <p>   leaders </p>                        <p>Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  school   interests</p>                        <p>venturesÂ Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  <b>business</b></p>                        <p>Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  community   confidenceÂ Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â    Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â empire</p>                        <p>Â </p>      </td>     </tr>            </tbody>   </table>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Find combinations that mean:</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p><b>1</b> an individual\'s business activities, especially those of politicians.  [$1, select:/leaders/school/interestes/ventures/community/confidence/empire] </p>      <p><b>2</b> a very large business organization. [$1, select:/leaders/school/interestes/ventures/community/confidence/empire]  </p>      <p><b>3</b> specific money-making projects, perhaps involving an element of risk. [$1, select:/leaders/school/interestes/ventures/community/confidence/empire]  </p>      <p><b>4 </b>an institution that awards qualifications in business studies.  [$1, select:/leaders/school/interestes/ventures/community/confidence/empire] </p>      <p><b>5</b> people involved in business in general. [$1, select:/leaders/school/interestes/ventures/community/confidence/empire]  </p>      <p><b>6</b> heads of important companies who speak for 5 as a whole. [$1, select:/leaders/school/interestes/ventures/community/confidence/empire]  </p>      <p><b>7</b> the morale of all those referred to in 5 and 6. [$1, select:/leaders/school/interestes/ventures/community/confidence/empire]  </p>      <p>Â </p>      <h3><b>2Â Â Â Â Â Â  Corporate partners. </b></h3>      <p>Â </p>      <p>The words in the box frequently occur after `corporateÂ´:</p>      <p>Â </p>      <table border=1 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0>     <tbody>       <tr>      <td width=611 valign=top>                     <p>Â </p>                        <p>   image </p>                        <p>Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â    strategy collapses</p>                        <p>Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  governanceÂ Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  <b>corporate</b>  culture </p>                        <p>Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â    logo ladder</p>                        <p align=center>performance</p>                        <p>Â </p>      </td>     </tr>            </tbody>   </table>      <p>   <table cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0>     <tbody>       <tr>      <td width=269 height=226 align=left valign=top>                     <table cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0 width=100%>       <tbody>             <tr>        <td>                                                                              <p><img width=248 height=214  src=img/./1Business%20partners_soubory/image002.jpg>               </p>                       </td>       </tr>                              </tbody>                     </table>      Â </td>     </tr>            </tbody>   </table>   </p>      <p><b>This is a rough place Â instead of a corporate</b></p>      <p><b>ladder we have a corporate food chain.</b></p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Find combinations that refer to:</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p><b>1</b> the perception that people have of a corporation. [$1, select:/image/strategy/collapses/governance/culture/logo/ladder/performance]</p>      <p><b>2</b> what corporate employees climb during their career. [$1, select:/image/strategy/collapses/governance/culture/logo/ladder/performance]  </p>      <p><b>3</b> company results over a period of time. [$1, select:/image/strategy/collapses/governance/culture/logo/ladder/performance]</p>      <p><b>4</b> the values that people have and the ways that things are done  in an organization. [$1, select:/image/strategy/collapses/governance/culture/logo/ladder/performance]  </p>      <p><b>5</b> the way a company is managed at the highest level. [$1, select:/image/strategy/collapses/governance/culture/logo/ladder/performance]</p>      <p><b>6</b> plans that companies have maintaining and developing their position  in the future. [$1, select:/image/strategy/collapses/governance/culture/logo/ladder/performance]  </p>      <p><b>7</b> company failures. [$1, select:/image/strategy/collapses/governance/culture/logo/ladder/performance]</p>      <p><b>8</b> a symbol, usually showing the company\'s name. [$1, select:/image/strategy/collapses/governance/culture/logo/ladder/performance]</p>      <p><br>   Now use these expressions to complete the extracts.</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p><b>a</b> Corporate [$1,text 12] and associated fiascos have exposed the failure  of the auditing industry as a whole, not just the failure of individual audits.</p>      <p><b>b</b> No single subject has so dominated the attention of managers,  consultants and management theorists as the subject of corporateÂ [$1,text 12].</p>      <p><b>c</b> While Wal-Mart grew, Walton was developing the practices that  were to give the firm one of AmericaÂ´s most-admired corporateÂ Â [$1,text 12]s.</p>      <p><b>d</b> Huet, remaining one step ahead on the corporateÂ [$1,text 12] , has been  transferred to NewYork as head of SociĂŠtĂŠ GĂŠnĂŠrale in America.</p>      <p><b>e</b> Good corporateÂ [$1,text 12] should be about stopping messes from happening,  not just cleaning up afterwards.</p>      <p><b>f</b> Few investors are prepared to look beyond conventional measures  for assessing</p>      <p>corporateÂ [$1,text 12] .</p>      <p><b>g </b>All phone books are to have identical blue covers with the British  TelecomÂ [$1,text 12]. It is part of the corporate [$1,text 12].</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Â </p>      <h3><b>3Â Â Â Â Â Â  Key sector exercise. </b></h3>      <p>Â </p>      <p>The same word is missing from all these extracts. What is it?</p>      <p align=center>Â </p>      <table border=1 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0>     <tbody>       <tr>      <td width=279 valign=top align=right>       The report shows about  150,000 jobs         <br>  were created last month, but some economists expect the        <br>  Â         <br>        <br>  The highest number of closures were in the        <br>  Â         <br>        <br>  Mr Renaud said that in the equipment<br>          Â         <br>  .. Scottish food and drink, Scotland\'s biggest        <br>  Â         <br>  But with a bit of luck, the worst in   the        <br>  Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â          <br>  In the        <br>  Â         <br>  Â         <br>  Â         <br>  Â         <br>  It illustrates the need for a strong        <br>  Â     </td>      <td width=76 valign=top>       <br>  <br>       <br>       <br>       <br>       <br>       <br>       <br>       <br> [$1,text 15]        <br>  Â <br>     </td>      <td width=257 valign=top>       <br>  sector to show a net loss of jobs.        <br>  Â         <br>        <br>        <br>  sector, followed by construction, finance and retail.        <br>  Â         <br>  sector, Philips would be hardest hit.        <br>  Â         <br>  sector, employing 70,000 people.        <br>  Â         <br>  sector could be over.        <br>  Â         <br>  sector there\'s evidence that small        <br>  companies may account for a larger        <br>  share of the nationÂ´s manufactured         <br>  exports than once thought.        <br>  Â         <br>  sector if the UKis to   maintain growth in living standards.    </td>     </tr>            </tbody>   </table>    Â     <p>Â </p>          <h3>4Â Â Â Â Â Â  Unpopular privatizations. </h3>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Read this article from <i>The Economist </i>and complete it using the words listed.</p>      <p>   <table cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0>     <tbody>       <tr>      <td width=261 height=167 align=left valign=top  bgcolor=white>                     <table cellpadding=0 cellspacing=0 width=100%>       <tbody>             <tr>        <td>                                                                              <p><img width=248 height=155  src=img/./1Business%20partners_soubory/image004.jpg>               </p>                       </td>       </tr>                              </tbody>                     </table>      Â </td>     </tr>            </tbody>   </table>     (a occurs five times, b twice, and c and d once each.)</p>      <p>Â </p>      <p><b>a</b> privatisation</p>      <p><b>b</b> privatised</p>      <p><b>c </b>nationalised</p>      <p><b>d</b> renationalised</p>      <p><b>Â </b></p>      <p><b>Â </b></p>      <p><b>Â </b></p>      <p><b>Â </b></p>      <table border=1 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0>     <tbody>       <tr>      <td width=611 valign=top>                     <h1>DISGUSTED</h1>                        <p><b><i>Most Britons see privatisation as a rip-off.   The evidence        </i>says <i>otherwise.</i></b></p>      </td>     </tr>     <tr>      <td width=611 valign=top>                     <p>Â </p>                        <p>To say that [$1,text 12]Â  in Britain is unpopular   in Britain is an understatement.  Every week brings fresh outrage at tales of   bosses ofÂ [$1,text 12] firms picking  up huge pay rises while over-charging   customers, sacking employees and cutting the pay of those who remain. The   ordinary man, who has never likedÂ [$1,text 12],  wants the water and electricity   industries, and maybe more, to beÂ [$1,text 12].</p>                                        <p>So what can be said in defence ofÂ [$1,text 12]?   Actually a great deal.  Back in 1979, Britain\'s public-sector firms were in   the doldrums, lacking  both entrepreneurial vigour and a concern for customer   service. Many were  losing large sums of money. According to the Treasury, theÂ [$1,text 12] industries  were then costing each tax-payer the   equivalent of Ĺ300 in today\'s money.</p>                                <p>SinceÂ [$1,text 12] - and the changes in management,   financial controls  and regulation associated with it - these firms have been   transformed. MostÂ [$1,text 12] companies were more profitable in real terms in   1994 than in the year before they were sold. Most had higher sales, and a   higher share price than in their first year afterÂ [$1,text 12]. They now pay   Ĺ2.5 billion a year in taxes (equivalent to Ĺ100 for each taxpayer).Â </p>                                        <p>Firms such as British Airways and British   Telecom have been turned  from corporate dogs into some of the world\'s most   admiredcompanies.Â </p>      </td>     </tr>            </tbody>   </table>      <p><b>Â </b></p>      <p><b>5Â Â Â Â Â Â  Fingers in many pies. </b></p>      <p>Â </p>      <p>Which key word in this section is missing, in its plural form, from all  these extracts?</p>      <p>Â </p>      <table border=1 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0 align=center>     <tbody>       <tr>      <td width=298>                     <p>ÂINSERT INTO `exercises` (`ex_id`, `ex_type`, `ex_name`, `ex_description`, `ex_creator`, `ex_task`, `ex_story`, `ex_difficulty`, `ex_options`) VALUES
