(854, 16, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Czech sentence structure', 'I think the number one barrier to understanding Czech sentence structure is its fundamentally different philosophy, called (or perhaps related to something called - dominik??) topic-focus perspective. In plain English, this means that instead of using sentence structure to show parts of speech (i.e. what\'s a noun, what\'s a verb, etc.), Czech uses sentence structure to show what information we expect the listener to already know, and what we are stressing is new/exciting/important.\r\n\r\nAt its most simple, the idea is: new information goes at the end (of the sentence or of a group of words that belong together). There are lots of exceptions, but you have to start with the rule :-). The only exception I\'ll mention is - sometimes na potvoru the new/stressed information goes naopak at the beginning.\r\n\r\nHere are a couple of examples to get you started:\r\nLenka podala solniÄku Honzovi - Lenka passed the salt to Honza (English-like sentence structure - in my experience, this usually means that no special emphasis is being placed on anything).\r\nLenka podala Honzovi sĹŻl - Lenka passed the salt (and not something else) to Honza.\r\nLenka tu solniÄku Honzovi jenom podala. - Lenka only <i>passed</i> the saltshaker to Honza (what someone might say if speaking to someone who claimed that Lenka had thrown it).\r\nHonzovi tu solniÄku podala. - This is one of those nasty cases where the new information goes at the beginning - perhaps because Honzovi-at-the-end is already occupied by the \"English-like\" structure. Meaning: Lenka gave the saltshaker to Honza, and not somebody else (and, as always, the speaker is assuming this is new information for the listener).\r\nTu solniÄku Honzovi podala Lenka. - Lenka, and not someone else, passed Honza the saltshaker. (I may have the word order wrong on this one; hopefully Dominik will help me out on this one.)\r\n\r\nThe real question here is, why do I feel forced to include \"tu\" before \"solniÄku\" in some of the sentences? I\'d be writing another half hour on that, and I\'m already cheating my employer out of my working hours :(. But in short - adding the demonstrative pronoun \"to\" before a noun is quite common in everyday speech when a person is excited/riled up - and these various new-information structures tend to involve a cranky or excited speaker.\r\n\r\nOf course, real life sentences tend to be even more complicated than this case, but it\'s a good start. Well, one more thing:\r\n\r\nthe same principle works inside of groups of highly related words, most notably nouns and their adjectives:\r\nĂşspÄÂnĂŠ projekty - successful projects (no special emphasis)\r\nprojekty ĂşspÄÂnĂŠ - the successful projects (as opposed to the other ones)\r\n\r\nNote that this is one of the ways in which Czech makes up for its \"lack\" of a/an/the.\r\n\r\nThere is a related phenomenon that I feel is the same one, but Dominik says it\'s not, and that\'s the nearly-universal placement of the most specific word/phrase within a proper noun at the end, even if it\'s an adjective (which it often is):\r\nHotel Forum, Restaurace U MÄdÄnĂŠ panve, DivadelnĂ­ Ăşstav Praha... this often gets carried over into Czenglish, e.g. \"Polymer Institute Brno\".\r\n\r\nHope this has given you a good start in understanding sentence ', '2003-02-21 15:50:44', 852, 1),
