(875, 17, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'ÄeskĂĄ hudba', 'DruhĂ˝ pokus o napsanĂ­ pĹĂ­spÄvky do sekce \"ÄeskĂĄ konverzace\" a nikdy jinak...\r\n\r\n(\"a nikdy jinak\" is a bad joke... it\'s a reference to the often-repeated phrase in Communist times \"Se SovÄtskĂ˝m svazem na vÄÄnĂŠ Äasy a nikdy jinak.\")\r\n\r\nOsobnÄ se velice zdrĂĄhĂĄm kupovat novou hudbu, a tak mĂĄm vkus ponÄkud zastaralĂ˝ - Äeskou alternativnĂ­ hudbu z devadesatĂ˝ch let, nespĂ­Â ze zaÄĂĄtku devadesatĂ˝ch. PĹedevÂĂ­m UÂ jsme doma, Znouzectnost, Zuby Nehty, Sto zvĂ­Ĺat, a DG 307. Z toho mĂĄla novÄjÂĂ­ch vÄcĂ­, co znĂĄm, moc se mi lĂ­bily alba od Äechomor a mĂĄlo znĂĄmĂ˝ch kapel NeoÄekanĂ˝ DychĂĄnek a Chorchestr.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-02-27 18:14:45', 0, 0),
(876, 15, 'Pavel', 'pavel.cerny@strath.ac.uk', 'Re: XP-Languages', 'I have English version of XP at home and I can change the keybord layout like in 98 etc. You have to install support for Czech language (no neeed for menus and help in Czech). Then you can add Czech keybord. The language selector bar (in XP the equivalent to keybord selector) can be either down where the clock is or somewher else on the screen. Hope this will work... Cheers', '2003-02-28 16:41:48', 818, 0),
(877, 15, 'Pavel', 'pavel.cerny@strath.ac.uk', 'Re: XP-Languages', 'I have English version of XP at home and I can change the keybord layout like in 98 etc. You have to install support for Czech language (no neeed for menus and help in Czech). Then you can add Czech keybord. The language selector bar (in XP the equivalent to keybord selector) can be either down where the clock is or somewher else on the screen. Hope this will work... Cheers', '2003-02-28 16:43:37', 818, 0),
(878, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@hotmail.com', 'Re: XP-Languages', 'Thanks, I actually figured it out a while back but forget to post it.  Thanks for the concern and though,\r\nScott', '2003-03-01 01:14:36', 877, 0),
(879, 13, 'Mike Allen', 'youthchaos@hotmail.com', 'Re: Another Translation (English to Czech)', 'Might I just add to this thread that it is extremely satisfying to speak to animals in Czech.  I truly feel they understand it better than English.', '2003-03-04 06:01:56', 850, 0),
(880, 15, 'Dale', 'dalem@marshall-tufflex.com', 'Re: When?', 'Good Question!\r\n\r\nI have been trying to register for over a year now, but I never even get a courtesy reply.\r\n\r\nFor the last 4 years I have been teaching myself Czech from various text books. The best one I believe is CZECH in THREE MONTHS. Obviously it takes a lot longer than 3 months but there are plenty of exercises to enforce the learning.\r\n\r\nGood luck\r\nDale', '2003-03-08 15:36:35', 758, 1),
(881, 13, 'Caitlin', 'Ovation226@aol.com', 'How do you say...', 'Does anyone know how to say passioniate in Czech and if you could spell it with all the accents and stuff cause i am getting a tattoo that says passioniate love and the passionate part will be in czech.', '2003-03-09 08:07:26', 0, 0),
(882, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: How do you say...', 'VĂĄÂnivĂĄ.\r\n\r\n(VĂĄÂnivĂĄ lĂĄska.)\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-03-10 16:32:57', 881, 0),
(883, 13, 'm.m.allen', '', 'za svetla i za tmy', 'Is there a generalised instance where \'za\' takes the genitiv, or is it idomatic only to these two terms?\r\nthanks,\r\nmike', '2003-03-17 00:11:30', 0, 0),
(884, 15, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilvoejesus.net', 'Re: When?', 'Hi, I asked Dominik the same question a few weeks ago.  He informed me that the program is not finished yet and therefore no one can use it yet.  However, if you have signed up then you are on the list, and as soon as it is up and running, you will be contacted.  I feel your anticipation!\r\nScott', '2003-03-17 09:34:51', 880, 0),
(885, 13, 'Carol Damioli', 'cdamioli@praguepost.com', 'Important Czech words', 'Does anyone know where I can find a list of the 1,000 most commonly used Czech words? It would help me focus my studying. Thanks.', '2003-03-17 17:10:26', 0, 1),
(886, 13, 'Dominik LukeÂ', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'No, \'za\' is used with the genitive always when it means \'during a period of time\' after a name for the period. E.g. \'za vlĂĄdy Klause\' (during Klaus\' reign), \'za komunistĹŻ\' (during the communist times) but NOT e.g. \'za stĹedovÄku\' (during the Middle Ages).', '2003-03-17 19:15:11', 883, 0),
(887, 13, 'Dominik LukeÂ', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'I don\'t know of any such list on its own. (There are some but they mostly describe literary language.) Try <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/dictionary>http://www.bohemica.com/dictionary</a> for a list of words used on this web site.', '2003-03-17 19:21:46', 885, 0),
(888, 17, 'Dominik LukeÂ', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: ÄeskĂĄ hudba', 'ZajĂ­mavĂŠ ukĂĄzky ÄeskĂŠho roku. VĹŻbec neznĂĄm DG307 a Chorchestr. Odkud jsou a jakĂ˝ typ hudby hrajĂ­?\r\n\r\nDominik', '2003-03-17 19:28:53', 875, 0),
(889, 13, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'Just out of curiousity, in which ways are \'od\' and \'na\' used, and in which cases are they.\r\n', '2003-03-18 01:55:19', 886, 0),
(890, 13, 'Scott', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'Just out of curiousity, in which ways are \'od\' and \'na\' used, and in which cases are they.\r\n', '2003-03-18 01:55:32', 886, 0),
(891, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'Sorry for the repetition, my computer froze.\r\n..... I understand their meanings, but get confused sometimes when using them.  In which other ways can \'od\' be used than saying \'from\' and \'na\' used than saying \'on\'.\r\nThanks, Scott.', '2003-03-18 02:04:27', 889, 0),
(892, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'Hi there,\r\n\r\nI just wasted a LOT of work time compiling the below, so forgive me for not explaining it in too much detail. Just a couple of notes:\r\n\r\nMethodology: I first searched for \"od\" in the top 30 pages in Google\'s listing overall for the word \"od\" within the .cz domain. I then switched to just the domain pes.eunet.cz for 10-15 more pages and continued searching for \"od\". I probably should have restricted the search to pes.eunet.cz from the start, as a whole-web search is always highly skewed towards the language of business, marketing, and computing. \r\n\r\nI haven\'t done \"za\" yet.\r\n\r\nEach slash is one instance of od, except where it was used many times in exactly the same way on one page, in which case I counted it only once for the group of occurrences, or sometimes twice if I was feeling nice. The subcases (dots) are not counted separately from the rest, that is, if you see twenty instances and ten dots for subcases, that\'s still twenty instances, not thirty.\r\n\r\nThis search surely didn\'t dig up all uses of \"od\", and in some ways a dictionary search would be much better, but I chose this method because it will draw your attention to what meanings are most important. Well, and because it was fun.\r\n\r\nAs silly as this might sound: NEVER believe that any Czech word \"means\" any English word (\"does \'od\' always mean \'from\'\"?). You\'ll only be slowing yourself down in learning. View it as a \"correspondence\" instead. I say this not as a word game, but as a way to take off your English-language glasses. \r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n\"od koho\" [source] ///////////////////\r\n	subcase od [author] . \r\nod Äeho [source] ///////////////////\r\n	subcase (untracked) @@$#$ DOG KENNELS! - highly idomatic - they just HAVE to be named \"od [core of name]\", like pubs start with \"u\"\r\n\"od [point in a temporal, spatial, financial etc. scale]\" ///////////////////////////////////\r\n	subcase \"od a do z\" (idiom) ..\r\n	subcase \"od [letter of the alphabet] ...\r\n	subcase \"od X korun\" .\r\n	subcase \"od roku X\" ...\r\n	subcase \"od dd/mm/yyyy\" ...\r\n	subcase \"od X hodin\" ........\r\n	subcase \"od [event]\"..\r\n	subcase \"od [time-noun, e.g. ode dneÂka]\" .....\r\n	subcase \"od [course of action] do [course of action]\" .\r\n	subcase \"od [description at start of process] do [description at end of process] (e.g. \"od fanouÂkĹŻ k barbarĹŻm\") ..\r\n	subcase \"od [thing] aÂ po [fundamentally different comparable thing]\" (e.g. od novorozeĹat aÂ po bĂĄbu DymĂĄkovou) .\r\n	subcase \"od [age] do [age]\" .\r\nNarozdĂ­l od (or: na rozdĂ­l od) Äeho/koho (unlike sth./sb.) ////\r\nod poÄĂĄtku Äeho (from the start of sth.) /\r\nbĂ˝t odstĂ­nÄn od Äeho (??? pĹĂ­p. Ĺekne DL) /\r\nna [direction] od [place] /\r\n[distance] od [place] //\r\npoÄĂ­tat od [number] (to start counting from x)/\r\nosvobozenĂ­ od Äeho (liberation from sth, waiving of sth) /\r\nmĂ­t co od [source of a bad smell, appearance, etc.] // (e.g. boty mÄl od blĂĄta)\r\nod [place of origin] ///\r\nÄas od Äasu (from time to time) /\r\nod podlahy (rip-roaring, in-a-rip-roaring-way) /\r\n[verb of motion] od [place] // (e.g. odsouvat od sebe, odlepit se od povrchu zemÄ)\r\nlĂ­Âit se od Äeho (to differ from sth) / \r\nodliÂnĂ˝ od Äeho (different than/from sth) /\r\nod [owner or \"owner\"] / (e.g. muzeum od Madame Tussaud, holka od Petra]\r\nrvat od Ăşst (to force (words, food, etc. out of someone\'s mouth) /', '2003-03-18 16:55:58', 891, 1),
(893, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'OK, here\'s a somewhat organized collection of instances of \"na\". All were collected from about 7 pages of pes.eunet.cz, which means that their value as a scientific sample is diddleysquat.\r\n\r\n\"Na\" is always 4th case (literal or figurative motion) or 6th case (location).\r\n\r\nBy \"nominalized verbs\" below, I mean verbs turned into nouns, e.g. \"arrest\" (from \"to arrest\") \"skiing\" (from \"to ski\"). I may be using this word too broadly... In any case, I always grouped, um, really similar words together to save space (hledÄt, pohledÄt, pohled, etc.)\r\n\r\n[stated or implied \"bĂ˝t\" or similar verb or nominalized verb] na [thing or location that is, or can be compared to, a surface or one that is being used more as a concept than a particular space]: ////////////////////////////////////////////////////////// (all 6th case)\r\n		CONCRETE PLACES\r\n	studovat, bĂ˝t,  pracovat, etc. na [school] ......\r\n	na zĂĄchodÄ (in the bathroom) .\r\n	na strĂĄnce (on a/the page) ..\r\n	na adrese ...\r\n	na [ship] .\r\n	na [planet], na povrchu (the surface of) [planet]  ...\r\n	na nĂĄmÄstĂ­ (on the square).\r\n	[presentation, concert etc.] na [presentation space] .\r\n	na dovolenĂŠ (on vacation) .\r\n	na svÄtÄ (in the/this world) ..\r\n	na MoravÄ . (and na Slovesnku, but v ÄechĂĄch)\r\n	na posteli, na luÂku (in bed) ...\r\n	na zemi (on the ground) .\r\n	na jizdĂĄrnÄ (at the riding hall) .\r\n	na [body part or whole body] ..\r\n	na chodbÄ (in the hall) .\r\n	na scĂŠnÄ (on the stage) ..\r\n	na ulici X (on X street) .\r\n	na pracoviÂti (at a/the workplace) .\r\n		ABSTRACT \"PLACES\"...\r\n	bĂ˝t na tom [dobĹe/ÂpatnÄ etc.] (to be doing well/poorly etc.) .\r\n	zvat/pozvat koho na [event] (to invite sb to [event]) ...\r\n	na [meeting] (e.g. na poradÄ) .\r\n	na Internetu ..\r\n	na poÄĂĄtku/zaÄĂĄtku/sklonku/konci (at the beginning/end) ....\r\n	na [station, e.g. ÄT1, ÄRo] ...\r\n	Ĺikat na rovinu (to say straightforwardly) .\r\n	bĂ˝t na dennĂ­m poĹĂĄdku (to happen all the time) .\r\n	ĂşÄast na Äem (participation in sth) .\r\n	na podzim, na jaĹe (in fall, in spring) .... (CAREFUL! v zimÄ, v lĂŠtÄ)\r\n	\r\n[stated or implied verb or nominalized verb of motion] na [location that is a surface or one that is meaningful more as a concept than a particular space] ////////////////// (all 4th case)\r\n	school .\r\n	poznĂĄmka na okraj (side note) .\r\n	na [planet] ....\r\n	na zem (to the ground) ..\r\n	na jizdĂĄrnu (to the riding hall) .\r\n	na prochĂĄzku (for a walk) .\r\n	na kopec (onto the hill) .\r\n	na drĂĄhu (on the track (...of [career]), onto a track, onto the track) .\r\n		ABSTRACT DESTINATIONS\r\n	dĂĄn na pospas Äeho (to be at the mercy of sth) .\r\n	odkĂĄzat koho na co (to point sb towards sth, also to leave sb with no choice but sth) .\r\n	co pĹipadnout na koho (for sb to end up getting sth) .\r\n	odsunout na vedlejÂĂ­ kolej (to siderail) .\r\n\r\n[the directing of literal or abstract \"vision\"] /////////////////\r\n	pohled na co (a view/look at sth) ..\r\n	myslet na (to think of) .\r\n	zamÄĹit se na co (to focus one\'s attentions on sth) ...\r\n	vzpomĂ­nat se na koho/co (to recall, to reminisce about sb/sth) ......\r\n	zapomenout na koho/co (to forget sb/sth) .\r\n	mĂ­t co na svÄdomĂ­ (to have sth on one\'s conscience) .\r\n	divat se na (to look at) ..\r\n	ukazovat na co (to point to, point out sth) .\r\n\r\n[purpose/goal/desire] ///////////////\r\n	mĂ­t Âanci na [accomplishment, 4th case] (to have a chance at sth) .\r\n	tĂĄhnout na [place, 6th case] (to head to attack [place]) .\r\n	tÄÂit se na co (to look forward to sth) - ...\r\n	moci na koho/co (to be able to have a go at sb/sth) .\r\n	[thing] na [purpose] (e.g. znalostnĂ­ soutÄÂ na rozehĹĂĄtĂ­ - warmup trivia contest, grant na hru - a grant for a play) ...\r\n	pĹĂ­stup na (access to) .\r\n	[source] na [product] (e.g. lom na zelenou bĹidlici) .\r\n	trvat na Äem (to insist on sth) .\r\n	rezervovanĂŠ na (reserved for) .\r\n	bĂ˝t dost [quality] na [purpose] (to be [quality] enough for [purpose]) .\r\n	dÄlat [activity] na zkouÂku (to do something as a test run) .\r\n\r\n[manipulation and things resembling manipulation] ////////////\r\n	[thing] na [thing to be manipulated] (e.g. mikrovlnka na mokrĂŠ knihy, testy na jezdce, kladivo na ÄarodÄjnice) ..\r\n	pracovat na Äem (to work on sth) ..\r\n	simulovat co na Äem (to simulate sth on sth) .\r\n	ovlivnit, mĂ­t vliv na co (to affect/influence sth) .\r\n	vyzrĂĄt na (to get the better of) .\r\n	bĂ˝t pĹipraven na co (to be prepared for sth) .\r\n	Äekat na (to wait for) ...\r\n	mĂ­t co na starosti (to have sth as one\'s responsibility) .\r\n\r\n[result/outcome] //\r\n	bĂ˝t na mrtvici z Äeho (to be about ready to have a stroke \'cuz of sth) .\r\n	na povaÂenou (enough to make you shake your head, literally \"to make you consider\") .\r\n\r\n[reply] //////\r\n	ĹĂ­kat na [statement or situation] [statement] (to say [statement] in reply to [statement or situation]) .\r\n	odpovÄdÄt na (to reply to) ....\r\n	reakce na co (reaction to sth) .\r\n\r\nna druhou stranu (on the other hand) /\r\nbĂ˝t zaloÂen na Äem (to be based on sth) //\r\nna [round number] (almost [round number]) /\r\nna rozdĂ­l od (unlike) / \r\nna tĂŠma Äeho (on the topic of sth) /', '2003-03-18 19:18:43', 891, 1),
(894, 13, 'Dominik LukeÂ', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: za svetla i za tmy', 'Great job Erik. Google is a great way of getting the info you need. Just to add another tool to everybody\'s linguistic armory here\'s a link to the public access point of the Czech National Corpus <a href=http://ucnk.ff.cuni.cz/verejny.html>http://ucnk.ff.cuni.cz/verejny.html</a>. There you can get a more manageable overview from a balanced corpus (samples from different genres and registers). It only shows 20 examples with a brief context but if you choose \'nĂĄhodnÄ\' (random) as the search parameter, you can get different examples with different searches. If you are researcher you can sign up for full access which is not as sluggish as the public access (which is also occasionally inaccessible).', '2003-03-18 19:29:31', 893, 0),
(895, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'The only frequency list I\'ve ever found on the Internet lists only three sets of thirty-most-common words (from three different years), and is located at http://www.czech-language.cz/words/slova-nej.html. This would be a good place to start. Here is a copy of the most recent of the three lists (from the 1983 FrekvenÄnĂ­ slovnĂ­k vÄcnĂŠho stylu, that is, the Frequency Dictionary of Practical Style):\r\n\r\na  \r\nv,ve  \r\nbĂ˝ti  \r\nna  \r\nten  \r\nkterĂ˝  \r\nÂe  \r\nze  \r\nten  \r\nse  \r\no  \r\nk,ke,ku  \r\ni  \r\nmĂ­ti  \r\npro  \r\ndo  \r\non  \r\nnebo  \r\nmoci  \r\nsvĹŻj  \r\njako  \r\nza  \r\npĹi  \r\nvÂechen  \r\nprĂĄce  \r\nale  \r\nnĂĄÂ  \r\naby  \r\nu  \r\n\r\nIn a modern list, \"prĂĄce\" would, I suspect, drop off the list and another word would become number 10 - that is, I suspect its high frequency is an artifact of the Communist era, when it was sort of a buzzword.\r\n\r\nFor information on viewing these words (and any others) in context, see DominĂ­k\'s post on \"za svÄtla i za tmy\".\r\n\r\nIf you don\'t mind a highly skewed list and you don\'t mind getting an Excel file, contact me by e-mail - my curiosity was piqued by your question, and I\'m gradually putting together a 1000-word list based on a hand-built, web-pages-based corpus - which by its nature is not very representative, but which costs only my labor (prĂĄce) :-).', '2003-03-19 14:57:08', 885, 1),
(896, 17, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: ÄeskĂĄ hudba', 'DĂŠGĂŠÄka byli odnoÂem Plastic People, hrĂĄli tam pĹedevÂĂ­m Milan Hlavsa a Pavel Zeman. Existovali na dvÄ pomÄrnÄ krĂĄtkĂĄ obdobĂ­ - v sedmdesĂĄtĂ˝ch a v ranĂ˝ch devadesĂĄtĂ˝ch. SlyÂel jsem jen ty \"novÄjÂĂ­\", kterĂŠ mÄli texty spĂ­Âe bĂĄsnickĂŠho rĂĄzu od Zemana. HudebnĂ­ styl mÄli mĂ­rnÄ psychedelickĂ˝ a nÄkdy hodnÄ experimentĂĄlnĂ­, napĹ. poslednĂ­ pĂ­sniÄku z alba UmÄle ochuceno schvĂĄlnÄ nechali ukonÄenou jakoby v pĹŻlce. Texty Äasto spĂ­Â odĹĂ­kĂĄvali neÂ zpĂ­vali.\r\n\r\nChorchestĹi jsou funky kapela, hrĂĄval tam jeden mĹŻj kamarĂĄd a dodnes hrajĂ­ vzdĂĄlenĂ­ znĂĄmĂ­ (?). Spousta dechovĂ˝ch nĂĄstrojĹŻ, sem tam revivaly Jamese Browna, atd. V poslednĂ­ dobÄ pouÂĂ­vajĂ­ mnohem vĂ­ce sytezĂĄtorĹŻ neÂ dĹĂ­ve, moÂnĂĄ proto je kamarĂĄd opustil.', '2003-03-19 15:06:16', 888, 0),
(897, 13, 'daniel', 'bustamad@wabash.edu', 'Advice', 'Hi:\r\n\r\nI need to learn Czech language as fast as possible. I\'ve got 2 months to do so, otherwise I\'ll miss a great Educational opportunity in the Cz.Rep.\r\n\r\nI must pass an exam with basic knowledge of the language.I\'m travelling to podebrady next week.\r\n\r\nWhich methods/books/techniques/onlineresources/exercises, etc would you recommend me?\r\n\r\nThanks', '2003-03-20 01:03:00', 0, 1),
(898, 13, 'Carol Damioli', 'cdamioli@praguepost.com', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'Yes, I\'d like to see your 1,000-word list when it\'s finished, even if it\'s an Excel file. Thanks.', '2003-03-20 16:59:39', 895, 1),
(899, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Advice', 'Hi Daniel,\r\nMy best advice to you would be to try to get a 1-to-1 teacher for the speed - Czech phonology can be quite tricky to a native English speaker and if you go for schoolarly sort of excersises, the amount of grammar might be quite off-putting (that\'s how I started with English and, frankly, after 5 years of various courses was quite lost in London for months after arrival). \r\nHow much info do you have about the exam? I mean, are you expected to have basic survival level of mainly spoken command or is it the equivalent of the English exam for academic purposes (ie. heavy reading and comprehention)?\r\nHolding my thumbs (=fingers crossed, I\'m actually not sure why we prefer that but there you go - good luck),\r\nJana', '2003-03-20 17:14:46', 897, 0),
(900, 13, 'Mike', '', 'Offering help', 'Hi everyone,\r\n\r\nI am a Czech university student of English. I find the idea of this web site quite interesting. I would like to offer help with learning Czech. Here is my ICQ number\r\n\r\n177329836\r\n\r\njust contact me and we can start talking about almost everything and in the meantime we can teach each other new language skills. Just dont be shy:)', '2003-03-24 02:45:57', 0, 0),
(901, 13, 'Dominik LukeÂ', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: Advice', 'See also <a href=http://www.bohemica.com/bookstore>http://www.bohemica.com/bookstore</a> on this site. If you\'re really commited, David Short\'s teach yourself is pretty good. Of course taking part in a summer school or some other intensive course is your best (if not only) bet. I assume that\'s what you\'re going to be doing in PodÄbrady, anyway. \r\n\r\nA course that offers a serious kick off is one based on suggestopedia (sometimes called alpha learning). But they\'re expensive (upwards of $500 for a week-long intensive) and hard to find for Czech.\r\n\r\nA technique that help me get on a more advanced level of a French course was literally transcribing the first six units of a textbook (but that worked for me). ', '2003-03-24 03:48:42', 897, 0),
(902, 17, 'Dominik LukeÂ', 'forum@bohemica.com', '17 pĂĄdĹŻ ÄeskĂŠho jazyka', 'Tohle jsem dostal nedĂĄvno emailem. Tak mÄ napadlo, Âe by to studentĹŻm mohlo pomoci porozumÄt tomu, jak fungujĂ­ ÄeskĂŠ pĂĄdy.\r\n\r\n\r\n\"17 pĂĄdĹŻ ÄeskĂŠho jazyka\" \r\n\r\n\r\nÄeskĂ˝ jazyk mĂĄ v souÄasnĂŠ dob&#236; 17 pĂĄdĹŻ. MezilidskĂŠ vztahy v naÂĂ­ \r\nspoleÄnosti si vyÂĂĄdaly (kromÄ pĹŻvodnĂ­ch sedmi) dalÂĂ­ch 10 pĂĄdĹŻ: \r\n\r\n\r\n8. pĂĄd  kdo z koho  korytativ  \r\n9. pĂĄd  koho, jak  buzerativ  \r\n10. pĂĄd  koho, kam  delimitativ  \r\n11. pĂĄd  kdo, koho  likvidativ  \r\n12. pĂĄd  jak na koho  intrikativ  \r\n13. pĂĄd  komu, co  podmazativ  \r\n14. pĂĄd  co za co  korupcional  \r\n15. pĂĄd  jak komu  protekcional  \r\n16. pĂĄd  kdo s kĂ˝m  kopulativ  \r\n17. pĂĄd  co na co  prezervativ  \r\n', '2003-03-24 23:11:18', 0, 0),
(903, 13, 'Mark', 'jrs@bigvalley.net', 'Czech Heritage?', 'Hi, I\'ve been researching my family tree for a project I\'m doing in my history class. The farthest I can date back to is through my grandmother\'s side to a man named John Huss. All I know about him is that he lived a little before the time of Martin Luther in his hometown of Prague in Czechoslavakia. However, I\'m curious as to whether he may have been Czech at all. I don\'t have a typical taste of what Czech names are like, but could Huss be one of them? i really am very curious about this unknown heritage! Might anyone be able to help me? Any help would sure be appreciated! Thanks.', '2003-03-26 00:45:11', 0, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(904, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech Heritage?', 'Hi Mark,\r\nI\'m no expert in history (used to hate it at school) but the reference might be to Jan Hus changed by translation (Jan is quite common Czech name and Hus might have been altered to help the pronunciation, Czech \'u\' never sounds like \'kjuu\' in e.g. \'cute\') in which case you\'ve good really quite famous ancestry! Have a look on http://mujweb.cz/www/historycz/\r\n(by the way, Czechoslovakia existed as a one country for historically quite short period of time, so it might help and narrow your search but would mean that John Huss can\'t be the Jan Hus who lived around 1400AD)\r\nIn any case, good luck with finding out,\r\nJana', '2003-03-26 20:38:57', 903, 0),
(905, 13, 'Jonathan Kosarev', 'jk190778@hotmail.com', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'The Primsleur Approach sounds very promising. There are actually supposed to be 2500 most frequently used words (in any language) that would give you communicative capability in that language. If you try it tell us how it turns out.\r\nhttp://www.pimsleurapproach.com/em.asp?learn=Czech&v=CZEGo01-01', '2003-03-26 23:27:52', 885, 1),
(906, 13, 'Erik Piper', 'erik@sky.cz', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'Hello Carol,\r\n\r\nI\'m working on this, but it will take a really long time. It appears that no publicly available version (like SYNEK) of the Czech National Corpus enables frequency analysis (finding the frequency of a word, let alone a dictionary-headword), and building one\'s own corpus with usable size and quality is sheer hell.\r\n\r\nIn a few months, a frequency dictionary of Czech will be published, and will have by its nature a wordlist of much better quality than what I could ever produce in the few hours per week I can devote to this. I feel a little silly, therefore, for trying.\r\n\r\nIn the meantime, here are two frequency lists from papers called Frekvence vzorĹŻ ÄeskĂ˝ch sloves and Frekvence vzorĹŻ ÄeskĂ˝ch substantiv by KlĂĄra OsolsobÄ, Karel Pala, and Pavel RychlĂ˝. They show the top 50 verbs and the top 100 nouns. The numbers you see below are numbers of appearances in the DESAM corpus, and were too much trouble to remove from the list.\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\nErik\r\n\r\nVERBS:\r\n\r\nbĂ˝t  33726\r\nmĂ­t (se)   6154\r\nĹĂ­ci    4768\r\nmuset    1708\r\nchtĂ­t    1313\r\naby    1152\r\njĂ­t    1147\r\nstĂĄt (se)     856\r\ndĂĄt (se)      705\r\nvÄdÄt      697\r\nzaÄĂ­t      663\r\nuvĂŠst          660\r\ndostat      625\r\nzĂ­skat      609\r\nplatit      503\r\nznamenat     593\r\nhrĂĄt (si)     493\r\npĹijĂ­t      488\r\nĹĂ­kat      487\r\nvidÄt      478\r\nvĂŠst      468\r\npatĹit      431\r\nexistovat     402\r\nnajĂ­t      385\r\npracovat     382\r\npotĹebovat     363\r\npovaÂovat     359\r\nzĹŻstat      356\r\nrozhodnout     353\r\ndojĂ­t      348\r\ndokĂĄzat     331\r\nzmÄnit      318\r\npĹipravit     315\r\nmyslit (si)     307\r\nstaÄit     296\r\ndÄlat (se)    295\r\nÄekat     292\r\ntvrdit     291\r\npodaĹit se    290\r\njednat     289\r\ndosĂĄhnout    288\r\ndĂĄvat     281\r\nobjevit    277\r\nvytvoĹit    276\r\nznĂĄt (se)    273\r\npoÄĂ­tat     272\r\ntvoĹit     271\r\nzdĂĄt se     263\r\ndodat     262\r\nnabĂ­zet     261\r\n\r\nNOUNS:\r\nrok	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â   Â Â Â  2654\r\nPraha	Â Â Â Â Â Â   Â Â Â Â Â  1958\r\nÄlovÄk	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â    1646\r\nauto	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â   Â  1361\r\ndoba	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â   Â Â Â  1341\r\ncena	Â Â Â Â Â Â   Â Â Â Â Â  1165\r\nstrana	Â Â Â Â   Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  1143\r\nkoruna	Â Â   Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  1063\r\nzemÄ	  Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  1061\r\nspoleÄnostÂ Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  1008\r\npĹĂ­pad 	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  982\r\nprĂĄce	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  971\r\nzĂĄkon	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  952\r\nden	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  943\r\nmĂ­sto 	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  936\r\nvlĂĄda	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  891\r\nsystĂŠm	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  887\r\nsvÄt 	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  827\r\nmoÂnost	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  799\r\nvÄtÂina Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  770\r\ntrh	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  763\r\npodnik	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  751\r\nproblĂŠm	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  737\r\nÄĂĄst 	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  730\r\nprogram	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  719\r\nskupina	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  715\r\nrepublikaÂ Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  679\r\nprocento Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  672\r\nmÄsto	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  652\r\nministr	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  622\r\nEvropa	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  615\r\nÄas	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  605\r\notĂĄzka	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  603\r\nvĂ˝sledek Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  603\r\nzmÄna	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  599\r\nĹada	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  584\r\nsituace	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  577\r\noblast	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  577\r\nsluÂba	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  560\r\nsmlouva	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  556\r\nprezidentÂ Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  554\r\nzpĹŻsob	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  544\r\ninformaceÂ Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  536\r\npoÄet	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  533\r\nkontakt	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  531\r\nsnĂ­mek	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â  527\r\nÄlen	Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â 