(908, 13, 'Matt Blewett', 'matthew.blewett@tiscali.cz', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'I had about two months to learn the language, but I didn\'t have to take a test...  if you have high speed internet access and need to learn communication, I recommend tuning into cz radio stations to get a feel for the speed and sound of the language.  Many links to online stations can be found at www.radia.unas.cz  hodnÄ ÂtÄstĂ­ = good luck.\r\n\r\n', '2003-04-02 00:52:20', 885, 1),
(909, 13, 'Miss Prompan Nam', 'r_lim@hotmail.com', 'Re: Translation (Czech to English)', 'Hello ÂINSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(964, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Where I was myself here he takes', '<b><i>So I have the text for this Czech song and I understand almost all of it except for one line: \"Kde jsem se tu vlastne vzal\".</b></i>\r\n\r\nIt is indeed a weird phrase. In short, in natural English: a cross between \"How the heck did I get here?\" and \"from where the heck did I come from/have I come?\"\r\n\r\nBelow are three more examples from the SYNEK extract from the Czech National Corpus. There were found using the following query:\r\n([] [word=\"kde\"] [] [word=\"se\"] [] [lemma=\"vzĂ­t\"])\r\nI was too lazy to learn how to look up the sources automatically (I\'m just learning to use it).\r\n\r\nA najednou vĂĄm... od jistĂŠ rychlosti... zaÄne brizance dÄsnÄ stoupat. Roste... kvadraticky. JĂĄ koukĂĄm jako blĂĄzen. Odkud se to bere? <b>Kde kde kde se najednou vzala</b> ta energie?\" nalĂŠhal Prokop zimniÄnÄ. \"Tak ĹeknÄte.\" \"Nu, tĹeba v atomu,\" mĂ­nil Plinius.\r\n(obviously taken from Äapek\'s <i>Krakatit</i>)\r\n\r\n\"Dostaneme knĂ­Âky a budeme ÄĂ­st. SnĂ­me nÄco, vypijeme skleniÄku vĂ­na a - nehĂ˝bejte se! JĂĄ vĂĄs neukousnu.\" \"Co mnÄ je?\" ptal se Prokop nesmÄle. Doktor se vztyÄil. \"No, nic uÂ. PoslechnÄte, <b>kde jste se tady vzal</b>?\" \"Kde tady?\" \"Tady, v TĂ˝nici. Sebrali jsme vĂĄs na podlaze a... Odkud jste, ÄlovÄÄe, pĹiÂel?\" \"JĂĄ nevĂ­m. Z Prahy, ne?\" vzpomĂ­nal Prokop.\r\n(obviously also taken from Äapek\'s <i>Krakatit</i>)\r\n\r\n6:11 - Ve stanici zastavuje vlak z Berouna, jenÂe bez Marky... DĂĄme jĂ­ akademickou Ätvrthodinku. Blbost, <b> kde by se tu vzala</b>, jeÂtÄ spĂ­, jdeme na to...\r\n(unknown source)\r\n\r\nAs for \"vzĂ­t se\" in general, here are other ways it is used in the first fifty (NOT top 50!) instances I found in SYNEK (of which some were thrown out due to my clumsy query):\r\n\"kdyÂ se to vezme (...)\" - when you take X (description of Y way, e.g. \"z hlediska ĂşplnĂŠho konce\")\r\n\r\n	kdyÂ se to vezme kolem a kolem - when all is said and done\r\n\r\n\"kdyÂ se to vezme do [e.g.] ruky\" - just the typical Czech reflexive equivalent of the English passive construction or \"you\"/\"one\" construction (when it\'s taken into someone\'s hand/when one takes it into its hand/when you take it into your hand)\r\n\r\n\"vzĂ­t se\" to marry, when speaking of both partners, as you mentioned.\r\n\r\n<b><i>I assume \"vzit se\" means not just get married but also put yourself, as in \"put yourself into somebody else\'s shoes\"</b></i>\r\n\r\nNope. Just an idiom (a weird one), like, for that matter, \"to end up\" in English.\r\n\r\n<b><i>And this sort of sentence is the kind that nearly gives me aneurysms on a regular basis, with the important parts on the ends and all the little particles in the middle.</b></i>\r\n\r\nIf you search the forum archives for the last two months or so, you\'ll find some posts I wrote for Scott touching briefly on Czech sentence structure - in short though, your complaint, intentionally or not, is a good succint, if simplistic, description of how Czech sentence structure works. I wouldn\'t worry about the simplistic part - you <i>should</i> start simple, and then refine or you\'ll just get frustrated, IMO.\r\n\r\n<b><i>I have twice as many problems with this sort of thing as I do with \"dostat\" and the imperfect and consonant clusters and adjective endings.</b></i>\r\n\r\nThat\'s understandable, I think, since it seems to be undertaught in Czech courses (it\'s so subtle and abstract on first glance that maybe Czechs don\'t even want to <b>try</b> to explain it - it\'s even worse than trying to explain English article English if you speak English IMO). The things you mention here are much more concrete.\r\n\r\n<b><i>Two other things: what does \"kudy dal\" mean?</b></i>\r\n\"which way to go on\", \"which way to go from here\", \"where to go from here\", \"where X should go on/from here\". Literally \"which-way onwards\".\r\n\r\n<b><i>And where is the preposition \"u\" used, aside from the names of pubs?</b></i>\r\nHere are the ways it is used in the first 50 hits in SYNEK: (adj./adv. in means adjective or adverb - either single-word, or adj/adv phrase.)\r\n\r\nat (e.g. u benzinovĂŠ pumpy, u pultu, u luÂky, u dveĹĂ­)\r\n\r\ncountless idioms dominated by other words, e.g.:\r\n\r\n	s pÄnou u pusy - foaming at the mouth (presumably, not 100% sure)\r\n\r\n	co musĂ­m udÄlat je odÂkrtanĂŠ u kaÂdĂŠ poloÂky - every item of what I must do is checked off\r\n	\r\n	Âeny pÄknÄ u plotny - th\'wimmn barefoot\'n\'pregnant like dey should (plotna means a burner on a stove)\r\n	\r\n	directly-or-indirectly-emotional-verb + u + srdce - chovat u srdce, hĹeje vĂĄs u srdce\r\n\r\nu X - at X\'s place\r\n\r\n[verb] u [organization] - with/for (e.g. pracovat u velkĂŠ spoleÄnosti, also: mĂĄ mĂ­sto u velkĂŠ firmy - he\'s got a job with a big company)\r\n\r\nby (e.g. u Ĺeky, u jezera, u plĂĄÂe, u luÂky (!), u nohou, u dveĹĂ­ (!)) - as you can see, u is used for both at and by, but IME it\'s not a problem to determine which is meant from the context.\r\n\r\nwith (e.g. zĹŻstal u nÄj)\r\n\r\nX je adjectoverbed u [group] - X is adjectoverbed among [group of people/animals] (e.g. pampeliÂky jsou oblibenĂŠ u vÄel - dandelions are much favored among bees, more naturally with this particular adjective - dandelions are one of bees\' favorite flowers)\r\n\r\nto/at (nĂĄvÂtÄva u koho)\r\n\r\nX [<--some issue] skonÄĂ­ u Y - X ends up on Y\'s \"plate\"\r\n\r\nX-which-can-be-a-part-of-Y u Y - a Y X (kapsiÄka u bundy - a jacket pocket, zip u kalhotek - a pants zipper, prsty u nohou - lower-limbs fingers - that is, yer toes!)\r\n\r\nX [usually a person] to mĂĄ u Y [adj.] - X is adj. with Y (e.g. Jestli umĂ­Â hrĂĄt, mĂĄÂ to u mÄ dobrĂ˝ - If you know how to play, you\'re alright with me)\r\n\r\nX je u person-Y [adj.] - X is [adv. or adv.-phrase] in Y\'s case/life/situation; X is adj. for Y (e.g. rozpory mezi Âivotem a vĂ­rou jsou u nĂ­ zanedbatelnĂŠ - in her case, the differences between life and faith are negligible; bylo to u nÄho vzĂĄcnĂŠ - it was rare for him; VĂ˝raz jeho tvĂĄĹe jsem Äasto vĂ­dĂĄval u hrĂĄÄĹŻ pokru - he wore an expression I\'d often seen in poker players).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nAs you can see, in the world of Czech (and BTW not only Czech) prepositions, saying a certain Czech preposition means a certain English one will only take you so far. It\'s helpful for a start, but then you have to throw it away and say... \"u\" means \"u\"!\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-05-07 17:59:28', 962, 0),
(965, 13, 'Charlotte', 'charloshka@yahoo.com', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'This is a great list. Do you have a corresponding list of these words in English? \r\nThank you,\r\nCharlotte', '2003-05-08 08:22:11', 906, 0),
(966, 13, 'Charlotte', 'charloshka@yahoo.com', 'Re: Important Czech words', 'This is a great list. Do you have a corresponding list of these words in English? \r\nThank you,\r\nCharlotte', '2003-05-08 08:22:38', 906, 0),
(967, 13, 'Mira', '', 'Woe is me', 'I really find the sentence structure infuriating, most likely because I\'m still trapped in my little English-speaking mind, and since English is a language whose sentence structure is made out of reinforced steel, as opposed to Czech whose sentences seem to be made of whatever material they make those bridges that sway back and forth. It also doesn\'t help that the prepositions can mean about eighty million different things. \r\n\r\nThank you, thank you, thank you, for your reply. It helped a whole lot.\r\n\r\nAlso: how do you say \"that makes me\" or \"you make me\" as in, \"You make me mad!\" or \"That made me happy!\"', '2003-05-08 10:59:08', 964, 0),
(968, 15, 'David Lester', 'lstr3@cs.com', 'what to take on trip', 'Hello,\r\nI am going to Czech in July.  I would like to take items that are difficult to obtain in the Czech Republic as gifts to the people I am living with.  Can you recommend things to bring?\r\nThank you,\r\nDave Lester', '2003-05-08 21:15:51', 0, 1),
(969, 15, 'Scott', '', 'Re: what to take on trip', 'Hi Dave,\r\n\r\nI know when I went to see my future mother-in-law, I took some things like chocalate chips, peanut butter, and various cooking things that are not common there.  I also brought some things for other people like Texas belt buckles(since I live in Texas), American Coin collection items (e.g. Birth Sets if you know the years of their birth), Shirts with things like American Flags on them(Anything with something American on it most children love or if you are in Britain, anything British would do the same), hats, and so on.  If you can talk to the people you are going to live with, I would ask them what their interests are and try to get a good image of them, then I would buy gifts based on what they are like.  I think a lot of it depends on the person.  I think that if you see something you think is special that it would be great.  I am not sure where you are from, but I know from experience that anything special I brought from America was greatly appreciated.  I hope this helps.\r\nMaybe someone else could give some more input.\r\n\r\nCheers!\r\nScott', '2003-05-09 00:43:30', 968, 0),
(970, 13, 'Penny', 'nickel@gwtc.net', 'Re: Need help translating', 'thank you again for your help with my translations.  Am doing family history and it is hard at times when there is a language barrier.', '2003-05-09 03:21:33', 941, 0),
(971, 13, 'Penny', 'nickel@gwtc.net', 'Re: Need help translating', 'Thank you again with your help with my translations.  Am doing family history and it is hard sometimes when there is a language barrier.', '2003-05-09 03:23:22', 945, 0),
(972, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Woe is me', 'Well, try to imagine how I feel trying to fit my wobly Czech mind into the English structure with all those \'auxiliaries\' and tenses and phrasal verbs and not just preposition but virtually anything having tons of meanings ...:))) \r\nTaky z toho silim!', '2003-05-11 23:13:03', 967, 1),
(973, 13, 'Jakub', 'xjacob@yahoo.com', 'Re: Woe is me', 'I fully understand your despair. Right now, trying to learn Hebrew, I can see how the language can twist and spill out of the bounds of my own czech+english mind frame. \r\nYou do, indeed, have to be very open-minded in learning Czech. The words can mean billions of things at the same time, but once you manage to learn a finite amount of these basic expressions, you can put them together in any way you desire to construct virtually any sentence - as opposed to English, where the grammar is set in stone, but to write a good sentence, you have to know a lot of fancy idioms. \r\nYet, concerning your question, be aware that \"Kde jsem se to vlastne vzal\" is an idiomatic expression. \"Vzit\" means \"to take\", while its reflexive form \"vzit se\" is rendered \"to marry\" - there are no more common generic meanings for \"vzit\", as far as I know. \"Kde jsem se tu vlastne vzal?\" is an idiom meaning \"How did I get here afterall?\". In feary tales, you can encounter \"Kde se vzal, tu se vzal\", meaning \"And out of nowhere, there he was/appeared\" - I wouldn\'t attempt to derive the meaning of such idioms from the dictionary definitions of individual words - all you can do with idioms is learn/memorize them. Cheers, Jakub ', '2003-05-13 07:22:45', 967, 0),
(974, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Woe is me', 'Hi Jakub,\r\n\r\njust a short note - \"How did I get here afterall?\" sounds quite unnatural; \"after all\" is an idiom meaning \"contrary to what was expected\" or \"you know\", as in:\r\n\r\nHe said he would be late, but he came after all.\r\nIt\'s a small world after all.\r\nLooks like it\'s not going to rain after all.\r\n\r\nAlso, watch out for that steel-tight English structure in \"Out of nowhere appeared X\" - although this is one of the few cases where subject/verb inversion doesn\'t look TOTALLY wacky, you should still be cautious. In general, I think it\'s better for Czechs to be too cautious about inverting English subjects and verbs than not cautious enough.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-05-13 15:30:12', 973, 0),
(975, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'The lovely English \'a\'', 'Hi,\r\nCould anyone please shed some logic into such a basic question as \'a\' or not to \'a\'?\r\nWhat I\'m trying to understand is where the heck do need to put it; well obviously in front of nouns but in my Czech logic,\'a\' is totaly redundant, plurals are usually marked quite sufficiently. I\'ve finally managed to tame the bloody tenses in most of the times, I hope, but this really escapes me.\r\nThanks a lot,\r\nJana\r\nEric, should this provoke your linguistic tendencies, may you please try to restrict yourself to succint and fullproof comments? (Only joking, I thing your linguistic knowledge is adorable!)', '2003-05-13 21:51:24', 0, 1),
(976, 13, 'Jakub', 'xjacob@yahoo.com', 'Re: Woe is me', 'Good point. I was just trying to find the cloesest expression possible that would express the Czech idiom.\r\nRegarding the \"after all issue, I would leave that unchanged. \"Kde jsem se tu vlastne vzal?\" is an expression that implies some dose of surprise, after all... :-) Although your translation \"How the heck did I get in here?\" might be better.\r\nAnyway, you are right, Czechs love to mess up the word order in just about any kind of English sentence. Do you have any idea what the most common mistakes are? In the years I have been living in English-speaking world, I am afraid I established my own Czech-English language, which is perfectly understandable to everyone, but is never corrected (people are simply too polite).\r\nI guess, other than simple grammar mistakes, word order is the no.1 fault, is it not?   ', '2003-05-14 11:04:08', 974, 0),
(977, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Succinctly, OK.\r\nI\'ll leave details to others.\r\nThe \"grammatical\" names of the two types of articles really do say something about them.\r\n\r\n\"a/an\" is, roughly speaking, indefinite - vague, unknown, previously unmentioned, unsure.\r\n\"the\" is definite - sure, already-mentioned, known, a known topic, a topic assumed known.\r\n\r\nYou MIGHT even think of it this way. If your sister comes into the room and says \"I found a cat,\" it means \"NaÂla jsem koÄku\". If she says \"I found the cat,\" it means \"NaÂla jsem... tu koÄku; tu naÂi koÄku; koÄku, co jsme o nĂ­ vÄera mluvili; koÄku, co vÂichni vĂ­Ăł*, o kterou jde\" etc.\r\n\r\nOne last hint in your quest. Learn what a <i>determiner</i> is. Articles are AFAIK only needed for singular nouns with no other determiner. There\'s five types, can\'t remember \'em all.... articles, possessives, demonstratives, maybe partitives, and thingamajigs.\r\n\r\nThat wasn\'t succinct. Sorry.\r\n\r\nErik\r\n* well maybe not YOUR sister... that was my Brno-ish voice speaking there.', '2003-05-14 16:49:47', 975, 0),
(978, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Wordlist - apology', 'Hello all,\r\n\r\nI\'m simply NOT going to be able to compose and give to anyone any sort of \"top 1000 czech words list.\" I\'ve found it\'s not possible for one person working alone (and busy with many other things). My apologies to those who were eagerly awaiting it.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-05-14 16:53:41', 0, 0),
(979, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Woe is me', 'Hmm... if you mean \"is word order the no. 1 fault for Czechs in general?\" I\'d say it\'s pretty well tied with article errors, capitalization errors, and the \"of of the of of the of\" error, which is theoretically just stylistic, but when you mix it with the other two, you get incomprehensible sentences like this (which I used to have to correct a LOT in the old days when the societal standards for translators were lower than today):\r\n\r\n\"Since 1990 increases quickly health of the Nature of mountains of region of Broumovsko thanks to realizing establishment of improved system of defended territories of the Nature.\"\r\n\r\nActually, that sentence (my own invention anyway) was still almost understandable. I\'ll have to dig up one of those torturous sentences from the bad old days.\r\n\r\nAnyway:\r\n1. Most of the time, people really are too polite to correct any but the grossest errors. Anyone willing to correct you is a treasure.\r\n2. Word order is the most alien and meaning-destructive fault, so it\'s very important to tackle. Man bites dog is not the same as Dog bites man. (<-- articles omitted here reflect newspaper-headline usage)\r\n3. Hard to say if it\'s the most common, really.\r\n\r\nBy the way, \"after all\" doesn\'t work with that sort of questions, which I guess is how I should have explained the problem. However, you can ask questions like \"He did come after all, didn\'t he?\" (in which case \"after all\" means \"pĹece\").\r\n\r\nErr... sorry for spamming this board, folks! I just wanted to answer Jakub!\r\n\r\n[slinks away]', '2003-05-14 17:07:36', 976, 0),
(980, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Well, exactly, what do you need the \'a\' for? If my sister found more than one (poor her), it would be \'cats\' and if it was her Micinka, it would by \'the cat\'.*\r\n\r\nAnyway, what actually induced this was that in some respects my essays got on a relatively decent level and although I now and then misspell (just realised how dependent I am on a spellchecker), mix some prepositions, twist collocations or miss the odd \'the\' (I mean definite article), the \'a\' seems to be the one most commented on by my lecturers.\r\n\r\nSo, please share with me if you have any practical tricks for not leaving them (= indefinite articles, how can one plularise an \'a\'?) out. So far I\'ve tried to watch out for cases where I\'d say \'one\' (of the whatever) but didn\'t help. And the trouble with me and grammar is (besides of being leathaly boring) that I find it quite difficult to apply the rules on pieces of writing where I\'m desperate to make sense (and impress my markers by the content.)\r\n\r\nThanks,\r\nJana\r\n\r\n*not sure but don\'t think she ever had one and finding several would probably feak her out', '2003-05-14 17:33:58', 977, 0),
(981, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Hello Jana,\r\n\r\n<b><i>Well, exactly, what do you need the \'a\' for? If my sister found more than one (poor her), it would be \'cats\' and if it was her Micinka, it would by \'the cat\'.*</b></i>\r\n\r\nWhat, exactly, does one need genders for? English gets by with implied gender except for a few words (pronouns) where it can be easily implied. What do we need rigid sentence structures for? Czech gets by (mostly) without them. What do we need the use of sentence structures to express old and new information for? English gets by (mostly) without it. Czech meanwhile gets by without articles at all (but replaces them to some degree with sentence structure). Why mark the subject\'s number and gender in the verb? In adjectives? We already noted them once!\r\n\r\nLanguages include redundancy, partly because they\'re created by humans and not computers (thank God), and partly because language is above all spoken, and redundancy means you can afford to miss a bit.\r\n\r\nAnyway - just keep in mind that if a singular noun doesn\'t need \"the\" and has no other determiner, then it needs, <i>needs</i> \"a\" or else it sounds alien. (I say \"alien\" because article omission is rare among speakers of West European languages, the foreign speakers we (Americans) know best, as mnost or perhaps all of these languages have articles.) Note that plural nouns get indefinite articles too -- it\'s called having nothing there. It makes linguistic sense, since most plural nouns are indefinite, whereas the ratio of a\'s to the\'s is much more balanced. Here the English language (and probably others, can\'t think right now) chose simplicity over redundancy.\r\n\r\nFor a quick check of what the other types of determiners are besides articles, see e.g. <a href=\"http://www.clas.ufl.edu/users/rthompso/gramdet6.html\">http://www.clas.ufl.edu/users/rthompso/gramdet6.html</a>. For more pages on determiners, you might try this google search: <a href=\"http://www.google.com/search?q=determiners+possessives+demonstratives&ie=ISO-8859-2&hl=cs&lr=\">http://www.google.com/search?q=determiners+possessives+demonstratives&ie=ISO-8859-2&hl=cs&lr=</a>.\r\n\r\n<b><i>So, please share with me if you have any practical tricks for not leaving them (= indefinite articles, how can one plularise an \'a\'?)</b></i>\r\nLike this:\r\na\'s.\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\nErik', '2003-05-15 00:04:49', 980, 0),
(982, 13, 'Scott Brown', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Hello,\r\n\r\nWhen you asked this question it really boggled my mind.  I am so used to using articles to express how many or what type something is that I have had to completely try to forget them being natural and think of them as a Czech would.  I know one trick you might try is just accepting that they have to be there.  I had to do this with Czech.  It is weird for me to not specify something with a word, but by the way it is said.  I had to just stop thinking of why it is this way and just start accepting that this is how it is.  Lets say you want to state something e.g. I have a dog, I would like a cat, I would like the cat, I need a cat, than it is imperative to have them.  \r\n\r\nI have thought of it like this:  \r\nLets say you want to talk about getting a cat OK. If you say <B>\"I got cat\" </b> or <b>\"I picked up cat\"</b> it does not specify how many or what type of cat has been picked up.  It almost sounds like you have ordered some to eat :) haha.  Anyway, when you say it like this without any articles it is open to interpretation as to what kind or how many there are, but to a native speaker it sounds like you have made cat plural without adding an \'s\', or rather it sounds like you are making a generalization to all cats or a group of cats.  However, if you say <B>\"I picked up the cat\", \"I picked up a cat\", \"I got some cats\", \"I bought several cats, \"I received two cats\",</b> and so on... Than you have specified how many cats have been picked up, therefore the articles or other adjectives are modifiers of the word \'cat\'.  In English when you diagram a sentence the articles are placed underneath the word just as any other modifier would be (adjectives, adverbs, phrases, etc.).  So, just as you would use any other modifier to describe or further support the noun or object you need atricles to do the same.  I think if you think of an article as a reinforcer of a word that has to be there to specify what type it is or how many there are, then it will benefit you greatly in this endeavor.  Almost always (atleast I can\'t think of any examples that say otherwise) if you have a noun in a sentence that is receiving some sort of action or of some type of object than there should always be an article.  If you say <b>\"A dog barked\"</b> it is not being specific as to which dog.  It is just saying there was some dog that barked.  However, if you say <B>\"that dog\"</b> or <b>\"the dog barked\"</b>, you have specified which one did it.  In making generalizations you can use <b>\"a or an\"</b>(indefinate articles) but if you want to be more specific say<b> \"the\"</b>(definate article) or use pronouns like <B>\"that, this, his, her\".</b>\r\n\r\nBasically articles are just fillers and must be in place so you know what the noun is referring to.  I am sorry I cannot be of more help or if I have not answered the question directly.  This is a very hard topic for me to explain.  Try this website: <a href=\" http://owl.english.purdue.edu/handouts/esl/eslart.html\"</a> and see if it can help.  I will ask my fiancĂŠ, who is Czech, how she understands articles and try to gain some more insight as to how you might feel about the topic.  I think if I can get her to explain what it is like coming from your point of view, than I might be able to be of some more help.  Also, try going to google and looking for \"article explanations on English grammer\" and similar wordings and I think you find some useful information.\r\n\r\nI hope this serves as some help.\r\nHave a great day!\r\nScott', '2003-05-15 01:00:57', 975, 0),
(983, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Hi, Scott!\r\nThanks a lot! I think that\'s exactly what its about - so natural for you, so \'can\'t-get-used-to-them\' for me. I\'ll try the web but I\'m much clearer already - thanks to your brilliant explanation! The distinction between \'a cat\' as one particular (although unknown/unspecified) animal and \'cat\' as refering to feline predators as a specie might work for me. \r\n...hope my essays will be \'a/an\'ed to perfection now :)\r\nYou\'ve been great help, much appreciated - anything I might try to help you with in return? (Well, I know, you probably have much better sources at hand and I\'m just starting to get the \'feel\' for English and how different the concepts are in comparison with Czech but would be happy to try my best.)\r\nThanks again,\r\nJana', '2003-05-15 18:05:29', 982, 0),
(984, 13, 'Scott Brown', 'scott.brown@ilovejesus.net', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Hi Jana,\r\n\r\nYou are very welcome.  I am just glad I was able to help a little.  Actually I do not have very good sources.  I am currently in Texas until August, and in the area in which I live there is no one who is interested in anything Czech.  :) In fact many people do not even know that Czech and Slovakia are two seperate countries, or rather they choose to stay ignorant and not learn that fact, even after I tell them a thousand times.  That becomes very annoying after two years of hearing that mistake from the same people :).  I appreciate the offer and may take you up on it sometime.  Right now I cannot think of anything.  I am currently trying to get a good grasp on the accusitive case.  The hardest part is trying to remember all the rules!!!!  \r\n\r\nBy the way, here are a few more links for you to check out:\r\n<a href=\"http://webster.commnet.edu/grammar/determiners/determiners.htm\">Articles and Determiners</a><BR><a href=\"http://grammar.englishclub.com/adjectives-determiners-the-a-an.htm\">English Club</a><BR><a href=\"http://www.pixi.com/~creativs/reptgakp/common.htm#1articles\">A good explanation of articles</a>\r\n\r\nSorry, I haven\'t had much time to search for more sites, but the last one I think will help the best.  HondnÄ ÂtÄsti!!!\r\n\r\nScott', '2003-05-17 01:00:56', 983, 0),
(985, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Well, if it helps, it\'s not that much different here in London and that\'s Europe and +/- 2hrs on a plane. Don\'t get upset, it must have somenthing to do with the ocean :)\r\nThanks, good luck with the cases (so glad I don\'t have to learn them via grammar rules, tried German ones and it was confusing enough) and brilliant time in sunny Texas (that\'s how I picture it in torrential London),\r\nJana', '2003-05-19 18:24:56', 984, 0),
(986, 17, 'Katrin', 'seine_ratte@web.de', 'MĂĄm otĂĄsku o Praze', 'Jsem studenka a chci studovat v Praze.\r\nMusĂ­m jeste hledat dobrĂĄ univerzita. Ted\' studujĂ­ cestinu a anglictinu v prvnĂ­ roce v Nemecky. \r\nMoje otĂĄska je: Jak muzeme nachĂĄzet nedrĂĄhĂ˝ byt v Praze? Jak je studentskĂŠ kolej v CeskĂŠ republice???', '2003-05-21 01:07:43', 0, 1),
(987, 13, 'mike', 'youthchaos@hotmail.com', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'I don\'t know if it\'s any help, but here\'s my succint but philosophical view on the indefinite article.  There is a mass of \"cat\" floating around in the universe.  We can think of it as the Platonic \"form\" of \"cat\".  Using the indefinite article summons up a single cat out of this general idea of cat...not a cat that we are neccessarily familiar with, but one single cat...', '2003-05-24 00:29:55', 980, 0),
(988, 13, 'Vlastik', 'va_nh@yahoo.com', '', 'Koukam ze je tady zhava debata.Kdyby nekdo potreboval pomoc,jsem z cech a ziji v usa.Moje zena je americanka tak by mi pripadne ten preklad do anglictiny opravila.Zatim cau Vlastik.', '2003-05-25 01:54:10', 0, 0),
(989, 13, 'Vlastik', 'va_nh@yahoo.com', '', 'Koukam ze je tady zhava debata.Kdyby nekdo potreboval pomoc,jsem z cech a ziji v usa.Moje zena je americanka tak by mi pripadne ten preklad do anglictiny opravila.Zatim cau Vlastik.', '2003-05-25 01:55:18', 0, 0),
(990, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: The lovely English \'a\'', 'Thanks, Mike, sounds logical, hope it\'ll help (I\'ll do my best to keep it in my mind when essay-wrestling).\r\nOn the other hand, how exactly do we actually deal with this distinction in Czech? Interesting... feels like Czech cats don\'t float in the universe but that must be wrong. I guess we would just use plural for the general idea or maybe modify the word (thanks god for Czech morphology, almost anything seems solvable by it).\r\nTHX,\r\nJ.', '2003-05-28 15:47:09', 987, 0),
(991, 15, 'petra', 'petrasmale@yahoo.com', 'looking for a czech teacher in Amsterdam, the Netherlands', 'I am living in Prague now, but will be back in Amsterdam next week. I would like to continue my lessons Czech in Amsterdam, in the Netherlands. If you know a teacher email me at Petrasmale@yahoo.com\r\n', '2003-05-28 16:42:47', 0, 0),
(992, 13, 'dan', 'woodspirt60@hotmail.com', 'translation', 'Can anyone give me a translation of the following?\r\nPotoeboval az k oici nico Eech', '2003-05-29 10:55:38', 0, 1),
(993, 13, 'dan', 'woodspirt60@hotmail.com', 'translation', 'Can anyone give me a translation of the following?\r\nPotoeboval az k oici nico Eech', '2003-05-29 10:56:39', 0, 1),
(994, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: translation', 'If \"Potoeboval az k oici nico Eech\" is the text you got, then it\'s been damaged by some problems with \"character sets\" - in other words, most of the letters with accent marks have been changed. I\'ve seen a lot of damaged texts in the past, so I can make some guesses here, but none of them make sense!\r\n\r\nPotoeboval - definitely \"PotĹeboval\" (for safety\'s sake: \"Potreboval\") - \"He needed\"\r\naz - no clue. Definitely not \"aÂ\", since the person was writing with accent marks.\r\nk - most likely means \"for\" in this case.\r\noici - maybe \"ĹĂ­ci\" - \"rici\" - \"to say\"\r\nnico - probably \"nÄco\" - \"neco\" - \"something\"\r\nEech - probably \"Äech\" - \"[from] Bohemia/the Czech Republic\"\r\n\r\nEven when you take into account Czech\'s free word order, it doesn\'t make much sense.', '2003-05-29 14:23:31', 992, 0),
(995, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: translation', 'IMHO\r\n\r\n\"PotĹeboval aÂ k ĹĂ­ci nÄco Äech\"\r\n- Translate it literaly back to English (it seems to be \"translated\" (bleah :-O...) from English originaly) to get the meaning:\r\n\"He needed -?- to say something Czech\".\r\nI\'m not sure with the original word that has been translated as \"aÂ\".\r\n\r\nWhat do you think about this hypothesis?', '2003-05-30 04:03:14', 994, 0),
(996, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: translation', 'Correction: - from English or similar analytic language originaly -', '2003-05-30 04:04:31', 995, 0),
(997, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: translation', 'Sorry for writing another correction:\r\n\"az\" could be \"aÂ\" in non-damaged text.', '2003-05-30 04:07:01', 995, 0),
(998, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: translation', 'Actually, this hypothesis occurred to me, but it seemed to crazy to bring it up. Still, your analysis in detail shows it has merit. I thought about the \"az = aÂ\" hypothesis too, but actually, if we start with the \"really bad translation from English\" theory, then \"to\" could easily have become \"aÂ k\".\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-05-30 14:55:58', 995, 0),
(999, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: translation', 'Infinitive \"to say\" was probably translated badly (literaly) as \"k rici\" instead as only \"rici\", because the author\'s or translator\'s mother tongue is not English and his English is really bad. (olny a hypothesisis)', '2003-05-30 23:08:48', 998, 0),
(1000, 15, 'Anton', 'anton_spierenburg@yahoo.co.uk', 'Czech teacher wanted, Southampton UK area', 'I am looking for someone to give me guidance and a push with learning Czech language. I have the basics and the possibility to practise immediately what I have learnt. I am living in Southampton and would be interested if there was something available in the area (max. 20 mile radius).\r\n\r\n', '2003-06-02 22:04:48', 0, 1),
(1001, 13, 'Mira', '', 'Re: Woe is me', 'Or like on that list of bad Czech-English translations, \"where she married herself, there she married herself\"? Below \"prostorekost\" - \"simplegreekness\" and \"Boleslav Polivka\" - \"Paincelebrate Soup\". ', '2003-06-06 11:11:14', 973, 0),
(1002, 15, 'Majda', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: PrĂĄci?', 'Erick\r\n\r\nJust a few corrections: \r\n\r\n- not OTELEFONOVAT but OBTELEFONOVAT\r\n- not OKOUĹIT but VYKOUĹIT (okouĹit does not mean anything,\r\nvykouĹit then mean exactly what Monica did :o)\r\n\r\nTo lick itself clean - animals = OLĂZAT, not OMĂVAT \r\nOMĂVAT/OMĂT = to wash off .. e.g. ovoce pĹed jĂ­dlem poĹĂĄdnÄ omyjte or\r\nrĂĄnu na noze musĂ­te 2 x dennÄ omĂ˝vat studenou vodou or Vltava omĂ˝vĂĄ \r\npraÂskĂĄ nĂĄbĹeÂĂ­ \r\n\r\nAhoj a ÄeÂtinÄ zdar! :)\r\n\r\nMajda', '2003-06-06 19:09:12', 950, 0),
(1003, 15, 'Majda', 'littlefox@atlas.cz', 'Re: News to me (Re: v centru?)', 'V CENTRU\r\n\r\n\"V\" becomes \"F\" ... but say quickly f c f c f c f c .. and what\r\nyou hear is \"sort of\" SSSS :)) \r\n\r\nMajda', '2003-06-06 19:18:12', 917, 0),
(1004, 15, 'Carolyn Trachtova', 'trachtova@hotmail.com', 'Czech teacher needed in Atlanta, GA', 'I am desperately seeking a Czech teacher here in Atlanta. I lived in Prague for almost six years and the little Czech I know is deteriorating at a rapid pace.  My level used to be intermediate-ish, with horrible grammar.  My husband is Czech and  I want my kids to be bilingual, but if I don\'t start working on my own Czech soon I\'ll be asking them to translate for me!\r\n\r\nThanks \r\n\r\nCarolyn Trachtova', '2003-06-10 00:25:55', 0, 1),
(1005, 16, 'Carolyn Trachtova', 'trachtova@hotmail.com', 'Czech teacher needed in Atlanta, GA', 'I am desperately seeking a Czech teacher here in Atlanta. I lived in Prague for almost six years and the little Czech I know is deteriorating at a rapid pace. My level used to be intermediate-ish, with horrible grammar. My husband is Czech and I want my kids to be bilingual, but if I don\'t start working on my own Czech soon I\'ll be asking them to translate for me!\r\n\r\nThanks\r\n\r\nCarolyn Trachtova', '2003-06-10 00:31:04', 0, 1),
(1006, 13, 'Dmitry Lovermann', '', 'sklonovĂĄnĂ­', 'Byl bych vdÄÄen tomu, kdo napĂ­Âe, jak se sprĂĄvnÄ skolonuje podstatnĂŠ jmĂŠno VEJCE. DĂ­k :)', '2003-06-10 20:29:14', 0, 0),
(1007, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: skloĹovĂĄnĂ­', 'Sg. vejce, vejce, vejci, vejce, vejce!, (o) vejci, vejcem\r\nPl. vejce, vajec, vejcĂ­m, vejce, vejce!, (o) vejcĂ­ch, vejci', '2003-06-11 13:35:22', 1006, 0),
(1008, 13, 'pjm', 'vl_509@hotmail.com', 'phonetic pronunciation of \"vl\" in Czech words?', 'Can someone please type in phonetically how to pronounce the Czech word \"vlcak\" (wolf) for example and is it the same \"vl\" sound as \"Vladimir\" for example? Thanks so much for any enlightenment!', '2003-06-12 18:22:10', 0, 0),
(1009, 13, 'J', '', 'Re: phonetic pronunciation of', '1) \"vlÄĂĄk\" doesn\'t meen a wolf, but an Alsatian (German) shepherd dog. Wolf is \"vlk\".\r\n\r\n2) The pronounciation is simple, but quite strange for foreigners: First sylable \"vl-\" is said [vl:] or [vl@], second \"-ÄĂĄk\" is normal. It\'s the same as \"vla-\", but instead of [-a] there is [-@]*.\r\n\r\nOr as in English second sylable\'s pronunciation in \"evil\" [i:vl@]. Try to say \"evil charm\" but without the [i:] at the beginning and with [-k] instead of [-(r)m].\r\n\r\n* @ = 180 degrees turned \"e\", a character that is not in common fonts.', '2003-06-12 22:03:36', 1008, 0),
(1010, 13, 'J', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Ad', 'vlÄĂĄk [vl@.tSa:k]\r\nvlk [vl@k]\r\n\r\n@ = 180 degrees turned \"e\", a character that is not in common fonts;\r\nS = also not present in common fonts, the phonetic symbol used to mark the pronounciation of eg. English \"sh\" or Czech \"Â\".', '2003-06-12 22:12:26', 1009, 0),
(1011, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: phonetic pronunciation of', 'sorry, not quite sure how to do it with this keyboard but will try to describe.\r\n\r\nyou\'re right about the \'vl\' in vlcak = \'vl\' in other words such as Vladimir, \r\nthe c is actually pronounced as \'ch\' in chocolate \r\nthe \'a\' is long as in marble\r\n(the \'k\' is the easy one: as \'c\' in cat)\r\n\r\nthe stress is on the long \'a\'\r\n\r\nhope this helps', '2003-06-13 22:32:00', 1008, 0),
(1012, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: phonetic pronunciation of', 'Hello,\r\n\r\nIf you are wanting to say to vlk (wolf) insead of vlÄĂĄk than this is how I would pronounce it, being American :).  When I heard people say it this is how it sounded to me.  The \"vl\" is indeed pronounced as Vladimir.  As for the whole word together, though, this is how I would think of it from a native English speaking mind.  Pronounce \"v\" as in \"vermine\", \"vault\", \"venice\"...  As for the \"lk\": think of the word \"old\".  Now take just the first two letters \"ol\"; say them aloud.  Now pronounce the \"l\" in \"vlk\" as if it had that same deeper \"ol\" sound.  Then the \"k\" is just a normal k sound.  So, when sounded out, it would sound like this (V-ol-k).  Now say it faster so that it loses that initial o sound.  When said fast it sounds similar to Volkeswagon only with a little more stress on the L as in Vladimir.  You donÂ§t pronounce the O so it sounds like Vlkswagon.  Sorry for the long definition.  I thought I would put every piece of my mind into it.  Hope I didn\'t confuse you :).\r\n\r\nHope this helps as well.\r\n\r\nScott ', '2003-06-15 11:52:19', 1008, 0),
(1013, 13, 'pjm', 'eastwick66@att.net', 'pronunciation wolf/vlk', 'Thanks to everyone who replied. Several online dictionaries gave \"vlcak\" as the word for wolf, not \"vlk\" so thanks for the clarification. Also, it gave \"vlcek\" as \"little wolf\" so I am entirely confused. I imagine the only way to learn a true pronunciation is to actually HEAR it! I will not be studying the Czech language because it is beyond my ken even though I have a healty IQ, I can not forsee comprehending it. My accolades to anyone who has mastered this enigmatic language!!!', '2003-06-15 20:39:53', 0, 0),
(1014, 13, 'Dmitry Lovermann', '', 'Re: skloĹovĂĄnĂ­', 'DÄkuji moc :) VÂdy mi pomĂĄhĂĄte s tou ÄeÂtinou :)', '2003-06-16 02:07:23', 1007, 0),
(1015, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: pronunciation wolf/vlk', 'I understand what you mean... One thing I have come to do is never trust dictionaries.  Get info from them, but when translating the words from one language to another, the definition given for the word might lead you to think it means something, but it may in fact have a totally different meaning from what you are trying to say or how you are trying to say it.  In czech one thing that you do when you want to say something is little or small you add Äek, sek, etc. (can\'t think of the other endings or spellings off the top of my head; maybe someone else could clarify)  Like in my name for example: In english you would say Scottie for a nick name or meaning little Scott, but in czech you would say something like ScottiÄek.  I also know a little boy whose nickname is MatĂ­sek (sounds to me like there is an accent on the i?), meaning little Matthew. Another example is doma or dĹŻm (House,Home) which would be domeÄek for little house.  \r\n\r\nCheers,\r\nScott ', '2003-06-16 10:50:15', 1013, 0),
(1016, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: pronunciation wolf/vlk', 'Both \"vlÄĂĄk\" and \"vlÄek\" are words derived from \"vlk\" (wolf, lat. lupus, gr. lykos, from proto-indoeur. ulkos) but with different meanings. \"VlÄĂĄk\" is a common colloquialism for Alsatian dog/German shepherd. The correct term is \"nÄmeckĂ˝ ovÄĂĄk\" (literally German shepherd, \"ovÄĂĄk\" is derived from ovce = ewe, lat. ovis). \"VlÄek\" means a little wolf.', '2003-06-16 16:31:36', 1013, 0),
(1017, 13, 'Mira', '', 'Just to show my brilliant expertise', 'The backwards e is called a schwa. ;)', '2003-06-18 09:16:47', 1010, 0),
(1018, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Just to be a horrible pedant', 'The latinism \"ad\" is not used in English. We use \"re:\" instead.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-06-18 14:03:06', 1010, 0),
(1019, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: Ad!', 'There is no schwa after/before L in VLK and VLÄĂK. The L is syllabic.\r\n\r\nAlso [i:vl@] (with a schwa at the end) is questionable.', '2003-06-18 14:50:48', 1010, 0),
(1020, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: Ad!', 'There is no schwa after/before L in VLK and VLÄĂK. The L is syllabic.\r\n\r\nAlso [i:vl@] (with a schwa at the end) is questionable.', '2003-06-18 15:25:37', 1010, 0),
(1021, 13, 'pjm', 'vl_509@hotmail.com', '\"cek\" or \"sek\" at end of first names?', 'Scott replied to my earlier question on this subject but, again, I\'m a bit unclear.  If I wanted to call a man or boy named Vladimir, for example, little Vlad....would I add cek or sek?  Vladicek or Vladisek?  And does this also apply to womens names?\r\nAlso, why do I notice that most Czech females last names end with \"ova\"...for example, if her last name is Hasek, is she called Hasekova and her brother is just Hasek???  Thanks for all replies! ', '2003-06-19 18:19:41', 0, 0),
(1022, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re:', 'I am not sure if you wanted to call a guy Vladimir little Vladimir how you would say it.  I am not a native speaker and do not have enough experience to hear that.  Especially since it is not a Czech name.  My name ScottiÄek was made up by some Czech friends because that is how they thought it should sound.  As for the \"Ova\" question:  Yes, when referring to any Slavic language, you will add and \"ova\" to the end of most names when the last name belongs to a female.   For example:  My fiancĂŠs last name is BaĹĂĄkovĂĄ.  Her fathers last name is BaĹĂĄk.  A very few names do not change.  I cannot think of any off the top of my head.  Maybe a someone else could help with that.  \r\n\r\nPretty easy concept eh?\r\nScott', '2003-06-21 13:24:51', 1021, 0),
(1023, 13, 'Christian', 'Chris17jaar@hotmail.com', '\"czo to za wiocha ja k mozna taka piosenka tak zeszpacic KASZANA wy dziady \"', 'can anyone translate that sentince? \"czo to za wiocha ja k mozna taka piosenka tak zeszpacic KASZANA wy dziady\" I don\'t understand it.', '2003-06-21 15:57:35', 0, 0),
(1024, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re:', 'I am not an expert in Polish, but I would that it looks like Polish to me. ', '2003-06-22 13:04:21', 1023, 0),
(1025, 13, 'micval', 'micval@temnet.org', 'Re:', 'about \"Little Vladimir\": you can say VlĂĄdĂ­Äek (there you have it, it\'s \"Äek\", but don\'t ask me why, i\'m just a native speaker i-)), VladimĂ­rek, VlĂĄdĂ­k, LĂĄÄa..\r\n\r\nand speaking about women\'s surnames, in most cases you use \'-ovĂĄ\', there are some exceptions like PetrĹŻ, JanĹŻ..\r\n', '2003-06-22 14:38:40', 1022, 0),
(1026, 13, 'micval', '', 'Re:', 'Oh and you don\'t call a woman HaÂekovĂĄ, but HaÂkovĂĄ.. Another good example could be Havel - HavlovĂĄ..', '2003-06-22 14:40:24', 1021, 0),
(1027, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re:', 'I haven\'t studied Czech morphology (quite complex thing, really, and I\'m Czech ;)) but I suspect that there are several rules (and even more exceptions, as it normally comes :)) \r\n1. gender: feminine, e.g. -enka, -icka, masculine e.g. -cek, -nek, neutrum e.g. -cko, -tko and 2. preceeding letter: e.g. Petr, Petricek but Ivan, Ivanek.\r\n\r\nI can imagine that the gender concept does not make the rich morphology any easier for an English speaker but at the same time the variety in options allows to espress the precise nuance just by a slight adaptation of the word.\r\n\r\nWithout going into too much grammar (boring!) I\'d say put -ik to the end (Vladik, Petrik, Skotik) or just soften the ending (Vlada, Peta, Scott doesn\'t work - foreign names can be \'Czehnglisezed\' only to a certain extend) and you should be fairly safe. Actually, maybe I should be more precise, -ik is not as strong as -cek/sek. How I feel it, is Vladicek would probably be a baby/small boy, Vladik a schoolboy and Vlada an adult (possibly a teanager, in his nice moments). It\'s not just the degree of affection you\'re expressing but also kind of relationship. \r\n\r\nIf you really want to go into this in detail, lots of grammar would be unavoidable, I\'m afraid. Maybe if there are specific names or words (it applies to objects as well and relates to kind of \'smallness\' and \'cuteness\' of the thing e.g. stul, stolecek) let me know the words; general explanations are more difficult and complicated by trying to cover the whole area.\r\n\r\nHope this helps a bit,\r\nJana', '2003-06-23 15:53:19', 1021, 0),
(1028, 16, 'Jennifer Keating', 'jennifer@hansetstainless.com', 'HledĂĄm uÄitelkou ceÂtiny v Portlandu, OR', 'I lived in the Czech Republic for 6 years and would like some help with grammar.\r\nDik!\r\nJennifer Keating', '2003-06-24 03:53:05', 0, 0),
(1029, 15, 'Jennifer Keating', 'jennifer@hansetstainless.com', 'HledĂĄm uÄitelkou ceÂtiny v Portlandu, OR', 'Mluvit umĂ­m.  PotĹebuju pomoc s gramatikou, prosĂ­m.\r\nDejkuji za pozornost.\r\njk', '2003-06-24 22:25:27', 0, 0),
(1030, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Diminutives, female names', 'Scott replied to my earlier question on this subject but, again, I\'m a bit unclear. If I wanted to call a man or boy named Vladimir, for example, little Vlad....would I add cek or sek? Vladicek or Vladisek? And does this also apply to womens names? \r\nAlso, why do I notice that most Czech females last names end with \"ova\"...for example, if her last name is Hasek, is she called Hasekova and her brother is just Hasek??? Thanks for all replies!  \r\n   \r\nHi,\r\n\r\nmost of your questions have already been answered as best as is possible without a lot of grammar. Here is a little bit more to fill in the gaps:\r\n\r\n<b>Diminutives:</b> The -cek, -ik, etc. at the ends of nouns are called diminutives. Nine years ago -- the last time I studied Czech in a school -- they spoke of two degrees of diminutives for most words, with some only being able to receive one. I like Jana\'s note, uninfluenced by textbooks, that you can get a sort of weak diminutive just by \"softening\" the ending, without ending the \"official\" diminutive endings. If I had known that, maybe I wouldn\'t have been so surprised and confused when my acquaintance called my friend \"Honziiii!\"\r\n\r\nDiminutives say either that something is small, that we like it, or we are being ironic and we don\'t like it at all, or we just have a close relationship to it. \r\n\r\nWhen talking to a baby, you would tend to use diminutives even for things that you\'d otherwise talk about normally. This can even lead to weird expressions like \"obrovitansky balvanicek\" (a nice little enorm-a-lormous boulder). Trust me, I\'m a parent.\r\n\r\nSometimes diminutive forms have become so tied to a certain meaning that to say that meaning, you use a diminutive even though you\'re not really trying to say something is \"small or dear\". For example, even though a \"vkladni knizka\" (some kind of banking thing, not sure how to translate it, literally a \"deposit book\") really is generally small, even a Guinness-sized 3 foot by 3 foot vkladni knizka would still be a vkladni knizka and not a vkladni kniha.\r\n\r\nIf it\'s a noun, it can be made into a diminutive generally. Women\'s names are no problem; they just have a different set of endings, generally with -ka as the \"end of the ending\". \r\n\r\nMany verbs and adjectives can be made diminutive too; some have special diminutive forms based on \"baby talk\" that are far from the original (jist -- papat), and are used even among adults talking among each other who need those words\' nuances.\r\n\r\nEven really abstract things can be made diminutive -- e.g. problemky, starustky (little worries) -- if there are cases where they are \"close to a life\" and/or if they are somehow \"little\" or someone wants to \"belittle\" them, e.g. statecek -- little state.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nJust like in English, women are linguistically \"owned\" by their father, and then by their husband in Czech... just more so. That is, \"Haskova\" means \"Hasek\'s\" (wife, daughter). Although as Czech linguist Pavel Eisner pointed out, not even Czech is as bad as old-fashioned English usage - they would never call Robert Piper\'s wife anything like \"Mrs. Robert Piper\"!\r\n\r\nBesides the \"Petru\" etc. that Jana mentioned, there are the \"adjective\" last names, which receive a female ending, but not one indicating their ownership - just the female form of the given adjective. For example, pani Zridkavesela, pani Brzobohata.', '2003-06-25 15:02:14', 1021, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(1031, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re:  PĂĄr poznĂĄmek ke zdrobnÄlinĂĄm.', '1) V ÄeÂtinÄ nenĂ­ pĹĂ­pona -sek. Ve slovÄ MatĂ˝sek je pĹĂ­pona -ek, souhlĂĄska \"s\" patĹĂ­ ke kmeni: MateS + ek -> MatĂ˝sek (kmenovĂŠ e se pravidelnÄ prodlouÂilo a zĂşÂilo: e -> ĂŠ -> Ă­/Ă˝, jako plech + ek -> plĂ­Âek, flek + ek -> flĂ­Äek).\r\n\r\n2) TotĂŠÂ platĂ­ pro domnÄlou pĹĂ­ponu -Äek, souhlĂĄska \"Ä\" je kmenovĂŠ \"Ä\" nebo kmenovĂŠ \"k/c\" zmÄnÄnĂŠ na \"Ä\" a patĹĂ­ ke kmeni, pĹĂ­pona je opÄt -ek:\r\n\r\nmĂ­Ä + ek -> mĂ­Äek\r\ndrak + ek -> drĂĄÄek\r\nptĂĄk + ek -> ptĂĄÄek\r\nvlk + ek -> vlÄek\r\nstĹevĂ­c + ek -> stĹevĂ­Äek\r\nmÄsĂ­c + ek -> mÄsĂ­Äek\r\n\r\n3) DalÂĂ­ bÄÂnĂĄ pĹĂ­pona deminutiv muÂskĂŠho rodu je -Ă­k:\r\n\r\nbiÄ + Ă­k -> biÄĂ­k\r\npes (ps-a) + Ă­k -> psĂ­k\r\nvĹŻz (voz-u) + Ă­k -> vozĂ­k\r\n\r\n4) PĹĂ­ponu -ek lze pĹipojit i k deminutivu. Tak vznikajĂ­ sloÂenĂŠ pĹĂ­pony ek + ek -> eÄek, Ă­k + ek -> Ă­Äek:\r\n\r\nhrad + ek -> hrĂĄdek + ek -> hrĂĄdeÄek\r\ndĹŻm (dom-u) + ek -> domek + ek -> domeÄek\r\nnos + Ă­k -> nosĂ­k + ek -> nosĂ­Äek\r\n\r\nnÄkdy deminutiva nahradila pĹŻvodnĂ­ slova:\r\n\r\n(jeÂ) + ek -> jeÂek + ek -> jeÂeÄek\r\n(krt) + ek -> krtek + ek -> krteÄek\r\n\r\n5) Deminutiva Âen. rodu se tvoĹĂ­ pĹĂ­ponou -ka:\r\n\r\nstrana + ka -> strĂĄnka\r\nkost + ka -> kĹŻstka/kostka (s jinĂ˝m vĂ˝znamem)\r\nzahrada + ka -> zahrĂĄdka\r\nruka + ka -> ruÄka\r\n\r\nSloÂenĂŠ pĹĂ­pony: ce (stÄ. cÄ) + ka -> eÄka; ice (stÄ. icÄ) + ka -> iÄka;\r\n\r\nzahrada + ce -> (zahrĂĄdcÄ) + ka -> zahrĂĄdeÄka\r\n(ov-) + ce -> ovce + ka -> oveÄka\r\nruka + ice -> (ruÄicÄ) + ka -> ruÄiÄka\r\nhlava + ice -> hlavice + ka -> hlaviÄka\r\n\r\n6) Deminutiva stĹ. rodu majĂ­ pĹĂ­ponu -ko a sloÂenĂŠ pĹĂ­pony -eÄko, -Ă­Äko: \r\n\r\nslovo + ko -> slĹŻvko\r\nzelĂ­ + ce -> (zelĂ­ce) + ko -> zelĂ­Äko\r\n\r\n', '2003-06-25 18:09:23', 1021, 0),
(1032, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re:  PĂĄr poznĂĄmek ke zdrobnÄlinĂĄm.', 'Thanks for the help Vladimir.  That makes a lot more sense.  I still have a long way to go before I get the feel of this language.  I know living there is going to help immensely.  Thanks for all the input everyone, as you have answered some of my brooding questions as well.  \r\n\r\nScott', '2003-06-26 11:36:17', 1031, 0),
(1033, 13, 'Zuzana', 'snablova@post.cz', 'Re:', 'Hello, I am czech and I want to answer your question. Vladicek you can use for a small boy, but for a grown-up you just say Vlada. Vladicek is too childish.', '2003-06-26 16:59:40', 1021, 0),
(1034, 13, 'Zuzana', 'snablova@post.cz', 'Re:', 'It is Polish, not Czech. And sorry, i can not understand to it. Only few words.  ', '2003-06-26 17:02:01', 1023, 0),
(1035, 13, 'James', 'jburton1@cfl.rr.com', 'Czech language diacritic codes--ProsĂ­m vĂĄs!', 'Can some,kind \"Someone\" out there please tell me where I can find a table for Czech language diacritic codes.  I can compose e-mails readily now in most western European languages.  But my search for consistently usable diacritic codes for Czech language has been unsuccessful.  I have experimented and found a few, but many (especially of the lower case letters using the hĂĄchek {sp} ) are inferior in quality and often will actually change font size in the middle of a sentence (with irreversible results). I am also unable to find the letter \"u\" with the angstrom diacritic mark (like a degree sign). Pomoc! Pomoc, prosĂ­m prĂĄtele (sp)!  Many thanks.  Dekuji (sp) pekne (sp).  JAMES  ', '2003-07-08 06:35:25', 0, 1),
(1036, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech language diacritic codes--ProsĂ­m vĂĄs!', 'Despite being a techie and having been involved in Czech for years, there\'s not much I can say from a distance and in a hurry. What I CAN note, however, that might be little-known to other people who will in other respects give you more useful replies, is that: <b>Outlook will often mess up accented Czech characters in a way resembling what you mention</b>. The bug is apparently in the core code, as I worked for the company that localized Outlook into Czech, the problem was reported to Microsoft, and they didn\'t do anything about it.\r\n\r\nI don\'t have time to explore the problem to give a guaranteed-accurate answer, but here are some general, <b>probably</b>-true statements about the matter:\r\n- what\'s actually happening is that, upon hitting an accented character, Outlook is switching to an entirely different font, can\'t remember which one but as far as I can remember, it\'s some font Asians use that\'s capable of displaying European letters. Yes, I know that\'s weird.\r\n- the problem doesn\'t occur while writing the mail, but rather while reading it and re-using it\r\n- it appears even when writing plain-text (where fonts should theoretically be irrelevant)\r\n- Especially after all these years since the testing where this was probed in detail, I have no idea what a recipient, let alone a non-Outlook recipient, sees when such a mail is sent out; considering that this problem occurs even when writing plaintext, there\'s plenty of cause to believe the problem will actually be irrelevant for the end recipient (coz snad potom umoznuje odpoustet Microsoft to, ze se na dany problem podle vseho zhluboka vykaslali)\r\n- on my machine right here (Czech Windows 98 SE) you can convert the weird-looking text into good-looking text simply by launching Notepad, cutting the text, pasting it into Notepad, cutting the text again, and pasting it back into Outlook. \r\n\r\nMaybe this is related to your problem, maybe not.\r\n\r\nIt\'ll probably help if you tell what environment (operating system(s) and computer program(s)) you want to be writing in, and where you want it to be correctly displayed.\r\n\r\nBy the way, most Czechs just buy their Czech Windows (or their computers with Czech windows installed), and... go. And when writing for any medium where they know accent marks will likely be damaged, they write \"cesky\", i.e. \"tsesky\", i.e. without accent marks. Or they stubbornly refuse.\r\n\r\nGenerally speaking: every Unicode font should normally have support for all characters -- and not in \"weird\" versions. That\'s what makes me suspect that you are running into ye auld Outlook bug or something similar.\r\n\r\n> I am also unable to find the letter \"u\" with the angstrom diacritic mark (like a degree sign).\r\n\r\nTry this page:\r\nhttp://www.bohemica.com/czechonline/czechkeyboard/\r\n\r\nCheers\r\n\r\nErik\r\nerik at NO SPAM sky dot I MEAN IT DAMMIT cz', '2003-07-08 15:51:35', 1035, 0),
(1037, 15, 'Brandon Sowers', 'stewardoftheearth@yahoo.com', 'Re: Czech teacher needed in Atlanta, GA', 'I am in Athens, GA, about 60 miles away, and I also would like a short-term Czech language tutor before I leave for a student exchange in late august.', '2003-07-11 01:47:37', 1004, 0),
(1039, 15, 'Scott', '', 'Internet Provider', 'I was wondering if any of you who live in Czech or who travel there quite frequently might know what the best free internet provider is in Cz.  I am going to search on google as well, but I figured that many of you might be able to tell me from personal experience.  \r\n\r\nThanks a million!\r\nScott', '2003-07-17 23:26:48', 0, 0),
(1040, 13, 'John', 'Tellman12@aol.com', 'the months', 'I read that every Czech month literally means something from that month, for example, that January is \'leden,\' which is from the Czech word \'led\', which means \'ice.\' Can someone please give me the etymology for all 12 Czech months? This will be greatly appreciated. -John', '2003-07-18 00:24:57', 0, 0),
(1045, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: the months', 'John, I don\'t think it\'s that straitforward (there seem to be some links and spotable origins but not all are clear to me). This is my best guess:\r\nLeden - led=ice\r\nUnor - not a clue\r\nBrezen - brezi=pregnant (for animal/mammals only)\r\nDuben - not sure, dub=oak (the tree, not for wood)\r\nKveten - kvet=flower, kvest=blossom\r\nCerven - not sure, cerveny=red (rippening fruit?)\r\nCervenec - don\'t know\r\nSrpen - srp=grass-hook (a hand instrument used to cut the grass)\r\nZari - zarit=shine/glare\r\nRijen - rije=don\'t know the English equivalent, its when stags (or other wild animals) fight for females (includes the actions that follows carried out by the winners ;))\r\nListopad - list=leaf, padat=fall\r\nProsinec - prosit=plead/ask/beg\r\n\r\nIt looks pretty much as if the names kind of reflect what was significant in that particular time (nature observations and seasonal rural work).\r\nHope it helps a bit,\r\nJana', '2003-07-18 18:21:31', 1040, 0),
(1047, 17, 'Raist', 'raistilin@centrum.cz', ':o)', 'Ahoj. PÄknĂĄ strĂĄnka(y). AlespoĹ koneÄnÄ vidĂ­m, Âe se neuÄĂ­me jen my cizĂ­ jazyky, ale Âe se takĂŠ nÄkdo uÄĂ­ i tu naÂi ÄeÂtinu. :D', '2003-07-20 03:10:51', 0, 0),
(1048, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: the months', 'The English equivalent of \"ĹĂ­je\" is \"rutting\".  We also say that rutting season is mating season.', '2003-07-20 11:52:22', 1045, 0),
(1049, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: the months', 'Thanks, Scott. I thought \'mating\' didn\'t neccessarily involve the fighting bit, does rutting? The 1st immage that springs to my ming for \'rije\' are 2 stags pushing each other with their antlers locked. Also, is \'rutting\' specific to stags or can it be used to all animals?\r\nTHX,\r\nJ.', '2003-07-20 21:54:15', 1048, 0),
(1050, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: the months', 'Ănor has the root -nor-, meaning is not clear, related words are:\r\n      noĹit (se), ponoĹit (se) = to immerse, to dive, to submerge\r\n      ponurĂ˝ = gray, dark, darksome, gloomy\r\n\r\nZĂĄĹĂ­ is not derived from \"zĂĄĹit\". The Old-Czech form is \"zĂĄĹuj\" from \"za ĹujÄ\" (= za ĹĂ­je, in rutting season).\r\n\r\nProsinec is probably derived from \"sinĂ˝\" (= grey, gray) as an oppositum to \"Äerven/Äervenec\" (ÄervenĂ˝ = red).\r\n\r\nBtw, \"ÄervenĂ˝\" is derived from \"Äerv\" (= worm) and originally means \"(cloth) dyed by means of worm\".\r\n', '2003-07-21 15:45:16', 1045, 0),
(1051, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: the months', 'This is the definition I got from dictionary.com:\r\n\r\nAn annually recurring condition or period of sexual excitement and reproductive activity in male deer. \r\nA condition or period of mammalian sexual activity, such as estrus.\r\n\r\n-- Yes it is mainly associated with the fighting side of it when people are talking about rutting in every day speech, but it does also include the sexual side that occurs afterwards, as this is the main reason the staggs react in such a way.\r\n\r\nScott', '2003-07-22 07:06:08', 1049, 0),
(1059, 15, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Internet Provider', 'Hi Scott,\r\n\r\nI don\'t know who\'s the BEST, but Volny is certainly the most famous and among the oldest. Their address is of course http://www.volny.cz.', '2003-07-28 14:39:18', 1039, 0),
(1060, 15, 'Scott', '', 'Re: Internet Provider', 'Thanks Erik.  I will check them out.', '2003-07-31 01:30:20', 1059, 0),
(1061, 17, 'Xema', '', 'question', 'Can somebody explain me one thing about Czech language?', '2003-07-31 19:12:00', 1047, 0),
(1062, 17, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: question', 'Which thing?', '2003-08-04 15:59:50', 1061, 0),
(1063, 17, 'Val Policky', 'vpolicky@cox.net', 'Help with phone calls', 'I need to make several personal calls to the Czech Republi but my Czech language skills are poor. I need to conference someone who can assist me with the language. I am willing to pay for your time. Can anyone help? my email is vpolicky@cox.net Thanks', '2003-08-08 00:56:16', 0, 1),
(1077, 17, 'Milan BroÂ', 'brozmilan@quick.cz', 'Re: Help with phone calls', 'If you want me to help you,I\'ll do it and it\'ll be free.I\'ll be pleased to speak to you and practise my conversdation skills again.Send me your e_mail please where you\'ll write what you want.Thanks a lot.\r\nBye Milan from the Czech republic', '2003-08-20 01:43:12', 1063, 0),
(1078, 17, 'Val Policky', 'vpolicky@palpro.com', 'Re: Help with phone calls', 'Milan:\r\n\r\nDekuju pro vas email.\r\n\r\nThat is pretty much the extent of my Czech language capability.\r\n\r\nMy email address is vpolicky@palpro.com.\r\n\r\nMy situation is that I am trying to contact several people in the south central part of the Czech republic to try to find a missing link in the ancestry on my father\'s side. I am not a genealogy nut or anything like that, but I am attending a reunion of my family this summer and there are about 2000 descendants from one person named Karel Policky who came to the United States in the mid 1800s and we would love to find the link to the old country. He was born in a village called Semikovice, near Rouchovany.\r\n\r\nI would like to contact these people and just talk with them, but my Czech skills make it frustrating for both sides.\r\n\r\nI have visited the Czech Republic on two different occasions and totally enjoyed it.\r\n\r\nIf you send me your phone number, I will be happy to call you direct to see if this can work.\r\n\r\nWhat city do you live in?\r\n\r\nHow did you learn English?\r\n\r\nAnxious to talk with you.\r\n\r\nHave a nice day.\r\n\r\nVal \r\nWork: 1-949-766-5342\r\nCell: 1-949-338-7900\r\n', '2003-08-21 18:50:11', 1077, 1),
(1082, 13, 'Mario', 'Suitbefit@aol.com', 'Meeting Czech People', 'My fiancee will be moving to the states soon. I do not speak Czech well she does not speak English well. We both want to learn the others respective language. I am also looking for Czech people to meet in the Atlantic City area. Please someone contact me . Thanks', '2003-08-24 07:07:53', 0, 1),
(1085, 15, 'buba', '', 'czech words used in everyday american life', 'does anyone know what czech words we use now in everydayh american life', '2003-08-27 08:47:12', 0, 0),
(1087, 15, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: czech words used in everyday american life', 'robot... (from Karel Capek\'s work R.U.R.)\r\n\r\nOther than that there are none, at least none of which I can be sure.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-08-27 20:31:28', 1085, 0),
(1089, 13, 'Scott', '', 'Re: Meeting Czech People', 'So, what is the question you are trying to ask?  Are you trying to ask which ways would be the best to teach eachother your native language?  I think that she will probably learn English much faster than you will learn Czech, because she will be in a position where she is forced to use it on an every day basis.  As far as meeting other Czechs in the Atlantic City area goes, I am not sure.  I know that many people have asked for help in certain areas, but I have never seen a reply post to their inquiry.  You might want to try looking around the community and asking questions as to if there are any clubs, meetings, or just if anyone knows of any Czech natives that live there.  You might want to contact the chamber of commerce.  I am not sure if they could help, but they may be able to.  \r\n\r\nGood luck!\r\n\r\nScott\r\n\r\n', '2003-08-28 03:33:46', 1082, 0),
(1092, 15, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: czech words used in everyday american life', '(in everyday military life)\r\n\r\npistol ... from pĂ­ÂÂala (= pipe,fife - music instrument)\r\nhowitz ... from houfnice (houf = crowd,drove)', '2003-08-28 13:37:05', 1087, 0),
(1094, 15, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: czech words used in everyday american life', '... howitzer ...\r\n\r\npolka ... a lively Bohemian dance tune in 2/4 time (2/4 = 1/2 = pĹŻlka)', '2003-08-29 20:20:31', 1092, 0),
(1095, 15, 'Scott', '', 'Re: czech words used in everyday american life', 'These two words come from Czech origin as well:\r\n\r\n<u><b><i>Pram:</i></b></u>A small dinghy having a flat, snub-nosed bow.\r\n\r\n<I><B><U>Calash</i></U></B>A light carriage with two or four low wheels and a collapsible top.\r\n\r\nThough they are not widely used today, they still coined from Czech words. ', '2003-08-30 00:19:52', 1085, 0),
(1098, 13, 'Veronica', 'stormborne@hotmail.com', 'Useful Czech Phrases Translation?', 'Hi everyone. I\'m new to the forum and I am wondering if anyone would be willing to help me translate some alternate Czech phrases? I have a friend in the Bohumin area of the CZR and, although he speaks English very well, I would really enjoy being able to converse more to him in Czech. Moreover, I am hoping to visit the country later this year. I have already written my own little \'translation book\' from information found on the internet, but it\'s all pretty basic know-how. I\'d like to learn more slang, how to give compliments & so on (at least until I can buy more books on the language). I have a number of sentences that would be very helpful to me if converted from English to Czech (which I\'ll list below), so if someone has the time to help me learn how to say these phrases, I\'d really appreciate it.\r\n\r\n\"Really?\"\r\n\r\n\"Okay.\"\r\n\r\n\"That\'s great!\"\r\n\r\n\"That\'s very nice of you.\"\r\n\r\n\"I would really like that.\"\r\n\r\n\"I don\'t know.\"\r\n\r\n\"Will you show me?\"\r\n\r\n\"Cool!\" (or any other happy slang word)\r\n\r\n\"What\'s new?\"\r\n\r\n\"It\'s so beautiful.\"\r\n\r\n\"I hope you enjoy/like it\" (as when giving a present)\r\n\r\n\"I love it\"\r\n\r\n\"Let\'s go!\"\r\n\r\n\"It\'s very nice to meet you.\"\r\n\r\n\"I think you are all very talented.\"\r\n\r\n\"I like it.\"\r\n\r\n\"I don\'t like it.\"\r\n\r\n\"It\'s delicious!\"\r\n\r\n\"It\'s really hot/cold out here.\"\r\n\r\n\"You\'re cute. (just in case...;) )\"\r\n\r\n\"Go away. (also just in case)\"\r\n\r\n\"Oh, shut up\"\r\n\r\n\"Just a minute...\"\r\n\r\n\"I\'m coming.\"\r\n\r\n\"I don\'t drink/smoke.\"\r\n\r\n\"You have a very nice family.\"\r\n\r\n\"Excuse me, do you know where the ______ is?\"\r\n\r\n\"So, what are your interests?\"\r\n\r\n\"Thanks for having me over.\"\r\n\r\n\"I\'ll see you later.\"\r\n\r\n\"Take care.\"\r\n\r\nThat\'s currently all I can think of. Again, if anyone would be willing to translate some or all of these phrases to Czech (some help regarding pronounciation would also be great) I\'d be one happy American ;). I know it\'s a lot to ask, but I would, truely, be ever so grateful.\r\n\r\nThanks for reading,\r\n\r\nVeronica', '2003-09-01 03:08:08', 0, 0),
(1099, 13, 'brett', 'idiobrett@hotmail.com', 'verbs', 'I want to know if any one can recomend a good english - czech book I am\r\ninterested in learning about infinitive verbs and their conjugations. for example...to read = cist\r\n\r\nI read = ja ctu\r\nyou read = ty ctes\r\nhe, she, it reads = on,ona,ono cte\r\nwe read = my cteme\r\nyou read (formal) (( vosotros in spanish)) = ???\r\nthey read = oni ctdu\r\n\r\nthe pattern must be outlined... this is what I seek. Please help....\r\nvisit my web page....www.idiomusic.com\r\n\r\nbrett', '2003-09-04 07:35:59', 0, 1),
(1106, 13, 'Jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: verbs', 'Brett, \r\ncan\'t really recommend a book but actually the formal form is just the same as the plural form - e.g. (vy) ctete is used for both, formal addressing 1 person and a group. \r\nThis applies to the present/future tense but the past tense combines an auxiliary in plural and a verb in singular (for formal) - e.g. (vy) jste cetl/cetla (BUT: vy jste cetli for a group). \r\nHope this helps a bit,\r\nJana', '2003-09-08 16:50:59', 1099, 0),
(1108, 14, 'Darren Napier', 'd.a.napier@talk21.com', 'Holesovice & Bubenec History', 'When visiting Prague I have always stayed in the Holesovice and Bubenec area. After spending alot of time in the area I now have the thirst for knowledge and would like to learn more about the areas history. \r\n \r\nThe best way I have concluded is to write a book on the history of Holesovice and Bubenec or at least attempt to. I am asking for the help of your readers to compile a book of memories, photos (old and new) stories and general historical facts that would build up a picture of the area of Holesovice and Bubenec. I hope to be in Prague November or December to carry out some research.\r\n \r\nI pick up books about Prague hoping to see something about the area I stay in but they all seem to cover the Prague 1 and 2 areas.\r\nAs a regular tourist to Prague I look at the city as a whole and not just the centre which most tourists do. I have had some great times in Holesovice and met some great people and would like to give something back to them as well as spreading the word to other tourists.\r\n \r\nI hope your readers can help me. If anyone can help me and be part of this book then either e-mail myself on d.a.napier@talk21.com or write to me at Darren Napier, 34 Ellicks Close, Bradley Stoke, Bristol, BS32 0ES, England.\r\n \r\n \r\nDarren Napier\r\nBristol, England       ', '2003-09-09 05:18:04', 0, 1),
(1112, 15, 'Vlad', '', 'Re: czech words used in everyday american life', 'ROBOT', '2003-09-10 03:45:34', 1085, 0),
