(1178, 15, 'lenka', 'lenkazontkova@seznam.cz', 'Re: The most interesting idiom in Czech', 'Mrzne az prasti\r\nZpity pod obraz\r\nBlby jak tago\r\nVstat hrobnikovi z lopaty\r\nSkareda jak ropucha\r\nHladky jak detska prdelka\r\nI have more of them but I would be here all day......', '2003-11-06 11:23:38', 1174, 0),
(1179, 17, 'Dima', '', 'Re: :o)', 'Jen do toho! Pokud mĂĄÂ dotazy anebo nebude ti jasnĂŠ, co znamenĂĄ ku pĹĂ­kladu \"Proti gustu ÂĂĄdnĂ˝ diÂputĂĄt\", tak nevahej a ptej se :) Ona ta ÄeÂtina je ve skuteÄnosti tÄÂkĂĄ, ale ty jsi dobrĂĄ a jak se koukĂĄm zvladĂĄÂ to bravĹŻrnÄ :) JĂĄ jsem potĹeboval hodnÄ Äasu na to, abych se poĹĂĄdnÄ nauÄil Äesky, i kdyÂ stĂĄle dÄlĂĄm chyby. No a kdyÂ uÂ jsme u jazykĹŻ, co kdybys ty nĂĄm pĹedvedla nÄco z rumunÂtiny? :)', '2003-11-07 20:03:12', 1120, 0),
(1184, 13, 'xaria', 'rcstokes@hotmail.com', 'New Love', 'I have recently met and begun to fall in love with a beautiful czech man. Could someone please assist me . . . I would like to tell him in a letter \"I miss you\" (in czech) and \"You inspire the beauty in my dreams\" (in czech)\r\n\r\nIf anyone can help me, I will be ever-so-grateful. Thank you! \r\n', '2003-11-12 06:10:07', 0, 1),
(1186, 16, 'Dmitri Lovermann', '', 'pĹĂ­prava ke stĂĄnĂ­ zkouÂce z ÄeÂtiny', 'MĹŻÂe mi nÄkdo pomoci? PotĹeboval bych se pĹipravit ke stĂĄtnĂ­ zkouÂce z ÄeÂtiny. Do sjs.cz se mi nÄjak nechce. NemĂĄm chuÂ sedÄt ve velkĂŠ skupinÄ, kde jsou i takovĂ­, kteĹĂ­ se ptajĂ­ na rĹŻznĂŠ vÄci, kterĂŠ jsem se nauÄil jeÂtÄ v prvnĂ­m roÄnĂ­ku jazykovky. MoÂnĂĄ, Âe by se ke mÄ pĹipojila mĂĄ znĂĄmĂĄ. TakÂe byla by to vĂ˝uka ve dvou. Jsme skoro na stejnĂŠ Ăşrovni a tak jsme se rozhodli, Âe individuĂĄlnĂ­ pĹĂ­prava bude mnohem efektivnÄjÂĂ­, neÂ ve skupinÄ.\r\n\r\nZajĂ­mĂĄ mÄ, jestli se dĂĄ vĹŻbec sehnat vyuÄujicĂ­ho, kterĂ˝ uÂ mĂĄ zkuÂenosti v pĹĂ­pravÄ cizincĹŻ ke zkouÂkĂĄm z ÄeÂtiny?\r\n\r\nJo, a jeÂtÄ jednĂĄ vÄc: za nÄjakou dobu (nevĂ­m ale kolik uplyne let..) budu chtĂ­t sloÂit i pĹekladatelskou zkouÂku. Jak se k nĂ­ pĹipravĂ­m? Jsou na to nÄjakĂŠ zvlĂĄÂtnĂ­ kurzy?', '2003-11-12 13:10:40', 0, 0),
(1187, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: New Love', 'Bohemica\'s volunteer love-letter translation service to the rescue once again...\r\n\r\nI miss you ==> ChybĂ­Â mi\r\nYou inspire the beauty in my dreams ==> Jsi inspiracĂ­ krĂĄsy mĂ˝ch snĹŻ.\r\n\r\nOthers will probably offer better translations of the latter, this is just if you\'re in a hurry to get your letter out.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-12 18:26:38', 1184, 0),
(1188, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: make to enjoy (WAS: S tebou me bavi svet)', 'Ahoj Jano!\r\n\r\nAh, illogical English. Sometimes you use the infinitive after a helper verb (He caused me to stub my toe), sometimes you don\'t (He made me stub my toe). Looking at problems like this in English, Czech seems easy in comparison. :-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-12 18:32:15', 1149, 0),
(1189, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: ano - jo', 'That\'s a good starting point for understanding it, but more for Czechs than for English-speakers, since if you learn that you should \"ano\" for \"yes,\" you can end up, in my opinion, sounding too formal (or even mildly peeved) some of the time.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-12 18:35:43', 1159, 0),
(1190, 15, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: The most interesting idiom in Czech', 'I\'m a grammar freak, so the one gets me is the much dryer \"v tomto slova smyslu.\" It\'s one of the few phrases that still gets me mumbling in English (\"in this of-the-word sense, in this of-the-word sense.... AAAARGH!!!\").\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-12 18:56:49', 1178, 0),
(1191, 16, 'Erik', '', 'Re: pĹĂ­prava ke stĂĄnĂ­ zkouÂce z ÄeÂtiny', 'O zkouskach vim jenom to, ze existuji, ale moje zkusenost je takova, ze se clovek muze zivit prekladanim uplne v pohode bez certifikatu. Doporucil bych se optat jeste v Yahoo group Czechlist, diskusni forum predevsim prekladatelu CZ<-->EN. Uvidis mnohem sirsi a asi i fundovanejsi skalu nazoru na otazku \"stoji zkouska za to\" a nejspis i na otazku \"jak se na ni nachystam\".\r\n\r\nOmlouvam se za tsestinu, su linej :-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-12 19:06:10', 1186, 0),
(1192, 16, 'Dima', '', 'Re: pĹĂ­prava ke stĂĄnĂ­ zkouÂce z ÄeÂtiny', 'No, je to pro mÄ jasnĂĄ vÄc, Âe ÂivnostenskĂŠ oprĂĄvnÄnĂ­ podnikat jako pĹekladatel dostanu i bez jakĂŠkoli zkouÂky, i kdyÂ se mi to zdĂĄ blby (dyÂ i cizinec, kterĂ˝ ani nemluvĂ­ Äesky mĹŻÂe poskytovat sluÂby v oboru pĹekladĂĄnĂ­ a tlumoÄnictvĂ­). Ale jde mi o to, Âe jĂĄ bych chtÄl bĂ˝t tĂ­m opravdovĂ˝m pĹekladatelem, ne jako jsem teÄ.. :) Ale hlavnĂ­ asi bude to, Âe tĂ­m pĹeklĂĄdanĂ­m vydÄlĂĄvat na chleba nechci. MoÂnĂĄ jen tak, jako student. A i to razĂ­tko pĹekladatele mĹŻÂe hodit jako takovĂĄ Ĺekl bych zĂĄchrana  v budoucnosti. Nikdo nevĂ­, jak se zmÄnĂ­ jeho Âivot a pĹekladatelem bych si nÄjakĂ˝ minimum zapespeÄil. TakÂe teÄ bych se zkusil pĹipravit a slouÂit ty zkouÂky jen pro to abych si dokĂĄzal, Âe jsem v tom dobrej. Co? Nejsem ', '2003-11-13 04:28:19', 1191, 0),
(1193, 16, 'Erik', '', 'Re: pĹĂ­prava ke stĂĄnĂ­ zkouÂce z ÄeÂtiny', 'Ani mi neÂlo o oprĂĄvnÄnĂ­, i kdyÂ mĹŻÂu tentokrĂĄt uÂ 100% potvrdit, Âe to dostaneÂ v podstatÄ za to, Âe se na panĂ­ v Â. Ăş. budeÂ hezky usmĂ­vat. No vlastnÄ je potĹeba diplom, ale ten mĹŻÂe bĂ˝t z Äehokoli -- jĂĄ ho tĹeba mÄl z francouzÂtiny a public relations.\r\n\r\nÂlo mi o skuteÄnĂŠ ÂivobytĂ­ -- a tady zase, podle mĂŠho, o nÄjakĂ˝ nÄjakou skupinou vymĂ˝ÂlenĂ˝ certifikĂĄt nejde, jsem si skoro jistĂ˝, Âe vÄtÂina pĹekladatelĹŻ co znĂĄm (soudnĂ­ jsou samozĹejmÄ vyjĂ­mkou) nic takovĂŠho nemĂĄ -- mĂĄ schopnost pĹeklĂĄdat, dobrou obchodnĂ­ povÄst, vypÄstovanou klientelu, atd. -- tyto zajistĂ­ ÂivobytĂ­, ne certifikĂĄt. ChtÄj ho tĹeba jako trofej, ale neÄekej, Âe bez nÄj to nepĹŻjde nebo Âe s nĂ­m to pĹŻjde jako po mĂĄsle.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-13 15:44:00', 1192, 0),
(1194, 16, 'Milan', 'brozmilan@quick.cz', 'Re: pĹĂ­prava ke stĂĄnĂ­ zkouÂce z ÄeÂtiny', 'ÂĂĄdnĂ˝ papĂ­r jeÂtÄ nikdy nedokĂĄzal,Âe mĂĄ nÄkdo patent na rozum.JĂĄ vaÂĂ­ ÄeÂtinÄ rozumĂ­m a je mi Âumafuk,Âe na to nemĂĄte papĂ­r.Ono se to stejnÄ vÂdy poznĂĄ,jestli tu ĹeÄ umĂ­Â nebo ne.I bez papĂ­ru.A nemusĂ­Â nikomu nic dokazovat.......................................', '2003-11-14 01:45:20', 1192, 0),
(1195, 17, 'Erik', '', 'Re: :o)', 'TÄÂkĂĄ? MoÂnĂĄ. Je vÂak aspoĹ logickĂĄ! No vÄtÂinou. CoÂ o angliÄtinÄ nelze ĹĂ­ci...\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-14 17:03:45', 1179, 0),
(1196, 16, 'Dima', '', 'Re: pĹĂ­prava ke stĂĄnĂ­ zkouÂce z ÄeÂtiny', 'Tady bych nesypal do jednĂŠho pytle ĹeÄ a psĂĄnĂ­. Jsou to dvÄ ÄeÂtiny a kaÂdĂĄ je jinĂĄ. KaÂdĂŠ se uÄĂ­me. HodnÄ cizincĹŻ se domluvĂ­, ale je spousta takovĂ˝ch, kteĹĂ­ pravopis nezvladajĂ­. DĹŻvodem je nejÄastÄji to, Âe vÄtÂinou mluvĂ­, a ne pĂ­Âou. I jĂĄ dÄlĂĄm chyby pĹestoÂe pĂ­Âu dost... \r\n\r\nKdyÂ se tak podĂ­vĂĄm na svĹŻj pĹedeÂlĂ˝ pĹĂ­spÄvek, citĂ­m, Âe nÄco nenĂ­ v poĹĂĄdku. A nenĂ­ to kvĹŻli chybĂĄm. NÄco se tam prostÄ nesedĂ­.. Asi postĹebuji vĂ­ce praxe.', '2003-11-14 18:16:10', 1194, 0),
(1197, 17, 'Dima', '', 'hovorovĂŠ vĂ˝razy', 'Asi se budete smĂĄt, ale obÄas potkĂĄm nÄjakĂ˝ vĂ˝raz, kterĂŠmu nerozumĂ­m. NaÂel jsem seznam takovĂ˝ch obrĂĄtĹŻ:\r\n\r\n1. \"...nemĂĄ kredit k tomu aby kafral do...\" (Pokud sprĂĄvnÄ chĂĄpu, znamenĂĄ to: ...nemĂĄ prĂĄvo se vmÄÂovat do ... (ve smyslu \"Co je mu do toho..!))\r\n\r\n2. \"nemĂĄ ani zbla pĹedstavu\" (znamenĂĄ to \"nemĂĄ ÂĂĄdnou pĹedstavu\"?)\r\n\r\n3. \"kdyÂ jeho firma dostane na frak\" nebo \"demokracie dostane na frak\" -- ? Tomu vĹŻbec nerozumĂ­m.\r\n', '2003-11-14 18:28:02', 0, 0),
(1198, 17, 'Milan', 'brozmilan@quick.cz', 'Re: hovorovĂŠ vĂ˝razy', 'PĹiznĂĄm se,Âe aÄkoliv tu Âiji vĂ­ce jak 26 let,ten prvnĂ­ vĂ˝raz je tedy ,alesponÄpro mne,Ăşplnou novinkou.Ale v podstatÄ bych Ĺekl,Âe ty prvnĂ­ dva vĂ˝razy jsi pochopila celkem sprĂĄvnÄ.\r\n\r\nKdyÂ nÄkdo dostane na frak,znamenĂĄ to,Âe v nÄÄem neuspÄl.HodnÄ se to pouÂĂ­vĂĄ ve sportu.KdyÂ napĹ. Sparta prohraje se SlaviĂ­ 0:6,tak mĹŻÂeÂ ĹĂ­ct,Âe spartÄani dostali pÄknÄ na frak.\r\n\r\nU firmy to tedy znamenĂĄ,Âe v nÄÄem neuspÄla.', '2003-11-15 04:10:47', 1197, 0),
(1199, 17, 'petmal', 'pet.malik@tiscali.cz', 'Re: hovorovĂŠ vĂ˝razy', 'Ahoj, pĹi jakĂŠ pĹĂ­leÂitosti byla ĹeÄena ta prvnĂ­ vÄta?', '2003-11-18 02:17:31', 1197, 1),
(1200, 13, 'Nikol Mitchell', '', 'How to say Merry Christmas in Czech', 'How do you pronounce Vesele Vanoce?  My 7 year old son is doing a report on the Czech Republic and needs to learn how to say Merry Christmas.  I\'ve found the term \"Vesele Vanoce\" but I do not know how it is pronounced.\r\n\r\nThank you.', '2003-11-18 09:15:29', 0, 0),
(1201, 13, 'DIMA', '', 'Re: How to say Merry Christmas in Czech', 'I think VEHSEHLEH VAANOHTSEH :) Anyway i can record it later ..', '2003-11-18 16:14:02', 1200, 0),
(1202, 17, 'Dima', '', 'Re: hovorovĂŠ vĂ˝razy', 'TeÄ nemĹŻÂu najĂ­t, kde jsem si to pĹeÄetl. Je to jeden ze dvou: Pavel Eisner nebo ekonom Robert Holman. AÂ najdu, tak vĂĄm Ĺeknu. DÄkuji za vaÂe odpovÄdĂ­.\r\n\r\nP.S. JĂĄ jsem CHLAP. CelĂŠ jmĂŠno znĂ­: Dmitri. PouÂĂ­vĂĄm zkrĂĄtku Dima. :)', '2003-11-18 16:18:58', 1198, 0),
(1203, 13, 'Lorenzo', 'exilems72@hotmail.com', 'Passive constructions', 'Hi everyone,\r\n\r\nI\'m sort of digging into Czech grammar  and I would like to ask a question about passive constructions. \r\nCould someone here please give me a brief (or extended if possible) overview of Czech passive constructions?\r\n\r\nWhat I know is that you need to add the auxiliary verb \"bĂ˝t\" to the passive participle of the verb as in \"Je milovĂĄna\" (she is loved) or \"Kniha je Ätena\" (The book is read). But I think it works differently with sense verbs (chutnat, Äichat, dotĂ˝kat se, slyÂet, vidÄt) as I know the translation of \"When the sky is clear mountains can be seen from there\" should be as follows: \"KdyÂ je jasnĂĄ obloha, jsou odtamtud vidÄt hory\" and here we have bĂ˝t+infinitive+noun (in accusative but I know it could also be in the nominative).\r\nIs that the way passive sentences are build with all sense verbs? Or is this just an exception?\r\nFor instance, would it be correct to say \"PotuÄek je slyÂet\"?\r\n\r\nBy the way, it seems to me that passive is not much used in Czech. I think it depends on the fact that, thanks to the use of cases, there\'s the possibility of switching \"subject\" and \"object\" in a sentence giving prominence on either one or the other element.\r\n\r\nThank you!\r\n\r\nLorenzo', '2003-11-18 21:58:37', 0, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(1204, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Passive constructions', 'No time for a full reply, just want to comment on the last part -- \"the passive is not used that much in Czech.\" Intuitively I wouuld agree, though it may just be that I don\'t notice the cases where it is used in Czech and not English as much as those where it is used in English and not Czech. Certainly <b>reflexive</b> constructions take a bite out of the uses of passive constructions:\r\n\r\nBorscht is eaten regularly throughout Central and Eastern Europe.\r\nBorÂÄ <b>se jĂ­</b> pravidelnÄ po celĂŠ stĹednĂ­ a vĂ˝chodnĂ­ EvropÄ.\r\n\r\nHe was born on Christmas Day.\r\n<b>Narodil se</b> o ÂtÄdrĂ˝ den.\r\n\r\nI went to have my hair cut.\r\nÂel jsem <b>si nechat ostĹĂ­hnout</b> vlasy.\r\n\r\nOf course there are also other Czech constructions that replace the passive, including the use of word order to express topic-focus perspective (I THINK that\'s what it\'s called), that is, the aspect you mentioned above:\r\n\r\nAnd here you see a building praised by King Bob himself.\r\nA zde najdete budovu, kterou pochvĂĄlil samotnĂ˝ KrĂĄl Bob.\r\n\r\nIf the passive is used because \"there is no actor\", Czech sometimes resolves the same problem by letting \"them\" take care of the job:\r\n\r\nThe victims were EATEN ALIVE!!\r\nObÄti snÄdli JEÂTÄ ÂIVĂ!!\r\n\r\nBTW sense verbs DO have less of a tendency to involve the passive than in English, but I\'m not qualified to say if they never use them.\r\n\r\nAnd on his bad days, his stink can be smelled even in the next room over.\r\nA kdyÂ mĂĄ zrovna ÂpatnĂ˝ den, je cĂ­tit ten jeho smrad i ve vedlejÂĂ­ mĂ­stnosti.\r\n\r\nLikewise \"je slyÂet\"; you already mentioned \"je vidÄt\". \"Je chutnat\" is something imaginable only perhaps in science fiction or fantasy (AFAIK you can\'t say \"JejĂ­ch vĂ­no je chutnat na kaÂdĂŠm roÄnĂ­ku HruÂickĂŠ koÂti\" for example), and since \"je cĂ­tit\" is already taken for the sense of smell, and since \"can be felt\" in the LITERAL sense of feeling is not very useful... well...\r\n\r\nEnough for now.', '2003-11-20 15:59:44', 1203, 0),
(1205, 15, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech is easy', 'TakĂŠ znĂĄm ty PRĂ, a myslĂ­m, Âe by je mÄl ÄlovÄk ĂşplnÄ ignorovat, protoÂe tato myÂlenka se prostÄ stala rozÂĂ­ĹenĂĄ a ÂĂ­ĹĂ­ se jeÂtÄ dĂĄl a dĂĄl vlastnĂ­ sĂ­lou, ne vlastnĂ­ pravdivostĂ­. NeĹĂ­kĂĄm, Âe to nenĂ­ moÂnĂĄ pravda, jenom Âe to nenĂ­ pravda jen proto, Âe se to tolik traduje.\r\n\r\nOsobnÄ znĂĄm francouzÂtinu a troÂililinku nÄmÄinu. MyslĂ­m, Âe co do \"oficiĂĄlnĂ­\" struktury, ÄeÂtina ji mĂĄ sloÂitÄjÂĂ­ neÂ tyto jazyky a angliÄtina, ale co do \"neoficiĂĄlnĂ­\", tÄÂko popsatelnĂŠ struktury, ÄeÂtina jĂ­ mĂĄ mĂ­Ĺ, a abych pravdu Ĺekl, je Äasto snaÂÂĂ­ se nauÄit to, co se podĂĄvĂĄ v uÄebnicĂ­ch... na druhĂŠ stranÄ, uÄebnice ÄeÂtiny bohuÂel IMHO zanedbĂĄvajĂ­ Äesky systĂŠm vyjadĹenĂ­ dĹŻleÂitost slov ve vÄtÄ pomocĂ­ slovosledu, coÂ se sice nehodĂ­ do prvnĂ­ kapitoly, ale kdyÂ se ÄlovÄk vĹŻbec nenauÄĂ­ o co gou, mĹŻÂe se mu zdĂĄt Äesky slovosled jako ÂĂ­lenÄ frustrujĂ­cĂ­ chaos.\r\n\r\nKaÂdopĂĄdnÄ si nedovedu pĹedstavit, Âe bych se nauÄil tĹeba arabsky nebo urdu rychleji neÂ Äesky... ale kdybych byl arab, bylo by tomu naopak. TakÂe opatrnÄ s tĂ­m \"tento jazyk \'je\' nejtÄÂÂĂ­\"...', '2003-11-20 16:10:07', 1157, 0),
(1206, 16, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: pĹĂ­prava ke stĂĄnĂ­ zkouÂce z ÄeÂtiny', 'Abych pravdu Ĺekl, stydĂ­m se \"pĹed\" tebou, protoÂe jsem tady deset let a ani teÄka neumĂ­m Äesky tak dobĹe jak ty. Je sice pravda, Âe jsem se tomu skoro nevÄnoval (na kurzy ÄeÂtiny jsem napĹ. chodil jen tak pĹŻl roku), ale stejnÄ. KaÂdopadnÄ se TY nestyÄ.', '2003-11-20 16:14:06', 1196, 0),
(1207, 13, 'Erik', '', 'Re: Passive constructions', 'One more example of an English contruction eliminated in Czech:\r\n\r\nI don\'t know how much money can be withdrawn from that account.\r\nNevĂ­m kolik penÄz <b>lze</b> z tohoto ĂşÄtu vytĂĄhnout.\r\n\r\nBy the way, take my exact wording in Czech with a pound of salt -- I\'m an American. But the \"anti-passive\" constructions should be sound.\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-20 19:35:43', 1204, 0),
(1209, 13, 'Stef', '', 'Happy Birthday', 'How do you write and say Happy Birthday in Czech? Thanks!', '2003-11-21 10:16:58', 0, 0),
(1210, 13, 'Lorenzo', 'exilems72@hotmail.com', 'Re: Passive constructions', 'Thank you, Erik, for your enlightening explanation and examples! \r\nI had not thought of reflexive constructions as a way to Âget aroundÂ passive but thatÂs how it works ! (also in Italian which is my mother tongue so I should have known!)\r\nYou wrote that youÂre American so you speak Czech as a foreign language? Just curious.\r\n\r\nLorenzo', '2003-11-21 16:51:02', 1207, 0),
(1211, 13, 'Lorenzo', '', 'Re: Happy Birthday', 'Hi Stef,\r\n\r\nit\'s \"VÂe nejlepÂĂ­ k narozeninĂĄm\"\r\n\r\nI\'m not sure how to render the pronunciation of these phrase in English but maybe the following could do \"Fsheh naylehpshi gh nahrozehninahm\"\r\n\r\nLorenzo', '2003-11-22 01:25:55', 1209, 0),
(1212, 13, 'Tony Peachment', 'apeachment@tiscali.cz', 'VÂdytÂ´', 'I\'m stuck for a rule (or at least a rough translation) for vÂdytÂ´. It has to be one of the most common utterances in spoken Czech and, at times I use it myself but I never know if it is correct. \r\n\r\nMy rough idea is that it is used when you correct a negative statement (?).\r\nOdpovÄd je \'strom\'\r\nVÂdyt jsem to Ĺekl!!!\r\n\r\nAnd while we\'re at it, could anyone give me an overview of the rules of keÂ bych.\r\n\r\nDiky\r\n\r\nTony', '2003-11-24 19:22:12', 0, 1),
(1214, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: VÂdytÂ´ (and  KĂŠÂ bych)', 'After mulling over several examples culled from a corpus, I\'d say vÂdyÂ is all about <i>emotionally stating an assertion imposing some other (sometimes only implied) assertion</i>. Here are some examples.\r\n\r\n<i>\"To vÂechno v tĂŠ tmÄ?\" zeptal se Wolfe. \"SamozĹejmÄ. ZnĂĄm tu kaÂdĂ˝ kĂĄmen, <b>vÂdyÂ</b> jsem se tady narodila.[....]\"</i>\r\n\r\nHere the word vÂdyÂ emphasizes the <b>weight</b> of the statement \"I was born here\" <b>as an argument overriding another one</b>; I would compare it to English \"after all\".\r\n\r\n<i>Ĺekl mi... ale <b>vÂdyÂ</b> je to jedno, co mi Ĺekl.</i>\r\n\r\nHere the word vÂdyÂ strengthens the statement \"je to jedno,\" standing in opposition to the preceding between-the-lines assertion that it was worth recounting what so-and-so said.\r\n\r\n<i>ReportĂŠr, pochopitelnÄ muÂ, prĂĄvÄ prohlĂĄsil, Âe doÂlo k nepĹĂ­jemnĂŠmu kontaktu nohou hrĂĄÄĹŻ, mĂ­sto aby Ĺekl jasnÄ, Âe si vzĂĄjemnÄ nakopali. Bylo by to v naprostĂŠm poĹĂĄdku, <b>vÂdyÂ</b> kopanĂĄ je od toho, aby se pĹi nĂ­ kopalo!</i>\r\n\r\nHere again the word vÂdyÂ emphasizes a statement in opposition to one just made (again, between the lines): the assertion that soccer, i.e. \"the kicking game\" (i.e. in slightly moldy Czech, <i>kopanĂĄ</i>) is all about people kicking (no matter what the target) during it, placed in opposition to the mentioned reporter\'s implied statement that people kicking each other during soccer is not good.\r\n\r\nÂenskĂĄ role, mateĹskĂĄ funkce, manÂelskĂĄ povinnost... SkonÄĂ­ to pĹĂ­ÂernĂ˝m slovem samoÂivitelka, coÂ je dokonalĂĄ ukĂĄzka zrĹŻdnostĂ­ a urĂĄÂek ÄeskĂŠho jazyka, kterĂ˝ch je schopna muÂÂtina. Proboha, <b>vÂdyÂ</b> jsme se dĂ­ky muÂskĂŠmu hranĂ­ na vojĂĄky, nezodpovÄdnÄ pĹetahovanĂŠmu do civilu a do dob mĂ­rovĂ˝ch, ocitli... Ano, tam, kde jsme. V paternalistickĂŠm svÄtÄ.\r\n\r\nIn this case, the assertion being countered is either way back before the start of the quote, or is assumed to be hanging in the air. I suppose it\'s something like \"women have little/no value\". Above all, you can feel the emotion wafting from this vÂdyÂ, although as always, its actual pronunciation is a nervous, tense, calm before the storm of an upcoming word.\r\n\r\nIn fact, this is true in general: vÂdyÂ indicates emotional opposition to an assertion, and though it is not itself pronounced with anything more than perhaps a shift up the musical scale one\'s voice is \"singing\", soon after it tends to come an explosion representing anger, resignedness, self-assuredness, whatever that led to using vÂdyÂ:\r\n\r\nVÂdyÂ jsem se tady NARODila (yes, I know, accent always on the first syllable... but I swear it sometimes leaks through to the second in cases like these)\r\nVÂdyÂ je to JEDno\r\nVÂdyÂ kopanĂĄ je od toho, aby se pĹi nĂ­ KOPALO!\r\n\r\nEtc.\r\n\r\n----------------------\r\n\r\n<i>KĂŠÂ bych</i> is not something you\'ll be using or hearing often...\r\n\r\nIn and of itself, \"kĂŠÂ\" is hyperformal (thus making it occasionally colloquial) and is basically like English\'s imperative \"may.\" Again examples (this time from the \'net, since keÂ is so rare that it doesn\'t appear in my minicorpus):\r\n\r\n<i>KeÂ [sic] by zato dostal tu rakovynu [sic]. SviĹa bilĂĄ [sic].</i>\r\nMay da basterd get cancer. Honky sh*thead.\r\n\r\nBut more relevantly... kĂŠÂ bych. I\'d most compare it to \"if only\". Like anything involving kĂŠÂ, it has Biblical undertones. \r\n\r\n<i>Achjo holky, <b>keÂ [sic] bych</b> vĂĄm to mohla ĹĂ­ct jak pĹibrat, pro mÄ je to taaak lehkĂŠ asi jako pro vĂĄs ubrat na vĂĄze ( coÂ bych zase potĹebovala poradit jĂĄ )... at se ÄlovÄk snaÂĂ­ jak se snaÂĂ­ zhubnout tak se nedaĹĂ­ :((</i>\r\n\r\nSpeaks for itself, I think. (These \"sic\"-d keÂ\'s are because I first looked for keÂ instead of the correct kĂŠÂ, and I don\'t want to throw away my examples...)\r\n\r\nThe rest of these are from my minicorpus (which merely couldn\'t cope with the misspelling).\r\n\r\n<i>SmĂ­ vĹŻbec bĂ˝t Âena tak troufalĂĄ? Och, BoÂe, <b>kĂŠÂ bych</b> to vÄdÄla!</i>\r\n\r\n<i>Ach, <b>kĂŠÂ bych</b> vÄdÄl, komu za to podÄkovat!</i>\r\n\r\n<i><b>[...]kĂŠÂ bych</b> byl i nadĂĄle tak zdravĂ˝ jako teÄ.</i>\r\n\r\nThe last one is not \"if only\", but rather the imperative \"may\" or \"let\" (May I be as healthy in the future as I am now); I think it\'s clear enough from the context when it\'s \"if only\" and when not.\r\n\r\nHope that helps!\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-25 16:34:31', 1212, 0),
(1215, 13, 'Erik', '', 'Re: Passive constructions', 'Yup!\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-11-25 16:34:51', 1210, 0),
(1216, 13, 'Anthony Peachment', 'apeachment@tiscali.cz', 'Re: VÂdytÂ´ (and  KĂŠÂ bych)', 'Thanks a lot, that helped. However your view that kĂŠÂ is hyperformal slightly perplexes me. I have heard it relatively often, of course not as often as pĹĂĄt si. How else does one say; \"I wish I were at home\" \"kĂŠÂ bych byl doma.\"\r\nI would agree that the use of the past kĂŠÂ is old-fashioned. \"I wish I had seen it\" \"KĂŠÂ bych bĂ˝val to vidÄl.\"\r\nWhat do you think?', '2003-11-27 00:21:19', 1214, 1),
(1217, 13, 'Anthony Peachment', 'apeachment@tiscali.cz', 'Re: VÂdytÂ´ (and  KĂŠÂ bych)', 'OK, you could say \'uÂ abych byl doma\'', '2003-11-27 15:16:45', 1216, 1),
(1218, 13, 'Milan', '', 'Re: VÂdytÂ´ (and  KĂŠÂ bych)', 'Are you really foreigner?I think ypur explanation is pretty good.You\'re more like a Czech person.\r\nMilan', '2003-11-29 04:30:35', 1214, 0),
(1219, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: VÂdytÂ´ (and  KĂŠÂ bych)', 'I\'m too busy lately to reply in full; here\'s a short statement that hopefully sums up what I\'d say in a long one:\r\n\r\nthe line between \"hyperformal\" and \"informal\" is often thiner than between \"formal\" and \"informal\".\r\n\r\nErik', '2003-12-01 18:12:12', 1216, 0),
(1220, 14, 'Paul Utesch', 'pauly_boj@hotmail.com', 'Re: trip to czech republic', 'Go to Olomouc!  I lived in Olomouc as a student for a while, and it was awesome.  It has things you\'d like in big city, but has small city mentality.  The people are really polite.  It is very historic, and has a very practical tram system.  I think they have an english version to their www.olomouc.cz website, but I cannot find it.  Give it a try!', '2003-12-02 03:37:32', 1147, 0),
(1221, 14, 'Paul Utesch', 'pauly_boj@hotmail', 'Re: more wierd-arse questions from nickie!', 'Hey Nickie... I was a foreign exchange student in my junior year of high school... I went to Olomouc, Czech Republic.. great place.  It sounds like you\'re a fun loving dude... and if you ever have any questions, drop me a line, because I know where you\'re coming from.  Well.. except, I guess I don\'t know where you\'re from?  I\'m guessing from the family situation it\'s USA?  I am from Iowa... but yeah, if you need any advice or encouragement, ask me.. Czech is the BEST choice... the place is awesome.  I\'m going back for college at Charles University in 2004... just e mail me dude', '2003-12-02 03:53:12', 320, 0),
(1223, 14, 'Anja', 'abs@gearbulk.com', 'Re: trip to czech republic', 'Hi Mark, I\'ll send you an email with some possebilities in Sumava if you\'re interested. Kvilda or Horska Kvilda. Full nature and relaxation.', '2003-12-03 17:29:25', 1147, 0),
(1224, 13, 'Cindy', 'riverdancer49@hotmail.com', 'Grandma', 'I\'ve been searching a long time.  All I want to know is what is the Czech name for Grandmother.  My grandmother was born in Prague and at the age of 4 moved to Austria.  She has been gone many years now but THIS time of year still brings back fond memories of her wonderful baked goodies, and the bubbles lights on her Christmas tree.  WE called her Babi(phonetically Bubbi).  Is that Czech?  All of a sudden at my age (51) I yearn to learn more about my heritage.  I find that in looking at some of her recipes, my mom\'s recipes, that there is an Austrian influence, Jewish as well as German. I know nothing of HER parents but when visiting my Dad next time plan to ask lots of questions :).  If anyone can help with the Grandma question, I would love to hear from you.  ', '2003-12-05 00:50:49', 0, 1),
(1225, 13, 'Cindy', 'riverdancer49@hotmail.com', 'Re: Czech Women', 'hmmmmmmm, am wondering what you mean by mad? ', '2003-12-05 00:55:33', 1171, 1),
(1226, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: Grandma', 'Cindy, babi is short for babiÄka the czech for grandmother. Babi is exactly what my daughter calls her czech grandmother and I think is used by  most czech children. ', '2003-12-05 04:30:53', 1224, 0),
(1227, 17, 'jitka', 'pjitka@post.cz', 'Re: vizum do ameriky', 'Ahoj, Petro, \r\nobjevila jsem prave tvoji nabidku. Mam zajem dozvedet se neco podrobnejsiho.  O jake studijni obory se jedna, existuje nejaka vekova hranice atd.?  Muzes postlat? Dekuji\r\nHezky den preje \r\nJitka\r\n(Co ty tam studujes?)', '2003-12-05 19:16:45', 1163, 0),
(1228, 13, 'jitka', 'pjitka@post.cz', 'Re: Passive constructions/1.', 'Ahoj Eriku,\r\npokud uz vysvetlujes cestinu na takove urovni, doufam, ze muzu odpovidat cesky. Cetla jsem Tvoje prispevky, ale protoze anglicky az tak dobre neumim, nerozumela jsem uplne vsemu a tudiz se s Tebou ani nemuzu poustet do nejake \"odborne\" jazykove diskuse. Ale pro presnost - dovolim si par oprav Tvych ceskych textu.\r\n1.Narodil se o Stedry den. > Narodil se na Stedry den /na Mikulase... \r\n                             3.os.c.j - nejedna se o pasivum (stejne: narodil \r\n                             jsem se ...\r\n2.Sel jsem si nachat ostrihnout vlasy.(perf.) >lepe.. ostrihat vlasy (imperf.)', '2003-12-05 19:51:31', 1204, 0),
(1229, 13, 'jitka', 'pjitka@', 'Re: Passive constructions!2.', '3. dalsi 3 ceske preklady nejsou v pasivu - ve srovnani s anglickym textem (chtel jsi tim ukazat rozdil mezi Cj a Aj?)\r\n4.  \"je slyset\", \"je videt\" , \"je citit\" nejsou pasiva. Vyraz \"je chutnat\" se neda pouzit. Posledni vetu muzeme vyjadrit pasivem napr.takto: Jejich(3.os.pl., takze kratke i) vino se ochutnava (\"kostuje\") na kazdem rocniku Hrusicke kosti. \r\n\r\nJen jeste dodatek - velice nerada \"deformuji\" svuj rodny jazyk tim, ze pisu bez carek a hacku, ale bohuzel se mi nedari doplnit je do sveho programu v pocitaci.  ', '2003-12-05 20:09:01', 1204, 0),
(1230, 13, 'jitka', 'pjitka@post.cz', 'Re: Passive constructions/3.', 'Eriku,\r\n-jeste jedna posledni oprava:\r\nNevim kolik penez lze z tohoto uctu vytahnout. > Nevim, kolik penez lze z toho uctu \"vybrat\" /\"vyzvednout\".( v souvislosti s penezi! a bankou/uctem)\r\nSloveso \"vytahnout\" muzes pouzit ve spojenich jako:\r\n... penize z penezenky/kapsy..\r\n... trn z paty\r\n... nekoho z \"bryndy\" atd.\r\nProzatim je to vsechno,\r\nhezky den preje \r\nJitka', '2003-12-05 20:16:45', 1207, 0),
(1231, 13, 'jitka', '', 'Re: New Love', 'Nevim, jestli se jeste nekdy vracis ke stare poste, ale treba ano.Pak se Ti mozna zalibi i druhy vyraz pro anglicke \"I  miss you\" : Styska se mi po tobe.\r\nZdravi Jitka', '2003-12-05 20:20:37', 1187, 0),
(1232, 13, 'Cindy', 'riverdancer49@hotmail.com', 'Re: Grandma', 'Kevin, thank you very much!', '2003-12-05 21:23:31', 1226, 1),
(1233, 13, 'Danny Lankford', 'lankforddl@yahoo.com', 'Pimsleur Audio', 'ahoj,\r\n\r\nI\'ve been trying to find  the Pimsluer audio tapes on MP3 or in some digital format. Does anyone know where these can be found or if they even exist? \r\n\r\ndÄkuji,\r\nDanny', '2003-12-09 12:16:01', 0, 1),
(1234, 17, 'Nadka', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: :o)', 'MĂĄm se bĂĄjeÄnÄ a jsem v Praze. Zrovna krĂĄsnÄ snÄÂĂ­ a venku panuje pĹedvĂĄnoÄnĂ­ nĂĄlada. Jak se daĹĂ­ vĂĄm? :) N.', '2003-12-16 14:35:03', 1162, 0),
(1235, 13, 'FĂŠlix', 'beafele@eremas.com', 'beatove', 'Queridos amigos,\r\nnecesito ayudo con la palabra \"beatove\". &#191;Tiene algĂşn significado en checo? Muchas gracias por anticipado.\r\nFĂŠlix, Espa&#241;a\r\n\r\nHallo Freunde,\r\nVielleicht kĂśntet ihr mir helfen. Bedeutet etwas \"beatove\" in czech?\r\nVielen Dank und herzliche GrĂźĂe\r\nFĂŠlix, Espa&#241;a\r\n\r\nDear friends,\r\nI need some help from you with the word \"beatove\". Does it mean anything in czech?\r\nThank you very much in advance.\r\nFĂŠlix Medina', '2003-12-18 22:10:56', 0, 0),
(1236, 13, 'unknown', '', 'Re: Pimsleur Audio', 'try to find out czech lessons throuhg p2p webs', '2003-12-20 22:52:52', 1233, 0),
(1237, 13, 'jana', '', 'Re: beatove', 'hi felix,\r\nit\'s just a guess but depending on context i\'d understand it as something having to do with modern music. \"beat\" is not a czech word but many borrowed words slipped from their original meaning. the ending \"-ove\" is one of the options to modify the stem to another word class (the morphology of czech language is quite rich). as an example, \"samet\" (velvet) can be modified to \"sametovy\" (velvety) in this way.\r\ni hope this helps, if you need a more precise meaning, pls let me know the context (particularly the noun following the \"beatove\").\r\nmerry xmas,\r\njana', '2003-12-21 18:56:11', 1235, 0),
(1238, 13, 'Cyndi', '', 'Re: phrase', 'I need to know how to phrase\r\n\"We love our grand children.\" in Czech.  This is for a Christmas present so please help fast.', '2003-12-22 00:41:32', 0, 1),
(1239, 13, 'A. Soukup', 'aws@pld.com', 'conjugations', 'I\'ve just started researching czech.  I am having trouble finding information over conjugating verbs.  The present tense seems straight forward enough.  But, I am having trouble finding information about the other tenses.  Does anyone know of any conjugation tables or such that may be of some use.\r\n\r\nThanks\r\n\r\nAndy', '2003-12-22 11:20:37', 0, 1),
(1240, 13, 'FĂŠlix', 'beafele@eresmas.com', 'Re: beatove', 'Thank you very much Jana. There is no context, I mean, \"beatove\" is a surname here in Spain and I was wondering if it could come from Czech Republic. Your answer has been very kind.\r\nI wish you merry christmas and an extraordinary happy new year for you and your lovely country.\r\nFĂŠlix', '2003-12-24 00:23:43', 1237, 0),
(1241, 13, 'lovermann', '', 'Re: conjugations', 'http://www.lingvisto.org/cze/konjugacio.pdf\r\nThis document was made by me for russian-speakers, so the translations and comments are in russian, but the conjugation forms of 12 verbs are in czech. ', '2003-12-24 03:55:24', 1239, 0),
(1242, 13, 'lovermann', '', 'conjugations -- la plej utila pagxo', 'Let mi present on of the most useful resourses on verb conjugation:\r\n(123 czech verbs)\r\nhttp://www.verba.org/verbi/all_verbs_index_cs.html\r\n\r\nOne can find there thousands of other cojugated verbs in many languages.', '2003-12-24 15:39:23', 1239, 0),
(1243, 13, 'Mark', 'mh@oosterhof.be', 'translation of the word \'asi\'', 'Hi, can somebody give me a translation of the word ASI as used in the next sentence: \"Asi bych nechtel prozivat.... \". Sorry, I don\'t have a Czech keyboard.\r\n', '2003-12-24 15:52:10', 0, 1),
(1244, 13, 'Jakub', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: translation of the word \'asi\'', 'Hi!\r\nIt depends on content, but the sentence should mean something like \"I would rather not to experience (something)\" or, literaly, \"Perhaps I would not want to experience (something)\".\r\n', '2003-12-25 21:33:21', 1243, 0),
(1245, 15, 'Michael Fiala', 'fiala@interlog.com', 'Re: The most interesting idiom in Czech', 'Erik, relax!\r\nIt really means: In essence...\r\nCheers, Michael F.', '2003-12-27 09:25:37', 1190, 0),
(1246, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: phrase', 'Cindi, the answer too late is \"Mame radi nase vnocata\" sorry did not see until after Xmas (and also I am without czech keyboard so haceks etc are missing). I hope you can use it at another time. You say it like ma-meh rah-dee nash-eh fnot-cha-ta.', '2003-12-28 23:35:27', 1238, 0),
(1247, 13, 'mark', 'mh@oosterhof.be', 'Levou botu', 'Here\'s another sentence from my coursebook that I can\'t translate properly: Levou botu Ăşz mĂĄ obutou a tedÂ´ si obouvĂĄ pravou botu. It is about a boy putting on his shoes at morning but I don\'t understand the exact meaning of the sentence.  \r\nBy the way, are verbs like obouvat used in common czech??? ', '2003-12-29 14:04:27', 0, 1),
(1248, 13, 'don', 'donek@xs4all.nl', 'help - translation', 'Ahoj Bohemica!\r\nWould someone be so kind as to assist me?\r\nI\'m trying to compose an imaginary book title in the style of the Harry Potter titles (for example: \'Harry Potter and the Enchanted Broom-Cupboard\')\r\nWhat I want to translate is:\r\n\"Will o\' the wisp Jirout and the Mystery of the Missing T613S spare-parts\"\r\nMy own effort (so far) is:\r\n\'Bludi?ka Jirout a zĂĄhada .. chyb?Ă­jĂ­cy T613S sou?ĂĄstky\' \r\nI\'m having trouble with \'mystery\' (I would like, if possible, the nuance \'enigma\') and how to make the adjective agree.\r\nAny advice gratefully received.\r\nPozdravem,\r\nDon\r\nP.S. Just to explain the background: I have a Tatra T613S, and my friend Zdislav gave a large quantity of spare parts to our friend Jirka (Jirout) who would bring them from Jezenik to me in Holland. That was more than two months ago, and it has become a standing joke. Jirka is well known for being an unpredictable whirlwind, always on the move and somewhat unreliable.\r\nP.P.S. Also I notice that I cannot type the letter with accent hook (hĂĄĂŠek) here, it appears as an e with a backward-sloping accent. But I think the meaning will be clear. ', '2003-12-29 22:13:55', 0, 1),
(1249, 13, 'Jakub', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: help - translation', 'Hi Don!\r\n\r\nI would translate it as \"BludiÄka Jirout a zĂĄhada* chybÄjĂ­cĂ­ch souÄĂĄstek T613S\"\r\n*or \"tajemstvĂ­\"\r\n\r\nadjectice forms are \"zĂĄhadnĂ˝\" and \"tajemnĂ˝\"\r\n\r\nOr, more freely, \"BludiÄka Jirout a zĂĄhadnĂŠ/tajemnĂŠ zmizenĂ­ souÄĂĄstek T613S\", but I am not sure whether this is the exact meaningÂINSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(1308, 13, 'Jan VanÄk jr.', 'jan . vanek . jr @ seznam . cz', 'Re: Czech Palindromes', 'I\'m replying with diacritics as they are very important.\r\ncĂ­tĂ­c = feeling or smelling (feminine gender of participle to be used in constructions like Feeling unwell, she didn\'t go out)\r\njetej - that would be colloquial masculine form of, literally, ridden with possible meanings of aged and damaged by use, or also drunk\r\njeÂej - colloquial for they bristle\r\njeÄej - coll. they yell\r\nmetem - coll. we sweep (there\'s also medem, instrumental case of honey)\r\nmĂĄvĂĄm - I wave\r\nmĂ­jĂ­m - I pass by\r\nmĂ˝m - a form of possessive pronoun my/mine\r\nnepotopen - unsunk\r\nnezasazen - unplanted\r\nnezaĹazen - unclassed (all three masculine)\r\nslils - you poured together/off\r\nvymyv - having washed out (participle like above, masc.)\r\nvÄdÄv would be having known (masc.), but I\'m not sure if it is formed properly.\r\n\r\nAs for the others, \"u obou\" (two words) is \"at both\" and vĂ˝kyv (asymmetric accent, BTW the same would hold for muÂĹŻm) a swing aside; vojov is Slovak plural genitive case of, um, archaic word for column of soldiers.\r\n\r\nJust to make sure you have them, I\'ll also add a few palindromic sentences: The best-known is Kobyla mĂĄ malĂ˝ bok (Mare has a small flank), and than there\'s Jelenovi pivo nelej (Don\'t pour beer to a deer), followed by AdĂŠla mu rum ale dĂĄ (Adela, though, gives it rum).', '2004-02-25 20:25:34', 1300, 0),
(1309, 14, 'NaÄka', 'katzerova@hotmail.com', 'Re: Slang, idioms, and other such fun things', '\r\nBowsprit is ÄelnĂ­ stÄÂeĹ (or ÄeleĹ) in Czech.', '2004-02-27 19:39:53', 1284, 0),
(1310, 13, 'Kylie', '', 'AFS exchange?', 'I am going to be an exchange student to europe, but I can\'t decide between going to france or the czech republic. I speak some french and a tiny bit of german and i would love to be fluent in french, but i think this is a great oppurtunity to learn a language i would never learn otherwise. Also, france does not seem as exciting! However my dad has scared me about going to the czech republic, he says the air is very polluted and it is still a dangerous country. How true is this? What does the average town look like? Is French a common language to study in school? Thanks anyone who can help!', '2004-03-06 03:29:00', 0, 0),
(1311, 13, 'Milly', 'elvinchick@hotmail.com', 'Re: AFS exchange?', 'If I had the chance, I\'d go Czech. French is a more popular language and you can take classes just about anywhere in the US on it. Czech is a different story, and I wouldn\'t miss out on the oppurtunity if it were me. Best of Luck to you! \r\nMilly M', '2004-03-07 02:04:43', 1310, 0),
(1312, 13, 'PhDr. Marie HĂĄdkovĂĄ, Ph.D.', 'maruskah@ffnw.upol.cz', 'Re: AFS exchange?', 'You can study in Olomouc - what about some Czech for foreigners + politics, culture etc. \r\nLet me know - if you are interested in university course of Czech for foreigners.\r\nM. HĂĄdkovĂĄ', '2004-03-08 21:01:52', 1310, 0),
(1315, 13, 'Milly', 'elvinchick@hotmail.com', 'Where can I learn Czech online?', 'I want to learn Czech, but my only resource is the internet. Where is the best place to learn Czech online?\r\nMILLY', '2004-03-12 09:48:10', 0, 1),
(1316, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: AFS exchange?', 'kylie,\r\ni\'ve heard going to the states is dangerous, everybody owns a gun and i\'ll be robbed by a drug addict in daylight. i don\'t really think such stories are based on facts, bad things can happen everywhere, just have a look on the crime statistics. i\'m sorry to hear that your dad has such low opinion about my country and surpriced how persistant the cold war propaganda can be. the damages will probably take long, long time to repair. \r\nthe pollution situation is improving, although there is still space for improvements. policies and regulations got much tougher in the past years but as with everythig, it\'s matter of funding.\r\nin my eyes, towns look everywhere more or less the same - some houses, streets and service buildings put to one place. if you\'re thinking of university level, you\'ll need a city and my recommendation would be prague. the english speaking community is quite large there, so you\'d have the option if you need to cushion the culture shock a little.\r\ni wouldn\'t say french is very common language in the czech republic, most young people take english and some of the older generation speak german. i\'m pretty sure you could study in english there but you\'d need to check this (as it might depend on what exactly you want to study).\r\ngood luck with your studies, with your enthusiasm i\'m sure you\'ll have lots of fun and learn much. \r\nfingers crossed,\r\njana', '2004-03-12 15:19:21', 1310, 0),
(1317, 13, 'John', '', '', 'How do you write \"Happy Birthday\" in Czech', '2004-03-12 22:31:24', 0, 0),
(1318, 14, 'Amnon', 'Wahle@yotvata.org.il', 'burning the wittch-april 30', 'Dear readers.    Does anybody know anything about an old tradition of \"burning the witch\", celebrated annually on the 30th of April ? (Sorry, I forgot the Czech name of this tradition or feast). Thank you for any information !', '2004-03-19 14:50:39', 0, 1),
(1319, 14, 'Amnon', 'Wahle@yotvata.org.il', 'burning the witch-april 30', 'Dear readers.    Does anybody know anything about an old tradition of \"burning the witch\", celebrated annually on the 30th of April ? (Sorry, I forgot the Czech name of this tradition or feast). Thank you for any information !', '2004-03-19 14:51:29', 0, 1),
(1320, 14, 'Amnon', 'Wahle@yotvata.org.il', 'Re: Welcome to Czech Culture Forum', 'Dear Lukes. Thank you for your fast reply (or was it an automatic one ?!)\r\nHopefully anyone will read my question and answer quickly too (I understand that not many people take active part in the \'Forum\'...)\r\nBy-the-way: One of the questions I saw in the \'Forum\' refered to Holesovice quarter in Praha. My grandfather was born there, lived there as a child and teenager and left in 1936. In 1992 he and both myself and my sister visited the area, renewing \"on site\" some of his memories. Is there more information about Holesovice you can direct me to ?  Thank you for your reply, and ofcourse - for the so interesting for me and for my father website you have generated !!      Amnon.   ', '2004-03-19 15:30:51', 217, 1),
(1321, 13, 'Lindsey', 'gabe41@msn.com', 'The Czech Language', 'I\'m a college student and I\'m trying to put together a Czech language presentation. I need to figure out what, if any, language challenges a native Czech speaker would encounter while learning Englsh. Also, any social challenges that they might encounter. If anyone has any input, I greaty appreciate it! Thankyou! Dekuju! \r\n', '2004-03-24 04:10:47', 0, 1),
(1323, 13, 'Aliga', 'uruelia@yahoo.com.ar', 'Translation', '\r\nPlease, can somebody tell me how to say \'Be quiet!\' in Czech (the way you\'ll say that to a friend or a little boy)?\r\n\r\nDÄkuji!!!', '2004-03-26 21:50:30', 0, 0),
(1324, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: Translation', 'I think it must be \"bud\' ticha\" as I hear that constantly aimed by the wife at our daughter. If it helps in English the pronunciation would be more like butch tickar.', '2004-03-28 05:13:20', 1323, 0),
(1325, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: The Czech Language', 'An first obvious challenge is the use of the and a.', '2004-03-28 05:16:03', 1321, 0),
(1326, 13, 'kevin', '', 'Re:', 'Ât\'astny narozeniny, probably spelt wrong', '2004-03-28 05:19:39', 1317, 0),
(1327, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: AFS exchange?', 'You\'re not really expecting an unbiased answer from a site of czechophiles and czechs. Theres a lot to be said for France its a wonderful and beautiful country. The language is far more likely to be of future use to you than czech in isolation. Having said that it will be a far uniquer thing to speak czech. It will also open the possibility of studying other slavic languages which overall then might be more use than French. The CR is changing rapidly and as each year passes there is less of the original character and more tourism, so it would be good to go before its just another part of greater europe. Lastly your Dad is talking rubbish. Go to CR but make some visits to France.', '2004-03-28 05:32:38', 1310, 0),
(1328, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: navrhnout', 'Blimey Gloria I amazed none of the clever dicks on this site have answered you. I assume you mean the conjugation, being pretty dense myself I hope I\'m right. Navrhnu, neÂ, ne, neme, nete, nou hopefully. Also slovesy should be slovesa, that I\'m a bit more certain of', '2004-03-28 05:52:24', 1302, 0),
(1329, 13, 'Sue', 'smkil01@aol.com', 'translation', 'Can someone translate for me?  I am trying to book a hotel room via e-mail - I think this response means there\'s not a room available, but wanted to be sure.\r\n\r\nJe mi velice lito, ale s timto hotelem nespolupracujeme.  \r\n\r\nS pozdravem.', '2004-03-29 00:55:09', 0, 0),
(1330, 13, 'Milan', '', 'Re:', 'If I were in your place,I wouldn\'t use this phrase Âtastny narozeniny.The Czechs usually say VÂe nejlepÂĂ­ k narozeninĂĄm,in informal way just VÂechno nejlepÂĂ­.Of course,you can use another phrase,but as i said I wouldn\'t use the phrase Stastny narozeniny.Cheers Milan', '2004-03-29 02:19:44', 1326, 0),
(1331, 13, 'Milan', '', 'Re: Translation', 'About the hotel:We don\'t collaborate with the hotel.Sorry.\r\nAbout BE QUIET.Either you can say Bud\' zticha or BudÄticho.Cheers', '2004-03-29 02:22:55', 1324, 0),
(1332, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: The Czech Language', 'articles, phrasal verbs, intonation, tags, word order\r\npoliteness concepts and markers, indirect signals\r\npls let me know if you need more specific info.\r\nj.', '2004-03-29 15:08:21', 1321, 0),
(1333, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: translation', 'The text means:\r\n\r\nWe are very sorry, but we do not work together with this hotel.\r\n\r\nBest regards.', '2004-03-29 20:52:25', 1329, 0),
(1334, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: The Czech Language', 'Not much to add, but:\r\n\r\npronunciation -- sounds that don\'t exist in Czech (hard and soft th, \"uh,\" etc.); carryover of Czech pronunciation rules, most notably Czech\'s g->k, d->t, z->s etc. conversion before certain consonants and at ends of words\r\n\r\nverb tenses, most notably the description vs. event distinction (he was coming vs. he came), which has no precise parallel in Czech (but unfortunately, does have the confusing imprecise parallel of dokonavy vs. nedokonavy)\r\n\r\ncarryover of Czech prepositions (a problem between any two languages), e.g. \"Welcome in Prague\"\r\n\r\nfalse friends (false cognates), e.g. \"actual news\" for up-to-date (aktuĂĄlnĂ­) news\r\n\r\nhorrible fake British accents ;-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-03-29 21:05:53', 1332, 0),
(1335, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: The Czech Language', 'oh yeap, prepositions! the trully are awful! i\'d say they probably are the most difficult one together with collocations. and one more i forgot: spelling!\r\nj.\r\n', '2004-03-30 15:16:59', 1334, 0),
(1336, 13, 'Heather Daniel', 'heatherdaniel10@msn.com', 'Help needed', 'How would you say in Czech - \'Can I buy you a drink?\'\r\n\r\nAny help would be greatly appreciated. \r\n\r\nThanks, Heather', '2004-03-30 22:56:33', 0, 1),
(1337, 13, 'Milan', '', 'Re: Help needed', 'You can say Mohu ti koupit pitĂ­?,Mohu ti koupit drink?Or you can say among friends MĹŻÂu tÄ pozvat na panĂĄka?Or just Zvu tÄ na panĂĄka.Or just MĹŻÂu tÄ pozvat?', '2004-03-31 00:12:48', 1336, 0),
(1338, 13, 'Milan', '', 'Re: Help needed', 'If you arenÂ¨t Â´being on first names,you have to say Mohu VĂM koupit drink(pitĂ­) or Mohu VĂS pozvat?', '2004-03-31 00:14:35', 1336, 0),
(1339, 13, 'PP', '', 'Translation', 'Can anyone teach me how to say \'I love you\' in Czech? I know it is \'mijuli te\' but I forgot the pronounciation. and also, what is \' I am exited to come in June.\'? Big Thanks! PP', '2004-04-01 02:17:28', 0, 0),
(1340, 14, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: burning the wittch-april 30', 'Looking this up on the web its the same as some German one word multi syllabled event. Didn\'t get much more info but I spoke to the wife and she said till the end of the communist times it was kept but called mĂ­rovĂŠ ohnÄ, rather than ÄarodÄjnice, to make it more acceptable alongside of May day. Is this a Czech tradition or is something imposed by the Austrians or whoever.', '2004-04-02 03:17:05', 1318, 0),
(1341, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Translation', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nit\'s \"miluji te^\" (i.e. the e has a hacek), and is pronounced:\r\nMIH-loo-jih tyeh\r\n\r\n\"I am excited to come in June\" shouldn\'t be translated literally, as it would sound a bit odd word-for-word. I\'ll leave the translation to the actual Czechs, as I\'m afraid I would come up with something unnatural-sounding.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-04-02 20:03:15', 1339, 0),
(1342, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: The Czech Language', 'I think the biggest problem with prepositions between any two languages is they\'re the first area where learners of their first foreign language realize (or fail to realize) the startling notion that \"vÂechno mĹŻÂe ĂşplnÄ, ale ĂşplnÄ jinak\", that words don\'t \"mean\" one or two things in another language, but just map to one or more words -- and with prepositions, it\'s really depressing, because e.g. \"everyone knows you go TO someone on a visit,\" whereas \"kaÂdy vĂ­, Âe se chodĂ­ na nĂĄvÂtÄvu ZA nÄkĂ˝m\" -- I mean, it\'s something that feels really fundamental and set in stone, and all of a sudden you\'re using \"NA\" to visit someone in English, or \"BEHIND\" to visit someone in Czech, and it\'s... frustrating.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-04-02 20:09:09', 1335, 0),
(1343, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Gypsies', 'There has been a tendency to name Gypsies, if you will, by the country they passed through, or were thought to have passed through, on the way to the country speaking a given language.\r\n\r\nGypsies - \"they must be from Egypt\"\r\nRomanichels (FR, almost certainly misspelled), Romany, RomovĂŠ - although this is probably based on their word for \"man\" (Rom), often people think on this basis that they come from Romania\r\nBohemes, perhaps? French? In any case, just based on the mistaken belief that when they said \"we got here from Bohemia\", that they must be Bohemians.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-04-02 20:18:47', 1296, 0),
(1344, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: burning the wittch-april 30', 'It\'s also called PĂĄlenĂ­ ÄarodÄjnic. \r\n\r\nhttp://www.ceske-tradice.cz/Carodejnice/Index.htm describes it in detail, but unfortunately, the webmaster decided it would be a good idea to include special code on the page to block any kind of copying from the page. It\'s thus kind of hard to do a (partial) translation.\r\n\r\n- German Walpurgisnacht, CZ ValpurÂina noc (not that I\'ve ever heard that in practice)\r\n- night from April 30th to May 1st\r\n- a night when evil forces allegedly have greater power than otherwise\r\n- to protect oneself from evil on that night, one traditionally was to keep at hand a fern leaf, holy chalk (?!?), a communion wafer, and other special objects.\r\n- people believed that the sky was full of witches that night, flying to meet up for a black sabbath, which included various strange aspects as bountiful food, mouthwatering to look out, but without flavor or nutrition.\r\n- various local and culture-wide practices related to protecting oneself from this night existed, no point translating...\r\n- ...but above all, as protection against witches, it become customary to burn a fire in an elevated place. This practice is extremely old, and apparently did begin in Germany, though it would not seem that they were not \"imposed,\" but rather that the practice spread naturally.\r\n\r\n...more later, posting from work.', '2004-04-02 20:39:05', 1340, 0),
(1345, 13, 'jana', 'slavek777@hotmail.com', 'Re: Translation', 'hm, it\'s probably a matter of personal choice, but i think i\'d rather say \"mam te rad(a)\", then \"miluji te\" - it sounds to me a bit, i don\'t know, theatral (well, i\'ve never used it).\r\n\r\nyou\'re right, the direct translation would be likely to give the wrong impression, \"vzruseni\" relates more to describing physical (i.e. sexual) states. i\'d say the translation closest to the intended meaning would be something like \"tesim se, az prijedu v cervnu\" (+/- i\'m looking forward to...). the register is neutral, it can be used it in any context (at least, i can\'t think of an inappropriate one). spontaneously, i\'d probably say \"tesim se na tebe\" (+/- i\'m looking forward to seeing you but stronger, conveys this kind of desire to be together with the other person).\r\nj.', '2004-04-05 19:14:59', 1341, 0),
(1346, 13, 'Dave Windsor', 'dave.windsor@mildmay.org', '\"Let\'s do ...\"', 'Would someone help me with a general translation for phrases beginning with, for example, \"Let\'s do..\" or \"Let\'s go....\".  But given my, albeit limited, knowledge of the Czech language, I fear that the answer may not be so possible and that there may not be a general \'one size fits all\' translation.\r\n\r\nDave.', '2004-04-07 15:44:33', 0, 1),
(1347, 13, 'Milan', '', 'Re:', 'I don\'t know if it is enough for you but I think the general phrase is:PojdÄme\r\nExamples:LET\'S have a drink--POJDÄME SE napĂ­t.\r\nLET\'S have a chat-POJDÄME SI povĂ­dat.\r\nLET\'S have a smoke-POJDÄME SI zakouĹit.(unfortunately for you,it is not the only possibility,but I do believe it will help you too.Cheers.Milan)', '2004-04-07 22:25:20', 1346, 0),
(1348, 13, 'Erik', '', 'Re: Translation', 'miluji TÄ -- I mean, not that I love you, Jana, I\'m just talking about the phrase -- yeah, you\'re right. It\'s the most subtle false friends that are the hardest to get rid of. I\'ll probably STILL go on using \"miluji TÄ\" even after this n-th reminder that I shouldn\'t, just because it feels so weird to me to say (what feels to me like) \"I like you\" to mean \"I love you\" that I can only do it when I\'m conciously thinking about what I\'m saying, which is rarely. :-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-04-14 17:54:51', 1345, 0),
(1349, 13, 'J', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: Let\'s do', 'Milan, and many other native speakers, including TV and other media, have a bad habit using exhortative phrases beginning with \"PojÄmeÂINSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(1424, 13, 'J', 'jakubv@email.cz', 'Re: Lhota', 'Yes, \"Lhota\" is medieval form of the word \"LhĹŻta\" which means a term or a time limit. It denoted the fact that newly established village was freed of taxes for some time, usualy a few years, to support the settlement and boost grow.', '2004-09-15 23:30:58', 1423, 0),
(1425, 13, 'John', '', 'Re: czech words', 'I\'m very limited in the Czech language yet, but \"You are very nice and pretty\" might translate as \"jsi velmi hesky a krasne.\" [\"Sih vel-mee hess-key uh krah-sneh\"] \"What is your name?\" = \"jak se jmenujete?\" [\"Yuk seh men-oo-yet-eh\"] \"Do you like it here in America?\" - hmmm, good question ... try: \"mate rad Amerika?\" [\"Mah-teh rahd America\"] Remember, emphasis is always on the first syllable.', '2004-09-20 11:24:37', 1416, 0),
(1426, 13, 'John', '', 'Actually ...', '... try to say it as \"Dyekwee,\" pronouncing the \"D\" almost as if it were followed by a soft \"g\" sound. It\'s kind of hard to explain. If you purchase one of those simple travel language courses that come with a cassette tape, you\'ll know what I mean. A few years back I found one at a bookstore for only $9.95.\r\n\r\nGood luck in the Czech Republic. I envy ya. :-)', '2004-09-20 11:37:44', 1379, 0),
(1427, 13, 'Lorenzo', '', 'Re: Checkmark', 'It should be correctly written \"haÄek\"... \"C\" with haÄek by the way ;-)\r\n\r\nP.S. HaÄek means little hook', '2004-09-20 13:55:55', 1412, 0),
(1428, 13, 'Danielle', '', 'Re: czech words', 'How do you say Peace is possible in czech ', '2004-09-21 22:53:28', 1420, 0),
(1429, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: knedliky', 'Just \"dumplings\" would be better, I think.\r\n\r\nCzech has a tendency to \"diminutize\" everything, and if you translate all the diminutives literally, you tend to end up with something that sounds unnatural in English. This would probably be one of those cases, I believe.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-09-22 21:17:19', 1422, 0),
(1430, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Lhota', '...so the next time someone mentions, say, ÄervenĂĄ Lhota to you, you can say \"I really like it over there in Red Tax Vacation\" and confuse the heck out of them. :-))', '2004-09-22 21:19:13', 1424, 0),
(1431, 13, 'jana', '', 'Re: czech words', 'more details pls, not sure what message you\'re trying to convey', '2004-09-22 22:29:35', 1428, 0),
(1432, 13, 'ABartonek', '', 'Re: knedliky', 'white bread dumplings would be my guess..', '2004-09-28 19:24:11', 1418, 0),
(1433, 13, 'Frank', 'FCernik@yahoo.com', 'Re: knedliky', 'Knedliky = Dumplings; Knedlik = Dumpling\r\n\r\nThere are several variations of dumplings, i.e. Bread Dumplings (houskove k.), Potatoe D. (bramborove k.) or Fruit D. (ovocne k.) served with sweat ricotta cheese or fried bread crumbs or even milled poppy seeds and bit of melted unsalted butter. In soups, you can also find \"knedlicky\" (with the accent above \"c\") - i.e. Liver D. (jatrove k.)\r\n', '2004-09-28 19:32:13', 1418, 1),
(1434, 13, 'Boban', 'srpska_nekultura@yahoo.com', 'Re: knedliky', 'dumplings, Czech dumplings, potato dumplings, these should work.', '2004-09-29 22:04:29', 1418, 0),
(1435, 13, 'Tony', 'Goodlife1218@aol.com', 'Czech Genealogical Help', 'Attempting to research family history from the town of Mydlovary.  I have been trying to translate passages from a book using a Czech dictionary from the local library.  Need help with the below sentences!!  \r\n\r\nTomu se odecetl jeho prodil 119 kop.\r\nDrive se zde rikalo take Kyfysar.\r\nRoku 1665 pustil Kaspar Pecka tuto zivnost Lukasovi, svemu vlastnimu synovi, za 300 kop grosu.\r\n\r\nAny help would be greatly appreciated.\r\n\r\n', '2004-10-04 20:26:38', 0, 1),
(1436, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'For a map showing Mydlovary\'s location within the country, see:\r\nhttp://mesta.obce.cz/vyhledat.asp?id_org=2621\r\n\r\nThe passages in question read:\r\n\r\nAnd that man\'s share (?), 119 kopas [of groÂ], was deducted from his something-mentioned-in-the-previous-sentence.\r\nIn the past, this place was also called Kyfysar.\r\nIn the year 1665, Kaspar Pecka passed this business on to Lukas, his own son, for 300 kopas of groÂ.\r\n\r\nRegarding the kopa and the groÂ:\r\n\r\nMy source here is http://www.ilcik.cz/download/penize.htm.\r\n\r\nCzech and Moravian minting is divided into four main periods:\r\n\r\n- the dinar period (roughly 950 to 1300)\r\n\r\n- the groÂ period (1300 to 1520)\r\n\r\n- the tolar period (1520 to 1892)\r\n\r\n- the crown period (1892 to present)\r\n\r\n[...]\r\n\r\n[Greatly paraphrased/\"relevantized\":]\r\n\r\nAlthough I have no such information for the 1600s, the author of the page quoted here gave average prices for the period from 1550 to 1600. (A \"dinar\" in the below means a seventh of a groÂ.) A pound (presumably - \"libra\") of beef was 4 to 4.5 dinars, a pound of mutton 3.5 to 4 dinars, a really big 10-12 lb. loaf of bread was 6.5 to 7 dinar, a mĂĄz (quart and a half, I think*) of wine was 5 to 6 dinars, a vÄdro (approximately one buttload) of wine was one gold piece, i.e. 30 groÂ, 1000 smithed nails was 10 groÂ, a horseshoe was 1-2 groÂ, a day\'s work in the vineyards brought 2 groÂ, a day\'s work as unskilled laborer 8-10 groÂ, a day\'s work as a skilled laborer (carpenter) - 25 dinars. After 1600, prices doubled, and after 1610, they went up a further 50%; wages, however, grew at about half this rate.\r\n\r\nTolar Coinage\r\n\r\nThere were 20 groÂ to the tolar, but groÂ were also still sometimes counted in kopas, as they had been in the groÂ period. It seems to me from the quoted text that the kopa was synonomus with the hĹivna kopovĂĄ, and thus a kopa was 60 groÂ (assuming the groÂ customs continued into the tolar period lock, stock, and barrel).\r\n\r\n* All guesses at units of volume are with the help of http://www.converter.cz/j_objem.htm, which gives the relation of various units of volume to one cubic meter.\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-10-05 20:20:39', 1435, 0),
(1437, 13, 'Tony', 'Goodlife1218@aol.com', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Erik\r\n\r\nThanks for the help!!\r\n\r\nCan you help with the following paragraph. It relates to my great great great grandfather. \r\n\r\nRoku 1803 postoupil Jan Pecka zivnost svemu synu Matejovi, ktery v ten cas slouzil u cisarske, \"artilerie\" (=delostrelectva).  Synovi zanechal par kobyl, par volu, 2 kravy, 1 jalovici, 8 ovci, 1 prasnici a naradi v celkove cene 400 zlatych 40 krejcaru - i se statkem pak melo vsechni dohromady cenu 843 zlatych 5 krejcaru.  Matej Pecka se narodil 8. 1. 1770 a ozenil se s Marianou Krejcarovou ze Zbudova.  Pozustala udova Mariana predala roku 1844 zivnost synu Vojtechovi Pechovi za 412 zlatych 25 krejcaru.  Roku 1876 byla vlastnici usedlosti Johanka Peckova, rozena Novakova ze Zahaji.\r\n\r\nMy cousin was able to visit the town of Mydlovary and Zahaji in August of this year.  The mayor of the town gave him the book where these translations mention my ancestors.  Any help that you can give is greatly appreciated since trying to make these translations from different dictionaries is difficult to say the least.\r\n\r\nThanks Again, Tony', '2004-10-07 07:32:19', 1436, 0),
(1438, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Sure. \r\n\r\nIn the year 1803, Jan Pecka passed on his business to his son MatÄj, who was at that time serving in the imperial artillery. He left his son a couple of mares, a couple of oxen, 2 cows, 1 heifer, 8 sheep, 1 sow, and tools with a total value of 400 gold pieces and 40 krejcars.  Counted together with the farm, the inherited property had a sum value of 843 gold pieces and 5 krejcars. MatÄj Pecka was born on January 8th, 1770, and married Mariana KrejcarovĂĄ [heh... her last name is from the word krejcar...] from Zbudov. The widow Mariana passed on the business in 1844 to her son VojtÄch Pecka, for 412 gold and 25 krejcars. In 1876, the estate\'s owner was Johanka PeckovĂĄ, nĂŠe NovĂĄkovĂĄ, from ZĂĄhajĂ­ [I think those are the accent marks - but then you should know in any case].', '2004-10-07 17:46:41', 1437, 0),
(1439, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Slight correction -- the occurrences of \"couple\" above should read \"pair\" (i.e. a breeding pair).\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-10-07 17:48:23', 1438, 0),
(1440, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'oh wait, that makes no sense either. Well, whatever.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-10-07 17:48:54', 1439, 0),
(1441, 13, 'Tony', 'Goodlife1218@aol.com', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Hi Erik\r\n\r\nCan you shead som light on these words for me:  pozustale, prizenil, mela, vyhorela, pustil, kterou and vrchnost.\r\n\r\nThis sentence also has me stumped.  Po nem se teto mydlovarske zivnosti zacalo rikat \"U Pecku\"  \r\n\r\nHere is what I came up with; After___ with this Mydlovar business ___ tell \"___ Pecka\"\r\n\r\nRoku 1663 pro chudobu nesplacel prodily.  In the year 1663 because of poverty _____ sell.\r\n\r\nI dont understand how uvadi is used in this sentence.  1670 uvadi gruntovnice: \"Prijdouc Lukas o sve zdravi, pustil 14. kvetna 1670 grunt Ambrozovi Kovari za 300 kop\"  \r\n\r\nHere is what I have: 1670 introduced the farmers wife: \"Prijdouc Lukas over her health, _____14. _______ 1670 the entire farm to Ambrose Kovari for 300 kopas\"', '2004-10-13 21:23:45', 1440, 1),
(1442, 17, 'Milena Kasic', 'milencekasic@hotmail.com', '', 'Ahoj!\r\nChtela bych trosicku diskutovat o falecnych pratelech (mislim ze to neni spravny pad!!!) Nekdo mi rekl ze nejaky Polak na Ceske Televize rekl neco jako : Ceska Republika pachne zahodem! Chtel rict ze ceska republuka voni Zapadem ale v Polstine pachne znamena voni a zahod zapad!!!!!!   ', '2004-10-14 15:48:45', 0, 0),
(1443, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Words can take on a lot of various endings in Czech, so I\'ve cut off the endings for some of the words you want help on and replaced them with \"-\".\r\n\r\npozĹŻstal- : adjective/noun meaning \"relative of the deceased.\"\r\npĹiÂenil: married into.\r\nvyhoĹel-: burned down (even though it\'s formed from \"out/up\"+\"burned\").\r\npustil-: various meanings, anything from \"let go\" to \"let in\" to \"let out\" to things like \"got into\" or \"took on\" (when used with the word \"se\" -- \"him/herself\")\r\nkter-: that/which.\r\nvrchnost: feudal lord, as far as I can tell. Whoever was the local power-that-be, I guess.\r\n\r\n<b>Po nem se teto mydlovarske zivnosti zacalo rikat \"U Pecku\"</b><br>\r\nOK, actual Czechs reading this will probably laugh at me for one of the interpretations below, but I\'m not sure which of the following this means, without the context:\r\n\"After this [event], this Mydlovary estate [AHA! I should have been calling Âivnosti \"estates\" in the previous replies, rather than businesses] began to take on the folk name \"U PeckĹŻ.\"\r\n-or-\r\n\"this Mydlovary estate began to take on the name \'U PeckĹŻ\' in honor of him.\" (I.e. his name was Mr. Pecka, so it began to take on the folk name \"U PeckĹŻ\", that is, \"The Peckas\'.\"\r\n\r\n(I understand your confusion with the word \"ĹĂ­kat,\" whose use here has nothing to do with its usual meaning of \"talk.\" At least not directly. Something-3rd case + se ĹĂ­kat + some name = something has such and such a folk name -- \"it\'s said\" (ĹĂ­kĂĄ se) that way. Also used for \"you sure can call x a y.\")\r\n\r\n<b>Roku 1663 pro chudobu nesplacel prodily.</b><br>\r\nHeh, you\'re better at this than you think, that is: you got the non-obvious first part right. The second part, well, a Google search for prodĂ­l comes up empty. Maybe it\'s \"podĂ­l\" -- share -- instead? Or maybe the word is real, but just too obscure to be anywhere on the Czech web -- certainly no impossibility.\r\n\r\n<b>1670 uvadi gruntovnice: \"Prijdouc Lukas o sve zdravi, pustil 14. kvetna 1670 grunt Ambrozovi Kovari za 300 kop\"</b><br>\r\nA 1670 entry in the land record book (deed of registers or whatever we call it) states: \"LukĂĄÂ, having lost his good health, has bequeathed [yeah, outdated word - hey, that use of pustit in Czech is outdated too :-)] his <i>grunt</i> -- his <i>Grund</i>, his ground, his land -- to AmbroÂ KovĂĄĹ for 300 kopas on May 14th, 1670.\"\r\n\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-10-14 21:17:14', 1441, 0),
(1444, 13, 'Tony', 'Goodlife1218@aol.com', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Hey Erik\r\n\r\nIt appears that your have a good command of the Czech language.  How did you learn the language, are you living in the Czech Republic?  Now that I have confirmed where my ancestors originated, my wife and I are hoping to make a return trip to the Prague and visit the town of Mydlovary where my grandfather was born.  \r\n\r\nMy cousin made the trip this past August and some of the translations you have been helping with are a result of his trip.  My grandfathers house, Mydlovary 13 is still standing however it is no longer in the family.\r\n\r\nI would like to be able to have a working knowlege of the language, what would be a good way to begin.  ', '2004-10-15 20:49:11', 1443, 0),
(1445, 13, 'katka', 'k.knotova@email.cz', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'ahoj eriku, chtÄla jsem se vrhnout na pĹeklad tÄch nÄkdy i pro Äecha zĂĄludnĂ˝ch vÄt, ale vidim, Âe nenĂ­ tĹeba. neÄetla jsem sice ĂşplnÄ vÂechno, nicmĂŠnÄ i tak ti musim sloÂit poklonu. Äesky teda vĂĄlĂ­Â! jak ses to prosimtÄ nauÄil? kdyby sis ÄistÄ nĂĄhodou s nÄÄĂ­m nevÄdÄl rady, tak se ozvi a rĂĄda ti poradim. katka', '2004-10-18 03:05:13', 1443, 1),
(1446, 13, 'katka', 'k.knotova@email.cz', 'Re: Checkmark', 'one more  correction, the spelling is hĂĄÄek /pronounced as - my guess of english transcription- haatschek/. hĂĄÄek also means this \" Ä \"diacritic sign above letter \"c\" in this word', '2004-10-18 03:16:17', 1427, 1),
(1447, 13, 'katka', 'k.knotova@email.cz', 'Re: infinitiv, intrumentĂĄl', 'tkaĂĄti je podmÄtem bych teda Ĺekla jen tehdy, kdyÂ bych to chtÄla setsakramentsky zdĹŻraznit, napĹ. bzch to nÄkomu vysvÄtlovala. jinak se bÄÂnÄ ĹĂ­kĂĄ \"...je podmÄt\"', '2004-10-18 03:28:29', 1396, 1),
(1448, 13, 'katka', '', 'Re: Translation', 'hi, how did you come up with this?: \' is a bottom left to top right accent. do you write it that way? the normal way of writing our ÄĂĄrka is from the top to the bottom.', '2004-10-18 03:44:04', 1361, 1),
(1449, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'I\'m afraid I can\'t provide advice on a good way to begin learning the language well from over in the States. (I live in the CR). Besides formal learning, I read a lot of children\'s books, which isn\'t really an option over there. I think you can find a lot of ideas on this very site, however, including earlier entries on the forum (if you change the settings, you can see pretty far back in time).\r\n\r\nI\'m sorry I can\'t help more.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-10-18 18:46:41', 1444, 0),
(1450, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'HlavnÄ by mÄ zajĂ­malo, jestli \"po nÄm\" v poslednĂ­m pĹekladu znamenalo \"po udĂĄlosti z pĹedchozĂ­, necitovanĂŠ vÄty\" nebo \"po panu Peckovi\". :-)\r\n\r\nA dĂ­ky. Jsem tady jedenĂĄct let (tak dlouho?!?), moc by se mi tady bez tĂŠ ÄeÂtiny ani nelĂ­bilo. :-)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nJĂŠĂŠ, zapomnÄl jsem dĂĄt jmĂŠno, a pak to nÄjak ani nezaregistrovalo. Snad to teÄka nebude dvakrĂĄt...\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-10-18 19:07:02', 1445, 0),
(1451, 17, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: faleÂnĂŠ pĹĂĄtelĂŠ', 'VĂ­m, Âe to asi do fora o ÄeÂtinÄ nepatĹĂ­, ale znĂĄm hodnÄ faleÂnĂ˝ch pĹĂĄtelech ve smÄru ÄeÂtina --> angliÄtina. \r\n\r\nNejzloÄinneÄtÄjÂĂ­: actualize, actual (od aktualizovat) -- mĂĄ to bĂ˝t current, relevant, apod.\r\n\r\nK tomu pĹĂ­bÄh: jedna mnou pĹĂ­liÂ nemilovanĂĄ firma na zaÄĂĄtku dÄjin ÄeskĂŠho webu mÄla kromÄ obvyklĂ˝ch public relations lÂĂ­ jeÂtÄ \"actual news\" (znamenĂĄ \"skuteÄnĂŠ  zprĂĄvy)... Äet jsem si to a Ĺek si \"aspoĹ nÄjakĂŠ skuteÄnĂŠ zprĂĄvy mĂ­sto jen lÂĂ­\"!\r\n\r\nDĂĄlÂĂ­ most wanted: \r\n\r\nrealise (aspoĹ co se ameriÄtiny tyÄe, britÂtina nevĂ­m 100-pro) -- mĂĄ to bĂ˝t execute, implement, do, nebo vĹŻbec to slovo vynechat (realizovat zaĹizovĂĄnĂ­ \r\norganizace --> organize :-), nebo pĹinejhorÂĂ­m coordinate the organizing of). AmE znamenĂĄ \"realize\" jen \"uvÄdomit se\" aÂ na pĂĄr hodnÄ specifickĂ˝ch vyjĂ­mek, kde to jde pouÂĂ­t \"Äesky\" (to realize a dream)\r\n\r\ncontrol (aspoĹ co se ameriÄtiny tyÄe, britÂtina nevĂ­m 100-pro) - mĂĄ to bĂ˝t check, verify, inspect. \"Control\" znamenĂĄ \"Ĺidit\".\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-10-18 19:17:01', 1442, 0),
(1452, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'ObÄ moÂnosti jsou ve hĹe. ZĂĄleÂĂ­ skuteÄnÄ na kontextu.\r\n\r\nV roce 1666 doÂlo k poÂĂĄru. Po nÄm se ... - t.j. po poÂĂĄru\r\n\r\nV roce 1666 usedlost koupil p. Pecka. Po nÄm se ... - t.j. po p. Peckovi\r\n\r\nnebo dokonce\r\n\r\nV roce 1666 doÂlo k poÂĂĄru, kterĂ˝ zaloÂil LukĂĄÂ Pecka. Po nÄm se ... - nejednoznaÄnĂŠ :-)\r\n\r\nBTW, pochybuji, Âe v roce 1670 bylo sprĂĄvnÄ: PĹijdouc LukĂĄÂ o svĂŠ zdravĂ­, pustil ...\r\n\r\nSprĂĄvnÄ je (a snad i bylo v r. 1670 - aÂ na pravopis): PĹiÂed o svĂŠ zdravĂ­, LukĂĄÂ pustil ...\r\n\r\nnebo KdyÂ byl pĹiÂel o svĂŠ zdravĂ­ (= plusquamperf.), LukĂĄÂ pustil ...', '2004-10-18 20:38:15', 1450, 0),
(1453, 13, 'katka', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', ' troufĂĄm si ĹĂ­ct, Âe s tĂŠmÄĹ stoprocentnĂ­ pravdÄpodobnostĂ­ jde o vĂ˝znam, Âe se Âivnosti ĹĂ­kalo u PeckĹŻ po tom Peckovi. chalupy menily jmĂŠna podle majitelĹŻ dost Äasto. koneckoncĹŻ, i kdyby to bylo po poÂĂĄru, pro pĹejmenovĂĄnĂ­ Âivnosti musel bĂ˝t jinĂ˝ dĹŻvod neÂ samotnĂ˝ poÂĂĄr.\r\n', '2004-10-19 00:14:51', 1452, 1),
(1454, 13, 'Tony', 'Goodlife1218@aol.com', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Here is another line from the archives.\r\n\r\nPozdejsi pisari pripisovali do berni ruly poznamky a z nich se muzeme napr. dozvedet, kdy ktera hospodarstvi vyhorela:\r\n\r\n-- roku 1671 vyhorely usedlosti:  Pecka, Kaplan, Kocian\r\n\r\n-- roku 1709 vyhorela temer cela ves; vyhorely usedlosti:  Erhart, Stefl, Tesar, Hasek, Ondok, Krejci, Mochtl, Pecka, Kaplan, Kocian, Klikar', '2004-10-20 03:00:56', 1449, 1),
(1455, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'Nejde o to, po kom a proÄ se nazĂ˝vĂĄ cosi \"U PeckĹŻ\", ale o to, jak pĹeloÂit vĂ˝raz \"po nÄm\". Erik sprĂĄvnÄ poznamenal, Âe bez znalosti pĹedchozĂ­ vÄty nenĂ­ pĹeklad do angliÄtiny jednoznaÄnĂ˝. ZĂĄjmeno \"nÄm\" mĹŻÂe zastupovat cokoliv muÂskĂŠho nebo stĹednĂ­ho rodu, pokud je to zmĂ­nÄno v pĹedchozĂ­ vÄtÄ (tu ale neznĂĄme!). JĂĄ jsem pouze spekuloval, jak by mohla pĹedchozĂ­ vÄta vypadat, aby to mÄlo vliv na pĹeklad.', '2004-10-20 17:46:08', 1453, 0),
(1456, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', '> BTW, pochybuji, Âe v roce 1670 bylo sprĂĄvnÄ: PĹijdouc LukĂĄÂ o svĂŠ zdravĂ­, pustil ... \r\n\r\n...a jsi si jistĂĄ, Âe tenkrĂĄt jeÂtÄ nebyli ĂşĹednĂ­ci-katovĂŠ ÄeÂtiny? :-))\r\n\r\nerik', '2004-10-20 17:47:13', 1452, 0),
(1457, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech Genealogical Help', 'First off: in the Czech blah blah that\'s been piling up, we\'ve been discussing whether the estate named \"The Peckas\'\" received its name \"after Mr. Pecka\" or \"after some event.\" Verdict: it really is ambiguous without knowledge of the preceding sentence.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nRegarding the latest translation:\r\n\r\nLater scribes added comments to the tax records [literally \"taxroll\"], and from these we can learn, for example, which estates burned down, and when:\r\n\r\n-- in 1671, these estates burned down: Pecka, Kaplan, KociĂĄn\r\n\r\n-- in 1709, nearly the entirely village burned down. Specifically, these estates burned down: Erhart, Stefl, Tesar, Hasek, Ondok, Krejci, Mochtl, Pecka, Kaplan, Kocian, Klikar. [Ouch!]\r\n\r\nErik\r\n', '2004-10-20 17:58:40', 1454, 0),
(1458, 13, 'stelingo', 'stelingouk@aol.com', 'nastavany', 'Can anybody tell me what the word \'nastavany\' means please? Can\'t find it in my dictionary.', '2004-10-22 03:24:44', 0, 1),
(1459, 13, 'stelingo', 'stelingouk@aol.com', 'Re: zapĹedenou', '\"Petr je uÄitelem.\" (Petr is a teacher.) \r\n\r\nVladimir, you say the instrumental is necessary in this type of sentence but I have seen/heard many examples where the nom is used, including my grammar books i.e. \'Petr je uÄitel\'', '2004-10-22 03:31:30', 1393, 1),
(1460, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: nastavany', 'A czech will probably correct me here, but it seems to me it\'s most likely a typo,  and the word should have been\r\n\r\nnastavenĂ˝\r\n\r\nor\r\n\r\nnaÂtvanĂ˝.\r\n\r\nIs the word also accentless in the text you have? If it\'s not, then it will be fairly easy to guess which typo is there, based on there being a \"Â\" (then it\'s naÂtvanĂ˝) or a \"s\" (then it\'s nastavenĂ˝).', '2004-10-22 15:41:15', 1458, 0),
(1461, 17, 'Milan', 'schonderg@aol.com', 'Re: Komicka Hra an Slova v Ruznych Jazycich', 'Ahoj Mileno,\r\nRad jsem cetl Vas vtipnou slovni hru. Moc jsem se ji zasmal protoze me se strasne libi tato forma humoru. Mam take par znamych Polaku a ten Vas vtip se jim take moc libil. Lidi kteri mlvi vic cizich jazyku maji lepsi pojem o zivote.\r\nDovolet abych se trchu predstavil, ja jsem se narodil v Cechach a emigroval jsem s rodici do spojenych Statu v roce 1966. Rodice pred par lety zemreli a ted Cesky troch zapominam coz nemam s kym mluvit.\r\nVase vyjadreni \"o falecnych pratelech\" je ve spravnem padu ale ja myslim ze se to rika \"o falesnych pratelich\" a to s ve falesnych je jako anglicke \"sh\" (nemame hacky na psaci klavesnici.\r\nJa jsem ted akorat poznal ze vetsina mych \"pratel\" byly falesni protoze jsem se akorat rozvedl z manzelkou a tak jem tim stratil vetsinu pratelu a tak hledam nove. \r\nZdelte mi prosim od kud Jste a ktere jazycky mluvite.\r\nJa mluvim Anlicky, Madarky a Rusky a trochu Hebrejsy a Yiddish.\r\nS Pozdravem, \r\nMilan.', '2004-10-28 01:21:17', 1442, 1),
(1462, 17, 'Milan S', 'schonderg@aol.com', 'Re: MĹŻj oblĂ­benĂ˝ ÄeskĂ˝ film', 'Souhlasim, ze Obchod na Kurze je krasny klasicky film. Moje oblibene filmy jsou filmy komicke direktora Jiriho Weisse jako Murder Czech Style (nevzpominam si jak se to jmenuje cesky) a novejsi filmy Jiriho Menzla jako Vesnicko Ma Strediskova oba s Rudolfem Hrusinskym.', '2004-10-28 02:15:55', 444, 1),
(1463, 13, 'Tony', 'Goodlife1218@sbcglobal.net', 'History', 'Erik\r\n\r\nJust changed my computer system and upgraded to a dsl service, so my email address has aslo changed.\r\n\r\nI need somemore help with this sentence:  Opis schwarqenberskeho sumare, porizeneho roku 1685 za casu knizete Ferdinanda Arnosta ze Schwarzenbergu a hejtmana Eliase Frantiska z Heidlbeku pisarem Ondrejem Schank - Frankstatterem.\r\n\r\nAlso, I cant find any reference to the work neslo in any of my dictionaries, the \"s\" has a u shaped mark above it.\r\n\r\nTony', '2004-10-30 03:16:37', 0, 0),
(1464, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: History', '> Just changed my computer system and upgraded to a dsl service, so my email address has aslo changed.\r\n\r\nNo problem. Above all it\'s a good thing that we\'ve started a new thread, as the old one was stretching most of the way across the screen. :-)\r\n\r\n> Opis schwarqenberskeho sumare, porizeneho roku 1685 za casu knizete Ferdinanda Arnosta ze Schwarzenbergu a hejtmana Eliase Frantiska z Heidlbeku pisarem Ondrejem Schank - Frankstatterem.\r\n\r\nTranscription of the Schwarqenberg [more likely actually Schwarzenberg] Summary [??], prepared in 1865 in the time of Count* Ferdinand ArnoÂt of Schwarzenberg and Marshall EliĂĄÂ FrantiÂek of Heidlbek [Heidelberg?] by the scribe OndĹej Schank-Frankstatter.\r\n\r\n* knĂ­Âe is often translated as prince, but overfrequently translating knĂ­Âe as prince seems to be a Czechism, and this guy being a prince doesn\'t make much sense to me.\r\n\r\n> Also, I cant find any reference to the work neslo in any of my dictionaries, the \"s\" has a u shaped mark above it.\r\n\r\nIt\'s no wonder, since it\'s very different from the infinitive, and of course the infinitives are used as headwords. It\'s the negative past tense of \"jĂ­t\", to go. Note that just like the English \"go,\" it\'s used in a lot of idioms, so its meaning in a given sentence may not actually be \"did not go.\" For example, \"neÂlo to obnovit\" means \"it-was-not-possible it to-renew,\" that is, \"it [or that] could not be renewed [or restored].\"\r\n\r\nCzech is a lot like English (and French, and probably most languages) in that the more fundamental a word is, the more likely it is to be quite irregular, grammar-wise... unfortunately. Usage and words are a bit like water and rocks... the former in large quantities tends to deform the latter over time. (Sometimes it\'s actually a case of a much-used word keeping an old grammar structure that dies out for the rest of the language, but the end result for the poor learner is the same.)\r\n\r\nI remember being quite frustrated by this when I was learning Czech since, unlike the case with French, I had only limited formal education in the language.\r\n\r\nA common trick of mine when I can\'t find a word in the dictionaries is to do a google search for:\r\n\r\n[the word in question, without these square brackets] \"English version\" [with the quotes]\r\n\r\nIf I\'m lucky, this pulls up a page or two twinned with an easily accessible English version of itself, and I can see how the word tends to be translated (though given the sorry state of the field of translation in the CR, this sometimes needs to be taken with a grain of salt). \r\n\r\nYou can sometimes also get some good results from the volny.cz dictionary. I can\'t seem to create a link that successfully leads straight to it, so:\r\n\r\n- visit web.volny.cz\r\n- click the \"slovnĂ­k\" tab in the group of tabs towards the top.\r\n\r\nThis is actually just Volny\'s branding of the Millenium dictionary, which, though short on quality (and allegedly ethics, as much of it is allegedly scan-stolen), is long on content. Naturally, it also uses infinitives and nominative singular as headwords, which is a pain for learners, but it includes a lot of phrases, and sometimes you\'ll luck out and one of them will contain the conjugation or declension, respectively, that you\'re working with.\r\n\r\nYou can also find many resources and links to resources in other parts of this website (Bohemica), of course!\r\n\r\nHope that helps!\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-11-01 16:18:46', 1463, 0),
(1465, 13, 'SwierQ', 'swierszcz@irc.pl', 'Translation! PLS Help', 'I nedd a Birthday grettings in Czech language. If somebody could write for me short grettings i will be plesed. my mail is swierszcz@irc.pl . Pls Help its very important for me!', '2004-11-04 19:45:18', 0, 0),
(1466, 13, 'Tony', 'Goodlife1218@sbcglobal.net', 'Re: History', 'I could use some help with this one!!\r\n\r\nRoku 1565 utratil hlubocky purkrabi a pisar Tomas ve Zbudove 12 grosu, kdyz jednali u tamniho rychtare ohledne sirotcich zalezetosti.\r\n\r\nHere is something that my cousin and I werent sure about.  When did the Communist Party come into power in the CR and did it have any effect on the small family farms in areas such as Mydlovary?  \r\n\r\nThanks, Tony', '2004-11-06 03:43:02', 1464, 1),
(1467, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: History', '> Roku 1565 utratil hlubocky purkrabi a pisar Tomas ve Zbudove 12 grosu, kdyz jednali u tamniho rychtare ohledne sirotcich zalezetosti.\r\n\r\nIn 1565, the proconsul/burgrave[1] and scribe TomĂĄÂ in Zbudov spent 12 groÂ when they were negotiating with that town\'s reeve/viscount [2] on matters related to orphans.\r\n\r\n[1] These are the two translations I found for this word. Considering I really have no idea what a proconsul or burgrave actually *are*, I suppose it\'s all the same...\r\n\r\n[2] Ditto.\r\n\r\n> Here is something that my cousin and I werent sure about. \r\n\r\n> When did the Communist Party come into power in the CR...\r\n\r\nFebruary 1948 (\"Red February,\" \"Victorious February\").\r\n\r\n> And did it have any effect on the small family farms in areas such as Mydlovary?\r\n\r\nGenerally, they ceased to exist. During collectivization, nearly everyone added their farmland to the cooperatives (JZDs), whether voluntarily, via confiscation, or simply under threat.\r\n\r\nHere\'s a nice introduction to that period, in English:\r\n\r\nhttp://archiv.radio.cz/history/history13.html\r\n\r\nAnd here\'s a bit more on collectivization specifically, thanks to totalita.cz (\"totalitarian-state.cz\").\r\n\r\nStage One - 1949\r\n\r\nOn Feb. 23rd, the Communist-controlled parliament accepted, in accordance with a resolution of the ĂV KSÄ [the Central Committee of the Czechoslovak Communist Party -- the Communists were VERY acronym-happy, and had to be, frankly, because everything had a bureaucratic, overlong name], the Act on Unified Agricultural Cooperatives (JZDs).\r\n\r\n[That is: after a year of mucking up more important things, the Communist leaders got around to mucking up agriculture. They did so via the usual path: they wrote a resolution, and Parliament rubber-stamped it into law.]\r\n\r\nThe campaign promoting creation of JZDs was launched quickly: in April. The campaign did not, however, meet with a positive response, and did not bring the expected results. At the end of 1949, only 2,039 JZDs existed, and these were generally Type I. [Dunno what the differences between the types was, but I guess we can assume that Type I was a weak type.]\r\n\r\nNeedless to say, the JZDs\' chairmen were installed on the basis of decisions by the district committees of the KSÄ.\r\n\r\n[On the totalita site, the text is interrupted here by a nice, or rather \"nice,\" photo of two poor souls being tried as saboteurs of the national economy for the felony of \"grain feeding\"; my hunch is that they fed their livestock with their grain rather than giving it up to the JZD, but really God only knows.]\r\n\r\n\r\nStage Two (1950)\r\n\r\nIn this year, it was ordered that all JZD\'s be transformed into Types 3 and 4. This met with great resistance, whose outcome was an attempt at mass exit from the JZDs.\r\n\r\nThe Communists responded with the \"buying up\" [\"vĂ˝kup\" -- quotes are authors\'] of agricultural equipment. The majority of machines was \"vykoupena\", but with no economic effect. The machines were immediately taken out of operation and sent to the scrap piles. The [JZDs\'] machinery stations* did not utilize them.\r\n\r\n\r\nStage Three (1952)\r\n\r\nOn June 3rd, 1952, a resolution of \"the party and the cabinet\" [as the phrase of the era went] was issued regarding further development of JZDs. What came next was quite simply a head-on attack on villages and their inhabitants. The attack was led in each district by a three- to four-member committee that was an attache of the district committee [OV] of the Communist Party.\r\n\r\nBrigades of functionaries rode out to rural areas, visited farmers, and \"convinced\" them to join the JZDs. The methods used for convincing became ever harsher. Most of the new JZDs were formed against the farmers\' will and in the face of farmers\' resistance.\r\n\r\n\r\n* Machinery Stations\r\n\r\nState organization newly founded in 1948, which owned agricultural machinery. Shipments of machines from these stations for the private sector did not exist.\r\n\r\n...Looking back at totalita (as I have been translating using a copy of the text that I pasted into a text editor), I see it offers a few of the definitions I regretted not having above (like the various Types). But this is already a bit long for an unsolicited explanation. :-)\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-11-08 16:28:14', 1466, 0),
(1468, 13, 'Clara', 'cvance75@aol.com', 'Translation needed Czech to English Kubicek Grave stone', 'I have been reseaching my family history and found the grave stone of my Great Grandparents Terezie and Josef Kubicek it is a two sided stone and on Tereze side it says ZDE ODPO CIVA Tereze Kubicek  ROZENA V ROKU 1855 ZEMRELA V ROKU 1901. Also there is a small stone that says MATKA. Can anyone can translate these words to English Please..Thank You,Clara', '2004-11-08 23:03:33', 0, 1),
(1469, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Translation needed Czech to English Kubicek Grave stone', 'Hello Clara,\r\n\r\nSure. \r\n\r\nThe inscription on the main stone reads:\r\n\r\nHERE RESTS Tereza KubĂ­Äek BORN 1855 DIED 1901.\r\n\r\nThe small stone reads:\r\nMOTHER\r\n\r\nBest regards,\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-11-09 15:15:11', 1468, 0),
(1470, 13, 'Clara', 'cvance75@aol.com', '', 'Erik, Thank you !!!Clara', '2004-11-09 21:24:26', 0, 1),
(1471, 17, 'Jan VanÄk jr.', 'jan . vanek . jr @ gmail', 'Re: faleÂnĂŠ pĹĂĄtelĂŠ', 'Naopak je jich taky spousta: eventually -> eventuĂĄlnÄ, nepĹĂ­jemnÄ se rozmĂĄhĂĄ pathetic -> patetickĂ˝ (ve smyslu ubohĂ˝, opovrÂenĂ­hodnĂ˝) a na spoustu dalÂĂ­ch si teÄ samozĹejmÄ nevzpomenu, i kdyÂ si na nÄ narĂĄÂĂ­m v jednom kuse.\r\n\r\nV roce 1990 a pak 1996 vyÂla celĂĄ knĂ­Âka ZrĂĄdnĂĄ slova v angliÄtinÄ (Josef HladkĂ˝, SPN); o SparlingovÄ English or Czenglish? ani nemluvÄ. A koukĂĄm, Âe existujĂ­ i ZrĂĄdnĂĄ slova v polÂtinÄ a ÄeÂtinÄ.', '2004-11-25 19:31:26', 1451, 0),
(1472, 17, 'milan', 'mynamemilan@seznam.cz', '', 'Hello everybody\r\nI don\'t know if you could help me but I told myself I could try it.My name is Milan and at the moment I am living in a nice city Bournemouth which is in the south of England.I seem to stay here for a longer time so I was wondering if there was any Englishman I could teach Czech.If you knew about anyone please let me know and write.Thanks a lot.\r\nMilan', '2004-12-04 16:45:51', 0, 0),
(1473, 13, 'Arzuv', 'famousasia@yahoo.com', 'I am looking for someone to study czech with', 'Dear all,\r\n\r\nI would like to invite anyone who would be interested in studying with me czech. I am planning on starting as soon as possible, the only problem is that there aren\'t any courses starting at this time and only after two months it is too late for me. \r\n\r\nThank you very much in advance!\r\n', '2004-12-14 19:38:47', 0, 0),
(1474, 17, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: faleÂnĂŠ pĹĂĄtelĂŠ', 'JĂŠ, dlouho jsem v tĂŠto ÄĂĄsti sajtu nebyl, takÂe toto mi uÂlo. Napadl mÄ jeÂtÄ sympathetic -] sympatickĂ˝ -- dalÂĂ­ pĹĂ­pad, kde pĹinejmenÂĂ­m DE, CZ, NL a FR jsou zajedno, jen angliÄtina ne. Mimochodem Äasto narĂĄÂĂ­m na rozhovory ÄechĹŻ a dalÂĂ­ch EvropanĹŻ, kterĂ˝m bych nerozumÄl nebĂ˝t zkuÂenostĂ­ s jinĂ˝mi evropskĂ˝mi jazyky neÂ angliÄtina.\r\n\r\nErik\r\n\r\nP.S. Sparling je bĹŻh, v ideĂĄlnĂ­m svÄtÄ by jeho knĂ­Âka byla zĂĄsadnĂ­ souÄastĂ­ naprosto kaÂdĂŠho kurzu angliÄtiny v Äesku.\r\n\r\nErik', '2004-12-20 21:27:10', 1471, 0),
(1475, 13, 'Lada', 'lada_moskva@rambler.ru', 'question about past tense', 'Ahoj pĹĂĄtelĂŠ!\r\n\r\ncould somebody help me?\r\nI know the rule how to make past tense, for example one must say \"byli jsme doma\", but at the same time i saw such sentence:\r\nvÄera byla dÄvÄata v divadle , so my question is where is \"jsou\"? and are both variants with \"jsou\" and without it right?\r\n\r\nDÄkuji.', '2004-12-29 21:16:00', 0, 0),
(1476, 13, '&#51060;&#47492;&#50630;&#51020;', '', 'Please recommend...', 'I want learn Czech more...\r\nPlease recommend some FREE sites...\r\n(Because here is little bit...--)', '2005-01-02 14:29:56', 0, 0),
(1477, 13, 'Jeremy', 'jking@fst.zcu.cz', 'degree equivalents', 'I\'m trying to find equivalents for Czech and English university degrees.It\'s starting to get a bit tricky! Does anyone know if a list exists? ', '2005-01-03 16:00:09', 0, 1),
(1478, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: question about past tense', '> where is \"jsou\"?\r\n\r\nFor 3rd person, the helper verb is unnecessary (and even forbidden) for forming the past tense.\r\n\r\n> and are both variants with \"jsou\" and without it right? \r\n\r\nOnly without.\r\n\r\nIncidentally, you\'ll occasionally also see this form:\r\n\r\nJĂĄ byl nemocnĂ˝.\r\n\r\nLeaving out the helper verb when you leave in the personal pronoun like this is also permissible, although rare. I wouldn\'t recommend you use it yourself, however, as it often sounds strange, and I can\'t say exactly when that \"often\" is.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-03 17:37:36', 1475, 0),
(1479, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: degree equivalents', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nI don\'t, but perhaps I can recommend some equivalents for particular words if you mention some?\r\n\r\nKeep in mind that not only are Czech and British degrees different, but so are British and American, so it\'s a real mess...\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-03 17:39:59', 1477, 0),
(1480, 13, 'Lada', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'DÄkuji Erik!\r\nCould you(or someone else) please answer some more questions though not about past tense?\r\n\r\nwhat is the difference between rudĂ˝ a ÄervenĂ˝? why do we say RudĂŠ namÄstĂ­ ALE ÄervenĂŠ vĂ­no?\r\n\r\nand what is the difference between anebo a nebo?\r\n\r\nThank you in advance.', '2005-01-03 22:00:29', 1478, 0),
(1481, 13, 'Jeremy', 'jking@zcu.fst.cz', 'Re: degree equivalents', 'Hi Erik\r\nI know Ing is similar to a master\'s and Bc is similar to a bachelor\'s degree (please correct me if I\'m wrong!), butwhat about Mgr,  MUDr,  JUDr, CSc  and Doc. ? I\'m looking for British English equivalents if possible...Thanks very much for your interest\r\nJeremy', '2005-01-04 17:43:47', 1479, 1),
(1482, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'Unlike in English, the color associated with Communism in Czech (and perhaps not only in Czech) is not red, but scarlet... perhaps because it is closer to the color of blood (the blood shed by the struggling workers, of course). \"RudĂ˝\" means scarlet. (Social democracy, meanwhile, is associated with the color pink. Of course -- we all know they\'re just pinkos in disguise...)\r\n\r\n> and what is the difference between anebo and a nebo? \r\n\r\nUhhh... good question. Well, I see that anebo gets 857,000 hits in Google versus only 490,000 for a nebo. My feeling is that a nebo is closer to the English \"and/or,\" whereas anebo is closer in meaning to the English phrase \"or perhaps.\"\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-04 21:41:25', 1480, 0),
(1483, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: degree equivalents', 'I\'m an American, so I can\'t give reliable British terms. Perhaps you\'ll be helped at least a little from this viewpoint. of an American immigrant to the CR.\r\n\r\nIng. is like a master\'s degree for a <i>technical</i> field, whereas Mgr. is like a master\'s degree for an <i>arts/humanities</i> field.\r\n\r\nI too would compare Bc to our Bachelor\'s.\r\n\r\nJUDr. is a law doctorate, and MUDr. a medical doctorate.\r\n\r\nDoc. I\'m not sure, though I do know it\'s something that can be in addition to the ordinary degrees. It might stand for Docent, which is, uh, docent. Whatever that is. (Czechs? Please help...?) \r\n\r\nCSc... crap, I don\'t know. Aha, here\'s some fellow being a blowhard and going on about it in the discussion forum on President Klaus\'s site a while back:\r\n\r\n[...] CSc [means] Candidatus Scientiarum, and it\'s the former equivalent of today\'s PhD. \r\n\r\n(Do keep in mind that information you acquire for free on the Internet is worth almost as much as you paid for it.)\r\n\r\nOne interesting side note -- from an American\'s perspective, the Czechs are absolute degree nuts. If a fellow is a MUDr. Doc Whatchamacallit, he makes double extra sure to include all the titles every time he mentions his names and heads off to mudrovat (to talk like a blowhard) :-) Case in point: a search for all five abbreviations \"bc csc judr mudr doc\" (without the quotes) will bring up an awe-inspiring 8,520 hits on Google.', '2005-01-04 21:56:33', 1481, 0),
(1484, 13, 'Lada', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'Thank you for your answers!\r\nTo tell the truth i am surprised that its scarlet colour which is associated with revolution but Red squere (in Moscow) doesnt deal with it anyhow, i am a bit confused...\r\nalso as i am not native english speaker, i dont know who are pinkos...\r\n\r\nhere\'s another question:)\r\nif as you said \"byla jsem doma\" is wrong, but if we speak about things? you know in my book there s a rule that we must use always \"jsem, jsi, je, etc.\" in the past.\r\nAhoj!\r\n\r\n', '2005-01-04 22:11:10', 1482, 0),
(1485, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: degree equivalents', 'A. Graduates (alumni): 1. Bc.(bachelor) 2. Mgr.(magister=master) or Ing.(ingenieur) 3. Dr.(doctor)\r\n\r\nIng. - technical universities, e.g. ÄVUT, VÂCHT (chem. technology)\r\nMgr. - other universities, e.g. Charles University, not only for arts/humanities field, also mat./phys., biology, astronomy, pharmacy, etc.\r\n\r\nDr. is usually specified:\r\nRTDr. rerum technicarum doctor (very rare)\r\nRNDr. rerum naturalium doctor - mat. phys. biology\r\nPhDr.(philosophy), JUDr., MUDr., MVDr., PaedDr.(paedagogy), etc.\r\n\r\nB. university teachers: 1. (assistent) 2. Doc.(docent) 3. Prof.(professor)\r\n\r\nC. scientists: 1. -- 2. CSc.(candidatus scientiarum) 3. DrSc.(doctor scientiarum)\r\n\r\nYou can read:\r\n\r\nDoc. Ing. XY, CSc.\r\nProf. MUDr. XY, DrSc.', '2005-01-05 14:12:26', 1483, 0),
(1486, 13, 'Jeremy', '', 'Re: degree equivalents', 'You\'re a gent and a scholar!', '2005-01-05 14:47:32', 1483, 1),
(1487, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: degree equivalents', 'Erik was right. CSc. is now Ph.D. (philosophiae doctor)\r\n\r\nIn Czech:\r\n\r\nBc. - bakalĂĄĹ \r\nBcA. - bakalĂĄĹ umÄnĂ­ (... of arts)\r\n\r\nMgr. - magistr\r\nMgA. - magistr umÄnĂ­ (... of arts)\r\n\r\nJUDr. - doktor prĂĄv (z lat. juris utriusque doctor)\r\nMUDr. - doktor medicĂ­ny (z lat. medicinae universae doctor)\r\nMVDr. - doktor veterinĂĄrnĂ­ medicĂ­ny (z lat. medicinae veterinariae doctor)\r\nPaedDr. - doktor pedagogiky (z lat. paedagogiae doctor)\r\nPhMr. - magistr farmacie - dĹĂ­vÄjÂĂ­ titul (z lat. pharmaciae magister)\r\nPharmDr. - doktor farmacie\r\nPhDr. - doktor filozofie (z lat. philosophiae doctor)\r\nRNDr. - doktor pĹĂ­rodnĂ­ch vÄd (z lat. rerum naturalium doctor)\r\nRSDr. - doktor sociĂĄlnĂ­ch vÄd\r\nThLic. - licenciĂĄt teologie\r\nThDr. - doktor teologie\r\n\r\nIng. - inÂenĂ˝r\r\nIng. arch. - inÂenĂ˝r architekt\r\n\r\nakad. mal. - akademickĂ˝ malĂ­Ĺ (painter)\r\nakad. soch. - akademickĂ˝ sochaĹ (sculptor)\r\nakad. arch. - akademickĂ˝ architekt\r\n\r\nMBA - Master of Business Administration - titul zĂ­skanĂ˝ po absolvovĂĄnĂ­ specializovanĂŠho postgraduĂĄlnĂ­ho vzdÄlĂĄnĂ­ manaÂerskĂŠho\r\nEuroinÂenĂ˝r - titul mohou zĂ­skat po dvouletĂŠ praxi absolventi 21 studijnĂ­ch oborĹŻ VysokĂŠho uÄenĂ­ technickĂŠho v BrnÄ\r\n\r\nPo roce 1990 doÂlo ke zmÄnÄ v zĂ­skĂĄvĂĄnĂ­ titulĹŻ:\r\n\r\nIng., MUDr. a MVDr. - praxe pĹidÄlovĂĄnĂ­ titulĹŻ stejnĂĄ, zĂ­skĂĄvĂĄ se bez rigorĂłznĂ­ho ĹĂ­zenĂ­, tedy automaticky po absolvovĂĄnĂ­ pĹĂ­sluÂnĂŠ vysokĂŠ Âkoly\r\nMgr. - automaticky zĂ­skĂĄvajĂ­ absolventi univerzitnĂ­ch, umÄleckĂ˝ch a teologickĂ˝ch fakult\r\nDr. - je moÂnĂŠ zĂ­skat pouze po absolvovĂĄnĂ­ tĹĂ­letĂŠho postgraduĂĄlnĂ­ho studia\r\n\r\nVÄdeckĂŠ hodnosti - uvĂĄdÄjĂ­ se za jmĂŠnem:\r\nCSc. - kandidĂĄt vÄd (z lat. candidatus scientiarum)\r\nDrSc. - doktor vÄd (z lat. doctor scientiarum)\r\n(tituly CSc. a DrSc. se od 1. 7.1998 neudÄlujĂ­)\r\n\r\nDr. - doktor - se pĂ­Âe za jmĂŠnem a nahrazuje i tituly kandidĂĄt vÄd a doktor vÄd (CSc., DrSc.).\r\nPh.D. - titul Ph.D. se uvĂĄdĂ­ za jmĂŠnem, napĹ.: Ing. Jan NovĂĄk, Ph.D. (pozn.: \"Ph.D.\" psĂĄno bez mezery)\r\n- byl pĹevzat z anglosaskĂ˝ch zemĂ­\r\n- doslovnĂ˝ pĹeklad je doktor filozofie\r\n- udÄluje se absolventĹŻm studia v doktorskĂ˝ch studijnĂ­ch programech\r\n- je srovnatelnĂ˝ s dĹĂ­vÄjÂĂ­m titulem kandidĂĄt vÄd - CSc. \r\nTh.D. - doktor - v oblasti teologie\r\n\r\nZkratky vÄdecko-pedagogickĂŠ hodnosti profesor a docent se pĂ­ÂĂ­ s poÄĂĄteÄnĂ­m pĂ­smenem malĂ˝m (prof., doc.).\r\n\r\nDr.h.c. - doctor honoris causa - ÄestnĂ˝ doktor - titul udÄlovanĂ˝ bez zkouÂek za vynikajĂ­cĂ­ zĂĄsluhy o vÄdu, umÄnĂ­, stĂĄt apod.\r\n\r\nDiS. - diplomovanĂ˝ specialista v oboru, uvĂĄdĂ­ se za jmĂŠnem - zĂ­skĂĄvĂĄ ho obvykle absolvent vyÂÂĂ­ odbornĂŠ Âkoly po studiu\r\nzakonÄenĂŠm absolutoriem (obhĂĄjenĂ­ diplomovĂŠ prĂĄce)\r\n', '2005-01-05 19:16:25', 1486, 0),
(1488, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'RudĂ˝ is nearly the same colour as ÄervenĂ˝, but rudĂ˝ is more pathetic and elevated. So we have: RudĂ˝ ĹĂ­jen (Red October), RudĂĄ armĂĄda (Red Army), rudĂĄ hvÄzda (red star), rudĂ˝ prapor (red flag), RudĂŠ prĂĄvo (newspaper, now only PrĂĄvo), but also RudĂŠ moĹe (Red Sea), RudĂŠ nĂĄmÄstĂ­, rudĂĄ skvrna (red sunspot), rozÂhavenĂ˝ do ruda (red-hot), etc. Mostly it is rather clichĂŠ than an exact colour determination.', '2005-01-05 20:33:13', 1484, 0),
(1489, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'We must use \"jsem, jsi, jsme, jste\" in the past tense, but \"jest, jsou\" (3rd person sing./plur.) is archaic, e.g. \"vstal jest z mrtvĂ˝ch\", \"pĹiÂli jsou z vĂ˝chodu\", etc. Now: \"vstal z mrtvĂ˝ch\", \"pĹiÂli z vĂ˝chodu\".\r\n\r\nPast tense \"nĂŠsti\":\r\n\r\n1. nesl (nesla, neslo) jsem\r\n2. nesl (nesla, neslo) jsi\r\n3. nesl (nesla, neslo)\r\n\r\n1. nesli (nesly, nesla) jsme\r\n2. nesli (nesly, nesla) jste\r\n3. nesli (nesly, nesla)\r\n\r\n\"nesl jest\" and \"nesli jsou\" is archaic\r\n', '2005-01-05 21:26:30', 1484, 0),
(1490, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'Y\'know, I dunno. To me, the color (I mean, the visual color) used in association with Communism in the CR is scarlet, not red.\r\n\r\nOff-topic... \"Pathetic\" here is a Czechism, a false cognate. We would say \"bombastic\" or one of its synonyms.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-06 00:56:56', 1488, 0),
(1491, 13, 'Jeremy', '', 'Re: degree equivalents', 'Wow! Marvellous, Vladimir. Thank you!', '2005-01-06 13:33:38', 1487, 0),
(1492, 13, 'Lada', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'Thank you VladimĂ­r for such detailed explanation:)\r\n\r\nDoes anybody know is there a list for short czech names (i mean like Julek for Julius)\r\n\r\nDÄkujĂ­', '2005-01-06 23:08:13', 1489, 0),
(1493, 14, 'Daphne', '', 'Re: Czech national symbols (flag)', 'Maybe...\r\nIt\'s can be from Moravian symbol...\r\nThe Moravian eagle is red and white like chess board...\r\nSorry about my bad grammar...', '2005-01-07 11:42:48', 298, 0),
(1494, 13, 'Amit', 'amit_chawla_1@rediffmail.com', 'Learning Czech Language through Internet', 'Hi Forum:\r\nGreetings from India!\r\n\r\nI am interested to learn Czech language through Internet. Please let me know some free websites.I am beginner and starting from scratch. I really thanks to Miss Marie who provided me information to join this forum.\r\n\r\nAn early reply will be appreciated!\r\n\r\nThanks and Regards\r\nAmit Chawla\r\n_________________________________________________________________________\r\nAmit Chawla\r\nEmail:amit_chawla_1@rediffmail.com\r\n', '2005-01-07 13:30:57', 0, 1),
(1495, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: question about past tense', 'www.vestirna.com, click on JMĂNA', '2005-01-07 14:42:00', 1492, 0),
(1496, 13, 'Daphne', '', 'January', 'January...\r\nLeden or Ledna?', '2005-01-07 18:39:45', 0, 0),
(1497, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: January', 'Leden. Or rather, leden. (It\'s not capitalized like in English.)\r\n\r\nLedna is the second grammatical case, the genitive. \r\n\r\nNominative: Leden je krĂĄsnĂ˝.\r\nGenitive: Od ledna budu tady.\r\nDative: Some cute sentence with \"to January\" (k lednu) goes here.\r\nAccusative: Vybrali leden.\r\nVocative: Ă leden, jak tÄ miluji! :-)\r\nLocative: PĹiÂel v lednu.\r\nInstrumentĂĄl: S lednem pĹichĂĄzejĂ­ nejvÄtÂĂ­ zimnĂ­ deprese.\r\n\r\nOK, Vladimir can now start castigating me for my horrible Czech. :-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-07 20:34:48', 1496, 0),
(1498, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: January', 'OK, I must \"castigare et punire\".\r\n\r\nThe vocative is \"ledne\"!\r\n------------------------\r\n', '2005-01-07 21:25:04', 1497, 0),
(1499, 13, 'Daphne', '', 'January', 'January...\r\nLeden or Ledna?', '2005-01-07 22:14:33', 0, 0),
(1500, 14, 'Lada', '', 'czech names', 'Hello!\r\nI\'d like to know what names are the most popular? i was shoked when i knew that there are so many german names v ÄeÂtinÄ ...are they also popular? and do people really give to children such names like TaĹa, NadÂ´a while the full names are TatÂ´jana a NadÄÂda.\r\n', '2005-01-10 22:58:00', 0, 0),
(1501, 13, 'Daphne', '', 'I have a question...', 'What is\r\n\'May I beg your pardon?\'\r\nin czech?\r\n(It is not Real begging^.^)', '2005-01-11 19:27:41', 0, 0),
(1502, 13, 'Daphne', '', 'Re: January', 'Oops...I Sended twice...\r\n-_-a', '2005-01-11 19:33:51', 1499, 0),
(1503, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: I have a question...', 'Its \"ProsĂ­m?\".\r\n\r\nSeriously. Just say the word with the same kind of voice you use when saying \"May I beg your pardon?\".\r\n\r\nThe word \"Pardon\" does exist in Czech. But it\'s only used when you something like bump into somebody or misspeak (\"Pardon, myslela jsem Honzu, nĂŠĂŠ LĂĄÄu\").\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-11 20:42:38', 1501, 0),
(1504, 13, 'Bernard Lock', 'bernard@brancaster.org.uk', 'Use of JĂĄ', 'I should like help please to understand the use of \"JĂĄ\" in the title of a book of Czech folk songs and carols, Ĺ¤JĂĄ, pĂ­nickaĹĽ (Petr JĂĄnskĂ˝, 1994).  Am I right in thinking that this is not the normal use of \"JĂĄ\"? ', '2005-01-11 23:45:25', 0, 1),
(1505, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: Use of JĂĄ', 'What is the \"normal\" use of a word? \"JĂĄ, pĂ­sniÄka\" means \"I, song\". It is similar to the title \"I, Claudius\" (Robert Graves\' novel).', '2005-01-12 13:10:37', 1504, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(1506, 13, 'Bernard Lock', 'bernard@brancaster.org.uk', 'Re: Use of JĂĄ', 'To VladimĂ­r\r\n\r\n    thank you for your help.  I tried that meaning at first, but wasn\'t really prepared for such a \'living\' personification of song.  I shall now put my trust in the language much more.\r\n\r\n    Thank you too for the website.  It is excellent.\r\n\r\n    Bernard\r\n', '2005-01-12 17:29:54', 1505, 0),
(1507, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Use of JĂĄ', 'A little off-topic; I\'m bored:\r\n\r\nYou\'re doing well to \"study\" JĂĄ, pĂ­sniÄka, as it\'s got a firm place in the Czech culture and in the very Czech (if gradually dying) tradition of \"tramping\" and of campfire singing. You will be able to build many bridges to the hearts of Czechs with the songs in that book.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-12 20:29:16', 1506, 0),
(1508, 15, 'love', 'nonamewebm@yahoo.fr', 'Zuzana', '\r\nplease help me \r\n\r\nhttp://pageperso.aol.fr/Tpetit224/\r\n\r\n', '2005-01-15 21:07:39', 0, 1),
(1509, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: czech names', 'Jan is probably the number one male name, and Jana might well be the number one female name. As to \"nicknamish\" official first names... (like, say, LĂĄÄa instead of Ladislav ;-)) apparently yes, these do happen. In any case, in day to day conversation, I\'d say you\'re even less likely to hear a TaÂjana being called TaÂjana instead of TĂĄĹa in the CR than you are to hear a Samuel being called Samuel instead of Sam in the US.\r\n\r\nFor many, many years, the fates of the Czech lands and what is now Austria and, to a smaller extent, what is now Germany were intertwined. This had to have an effect on naming, even on first names, and it did. Above all, you\'ll find people named Jan but called Honza (from Hans) a lot more often than you\'ll find those called Jenda.\r\n\r\nI checked the Internet for a page with information on the most popular first names, but alas. With only 10 million Czechs in the world, none have yet had the charity to provide such information on the Web, especially not for free. I did find one interesting-looking site:\r\n\r\nhttp://bimbo.fjfi.cvut.cz/~karpisek/kalendar/data_cz/prehledy/kapjmena.php?pristup=\r\n\r\n...but, since it is registration-only (although registration is, mind you, free), I can\'t say more.\r\n\r\n', '2005-01-17 16:45:59', 1500, 0),
(1510, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: I have a question...', 'I\'m not sure this \"may I beg your pardon\" is not more along the lines of \"nezlobte se\"? If it is meant more as I\'m sorry to trouble you.', '2005-01-18 06:14:38', 1501, 0),
(1511, 15, 'June', 'jechante17@yahoo.com', 'Masaryk University - Brno - summer Czech program', 'Has any of you attended the Czech language summer school at Masaryk University in Brno? I am interested in knowing (1) How good the teachers are (2) What is the housing like, and is it far from the school where the classes are held (3) Is there a mini-refrigerator in each dorm or hotel room (4) Is the provided food edible? I had a bad experience at the Univerzita Karlova (Charles University). I found that the level of teachers is uneven ... some are highly competent, while others are not. Also, while the dorm rooms were liveable, the food (except for breakfast) was horrible. Dinner was a piece of meat that was mostly fat, swimming in gravy, with 6 knedliky and sugary & greasy zely. The vegetarian meal one night was three coconut-covered donuts. Unreal! I\'d appreciate hearing from anyone who has had experience in Brno during the summer. Dekuji!', '2005-01-18 07:58:54', 0, 1),
(1512, 15, 'June', 'jechante17@yahoo.com', 'Re: czech words used in everyday american life', 'I believe that mish-mash originated in Czech. It is spelled mismas in cesky ... that\'s with a hacek over each s!', '2005-01-18 08:06:03', 1085, 1),
(1513, 15, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Masaryk University - Brno - summer Czech program', 'I attended classes not at the Summer School, but just in the Katedra ÄeÂtiny pro cizince during the \"school year\", about ten years back, so there\'s not much I can say, but I\'ll say what I can.\r\n\r\n1) They were good, IME.\r\n\r\n2) Dunno; I had my own housing and of course the school offered none. Probably you\'d be put up at the koleje, basically a plain old dorm, though perhaps not as fancy as you\'re used to from the States (if you\'re from the States).\r\n\r\n3) Probably not.\r\n\r\n4) Dunno if food is provided at all. If you get typical menza (cafeteria) food or the chance to buy menza tickets (and thus eat cheaply)... yep, what you write about matches my memories of menza food. Although I would have hoped that the quality has gone up over the years, I see that at the very least the UK (the college, not the country) menza is still where the MU one was back then, and so I wouldn\'t be surprised if the MU one is also treading water.\r\n\r\n> Dinner was a piece of meat that was mostly fat, swimming in gravy, with 6 knedliky\r\n\r\nAKA in Franta Farmboy\'s eyes: delicious. :-)\r\n\r\n> sugary & greasy zely\r\n\r\nSweet-and-sour is quite a typical flavor for kysanĂŠ zelĂ­*. Downright sugary is not, however; blame that on the menza.\r\n\r\n> The vegetarian meal one night was three coconut-covered donuts. \r\n\r\nThe Czech mentality is that certain sweet breadlike foods can be considered \"meals,\" and they look at you funny when you look at them funny about it. I know, I don\'t get it either.\r\n\r\nErik\r\n\r\n* Not zely - -y is a plural ending for many masculine and feminine nouns, while zelĂ­ is a neutral and above all collective, \"unpluralizable\" noun, just like cabbage in English for that matter (but OK, OK, I know endings in Czech are a bit of a nightmare to master).', '2005-01-18 21:26:14', 1511, 0),
(1514, 14, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: czech names', 'See http://www.mvcr.cz/statistiky/jmena/muzi/poradi.html', '2005-01-21 21:14:32', 1500, 0),
(1515, 13, 'Paul Kochis', 'kochisp@sbcglobal.net', 'Question on origin of my last name', 'I have been doing some research on my family history.  My great grandfather was supposedly from Czech.  I am trying to find out if that is true.  Can anyone tell me what my last name means?  The original spelling was Koc\'is.  Any help would be greatly appreciated. ', '2005-01-24 00:07:57', 0, 1),
(1516, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Question on origin of my last name', 'There is no word \"KoÄis\" in modern-day Czech. A googling of the word reveals a mere 3 dozen pages, all of them (including the sole Czech reference) of them referring to non-Czechs -- mostly Poles, Latvians, Lithuanians, Hungarians, and Slovaks. Interesting is this reference from Slovakia, even though it turns out to be a KoÄiÂ rather than a KoÄis:\r\n\r\nhttp://www.cassovia.sk/korzar/clanok.php3?sub=31.12.2004/5916KV\r\n\r\nIn the KoÂice phone book you\'ll find surnames that will make you smile despite yourself\r\n\r\n[...]\r\n\r\nIn connection with the surname KoÄiÂ there is the well-known rhyme KoÄiÂ, po pivo mi skoÄĂ­Â (KoÄiÂ, you\'ll run grab me a beer)... This doesn\'t apply for director of the Regional Administration of the Slovak Statistics Office Ing. KoÄiÂ; nobody has ever sent him for a foamy brew. \'About all that\'s connected to my surname are tongue-twisters. For example Hungarians [KoÂice lies near Hungary] have trouble spelling it. Meanwhile, I\'m just an ordinary Slovak KoÄiÂ, so write it like you hear it. Even more confounded by my surname are the English and others who speak English. Most often they end up with \'KoÂic.\' Even worse was when I once presented myself and said \'I\'m KoÄiÂ from KoÂĂ­ce [z KoÂĂ­c].\" They repeated after me something like KoÂic z KoÄis.\' As a boy, where E. KoÄiÂ grew up in a village, where people aren\'t called by their first name and surname, but usually by their grandfather and greatgrandfather, people mostly know him a Emil from Sopek.\"\r\n\r\nLooking at all the hits, I\'d say somewhere in the border region between Hungaria and Slovakia, perhaps.\r\n\r\nIf it is instead KoÄiÂ, however, we get a vastly larger number of hits, a much stronger tie to the Czechoslovak region, and finally some true Czech references.\r\n\r\nCouldn\'t find an etymology, though.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-24 15:26:58', 1515, 0),
(1517, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: czech names', ':-O\r\n\r\nYou are a god.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-24 15:27:56', 1514, 0),
(1518, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: Question on origin of my last name', 'KoÄis is probably an improper Czech (Slovak, etc.) transcription of Hungarian \"kocsis\". The correct transcription is KoÄiÂ (in Hungarian: sz = s, s = Â, cs = Ä). The Hungarian word \"kocsis\" means \"driver of a coach (horse-drawn carriage)\", derived fron \"kocsi\" = Eng. coach, Fr. coche, Ger. Kutsche, Cz. koÄĂĄr, etc. See Merriam-Webster (www.m-w.com): \"coach\". The Czech equivalent of \"kocsis\" is \"koÄĂ­\".', '2005-01-24 16:51:57', 1516, 0),
(1519, 14, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: czech names', 'VelkĂ˝ Bratr o nĂĄs vĂ­ vÂechno!', '2005-01-24 18:20:45', 1517, 0),
(1520, 13, 'Paul Kochis', 'kochisp@sbcglobal.net', 'Re: Question on origin of my last name', 'So does that mean that Kocis came from Hungary?  Would that mean that my family may actually be from Hungary then, and not Czech.  I was tlod my Great Grandfather was from Czech, and Great Grandmother was from Austria.  So that could be incorrect?  My Grandfather changed the spelling to Kochis so people could relate to the h sound in the name.  It is pronounced the way this sounds, (coachis).', '2005-01-25 04:35:24', 1518, 1),
(1521, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Question on origin of my last name', 'The Austro-Hungarian empire was a great melting pot, or at least a salad bowl with dressing soaking into absolutely everything. Your great-grandfather may well have lived in the Czech lands, or even more likely modern-day Slovakia, and still have had Hungarian heritage. More common was the migration of people from the periphery to the center (as the vast number of Czech-named businesses remaining in Vienna to this day will attest) or from one part of the periphery to the other, and Hungarians were more common in what is now Slovakia than what is now the Czech Republic, but it\'s certainly possible.\r\n\r\nAs Vladimir noted, a Czech or Slovak could very well have mistranscribed Kocsis to KoÄis... to a speaker of one of those two languages (perhaps more likely Czech, with its weaker contact with Hungary) with no understanding of Hungarian, the \"cs\" would clearly suggest a \"Ä\" (a \"ch\"), but the \"s\" being \"sh\" would not be so clear.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-25 17:53:13', 1520, 0),
(1522, 13, 'Paul Kochis', 'kochisp@sbcglobal.net', 'Re: Question on origin of my last name', 'So does that mean that the spelling kocis could be wrong?  I notice you keep writing koeis.  Also what is that mark called that goes over the \"e\" when you type it, I can\'t figure out how to type it.', '2005-01-25 21:54:48', 1515, 1),
(1523, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Question on origin of my last name', 'Hi Paul,\r\n\r\nIt\'s not we who are writing koÄis, but rather your computer. The character in question is c-with-hacek (with the little \"v\" over the top). \r\n\r\nA short excursion into the wide world of what are called \"character set problems\" is needed here. Letters are stored on a computer as numbers, and the lists of what characters we are working with and what numbers they are associated with are called \"character sets\" or \"code pages.\" (Though those are probably subtly different, they\'re all the same to me... I\'m not such an expert.) For an imaginary example: \"1\" means \"a\", \"2\" means \"b\", 27 means \"A\", 52 means \"Z\", etc.\r\n\r\nNow there are many code pages that are quite similar, until you get into special characters, at which point everything is mucked up. Such is the case with Windows-1250 (the code page for Central European languages) and Windows-1252 (the code page for Western European languages). If your computer is using the code table for West Europe to show you material encoded in the Central European codepage, then a-z, A-Z, 1-0, space, and other basics will be OK, but most accented characters will be represented incorrectly. For example, c-with-hacek will be shown as e-grave. Basically the computer sees it should show you \"character number 125\" (or whatever) and, because it\'s using an almost-right-but-not-quite list to look up what\'s what, it mistakenly thinks \"character number 125\" should be e-grave.\r\n\r\nSolution: \r\n* First double-check that it\'s not just your Web browser\'s settings that are wrong. I assume you\'re using Internet Explorer? Go to the View menu, then the Encoding menu. If there\'s an item called \"Central European - Windows\", switch to it. Now the question is only why the encoding wasn\'t autodetected (every page on Bohemica.com declares itself to be encoded in Windows-1250, so there\'s no excuse for IE not to know the encoding).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nWow, I can imagine you\'ve been pretty confused all this while seeing an e-grave everywhere instead of the c-with-hacek...\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nAlthough there are various, somewhat complicated ways to type e-grave and, more importantly, c-with-hacek without having the national keyboard layout installed, it\'s a lot simpler just to install the needed keyboard layout once and then just switch keyboards to type Czech characters. Which Windows are you on?\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-26 16:19:18', 1522, 0),
(1524, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Missing a point in my \"solution\"', 'I forgot to write the part about what to do if you find you\'re not even offered the chance to use Central European encoding to show the page!\r\n\r\nIn that case, you\'ll have some Windows installing work to do... here again, the question is: which version are you on?\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-01-26 16:21:12', 1523, 0),
(1525, 15, 'Jan Kuzebauch', 'etroi@seznam.cz', 'writing with somebody', 'I am from the Czech Republic and I want to write with somebody from the USA ( czech, english ) through ICQ or E-mail. I think it will be profitable for me and for you.', '2005-02-05 01:13:16', 0, 0),
(1526, 15, 'Gary Toops', 'gary.toops@wichita.edu', 'Re: Masaryk University - Brno - summer Czech program', 'I attended the Czech summer school at Masaryk University in Brno about 10 years ago, so I don\'t know if my experience is still repeatable.  We were housed in a student dormitory, two to a room, with each double room sharing a connecting bathroom.  Getting to classes required taking a short (approx. 5 minute) streetcar ride.  The food was very good, served in a large dining hall near where the classes were held, not in the dormitory.  Some afternoons I would, however, get lazy, skip supper, and simply start drinking beer at one of two pivnice located directly behind the dormitory.  If I got hungry, I could always order smazeny syr and hranolky (among other things).  It was a great way to meet and converse with Czechs.  The excursions were well organized, and the students participating in the summer school came from many different countries.  My roommate was from France, and he and I usually conversed in French.  The night life was okay back then, but I gather from browsing the Web that it has gotten much better in the past ten years.  Brno is not very big, so getting around town late at night even by cab is not prohibitively expensive or inconvenient.  As for Prague, I last attended a summer school there in 1997.  While the food situation was more than adequate, I found the instruction deficient.  Generally speaking, the more advanced you are, the less likely you are to get good teachers, by which I mean the young, enthusiastic ones.  The stodgy older teachers seem to be assigned almost invariably to the advanced classes.', '2005-02-06 06:52:04', 1511, 1),
(1527, 15, 'Gary Toops', 'gary.toops@wichita.edu', 'Re: Masaryk University - Brno - summer Czech program', 'I attended the Czech summer school at Masaryk University in Brno about 10 years ago, so I don\'t know if my experience is still repeatable.  We were housed in a student dormitory, two to a room, with each double room sharing a connecting bathroom.  Getting to classes required taking a short (approx. 5 minute) streetcar ride.  The food was very good, served in a large dining hall near where the classes were held, not in the dormitory.  Some afternoons I would, however, get lazy, skip supper, and simply start drinking beer at one of two pivnice located directly behind the dormitory.  If I got hungry, I could always order smazeny syr and hranolky (among other things).  It was a great way to meet and converse with Czechs.  The excursions were well organized, and the students participating in the summer school came from many different countries.  My roommate was from France, and he and I usually conversed in French.  The night life was okay back then, but I gather from browsing the Web that it has gotten much better in the past ten years.  Brno is not very big, so getting around town late at night even by cab is not prohibitively expensive or inconvenient.  As for Prague, I last attended a summer school there in 1997.  While the food situation was more than adequate, I found the instruction deficient.  Generally speaking, the more advanced you are, the less likely you are to get good teachers, by which I mean the young, enthusiastic ones.  The stodgy older teachers seem to be assigned almost invariably to the advanced classes.', '2005-02-06 07:12:27', 1511, 1),
(1528, 22, 'Dominik LukeÂ', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Welcome to metaphor forum', 'Welcome to metaphor forum. Any and all contributions on metaphor are welcome. I myself am more inclined toward the conceptual metaphor theory but I am aware that there is a lot of other research that is valuable out there that has a lot of interest to say on the subject.\r\n\r\nDominik', '2005-02-06 17:10:45', 0, 0),
(1529, 15, 'June', 'jechante17@yahoo.com', 'Re: Masaryk University - Brno - summer Czech program', 'Gary: Thanks very much for the info. I\'m curious to know whether the school you attended in Prague in 1997 was the Letni Skola of the Univerzita Karlova. Thanks again! June', '2005-02-06 19:51:10', 1526, 0),
(1530, 14, 'juliet007', 'juliet007@wp.pl', 'fables, tales and cartoons', 'Hi,\r\nI study bohemistics in Wroclaw. I\'ve got to write a paper on Czech culture. So I thought I could write about this weird thing I have noticed in Czech and only there. WHY DO CHECH PEOPLE LOVE FAIRYTALES? You know, all those princesses, kings, fairytales etc.\r\n\r\nWhere can I find any material about it?\r\nI\'ll be veeeery thankful for any infos.\r\nJuliet\r\n\r\nPs. The paper is due this Friday! :(', '2005-02-07 16:43:01', 0, 0),
(1531, 14, 'Dominik LukeÂ', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: fables, tales and cartoons', 'Why do you think that only Czech people like fairytales? Just look at the Red Bull campaign which has a princess in a tower - you\'ll find that that did not originate in the CR and is quite easily understandable in all cultures. Here in England fairytales are extremely common - just look at Terry Pratchett\'s books which spoof many of them. Or Buffy the Vampire Slayer in the US. Or the Russians with all the baba yaga stuff. They also contributed the great Morphology of the fairytale by Vladimir Propp. Which might provide a useful angle for you.\r\n\r\nThe really interesting question is, what are the differences between fairytales in various cultures. Czech culture has <i>vodnĂ­k</i> which has no equivalent in English folklore but has a very limited lore regarding <i>vampires</i> or no mention of a <i>toothfairy</i>. \r\n\r\nThe cultural representations may also be influenced by notable examples in film and print. In Czech, the tradition of NÄmcovĂĄ and Erben is still very strong but there was also a series of films in the 50s and 60s which were based on a fairytale and are still regularly repeated on TV. Just off the top of my head: PyÂnĂĄ princezna, Princezna se zlatou hvÄzdou na Äele, SĹŻl nad zlato, CĂ­saĹĹŻv pekaĹ a pekaĹĹŻv cĂ­saĹ, Jak se budĂ­ princezny, and others with a fairytale or fantastic theme e.g. Jak utopit doktora MrĂĄÄka. There were a few TV shows that might feed off this, most notably Arabela (which was popular in many countries, I met people who know it in Romania) and many others for children only, including the good night story on TV called VeÄernĂ­Äek. But don\'t forget that one of the most enduring Christmas films based on a fairytale is MrazĂ­k, which is Russian. I\'m not sure about Polish culture, but I\'m sure that in Russia, a similar tradition could be drawn up. Of course, there was also the fairytale of communism. \r\n\r\nIf would of course be interesting to compare fairytale representations in the popular cultures of different traditions. There would be many differences and it is possible that certain fairytale representations (such as princezna) would be more common in the Czech culture than others.\r\n\r\nDominik\r\n\r\n', '2005-02-07 16:59:42', 1530, 0),
(1532, 14, 'juliet007', 'juliet007@wp.pl', 'fables, tales and cartoons', 'Hi,\r\nI study bohemistics in Wroclaw. I\'ve got to write a paper on Czech culture. So I thought I could write about this weird thing I have noticed in Czech and only there. WHY DO CHECH PEOPLE LOVE FAIRYTALES? You know, all those princesses, kings, fairytales etc.\r\n\r\nWhere can I find any material about it?\r\nI\'ll be veeeery thankful for any infos.\r\nJuliet\r\n\r\nPs. The paper is due this Friday! :(', '2005-02-07 17:00:57', 0, 0),
(1533, 14, 'juliet007', '', 'topic for a paperwork', 'I guess you\'re right.\r\nAnd of course, I know Arabella (everybody knows arabella!!) :)\r\nIt\'s just that my grammar teacher (who is Czech) always says it should be a very interesting topic for an article, cause Czech people (adults as much as children) love those \"princezna\'s stories\".\r\nAnd they are often in tv (mAm Ceskou jedinku).\r\n\r\nMaybe You could also say, what you think about Czech movies after 1990. Do they complete into some kind of tendency or sth?? (I really need an interesting topic for this semestralku!)\r\nThanks,\r\njuliet', '2005-02-07 17:08:56', 1531, 0),
(1534, 14, 'juliet007', '', 'topic for a paperwork', 'I guess you\'re right.\r\nAnd of course, I know Arabella (everybody knows arabella!!) :)\r\nIt\'s just that my grammar teacher (who is Czech) always says it should be a very interesting topic for an article, cause Czech people (adults as much as children) love those \"princezna\'s stories\".\r\nAnd they are often in tv (mAm Ceskou jedinku).\r\n\r\nMaybe You could also say, what you think about Czech movies after 1990. Do they complete into some kind of tendency or sth?? (I really need an interesting topic for this semestralku!)\r\nThanks,\r\njuliet', '2005-02-07 17:09:07', 1531, 0),
(1535, 14, 'Dominik LukeÂ', 'forum@bohemica.com', 'Re: topic for a paperwork', 'I haven\'t followed Czech movies much after the 1990s but I think the trend continues. You have the successful Princezna ze mlĂ˝na (1 and 2) and the DrvoÂtÄp variation by SvÄrĂĄk (ZdenÄk not Jan). Which reminds me, another fairly uniquely Czech character is \'Äert\' which is usually depicted in a much more benign way and more removed from any Biblical undertones than elsewhere. See films like S Äerty nejsou Âerty, DalskabĂĄty hĹĂ­ÂnĂĄ ves, or again Arabela. There is a certain tendency to sanitize things in Czech postwar culture, for instance Pleva\'s reworking of Robinson Crusoe into a proponent of multiculturalism vs. the original colonial bigot you find in Defoe.\r\n\r\nAnother tack you could take, is the fantastic in Czech film and culture. You have stuff like NĂĄvÂtÄvnĂ­ci, LĂŠtajĂ­cĂ­ ÄestmĂ­r, Spadla z oblakov (which is actually Slovak) on TV but also AdĂŠla jeÂtÄ neveÄeĹela, ZĂ­tra rĂĄno vstanu a opaĹĂ­m se Äajem, Pane vy jste vdova, CoÂ takhle dĂĄt si ÂpenĂĄt or even Kdyby tisĂ­c klarinetĹŻ which formed much of the imagery of the 60s, 70s and 80s, pretty much in conjunction with fairytale. (But then you have stuff like Sexmise and Solaris in Poland, so that may not be as interesting).', '2005-02-07 17:20:23', 1533, 0),
(1536, 14, 'juliet007', '', 'thank you', 'thank you', '2005-02-07 17:23:03', 1535, 0),
(1537, 14, 'juliet007', '', 'thank you', 'thank you', '2005-02-07 17:23:19', 1535, 0),
(1538, 14, 'Daniel Butler', 'daniel.butler@atlas.cz', 'Czech family names', ' I have been living in Prague on and off for the last 8 years and keep wondering about the REAL origin of Czech family names.  I am no great linguist, researcher or \'name\' anthropologist but you might agree with me when I say that around the world there are perhaps 3 most common origins for family names: Using czech and English examples, the place of ancestry, Jan Nemec - John German; the profession or trade, Pavel Mlynar\' - Paul Miller (or Butler, Baker, Smith, etc); and the family original first name such as Johnson, Paulson, Richardson or in some double examples in a Russian name Michal Michalovich, Petr Petrovich. \r\n\r\nBut only in Czech do you come across more visual and sometimes more amusing last names. For example : Pane Pobud - Mister Smelly place, Richard Skacel - Richard Sound-of-tree-falling, Pepa Pospisal - Joe Running-late; Tomas Cibule - Tom Onion.   \r\n\r\nI have asked time and again why do Czech names appear to be 10 times more colorful than the names of many other languages. I am sure there are others but I can only think of one other culture that has something as visual and descriptive and that is of the North American Indians with names such as Running-bear or Sitting-bull. (And if you do not beleive me, pull out the white pages and your Cesko-Anglicko dictionary and start translating the names; a few bottles of Budvar or Gambrinus and a few friends makes it even more enjoyable). \r\n\r\nFrom what I am told this descriptive naming system doesn\'t even appear in Poland, Hungary, or  other neighboring countries to even a small extent. My simple question is, Why in Czech Republic, did these names begin? I have heard theories about the controlling and yet foreign ruling empire may have enforced a census and, as a protest, the local peasantry decided to ridicule the system by naming themselves in more amusing manner. Any other theories - but hopefully the truth - would be greatly appreciated. ', '2005-02-07 20:44:04', 0, 1),
(1539, 13, 'Gerard', 'gmcgrana@tcd.ie', 'Words of a song', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nI have a Czech song on CD but sung by a Swedish man.  I would like to have the lyrics in Czech, so i can learn it myself!!  Does any one here have the words??\r\n\r\nMany Thanks\r\n\r\nGerard', '2005-02-08 02:04:23', 0, 1),
(1540, 13, 'Gerard', 'gmcgrana@tcd.ie', 'Forgot Name of Song', 'Sorry!!\r\n\r\nThe song is called Zadnej nevi jako ja!  \r\n\r\nGerard', '2005-02-08 02:07:18', 1539, 1),
(1541, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech family names', 'I\'d say that generally, such last names started as nicknames given to someone by the local community and carried down from generation to generation. I find any \"protest by silly surname\" theory a bit implausible. I doubt that there is anything specifically Czech about such nicknames; perhaps what is specifically Czech is that they weren\'t *killed off* via the censuses. Now this fact could indeed be related to the fact that a nationality speaking another language ran the first censuses. It\'s hard to tell someone they\'ll have to get another name than their old name of \"Ate a kid\'s porridge\" (SnÄdldĂ­tÄtĂ­kaÂi) if you don\'t know what it means. But it\'s hard to say.\r\n\r\nYou can find a short but interesting work on Czech surnames at the following address:\r\n\r\nhttp://members.tripod.com/~zlimpkk/Genealogy/czechsurnames.html\r\n\r\nI think you\'ll especially like section h). :-)\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-02-09 17:42:26', 1538, 0),
(1542, 14, 'Daniel Butler', 'daniel.butler@atlas.cz', 'Re: Czech family names', 'Erik, I appreciate the reply. And thanks very much for the link. This list of potential name origins could be applied in many countries. In fact, using that presentation of THE ORIGIN OF CZECH SURNAMES I would indeed strenously suggest that my case is indeed on more solid ground. Especially in the case of h) Surnames describing the activity or status. and to a lesser extent even c) Personal Features of an Individuum. (Perhaps there should be another categorization, j) silly names arising from having drunk too much beer). \r\nAgain, I will suggest that there are more curious and amusing names in the Czech Republic than other European countries. I mean, really. Take a look at this sample (from the page you linked to) and consider that there are hundreds more, just as enjoyable. I can think of only 10 or 20 that are amusing in English! \r\nPRECECHTEL  > And-he-wanted-it-after-all, \r\nSTOJESPAL > He-slept-standing , \r\nZLAMALJELITO > He-broke-the-black-pudding), \r\nSNEDLDITETIKASI > He-ate-the-squash-to-the-child),\r\nor even NASRALVHRNEC > (I won\'t write this one either, but its what they did in the olden days before toilets). \r\nI certainly would welcome other entrants to this discussion to learn more. I just enjoy hearing a new and amusing Czech name. \r\n', '2005-02-09 19:20:43', 1541, 1),
(1543, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech family names', 'Or even just BrzobohatĂ˝ (Quick-rich... OK, this is in English too) and ZĹĂ­dkaveselĂ˝ (Rarely-happy).\r\n\r\nWhat I really can\'t figure out is why the recipients of some of these names didn\'t refuse to let people call them such names... either community pressure to take the nickname you were given was really strong, or people turned it around into a badge of pride, or something. One thing I can judge, since it involves the present day, is that I saw a lot more nicknames among young adults (when I was still -- just barely -- one myself) than I did growing up in the States, and you\'ll still find some here and there among people of career age.\r\n\r\nDo note, however, that sometimes it\'s easier to notice strange things in a foreign language than one\'s own. A Czech might point out plenty of strange English names for our amusement, assuming he could get past the severe mutations of English (compared to Czech) over the centuries.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-02-10 22:24:59', 1542, 0),
(1544, 16, 'Dalibor TobiĂĄÂ', 'dobiasd@yahoo.com', 'LektorĂĄt ÄeÂtiny v MoskvÄ', 'Dobry den,\r\nprosim Vas o zverejneni informace o moznosti nastoupit od sk. roku 2005/6 na misto lektora cestiny na Fakulte cizich jazyku Lomonosovovy statni univerzity v Moskve.\r\nNaplni prace je vyuka cestiny a vedeni odbornych kursu dle zajmu a specializace lektora. Pozadavky: abslovent filologickeho oboru, nejlepe cestiny, s cestinou na urovni rodilych mluvcich a alespon zaklady rustiny, znalosti ruskych realii, spolehlivy a pracovity, se zajmem o danou praci a iniciativni, ochotny spolupracovat na rusko-ceskych projektech. Jedna se o misto na 1-3 roky, placene zcasti MSMT CR a zcasti MSU. Zajemce prosim o zpravu na adresu:\r\ndobiasd@yahoo.com. Dekuji.\r\nS pozdravem\r\n\r\nDalibor Dobias', '2005-02-17 23:28:12', 0, 0),
(1545, 13, 'Joe Bouril', 'bourilfamily@comcast.net', 'name and meaning', 'My research shows that \"bouril\" translates as \"stormed\". I was wondering if it refers to the past tense in terms of: weather, charging in or out, a grand entrance or tour, or the like. Any input is greatly appreciated.', '2005-02-18 07:20:44', 0, 1),
(1546, 13, 'Pavel', '', 'Re: name and meaning', 'Bouril (in Czech it would be BouĹil) is actually a past participle of the verb bouĹit (bourit - \"r\" with a little hook over it) that is associated with the noun bouĹe - a storm. So Bouril meant someone whose personality was like a storm. It is my explanation. It could also be contaminated with the verb \"boĹit, bourat\", past participles \"boĹil, boural\", meaning to destroy (a building). Pavel.', '2005-02-20 19:20:15', 1545, 0),
(1547, 22, 'cedric detienne', 'cedricdetienne@yahoo.fr', '', 'I created a French-speaking forum on the metaphor! \r\n\r\nI am happy that there is another forum! \r\n\r\nhttp://www.info-metaphore.com\r\n\r\n', '2005-02-22 03:54:40', 0, 0),
(1548, 14, 'Dagmar Dornbierer', 'dornbierer@stauffacher.com', 'Re: Czech family names', 'You might know Pavel Eisner - a a highly gifted czech linguist, musician and author of wonderful books on Czech language. Those books are quite untranslateable, well, they are dealing with czech... Eisner wrote some nice chapters on czech family names, on czech \"language behaviour\" and a special chapter on the name \"Maria\", which was the most used female name until the mid of the 20th century.', '2005-02-22 17:19:47', 1538, 0),
(1549, 22, 'CĂŠdric detienne', 'cedricdetienne@yahoo.fr', 'transdisciplinary metaphorology', 'I created a website of documentation on the metaphor. Its goal is to constitute a \"transdisciplinary metaphorology\". Does this concept mean something for you? What do you think of this project? \r\n\r\n(The website www.info-metaphore.com is French-speaking but it will be translated soon into English... If you want help me to translate this website, send me a mail)\r\n\r\nhttp://www.info-metaphore.com\r\n\r\n', '2005-02-23 01:17:21', 0, 1),
(1550, 22, 'Melvyn Clarke', 'zehrovak@volny.cz', 'Guardian article', 'Hi,\r\n\r\nThought you might like to know there\'s an interesting item in the Guardian on use of metaphor in different cultures:\r\n\r\nHow time flies \r\n\r\nFor the Aymara people living in the Andes, the past lies ahead and the future lies behind. Laura Spinney looks at how different languages reflect, and shape, our conception of time\r\n\r\nhttp://www.guardian.co.uk/life/feature/story/0,13026,1423455,00.html\r\n\r\nBTW it seems the ancient Greeks had a similar conception of time - according to the following essay:\r\n\r\nhttp://www.cosmos-club.org/journals/1995/knox.html\r\n\r\nBR\r\n\r\nMelvyn\r\n', '2005-02-26 05:01:17', 0, 1),
(1551, 15, 'sokki', '', 'www.sokker.org', 'ako te interesira football to je dobar manager', '2005-03-01 21:11:11', 0, 0),
(1552, 14, 'Cara', 'Javajunkie56@aol.com', 'War Of The Newts', 'Hi there - I would like to find a copy of War Of The Newts in Czech for my father. does anyone have any ideas? Thanks', '2005-03-02 21:54:18', 0, 1),
(1553, 14, 'Steven Santayana', 'gloin@hotmail.com', 'Exchange Program', 'I am currently considering, going to czech in the AFS exchange program as a student, I dont speak czech but would like to learn, I am only 14, does this sound over my head or possibly to difficult for me to undertake? Or is this a good idea? \r\n\r\nThank You Steven Santayana', '2005-03-04 08:31:29', 0, 1),
(1554, 14, 'jana', '', 'Re: Exchange Program', 'Hi Steven,\r\nI think learning another language is always a good idea. At the same time, coping with native speakers, and an environment that is culturally quite different from the one you\'re familiar with, might be quite a big step to take without preparation (exchange programs should help with this but I don\'t really know AFS).\r\n\r\nYour age might make it easier and more difficult at the same time - easier because language learning gets harder with age and more difficult because felling lonely and homesick is easier to overcome with experiance.\r\n\r\nMy best advice would be to think about your motivations, why exactly do you want to take part in an exchange program? Why Czech, what do you know about the language and culture? What do you actually expect to learn? Are you ready for both - good and bad experiance (both are valuable for learning but not easy to live through)? \r\n\r\nI went to London and lived (actually still do, started a university and am doing my MPhil now) but I was twice your age and spoke English more or less fluently. It didn\'t help that much, I could understand the words (so would you, many Czechs can speak some English) but I had no clue what people actually meant (the culture really is quite different). Hard, very hard (well, I had to manage the finance on the top of it) but opened my eyes the way I wouldn\'t think possible any other way.\r\n\r\nWhatever you decide, I wish you good luck and lots of fun.\r\nJana', '2005-03-05 22:50:37', 1553, 0),
(1555, 22, 'Pattarawan Youyen', 'pttwn_2p@yahoo.com', 'Re: Welcome to metaphor forum', ' I am sure that the metaphor forum will provide the chance for people to share some linguistic ideas.\r\n I am really interested in (conceptual) metaphor and now doing kind of contrastive study paper on metaphorical expressions using body parts in Thai and Japanese.\r\n', '2005-03-07 21:11:53', 1528, 0),
(1556, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: War Of The Newts', 'Sending answer by e-mail, in case you don\'t return here. (Announcing here, so nobody duplicates my work.)\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-03-08 18:12:37', 1552, 0),
(1557, 13, 'Petoro', 'petrvondrous@email.cz', 'Re: I have a question...', 'may i beg your pardon could be translated\r\nlike that ^Nezlobte se prosim.^\r\nor ^Muzete mi odpustit?^\r\nor easily: ^Prominte.^ = Sorry.\r\n\r\ni am not using diacritics, tak se nezlobte :)', '2005-03-10 10:48:23', 1510, 0),
(1558, 13, 'Kerr', 'kafmcg@hotmail.com', 'I need help with some translation please', 'Hello, \r\n\r\nIf possible, I would love to have the following phrases in English translated into Czech:\r\n\r\n  \"I love to learn!\" and \r\n  \"Sincerely\" (this greeting will be used at the end of a letter, to \r\n     sign off with)\r\n\r\nI hope someone will be able to help me, I\'d really appreciate it.  Thank you so much!\r\n\r\nKerr', '2005-03-11 20:41:29', 0, 1),
(1559, 13, 'Jarek', 'tomiczek@gmail.com', 'Re: I need help with some translation please', 'Hi,\r\none way how to say that, might be:\r\n\"Velice rĂĄd se uÄĂ­m.\" - I like learning a lot.\r\n\r\nA formal greeting at the end of a letter might be:\r\n\"S pozdravem\", or informal \"ZdravĂ­\".\r\n\r\nZdravĂ­,\r\nJarek\r\n', '2005-03-12 04:50:36', 1558, 0),
(1560, 13, 'Jarek', 'tomiczek@gmail.com', 'Re: I need help with some translation please', 'Hi,\r\none way how to say that, might be:\r\n\"Velice rĂĄd se uÄĂ­m.\" - I like learning a lot.\r\n\r\nA formal greeting at the end of a letter might be:\r\n\"S pozdravem\", or informal \"ZdravĂ­\".\r\n\r\nZdravĂ­,\r\nJarek\r\n', '2005-03-12 04:50:36', 1558, 0),
(1561, 13, 'Jarek', 'tomiczek@gmail.com', 'Re: Forgot Name of Song', 'What about this one? \r\n\r\n\r\nÂĂĄdnej nevĂ­ jako jĂĄ\r\njak se seÄe otaviÄka\r\nÂĂĄdnej nevĂ­ jako jĂĄ\r\njak se seÄe otava\r\n\r\nOtava se pÄknÄ seÄe \r\njako kdyÂ se pole vleÄe\r\nÂĂĄdnej nevĂ­ jako jĂĄ\r\njak se seÄe otava\r\n\r\nÂĂĄdnej nevĂ­ jako jĂĄ\r\njak je v noci cesta dlouhĂĄ\r\nÂĂĄdnej nevĂ­ jako jĂĄ\r\njak je v noci cesta zlĂĄ\r\n\r\nKdyÂ jĂĄ pĹĂ­jdu k svĂ˝ DorliÄce\r\nona mi otevĹĂ­t nechce\r\nÂĂĄdnej nevĂ­ jako jĂĄ\r\njak je v noci cesta zlĂĄ', '2005-03-12 05:15:56', 1540, 1),
(1562, 13, 'Ms. Hresko', 'chsexc@aol.com', 'My Last Name', 'Does anyone know the origin of my last name? Is there a masc. and femin. version of it? I have heard that if I lived in the Czech. Repblic since I am female I would be Ms. Hreskova (instead of Hresko).', '2005-03-13 10:15:51', 0, 1),
(1563, 13, 'Kimmie', 'chsexc@aol.com', 'Translation \"the good life\"', 'How do you say \"the good life\" in Czech?', '2005-03-13 10:17:37', 0, 0),
(1564, 13, 'Kimmie', 'chsexc@aol.com', 'Translation \"the good life\"', 'How do you say \"the good life\" in Czech?', '2005-03-13 10:17:39', 0, 0),
(1565, 13, 'Kimmie', 'chsexc@aol.com', 'Translation \"the good life\"', 'How do you say \"the good life\" in Czech?', '2005-03-13 10:17:40', 0, 1),
(1566, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: My Last Name', '&#1043;&#1088;&#1077;&#1096;&#1082;&#1086; (Greshko) - probably East Slavic origin, definitely not Czech.\r\n\"&#1043;\" is pronounced \"G\" in Russian, but \"H\" in Ukrainian (Hreshko).\r\n\r\nThe Slovak and Czech transliteration is either HreÂko or GreÂko (Â = s with hacek).\r\n\r\nThe feminine form is HreÂkovĂĄ, GreÂkovĂĄ in Czech and Slovak.\r\n\r\nIn Russian/Ukrainian \"&#1043;&#1088;&#1077;&#1096;&#1082;&#1086;\" is indeclinable and have no feminine form,\r\na similar last name \"Greshkov\" is however declinable and have the feminine form \"Greshkova\".\r\n', '2005-03-15 14:29:33', 1562, 0),
(1567, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: My Last Name', '&#1043;&#1088;&#1077;&#1096;&#1082;&#1086; (Greshko) - probably East Slavic origin, definitely not Czech.\r\n\"&#1043;\" is pronounced \"G\" in Russian, but \"H\" in Ukrainian (Hreshko).\r\n\r\nThe Slovak and Czech transliteration is either HreÂko or GreÂko (Â = s with hacek).\r\n\r\nThe feminine form is HreÂkovĂĄ, GreÂkovĂĄ in Czech and Slovak.\r\n\r\nIn Russian/Ukrainian \"&#1043;&#1088;&#1077;&#1096;&#1082;&#1086;\" is indeclinable and have no feminine form,\r\na similar last name \"Greshkov\" is however declinable and have the feminine form \"Greshkova\".\r\n', '2005-03-15 14:42:53', 1562, 0),
(1568, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: My Last Name', '&#1043;&#1088;&#1077;&#1096;&#1082;&#1086; (Greshko) - probably East Slavic origin, definitely not Czech.\r\n\"&#1043;\" is pronounced \"G\" in Russian, but \"H\" in Ukrainian (Hreshko).\r\n\r\nThe Slovak and Czech transliteration is either HreÂko or GreÂko (Â = s with hacek).\r\n\r\nThe feminine form is HreÂkovĂĄ, GreÂkovĂĄ in Czech and Slovak.\r\n\r\nIn Russian/Ukrainian \"&#1043;&#1088;&#1077;&#1096;&#1082;&#1086;\" is indeclinable and have no feminine form,\r\na similar last name \"Greshkov\" is however declinable and have the feminine form \"Greshkova\".\r\n', '2005-03-15 14:45:27', 1562, 0),
(1569, 13, 'jana', '', 'Re: Translation', 'meaning? translating without any kind of context is the best way to confusion or maybe it\'s me - i\'m not familiar with this expression', '2005-03-17 23:20:09', 1565, 0),
(1570, 15, 'claudia', 'piriklo@katamail.com', 'Re: writing with somebody', 'Hi Jan, I\'m sorry I\'m from Italy and not USA but I have the same problem as u have...I\'d like to write to someone frome the Czech Republic but can\'t manage to find anyone!I\'m coming to Plzen in July and I\'d like to practice a little bit my very bad czech...would you help me please?\r\nPlease, please please!\r\nWe can also write in english if you wish, but maybe you could write in czech sometimes just to give me an idea of what it is like.\r\nHope you\'ll write back!\r\n\r\nSee u soon, Claudia.', '2005-03-21 02:02:47', 1525, 0),
(1571, 13, 'Tom Konop', 'tdkonop@earthlink.net', 'MRDA-KOVE', 'Hello, I\'m doing some research on my ancestry and am unsure of the meaning of MRDA-KOVE. The context is \"He married <name> in MRDA-KOVE, Czechoslovakia\". It sounds like a town name, but that doesn\'t show up on any maps.\r\n\r\nThankyou\r\nTom', '2005-03-26 23:31:23', 0, 1),
(1572, 13, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'MRĂKOV', '\"MrdĂĄkovÄ\" is the locative case of \"MrdĂĄkov\", a village near DomaÂlice.\r\n\r\nMrdĂĄkov was renamed to \"MrĂĄkov\" in 19th century.\r\n', '2005-03-29 13:22:18', 1571, 0),
(1573, 13, 'VV', 'vladimirv@centrum.cz', 'Re: Translation', 'In Czech it is \"DobrĂ˝ Âivot.\"\r\n\r\n', '2005-03-31 21:58:50', 1563, 0),
(1574, 13, 'Erik Pioer', '', 'Re: MRĂKOV', 'I can imagine why! ;-)\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-04-01 17:38:44', 1572, 0),
(1575, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Translation', 'It\'s you, Jana. :-) \"The good life\" is the life of someone who \"si dobĹe Âije\", who \"se mĂĄ\".\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-04-01 17:57:05', 1569, 0),
(1576, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Translation', 'It\'s you, Jana. :-) \"The good life\" is the life of someone who \"si dobĹe Âije\", who \"se mĂĄ\".\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-04-01 17:57:26', 1569, 0),
(1577, 13, 'Daniel', 'ddfroman@juno.com', 'how to pronounce God and Family', 'I looked on the translation site and it said that god and family is Buh a rata, the u has a circle over it and one of the a\'s in rata has the inverted carat mark.   How is this pronounced in the czech language?', '2005-04-02 15:00:59', 0, 0),
(1578, 13, 'VV', 'vladimirv@centrum.cz', 'Re: how to pronounce God and Family', 'Sorry, but your translantion is utterly wrong. \"rata\" is not a Czech word at all. Correct translation is \"BĹŻh a rodina\" - there is a circle above \"u\".\r\nPronunciation might be like this (I\'m using an international phonetic alphabet used in all english - english explanatory dictionaries\"\r\n[bu:h ^ rodjin^] - a symbol ^ stands for the sound like in the middle of an english word \"cut\".\r\n\r\nVV', '2005-04-02 20:38:27', 1577, 0),
(1579, 13, 'VV', 'vladimirv@centrum.cz', 'Re: I have a question...', 'Be very careful. If you use this expression in this way (with the intonation mentioned), it will most likely mean (and express) something like \"You don\'t mean it\" or \"Are you kidding?\" or rather \"Are you mad you f.... asshole???!!!\"\r\n\r\nWe often use it if someone says or does something what you feel is offending you or makes you angry and mortified. In this case the stress is on the last sylable, the whole sound of the word is rising and sometimes ends with a very high pitch:-)\r\n\r\nVV', '2005-04-02 21:59:33', 1503, 0),
(1580, 13, 'Hila', 'mis_marple@hotmail.com', 'Question about adding \"A\" to a name', 'Hello - I recently gave an offical document to translation in Czech and the translator added \"A\" after my grandfather\'s first and last name - do you know why in certain cases you add \"A\" to a male name?\r\n\r\nThanks in advance', '2005-04-03 23:37:59', 0, 1),
(1581, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: how to pronounce God and Family', 'The sound in the middle of US English \"cut\" doesn\'t exist in Czech; perhaps Br E is different. For Czech \"a\", the sound in the middle of \"hot\" would do. If you want to be a perfectionist, try (if you\'re a speaker of Am E at least) saying \"hot\" and \"paw\" to yourself and learning to recognize the difference (the slight \"w\" at the end of the sound of the a in paw), then making sure the \"a\" you use is as in \"hot\".\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-04-04 16:23:43', 1578, 0),
(1582, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Question about adding', 'Why do you add \"s\" to \"Jack\" when describing three Jacks, three fellows named Jack, in a room? Because of grammar. And it\'s the same here, though it\'s not a plural as with the English -s. At the end of many male names, \"-a\"  indicates what\'s called the \"second case,\" which, to oversimplify, indicates possession or replaces the word \"of\" before a word. So for example if you grandfather was named Adam Vrut, and the document talked about his apartment, it might mention \"byt Adama Vruta\" (asuming I\'m not screwing up here and one or both of these doesn\'t use something other than -a... I still make mistakes here after all these years). That would mean \"Adam Vrut\'s apartment.\"\r\n\r\nBest regards,\r\n\r\nErik Piper', '2005-04-04 16:32:48', 1580, 0),
(1583, 15, 'Pero', 'bacciap4@kulawireless.net', 'Re: www.sokker.org', 'Pa ne interesira me foodball bas mnogo ali bi rado kontaktirao sa ljudima koji znaju ceski i hrvatski, srpski ili bosanski', '2005-04-06 00:17:17', 1551, 1),
(1584, 15, 'Robin', 'robin.cooper+bohemica@gmail.com', 'Most common words in Czech', 'Hello everyone, \r\n\r\nI\'ve been studying Czech for a while now and I think I\'m making slow but sure progress. \r\n\r\nA great resource for people learning English as a foreign language is the list of the 2000 or so most common words. most daily conversations take place withing this word-space (or so I\'ve heard) Here\'s an example list:\r\nhttp://www1.harenet.ne.jp/~waring/vocab/wordlists/lemma.html\r\n\r\nDoes a similar list exist for the Czech language?\r\n\r\nI\'m assuming that a listing for one word would imply it\'s derivatives as well: \r\neg. Adjective (male version, female version, neuter version, -er, -est etc.)', '2005-04-08 14:53:28', 0, 1),
(1585, 13, 'Aaron', 'azdesign.media@gmail.com', 'Slovak CD ROM', 'Does anyone know where I can obtain a copy of the Peace Corps Slovak CDROM?\r\n\r\nDakujem\r\nAaron', '2005-04-14 00:24:24', 0, 1),
(1586, 14, 'Lisa Uhlir', 'brewske@juno.com', 'czech culture', 'Hello,\r\n\r\nI have been asked by a teacher at my children\'s school to put together a small display of items to represent the czech heritage.  I am not czech, however my husband is.  What things would you recommend I try to obtain for the purpose of informing 700 children about this country.  Mind you most of the children in this school happed to be asian and hispanic, with limited english to begin with.  I have considered trying to make a traditional costume for my daughter to wear.  I also considered making kolace and cutting it up small for some to try.  All suggestions at this point would be appreciated.  I have about 5 weeks to get ready for this event.  Thank you,  Lisa', '2005-04-14 07:40:23', 0, 0),
(1587, 14, 'Lisa Uhlir', 'brewske@juno.com', 'czech culture', 'Hello,\r\n\r\nI have been asked by a teacher at my children\'s school to put together a small display of items to represent the czech heritage.  I am not czech, however my husband is.  What things would you recommend I try to obtain for the purpose of informing 700 children about this country.  Mind you most of the children in this school happed to be asian and hispanic, with limited english to begin with.  I have considered trying to make a traditional costume for my daughter to wear.  I also considered making kolace and cutting it up small for some to try.  All suggestions at this point would be appreciated.  I have about 5 weeks to get ready for this event.  Thank you,  Lisa', '2005-04-14 09:49:30', 0, 0),
(1588, 15, 'VladimĂ­r', '', 'Re: Most common words in Czech', 'http://nlp.fi.muni.cz/nlp/aisa/NlpCz/Frekvence_slov_lemmat.html', '2005-04-14 12:18:06', 1584, 0),
(1589, 15, 'Robin', 'robin.cooper+bohemica@gmail.com', 'Re: Most common words in Czech', 'D&#277;kuji vĂĄm!\r\n', '2005-04-14 13:20:35', 1588, 0),
(1590, 13, 'Beth Skinner', '', '', '', '2005-04-23 01:18:32', 0, 0);
INSERT INTO `messages` (`id`, `board_id`, `author`, `email`, `subject`, `text`, `sent`, `following`, `send_note`) VALUES
(1591, 13, 'Beth Skinner', 'BETHSKINNER@msn.com', 'Czech Culture', 'How is negative news handled in the Czech Republic?\r\nHow would typical business communicators refuse a request for a business favor(such as a contribution to a charity)?\r\nHow would typical business communicators refuse a customer\'s clain?\r\nHow would an individual be turned down for a job?', '2005-04-23 01:22:24', 0, 0),
(1592, 14, 'Shery Russ', 'writershery@yahoo.com', 'Looking for quotes from Czechs', 'Hi, I\'m Shery Russ, a freelance writer. I\'m doing a feature article on Czech culture, food & diet. \r\n\r\nI\'m looking for someone who is from the Czech Republic whom I can send a few questions (not more than 5) about his/her \r\n\r\ncountry via e-mail. I\'d like to be able to use a quote or two from someone who is from this country. If you prefer not to be \r\n\r\nidentified in my article, then I won\'t use your name, only your quote.\r\n\r\nThe article I\'m writing is for a web site that features recipes from around the world.\r\n\r\nI\'d appreciate it if you\'d be kind enough to share your knowledge of your country\'s culture. Please e-mail me at writershery \r\n\r\nAT yahoo DOT com.\r\n\r\nBest,\r\nShery Russ', '2005-04-26 21:07:39', 0, 1),
(1593, 13, 'zach', 'Jessrs@epix.net', ' need info about czech culture', ' I AM Doing a report  and need info about czech culture', '2005-04-27 01:07:47', 0, 0),
(1594, 13, 'zach', 'Jessrs@epix.net', ' need info about czech culture', ' I AM Doing a report  and need info about czech culture', '2005-04-27 01:08:25', 0, 0),
(1595, 14, 'Kristina', 'sugarbaby2o2@yahoo.com', 'Re: Exchange Program', 'I am fifteen, and I have lived in the Czech Republic almost every summer of my life.  It is the most gorgeous country with a beautiful and wonderful culture.  It would be an amazing experience for you, but the language is extremely difficult.  You could learn a great deal about the language, but you would probably not be able to have anything but a primitive conversation with the people living there.  Nevertheless, I am positive you would not regret going there as an exchange student. ', '2005-04-28 02:33:00', 1553, 0),
(1596, 14, 'Kristina', '', 'Re: czech culture', 'Trying to explain what Czech culture is like is almost impossible, but I think the costume idea is great!  You could also bring an elephant and talk about how that is known as a good luck charm in the Czech Republic.  You could bring oplatky (a type of round, thin, flat, sweet-like cookie) for everyone to taste if there were a smaller amount of children.  You could bring a poster of a castle in the CR.  Well, I hope these suggestions might help you a little!  Good luck!', '2005-04-28 02:38:16', 1586, 0),
(1597, 13, 'bartek', '', 'czech literature', 'Hi\r\nIm looking for some information about most popular genres in \r\nCzech literature in XIV/XV century(\"husitctvi\").Can anyone tell me where can I \r\nfind such information on the web?\r\ngreets\r\nbartek', '2005-05-01 15:13:28', 0, 0),
(1599, 14, 'Loretta Heisler', 'bapper26@aol.com', 'Greetings in Czech language', 'My daughter is doing a project on Czech Republic and she has to greet her class in Czech \"Hello, my name Emily\".  Could you please translate that sentence for us and give us the proper pronounciation.  Thank you so much. Gratefully, Loretta Heisler', '2005-05-05 11:41:45', 0, 0),
(1600, 13, 'jana', 'j.svobodova@voly.cz', 'Re: Translation', 'Hi Erik! good to know! (i stand corrected ;))\r\nIs it the \"the\"? (i\'ve just about given up on articles, they really are impossible to learn)\r\nhow would you get to a noun phrase, then? this is interesting, \'kdo se ma\' describes the person but can you think of an independent expression (embarassingly enough, i can\'t...)?\r\nj.\r\nps doing a project en-cz indirectness now (comparatice pragmatics), interested? ', '2005-05-08 00:02:01', 1575, 1),
(1601, 13, 'jana', '', 'Re: Czech Culture', 'hi Beth,\r\nnot sure how \'typical\' my views are but I\'d say that in general Czechs are much more direct, if they think \'no\' they will say \'no\' (well I do and get myself into all kinds of trouble for that quite reliably :) and maybe try to mitigate by giving reasons and explanations. ', '2005-05-08 00:11:12', 1591, 0),
(1602, 13, 'martin', '', 'Re: Question about adding \\', 'first: what is your last name?\r\n\r\nIn Czech it\'s quite normal for males to have a surname ending in Ă˝, if you\'re female it\'ll be changed to ĂĄ.\r\n(but that\'s czech grammar)\r\n\r\nMore likely, your grandpa was in the genitive or accusative case which in Czech results in an -a suffix.\r\n(my name, curiously, thus becomes Martina in the accusative...)', '2005-05-08 06:47:14', 1580, 0),
(1603, 13, ';-)', '', 'Re: how to pronounce God and Family-rubbish comment', 'Please, do try to learn proper English, American won\'t do.', '2005-05-08 06:57:02', 1581, 0),
(1607, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Greetings in Czech language', '\"DobrĂ˝ den, jmenuji se Emily.\"\r\n\r\nDOH-bree den, YMEN-oo-yee seh Emily.\r\n\r\nThe \"r\" is trilled in the front of your mouth, as in Spanish or in a Scottish accent (at least, so I\'ve heard regarding Scottish).\r\n\r\nThe \"Y\" in \"YMEN\" is pronounced as in \"yellow\".\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-05-11 20:08:53', 1599, 0),
(1608, 14, 'Jessica Wade', 'j_wade_m@excite.com', 'Czech immigration to Americas?', 'I am looking for some history on Czech immigration to the Americas. Does anyone know where I could find some historical information or anecdotal information?', '2005-05-12 00:17:52', 0, 1),
(1609, 13, 'Hila', 'mis_marple@hotmail.com', 'Re: Question about adding', 'Thank you for your help. In the same document my Grandmother\'s name was changed from Elsa to Elsy. Does it stem from the same Grammar rules of adding a/ĂĄ etc? Also could it be that you sometimes add novĂŠ instead of novĂĄ at the end of a female surname?', '2005-05-12 16:06:11', 1602, 1),
(1610, 13, 'Jar', 'jar_joyce@hotmail.com', 'Czech teacher Ipswich,UK', 'I would like to locate a Czech language teacher based in Ipswich, Suffolk.UK.\r\nI am a beginer and although I have worked in Brno I never got round to learning the language when I was based there. Now I am back in UK have decided to learn it!', '2005-05-13 00:52:07', 0, 1),
(1617, 17, 'VicenĂ§', 'vicen_ema@telefonica.net', 'Stanislava CoufalovĂĄ', 'Hi. I\'m Spanish, and I\'m searching pics of the Czech actress Stanislava CoufalovĂĄ. I find nothing but text through internet. \r\nCould anyone send me pics? It would be fantastic to have pics of the adult actress, and some of the film \"Sonata pro zrzku\" (I saw that film when I was a child).\r\nIs it possible to buy some film of Stanislava CoufalovĂĄ?\r\nThanks for all.\r\n ', '2005-05-23 16:45:33', 0, 1),
(1618, 13, 'Peter', 'peter.dain@tesco.net', 'hospoda, hospodarnost, Hospodin', 'Is there a common linguistic root \"hospod\" for all these words (pub, economy, the lord god) and similar words. What is the origin of it?\r\n ', '2005-05-24 01:29:29', 0, 0),
(1620, 15, 'online poker', 'absolut5134@freemail.com', 'online poker', ' <h1>You may find it interesting to check some information about<A HREF=\"http://www.williamchibbard.com/online-poker.html\"> online poker </A>  online poker  <A HREF=\"http://www.williamchibbard.com/online-poker.html\">http://www.williamchibbard.com/online-poker.html</A> <br/><A HREF=\"http://www.texas-hold-em-world.com/texas-hold-em.html\"> texas hold em </A>  texas hold em  <A HREF=\"http://www.texas-hold-em-world.com/texas-hold-em.html\">http://www.texas-hold-em-world.com/texas-hold-em.html</A> <br/><A HREF=\"http://www.texas-hold-em-world.com/online-poker.html\"> online poker </A>  online poker  <A HREF=\"http://www.texas-hold-em-world.com/online-poker.html\">http://www.texas-hold-em-world.com/online-poker.html</A> <br/><A HREF=\"http://www.e-online-poker-777.com/texas-hold-em.html\"> texas hold em </A>  texas hold em  <A HREF=\"http://www.e-online-poker-777.com/texas-hold-em.html\">http://www.e-online-poker-777.com/texas-hold-em.html</A> <br/><A HREF=\"http://www.e-online-poker-777.com/online-poker.html\"> online poker </A>  online poker  <A HREF=\"http://www.e-online-poker-777.com/online-poker.html\">http://www.e-online-poker-777.com/online-poker.html</A> <br/>... Thanks!!! </h1>', '2005-05-24 18:29:49', 0, 0),
(1621, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: hospoda, hospodarnost, Hospodin', 'I don\'t know, but it\'s clearly quite related to caring for people or things. It\'s also in (just examples; many more could be found)...\r\n\r\nhospodĂĄĹ (farmer, basically)\r\nhospodĂĄĹskĂ˝ (the intersection of the words \"financial\" and \"economic\")\r\nhospodaĹit s (to manage (a resource))\r\nhospodaĹstvĂ­ (essentially \"economy\")\r\nhospodskĂ˝ (barkeeper)\r\n\r\nHospodĂĄrnost is economy in the sense of efficiency. It\'s not a word you see very often, though.\r\n\r\nThere\'s a \"deeper\" root here, and that\'s hos- itself. Outside of the hospod- words, it usually (always?) appears as host-; it appears in host (guest), hostitel (host), hostinec (another word for a pub), vyhostit (to throw (a person) out of a place or country), etc.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-05-25 18:23:36', 1618, 0),
