(1638, 13, 'Majk', 'vesmir@gmail.com', 'prs/prsy vs prso/prsa', '\r\nCan someone give me a concise answer on the relationship between these two forms of the word?  It seems to be a lot more complicated than a simple colloquial/literary dichotomy.  The simplest answer seemed to be that prsa is for when speaking of the breast in a general way (ie, also among men), whereas prs is the word for a single breast, but I\'ve also come across the word prso in what seems to be technical literature, so I\'m still left unsatisfied..\r\nthanks, mike', '2005-06-05 13:53:30', 0, 0),
(1639, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tecso.net', 'Re: Czech postcard: can someone please translate these 2 lines?', 'Dobry den is good day. Dobrou chut, means good appetite, and je ta \r\ncesta means, this way is passable, or maybe is this way passable? Put together\r\nI\'m not sure what it means, maybe it is an idiom, or something you will get knowing the sender.', '2005-06-05 14:10:53', 1636, 0),
(1640, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: It is a question.', 'Bricho (hacek over r) or maybe zaloudek (hacek over z) if this is what you meant?', '2005-06-05 14:19:45', 1624, 0),
(1641, 13, 'kevin', 'handleyf@tesco.net', 'Re: It is a question.', 'Bricho (hacek over r) or maybe zaloudek (hacek over z) if this is what you meant?', '2005-06-05 14:22:35', 1624, 0),
(1642, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: prs/prsy vs prso/prsa', 'It\'s always seemed to me that there are some issues around the Czech language where not even the Czechs are sure which of two possibilities to use, with ratios of usage like 50/50 or at best 70/30. This seems to be one of them. \r\n\r\nSee for example this forum discussion (warning -- America-bashing): \r\nwww.novinky.cz/02/50/57.html\r\n\r\nOn the other hand, hmm. Here we have people who should know using prs consistently:\r\nwww.kojeni.cz/poradna.php?step=view&id=2\r\n\r\nJust like not every college professor has perfect spelling, it\'s possible that the technical literature was just bad usage -- entirely understandable due to the nasty gender jump in mid-declination. Those are hard enough to handle for everyday words, let alone something most people don\'t need to say every day, grumble grumble.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-06-06 15:23:02', 1638, 0),
(1643, 13, 'Erik', '', 'Re: prs/prsy vs prso/prsa', 'One other thing -- it\'s possible that both plurals are correct, just in different circumstances, with one of them being a duĂĄl -- like ucha tÄch hrnkĹŻ vs. uÂi tÄch muÂĹŻ. Prsa would be the not-the-two-of-a-body-part variant in that case, I guess. Plus even for the duĂĄl, the accusative plural reverts back to normal declination IIRC, so there too you\'d get prsa even for two good old-fashioned actual breasts as in two ears.\r\n\r\nAll this being, of course, just my castles in the air. :-) I really oughta just pull out FronÄk when I get home, but I doubt I\'ll remember. :-/\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-06-06 15:28:53', 1642, 0),
