(1730, 13, 'Perica BaÄa (BaÂa)', 'bacciap4@kulawireless.net', '***Why names of months are so different from other languages?', 'Also Croatian, Ukrainian, Byelorussian and Polish language has theirown names of months which are similar with Czech.', '2005-08-05 01:53:01', 1715, 1),
(1731, 13, 'Perica BaÄa (BaÂa)', 'bacciap4@kulawireless.net', 'Re: To Erik Piper', 'Director of Slavic department of Faculty of Filology Belegrade\r\n\r\n&#1059;&#1087;&#1088;&#1072;&#1074;&#1085;&#1080;&#1082;:\r\n\r\n&#1087;&#1088;&#1086;&#1092;. &#1076;&#1088; &#1055;&#1088;&#1077;&#1076;&#1088;&#1072;&#1075; &#1055;&#1080;&#1087;&#1077;&#1088; (Predrag Piper)\r\nhttp://www.fil.bg.ac.yu/katedre/slavistika/index.htm\r\nIs he your relative? Do you speak other language (serbo-croat)? I speak!\r\n\r\n\"Piper\" is name of one Slavic tribe from Montenegro :)\r\nI suppose next year \"Independent\" state!\r\n\r\nBye!', '2005-08-05 02:09:37', 1718, 1),
(1732, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: names', 'You are correct and I was in error. The worst part is, I know this; I just didn\'t check back over my post.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-05 16:04:53', 1729, 0),
(1733, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: names', '-i endings for fifth-case endings are quite common where I live (Brno) -- but I think in general, the Moravian dialect group is somewhat more \"Slovakish\" than the Bohemian one -- it only makes sense due to us being nearer to the Slovaks geographically than the Bohemians are.\r\n\r\nI\'m surprised to see the -ko endings above, since these are connected with feminine nouns and with masculine nouns ending in -a, but not with the -k diminutive endings. Are you sure they\'re not -ku?\r\n\r\nRegarding Karolina getting the colloquial root for KateĹina -- it could just be someone (including her herself) wishing she were named KateĹina and acting to fit. :-) Or perhaps a middle name... these aren\'t as big a deal in Czech as in (American?) English, but they still have their place.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-05 16:13:01', 1724, 0),
(1734, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: To Erik Piper', 'Actually, I\'ve always wondered why other people can\'t spell Erik. :-)\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-05 16:15:40', 1726, 0),
(1735, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: To Erik Piper', 'I get no farther in Croatian than DobrodoÂli a Dobar dan. :-) And I have no idea if those are the same throughout Serbo-Croat, especially since everyone started beating it into two languages out of spite (as far as I can tell). My blood is mostly from the Isles and from Germany, plus an eighth from Poland (but that has no connection to my being here in CZ).\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-05 16:18:58', 1731, 0),
(1736, 13, 'Perica BaÄa (BaÂa)', 'bacciap4@kulawireless.net', 'Re: To Erik Piper', 'It\'s interesting for me that your surname is \"Piper\" and director of Slavic department of Filological Faculty Prerag Piper. Surname \"Piper\" is rare in the World as mine BaÂa (BaÄa) - shortly means BatinĂĄÂ (BaÂa as TomĂĄÂ BaÂa from ZlĂ­n).\r\n\r\nSerbian and Croatian languages now are very similar. Croatian is 99%understandable in Serbia and Serbian is 99% understandable in Croatia! Standard Serbian and Croatian derived from neighbour similar dialects: Dialect of Dubrovnik (Croatian) Dialect of East Herzegovina (Serbian). That was the problem! Distance between these two (or one) dialects are about 20km. Croatia has a large number of dialects. But a large Serbs settled all ower Croatia during Ottoman period to defence zone \"Krajina\". These Serbs speak this dialect of Dubrovnik-East Herzegovina. So Croatian Reformer Ljudevit Gaj stopped one literar language (kajkavski - Zagreb region) and suggest to be used that dialect. The reason of that is creation of united Croatian language. This dialect was well known between Croatians. One time the Serbian and Croatian was united. But 10 years before war 1991 Serbian and Croatian was divided in two languages. Now there are Bosnian, with turkish and arabic words and the newest Montenegin, the language of Montenegro. But it\'s one language. Croatian has very large number of words with Czech origin. Montenegrin has a lot of words that are similar with Czech(npr. ÂtÄpan - ÂÄepan, ul - ul(iÂte)...).\r\n\r\nI used ex-Serbo-Croat term becouse I would like to comunicate with all people from ex-Yugoslavia. I suppose that tolerant peoples understand me.\r\n\r\nEric, I appologise if I cause embarassment, to force you to count blood cells.\r\nThanks for answer!\r\nBye!', '2005-08-05 17:39:36', 1735, 1),
(1737, 14, 'Gloria McMillan', 'glomc@dakotacom.net', 'Czech painter Staffa or Stauffa from 1890s', 'Dear Forum,  This is a real stumper.  Can anyone tell me about a Czech\r\npainter named Staffa or Stauffa?   I have two little paintings by this painter\r\nof his daughters dressed in folk costumes.  Staffa or Stauffa lived in Prague\r\nandf these paintings were from about the 1890s.\r\n\r\nI was wondering is these have other than sentimental value or where they might\r\nbe appraised in addition.\r\n\r\nSince we are going to Praha next summer, I need to finance part of this trip.\r\nWhile I like these paintings by Staffa, I could part with them if they\r\nhelped with that.\r\n\r\nThanks for any tips,\r\nGloria McMillan', '2005-08-09 02:51:05', 0, 1),
(1738, 14, 'Gloria McMillan', 'glomc@dakotacom.net', 'Re: Czech painter Staffa or Stauffa from 1890s', 'BTW, there is a hacek over the initial \"S\" in Staffa...', '2005-08-09 02:53:00', 1737, 0),
(1739, 13, 'Brian', 'bjlanc@yahoo.com', 'confused!', 'I have been attempting to learn the czech language and it hasn\'t been as easy as I thought it would be.  I bought a book to teach czech and I also got a czech english dictionary and all too often, there are a lot of different czech words for one word, for example,\"bring\".  I ask my mom and she only knows of one of the czech words for an english word.  She didn\'t know what the other czech words were.  My Grandpa is the same way.  Please help me to know why so many czech words for one enlish word.  THANK YOU!!', '2005-08-10 02:47:09', 0, 0),
