(1745, 13, 'Ralph', 'ralbion@hotmail.com', 'Obalis', 'Hello everybody,\r\n\r\nI come from Switzerland and my sister wants to call her daughter Obalis. Nice name, but I noticed that it means something in Czech...\r\n\r\nSo, what does Obalis means in Czech ?\r\n\r\nI would greatly appreciate your quick reply. Greetings from Switzelrand.\r\n\r\nRalph', '2005-08-10 16:33:33', 0, 0),
(1746, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re:', '\"Pak budu mĂ­t nÄjakou nadÄji\", I think.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-10 20:07:17', 1744, 0),
(1747, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Obalis', '\"Obalis\" means nothing.\r\n\r\n\"ObalĂ­Â\" means \"you [tu/du not vous/Sie] will wrap\".\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-10 20:09:33', 1745, 0),
(1748, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: confused!', 'There are a lot of words in *any* language for many of the words in *any* language. Language is a map of meaning; it\'s not meaning itself. Think of a political map of the USA and a geographic map: what is just \"Ohio\" in one will contain parts and wholes of a variety of geographic features in the other. What is just \"the Appalachians\" will contain parts of several states in the other. \r\n\r\nSometimes what one language likes to divide into several shades with different words gets lumped into one word in another language. More often it\'s even more complicated than that, and each language is using various shades, but they cover different things.\r\n\r\nSo for example:\r\n\r\ncarry - nĂŠst, pĹinĂŠst (carry to), odnĂŠst (carry from), etc., nosit, pĹinĂĄÂet, etc. ...\r\nbring - pĹinĂŠst, pĹinĂĄÂet ...\r\n\r\nand much much more...\r\n\r\nGood dictionaries will give at least a synonym for each word on the \"other side\", but since only the Czechs really care enough to make good Czech/English dictionaries, this will usuallly mean a Czech synonym. If you take a look at a good French/English dictionary in a bookstore, you\'ll see what I mean.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-10 20:19:48', 1743, 0),
(1749, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Re: ***Why names of months are so different from other languages?', 'Good information. They should have a same root. Somebody, tell me the origine, please.', '2005-08-11 10:16:17', 1730, 0),
(1750, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Names of floors of a building', 'The first floor is actually upstairs in England. It differs from ground floor. So is in many european languages including Russian. But in US and Japan first floor is equal to the ground floor. Someone told me Czech is the same as in US and Japan. Is it true? And why? Somebody tell me the reason.', '2005-08-12 11:22:16', 0, 0),
(1751, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Difference between venkov and vesnice', 'What is the difference betrween venkov and vesnice? In Russian dictionary both are dipicted as de\'revnya( &#1076;&#1077;&#1088;&#1077;&#1074;&#1085;&#1103; ). The russian is not so strict - this may be an answer.', '2005-08-12 11:28:06', 0, 0),
(1752, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Names of floors of a building', 'It\'s false. In Czech the \"prvnĂ­ patro\", also called the \"prvnĂ­ poschodĂ­\", is the one above the ground floor, which is called the \"pĹĂ­zemĂ­\" (\"pĹi\" is \"by\" and \"zemi\" is \"the ground\").\r\n\r\nI had trouble with it too, despite previous experience with French. :-)\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-12 15:46:25', 1750, 0),
(1753, 13, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Difference between venkov and vesnice', 'In Czech the difference is quite simple and fundamental - \"venkov\" is \"rural areas\" (but it\'s a collective noun in Czech), while \"vesnice\" is \"village\".\r\n\r\nVesnice is an ordinary part of day to day speech, while venkov is used in some specific contexts...\r\n\r\n- the naming of administrative districts, e.g. Brno-venkov (Brno being my \"home\" city) means \"Brno\'s administrative district for the rural areas that are within its competency.\" (Actually, looking at the Web pages containing \"venkov,\" it seems that maybe no other district is named \"venkov\". Maybe my vision is distorted by my location.)\r\n- the rural areas of (generally) the Czech Republic, as a whole, e.g. \"Centrum obnovy venkovy\"... although phrases like \"ÄeskĂ˝ venkov\" and \"nĂĄÂ venkov\" are also used. (However, \"nĂĄÂ venkov\" -- which I saw with no prior direct indication   that \"our\" means \"Czech\" -- is a nice example of the tendency to use \"our\" to mean \"Czech\" in Czech.)\r\n\r\nOkay, enough, enough.\r\n\r\ne.', '2005-08-12 15:56:37', 1751, 0),
(1754, 14, 'Erik Piper', '', 'Re: Czech painter Staffa or Stauffa from 1890s', 'Sadly, Google is of no help -- no painter Âtaffa\'s are coming up.\r\n\r\nErik', '2005-08-16 18:31:55', 1737, 0),
(1755, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Re: Names of floors of a building', 'Thanks, Erik. I will look into this matter further.', '2005-08-22 13:38:10', 1752, 0),
(1756, 13, 'nomonomo', '', 'Re: Difference between venkov and vesnice', 'Thaks a lot, Erik. It is very kind and informative. Actually, I fell in love with Czech!', '2005-08-22 13:42:00', 1753, 0),
