(1831, 13, 'Dave C.', 'Eveninthedark73@aol.com', 'Pronouncing a Czech name', 'I will be hosting an exchange student from the Czech Republic starting from June 2006-2007. His name is Josef VĂ­&#232;kosky. From the best I can figure out his name is pronoused YAH-sef VEE-ay-kah-skee.', '2006-02-14 14:55:37', 0, 1),
(1832, 13, 'V.', '', 'Re: Difficulties with English', 'The problems concerning the English grammar must be exactly the same as the Czech and Polish languages are very similar (West Slavic):\r\n\r\n- articles ( /the?/ transformer is /a?/ device ..., we use no articles in such cases)\r\n- verbal system of tenses (I have written vs. I wrote, I am writing vs. I write)\r\n- phrasal verbs (get /on, off, in, out, .../, put, go, give, break, and many others), that hardly correspond with the Czech/Polish ones\r\n\r\nAbout pronuntiation:\r\n\r\nhttp://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-native_pronunciations_of_English#Hungarian\r\n', '2006-02-15 17:20:14', 1829, 0),
(1833, 13, 'V.', '', 'Re: Difficulties with English', 'http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-native_pronunciations_of_English#Czech', '2006-02-15 17:30:39', 1832, 0),
